          "file": "Juris/Unit5_Sources_of_Law_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "cases",
          "facts": "The question whether the Jain community's custom — a widow may adopt without her husband's consent — prevails in the community \"as such,\" across sects and localities, or only in particular localities. Earlier authorities: (1947) 74 IA 254 (AIR 1948 PC 177); Sheo Singh Rai v. Mussumut Dakho (1878) 5 IA 87; Chotay Lal (1878) 6 IA 15.",
          "ratio": "The Supreme Court, reviewing \"the large number of cases having a bearing on this point,\" sustained the custom as one prevailing in the Jain community as such , irrespective of the locality where they reside or the sect in which they are divided (with the Madras and Punjab exception noted).",
          "issues": "Is a custom sustainable for a whole community irrespective of locality and sect?",
          "arguments": "For the community-wide custom: the evidence and decisions showed uniform Jain usage (except in Madras and Punjab); the community's identity is not territorial. Against: custom is territorial and sect-bound; a community-wide claim cannot be proved as one custom. (The reading reports the outcome and the cases reviewed.)",
          "principleEvolved": "<p><b>Principle Evolved & Doctrinal Significance:</b> The Supreme Court, reviewing \"the large number of cases having a bearing on this point,\" sustained the custom as one prevailing in the Jain community as such , irrespective of the locality where they reside or the sect in which they are divided (with the Madras and Punjab exception noted).. This authority establishes foundational principles governing judicial precedent, statutory construction, and constitutional jurisprudence under the University of Delhi curriculum.</p>"
