          "file": "Juris/Unit5_Sources_of_Law_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "cases",
          "facts": "One occurrence, two litigations: furnace oil was carelessly allowed to spill overboard from the S.S. Wagon Mound into Sydney Harbour; fire damage followed. No. 1: the Privy Council proceeded on the lower court's finding that, according to the scientific opinion of the day, ignition in such circumstances was not reasonably foreseeable — no liability for fire damage. No. 2 (different plaintiffs, same occurrence): the lower court stated that a bare possibility of fire might have been foreseeable, but that the chance was \"so remote that it should be ignored\" — no liability.",
          "ratio": "No. 2: reversing the lower court, the defendants were held liable in negligence for fire damage after all. (The reading also notes: Re Polemis , disapproved in Wagon Mound though never overruled, was \"declared to be no longer law\" in the light of the changed law of remoteness — the changed-conditions principle.)",
