          "unit": "Western School of Thought – I (Positivism)",
          "file": "Juris/Unit6_Western_School_of_Thought_I_Positivism_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "cases",
          "facts": "<p>Foundational analytical positivist work of John Austin (1832). Austin set out to distinguish 'laws properly so called' from positive morality, customary norms, and divine laws, seeking a scientific, non-metaphysical definition of jurisprudence.</p>",
          "ratio": "<p>Austin's Command Theory of Law: Law is the <b>command of the sovereign, backed by the threat of sanctions, habitually obeyed by the political society, with the sovereign not in the habit of obedience to any human superior</b>. Customary law and international law lack sovereign command and are classified as 'positive morality'.</p>",
          "issues": "<p>(1) What is law properly so called? (2) What are the essential constituent elements of positive law (the command theory)? (3) What distinguishes legal obligations from moral and religious norms?</p>",
          "arguments": "<p><b>Analytical Positivism (Austin):</b> Law is a species of command. A command is the expression of a wish by a rational being that another shall do or forbear, accompanied by the threat of evil (sanction) in the event of disobedience. Only commands issued by a political superior (sovereign) to political inferiors constitute positive law.</p>",
          "principleEvolved": "<p><b>Imperative / Command Theory of Law (Austin):</b> (1) Law is strictly separated from morality (the 'is' vs 'ought' dichotomy). (2) Four essential elements of positive law: Command, Duty, Sanction, and Sovereignty. (3) Sovereignty is legally illimitable, indivisible, and continuous.</p>"
