          "unit": "Western School of Thought – I (Positivism)",
          "file": "Juris/Unit6_Western_School_of_Thought_I_Positivism_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "cases",
          "facts": "<p>H.L.A. Hart's masterwork (1961) offering a linguistic and analytical critique of John Austin's command model, introducing law as a complex social phenomenon comprising a union of primary and secondary rules.</p>",
          "ratio": "<p>Law is the <b>Union of Primary Rules of Obligation</b> (imposing duties: e.g. criminal law, tort) and <b>Secondary Rules</b> (conferring powers to cure defects): (i) <i>Rule of Recognition</i> (curing uncertainty), (ii) <i>Rules of Change</i> (curing static nature), and (iii) <i>Rules of Adjudication</i> (curing inefficiency).</p>",
          "issues": "<p>(1) Why is the 'gunman writ large' model of Austin deficient in explaining legal authority and obligation? (2) How does a legal system transition from a primitive pre-legal society to a mature legal order?</p>",
          "arguments": "<p><b>Hart's Linguistic & Hermeneutic Critique:</b> Austin confuses 'being obliged' (gunman situation coerced by fear) with 'having an obligation' (internalized rule-following). A legal system cannot function on habitual obedience alone; it requires an 'internal point of view' where officials accept rules as common standards of behaviour.</p>",
          "principleEvolved": "<p><b>Hart's Rule of Recognition & Legal Positivism:</b> (1) The criterion of legal validity in a modern legal system is the <b>Rule of Recognition</b>, which is an empirical social practice accepted by officials from the internal point of view. (2) Soft Positivism: Legal validity may incorporate moral principles if the Rule of Recognition so specifies, but law and morality are not conceptually identical.</p>"
