          "examStrategy": "Unit 2 completes the arc that Unit 1 began. You have now followed the Bharatiya idea of law from its cosmic ground (Ṛta) through its human obligation (Kartavya), its institutional form (Kautilya's state and courts), its political origin (Rājyotpatti — from matsya-nyāya to the Dharma-based contract), and its perfected ideal (Rāmarājya, the welfare state of Dharma embodied). The four readings are a single argument: law in the Indian tradition is not the will of the sovereign but the order of the cosmos, and the sovereign is its servant. That is why the modern Indian state's own motto is \"यतो धर्मस्ततो जयः\" — and why, in any comparison you are asked to write, the Indian thesis is not memory but theory: a Grundnorm with content, a contract under a higher law, a king who is divine only while he is dharmic, and a justice that even a dog can demand. Master the texts, quote the verses, name the comparisons — and write, as the DU material does, with pride and precision. Faculty of Law, University of Delhi, Jurisprudence – I (Legal Method), Course Code LB-106 , I Semester LL.B. (August 2025), pp. 38–77 — four prescribed readings (Seema Singh; Radhakrishnan Pillai; Ruchi Rawat; Vinayak Sharma & Ruchi Rawat), and pp. 1–38 of the same casebook for the cross-fed Unit-1 material (Parasain; Dhyani; Seema Singh & Yadav; Dhavan; Sudame; DU Case Material Jurists). Official pattern reference: DU Ist Term – Jurisprudence-I (LB-101) question paper, December 2015 (transcribed in the Unit-1 companion file). Case law cited by the readings and cross-verified reporters: A.S. Narayana Deekshitulu v. State of A.P. (1996) 9 SCC 548; Gujarat Urja Vikas Nigam Ltd. v. Essar Power Ltd. (2008) 4 SCC 755; Abhiraj Kuer v. Debendra Singh AIR 1962 SC 351; Vytla Sitanna v. Marivada Venkataratnam (1934) 36 BOMLR 563 (PC); Dnyaneshwar B. Ganwat v. Karyakari Abhiyanta, 2010 SCC OnLine Bom 766; Rattan Lal v. Vardhu Chand AIR 1976 SC 589; Ashok Kumar v. Union of India AIR 1991 SC 1792; Ramon Services Pvt. Ltd. v. Subhash Kapoor AIR 2001 SC 207; Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) 6 SCC 241; Bangaluru Medical Trust v. B.S. Muddappa AIR 1991 SC 1902; ADM Jabalpur AIR 1976 SC 1207; Maneka Gandhi AIR 1978 SC 597; and the Privy Council custom cases (Collector of Madura; Bhyah Ram Singh; Rutcheputty; Balwant Rao; Jogdamba; Muthukaruppa). Comparative authorities: Hobbes ( Leviathan ), Locke, Rousseau, James I (1609), Plato ( Republic ), K. Popper ( The Open Society and its Enemies ), Austin, Kelsen, Hohfeld, Karl Marx & Swami Karpatri Maharaj ( Marxwaad Aur Ramrajya , as cited in the reading).",
