          "examStrategy": "Unit 4 is the institutional photograph of everything the first three units argued. Its four sub-topics are the four components of a legal system: the Preamble gives the principles — \"Justice, social, economic and political,\" and the philosophy of equal justice that right-from-the-start colours the whole reading; the hierarchy gives the institutions — one pyramid, specialised tribunals and regulators, and every appeal path converging on the Supreme Court, whose Art. 136 is the modern echo of the ancient king's final court; legal services give the access — a constitutional mandate that is \"not charity… but a constitutional obligation and compulsion,\" enforced by a trilogy of judgments from Hussainara to Suk Das , and institutionalised in CILAS, the 1987 Act, NALSA, PIL and the Lok Adalat, \"the jurisprudence of peace\"; and the profession gives the personnel — a unified, self-governing Bar, born of a colonial monopoly in 1774 and completed in 1961, whose own rules make \"legal services to the poor… one of the social obligations of every lawyer.\" The casebook's own test of the system is the question it puts in its pages: \"What is the use of the system, which does not help lowly and lost, poor and downtrodden and which creates distance between law and justice?\" — and its answer is the unit you have just studied: promise, structure, access and profession, designed so that the distance between law and justice need not exist. Write it as one system, not four topics — that is the difference between a pass and a distinction. Faculty of Law, University of Delhi, Jurisprudence – I (Legal Method), Course Code LB-106 , I Semester LL.B. (August 2025), Unit 4, pp. 108–138: (1) Anupam Jha, \"Hierarchy of Courts, Tribunals & Commissions in India & their Jurisdiction\"; (2) R. Swaroop, \"Legal Service/Aid and Lok Adalat\"; (3) A.N. Veera Raghavan, \"Legal Profession and The Advocates Act, 1961,\" 14 Journal of the Indian Law Institute 228–262 (1972). Cross-fed from the same casebook: Menon, \"Our Legal System\" (Unit 3, pp. 103–107); Sudame (Unit 3, pp. 83–102); the Unit-1 and Unit-2 readings (pp. 1–77). Authorities cited by the readings and cross-verified: Hussainara Khatoon v. Home Secretary, State of Bihar , AIR 1979 SC 1369; Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India , AIR 1978 SC 597; Khatri v. State of Bihar , AIR 1981 SC 928; Suk Das v. Union Territory of Arunachal Pradesh , AIR 1986 SC 991; Centre of Legal Research v. State of Kerala , AIR 1986 SC 2195; People's Union for Democratic Rights v. Union of India , AIR 1982 SC 1473; S.P. Gupta v. President of India , AIR 1982 SC 149; M.C. Mehta v. Union of India , AIR 1987 SC 1086; Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India , AIR 1984 SC 802; Raj Kishore v. State , AIR 1969 Cal 321; Aswini Kumar Ghosh v. Arabinda Ghosh , AIR 1952 SC 369; State of Kerala v. Kuttan , 1988 Cri LJ 453; Dineshbhai Dhemenrai v. State of Gujarat , 2001 (1) GLR 603; Rhem v. Malcolm , 377 F. Supp. 995; Jackson v. Bishop (US). Instruments: Regulating Act, 1773; Charter of 26-3-1774 (Cl. 11); Bengal Regulations VII of 1793, XXVII of 1814, XII of 1833; Bengal Practitioners Act, 1846; Legal Practitioners Acts, 1855 and 1879; Legal Practitioners (Women) Act, 1923; Indian Bar Councils Act, 1926; All-India Bar Committee (Justice S.R. Das); Law Commission Reports (14th, 41st, 48th); Legal Practitioners Bill, 1959; Advocates Act, 1961; Federal Court Rules, 1942; Supreme Court Rules, 1950 (Order IV); Supreme Court Advocates (Practice in High Court) Act, 1951; Constitution of India (Arts. 14, 19, 21, 22(1), 32, 136, 137, 143, 145(1), 226, 227, 39-A, 51-A; 42nd Amendment); CrPC, 1973 (ss. 303–304); Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987; and the parent Acts of the regulators and tribunals in Jha's diagrams (Competition Act, 2002; Electricity Act, 2003; PNGRB Act, 2006; TRAI Act, 1997; IBC, 2016; SEBI Act, 1992; IRDA Act, 1999; PFRDA Act, 2013; Administrative Tribunals Act; Consumer Protection Act; Industrial Disputes Act; Motor Vehicles Act; Income Tax Act, 2025; Delhi Rent Control Act; Recovery of Debts and Bankruptcy Act; Railways Act; Companies Act; RTI Act; CGST Act, 2017; Commercial Courts Act, 2015).",
