          "facts": "Minors executed a mortgage bond for ₹12,000 falsely representing themselves as adults. The mortgagee sued to enforce the mortgage against the property or seek personal decree for repayment of the money advanced.",
          "issues": "Whether a minor can be compelled to refund money consideration where the lender is the plaintiff suing to enforce the claim.",
          "ratio": "Sulaiman CJ strictly applied Leslie v. Sheill, holding that money cannot be traced once spent, and granting an order for repayment of money against an infant defendant would be indirectly enforcing a void contract. Restitution under Section 41 SRA 1877 was available only when the minor was the plaintiff seeking equitable cancellation.",
          "examTips": "Note that the 1963 Specific Relief Act (Section 33(2)) resolved this conflict by adopting the Khan Gul view, allowing restitution whether the minor is plaintiff or defendant.",
          "principleEvolved": "Note that the 1963 Specific Relief Act (Section 33(2)) resolved this conflict by adopting the Khan Gul view, allowing restitution whether the minor is plaintiff or defendant.",
          "arguments": "<p><b>Plaintiff:</b> A minor who deceitfully obtains money by falsely claiming full age should not be allowed to retain the ill-gotten gains in a court of equity.</p><p><b>Defendant (Minor):</b> Under Section 11 ICA, an infant cannot be sued for breach of contract or in tort if the tort is directly connected with the contract; equitable restitution extends only to traceable property, not money.</p>"
