          "marks": "20 marks",
          "unitNumber": 5,
          "unit": "Topic 5: Capacity to Contract (Minor's Agreement & Relief)",
          "file": "Contract/DU_LB102_Topic5_Capacity_to_Contract.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq-5",
          "question": "Is a minor liable for necessaries supplied to him? Discuss the scope of Section 68 of the Indian Contract Act with the help of decided cases.",
          "modelAnswer": "<h5>Model answer (skeleton)</h5>\n        <ul>\n          <li>Position: a minor cannot contract even for necessaries, so S.68 creates a <b>quasi-contractual/statutory</b> claim.</li>\n          <li>Conditions: supply to incapable person (or one he is legally bound to support); necessaries suited to his condition in life; claim is <b>from the property</b> of the incapable person, not personally (Mohori Bibee — quote the observation).</li>\n          <li>What are “necessaries suited to condition in life”: food, clothing, shelter, education; not mere luxuries; and in Ajudhia Prasad <b>marriage expenses were held not “necessaries”</b>, so S.68 failed.</li>\n          <li>Illustrations (a) lunatic, (b) wife &amp; children.</li>\n        </ul>"
