          "file": "Contract/DU_LB102_Topic5_Capacity_to_Contract.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq-6",
          "question": "Can a mortgagee enforce a mortgage executed by a minor against him after he attains majority? Discuss with reference to Mohori Bibee, Khan Gul and Ajudhia Prasad.",
          "modelAnswer": "<h5>Model answer (skeleton)</h5>\n        <ul>\n          <li>Mohori: minor’s mortgage is void ab initio; no enforcement; Ss. 64/65 don’t apply.</li>\n          <li>Khan Gul: minor not estopped, but equity may order restitution (majority).</li>\n          <li>Ajudhia Prasad: the controlling case — a mortgagee cannot get a <b>money-decree or a decree for sale</b> against minor-mortgagors on a void mortgage, even for fraudulent concealment of minority; S.43 T.P. Act inapplicable.</li>\n          <li>Conclusion: after majority the void mortgage is still void (cannot be ratified); the mortgagee has no remedy in contract, and no restitution of money against the minor as defendant.</li>\n        </ul>"
