          "examStrategy": "(1) Distinguish definite tender from standing offer immediately. (2) If an agency accepts a standing offer, remember: NO contract for whole quantity until individual supply order is issued. (3) Cancellation of standing offer is valid for future unplaced orders.",
          "caseMap": "Maddala Thathiah — SC 1966 · Rajendra Kumar Verma — MP HC 1972 · Union of India v. Maddala Thathiah.",
          "htmlContent": "<section id=\"revision\">\n    <h2><span class=\"secnum\">6</span> Rapid-Revision Pack</h2>\n    <p class=\"hsub\">Memory kit for Topic 3.</p>\n    <h3>6.1 One-line takeaway from each case</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Maddala Thathiah</b> — tender = offer; definite-quantity acceptance = contract; maximum-quantity acceptance = standing offer; each order = separate contract; cancellation clause covers only un-ordered supplies.</li>\n      <li><b>Rajendra Kumar Verma</b> — a tender may be withdrawn before acceptance; a tender clause cannot take away S.5’s right of revocation.</li>\n      <li><b>Kanhaiya Lal Aggarwal</b> — courts rarely interfere in Government awards unless arbitrary/mala fide; essential-vs-collateral condition test; a rebate offered with the tender is valid.</li>\n      <li><b>Haridwar Singh</b> — auction “subject to confirmation”: provisional acceptance ≠ contract; needs confirmation of a subsisting offer + communication to the bidder.</li>\n      <li><b>Indian Airlines v. Madhuri</b> — standard-form ticket binds if terms noticed; ICA (incl. S.23) does not govern common carriers; a clear exemption clause may exclude even negligence.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>6.2 Quick formulas</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Invitation to tender / auction notice → invitation to treat.</b></li>\n      <li><b>Tender / bid → offer (S.2(a)), revocable before acceptance (S.5).</b></li>\n      <li><b>Acceptance of definite-quantity tender → contract; of maximum-quantity tender → standing offer.</b></li>\n      <li><b>Fall of hammer → acceptance (subject to reserve &amp; confirmation).</b></li>\n      <li><b>“Subject to confirmation” → provisional acceptance → no contract till confirmed &amp; communicated.</b></li>\n      <li><b>Ticket with printed terms → standard-form contract binding if notice given.</b></li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>6.3 Traps to avoid</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Do not treat the advertisement inviting tenders, or the auctioneer’s call for bids, as an offer.</li>\n      <li>Do not say “acceptance of a tender always creates a contract” — for a maximum quantity it creates only a standing offer (Maddala).</li>\n      <li>Do not treat provisional acceptance in a confirmation-auction as a concluded contract (Haridwar).</li>\n      <li>Do not forget <b>communication</b> of the acceptance to the bidder — it is essential (Haridwar).</li>\n      <li>Do not apply S.23 ICA to strike down a common carrier’s exemption clause (Madhuri) — the ICA does not govern carriers; construe the clause strictly.</li>\n      <li>Do not say a tenderer can never withdraw — he may withdraw before acceptance, and a tender clause cannot remove that right (Rajendra).</li>\n    </ul>\n    <h3>6.4 Glossary</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Invitation to treat:</b> an inducement inviting offers (tender notice, auction).</li>\n      <li><b>Tender / bid:</b> an offer to contract on stated terms.</li>\n      <li><b>Standing offer:</b> an open offer to supply up to a maximum; accepted piecemeal by orders.</li>\n      <li><b>Provisional/conditional acceptance:</b> acceptance subject to confirmation — does not conclude the contract.</li>\n      <li><b>Reserve price:</b> the minimum below which an auctioneer may refuse bids.</li>\n      <li><b>Standard-form / exemption clause:</b> pre-printed terms (often excluding liability) in a ticket/contract.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n\n  <div class=\"footer\">\n    <p><b>Sources:</b> DU LL.B. I-Term case material — <i>Principles of Contract (General Principles)</i>, Faculty of Law, University of Delhi (Jan 2023), Topic 3 (pp. 30–54) incl. the rubric and the quoted authorities (Authoritative Contract Precedent, Tata Cellular, G.J. Fernandez, Irrawaddy Flotilla Co., Ludditt, etc.); <i>The Indian Contract Act, 1872</i> (Act 9 of 1872) §§ 2–10. Case extracts and quotations are from the DU case-material reproduction of the judgments.</p>\n    <p><b>Prepared for exam revision of Topic 3 — “Making of an Agreement — Special Situations: Tenders and Auctions.”</b> This is a study aid; it does not replace the primary statute or the official DU case material. Verify citations against the latest editions. Hypothetical “scene” visuals are schematic learning aids, not official depictions.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:10px\">◉ All diagrams and scene visuals are inline SVG; the file needs no external resources.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>"
