          "examStrategy": "(1) In any minor problem, first state: minor's agreement is void ab initio (Mohori Bibee). (2) Reject mortgage enforcement immediately. (3) If lender asks for money refund, cite Leslie v. Sheill (no tort indirect enforcement of contract), then apply S. 33(2) Specific Relief Act 1963.",
          "caseMap": "12. Mohori Bibee (1903) · 13. Leslie v. Sheill (1914) · 14. Khan Gul (1928) · 15. Ajudhia Prasad (1938).",
          "htmlContent": "<section id=\"revision\">\n    <h2><span class=\"secnum\">8</span> Rapid-Revision Pack</h2>\n    <p class=\"hsub\">One-glance memory hooks for the last hour before the paper.</p>\n\n    <h3>8.1 One-line rules</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>S.11:</b> competent = majority + sound mind + not disqualified.</li>\n      <li><b>S.12:</b> sound mind judged <i>at the time</i> by understanding + rational judgment; lucid intervals allow contracts.</li>\n      <li><b>Minor’s agreement:</b> void ab initio (Mohori Bibee).</li>\n      <li><b>Ss. 64/65:</b> presuppose a contract between competent parties — do NOT apply to a minor (Mohori; Ajudhia).</li>\n      <li><b>S.68:</b> necessaries to an incapable person — reimbursement only <b>from his property</b>, not personally.</li>\n      <li><b>Estoppel:</b> a minor is not estopped from pleading minority (Khan Gul).</li>\n      <li><b>Restitution:</b> no money-decree against a minor-<b>defendant</b> (Ajudhia Prasad); specific traceable property may be restored; a minor-<b>plaintiff</b> must do equity (SRA § 33).</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>8.2 Case one-liners</h3>\n    <div class=\"grid\">\n      <div class=\"card tone1\"><h4>Mohori Bibee (PC, 1903)</h4><p>Minor’s mortgage void ab initio; Ss. 64/65 inapplicable; no estoppel where the lender knew of infancy; no repayment ordered.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"card tone2\"><h4>Khan Gul (Lahore FB, 1928)</h4><p>Minor NOT estopped from pleading minority; but majority held he may be compelled in equity to make restitution of the benefit obtained by fraud, even as defendant.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"card tone3\"><h4>Ajudhia Prasad (All. FB, 1937)</h4><p>No money-decree/sale against minor-mortgagors on a void mortgage; S.65 &amp; S.43 T.P. Act inapplicable; rejects Khan Gul money-restitution; restitution only where minor is plaintiff/specific property.</p></div>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>8.3 Self-test</h3>\n    <details><summary>Q1. X, aged 17, mortgages his house to Y for ₹50,000. Y knew X was a minor. Can Y enforce the mortgage?</summary><div class=\"body\"><p><b>No.</b> The mortgage is void ab initio (Mohori Bibee). Ss. 64/65 don’t apply; and as Y knew of the minority there is no equity for repayment.</p></div></details>\n    <details><summary>Q2. Same facts, but X falsely stated he was 21 and Y did not know. After X attains majority, Y sues for sale. What is the result?</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>X is not estopped (Khan Gul), and under Ajudhia Prasad a mortgagee cannot get a money-decree/sale against a minor (void ab initio, no ratification). The void mortgage remains unenforceable.</p></div></details>\n    <details><summary>Q3. A minor, misrepresenting his age, buys a bicycle and it is still with him, identifiable. Can the seller recover it?</summary><div class=\"body\"><p><b>Yes</b> — restitution of <b>specific traceable property</b> may be ordered in equity, as the property belongs to the seller and can be followed; this does not enforce the void contract.</p></div></details>\n    <details><summary>Q4. A grocer supplies milk and clothes to a minor’s family. Can he recover the price from the minor?</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Not personally from the minor, but he is entitled to <b>reimbursement from the minor’s property</b> for necessaries suited to his condition in life (S.68) — including, by Illustration (b), supplies to those the minor is legally bound to support.</p></div></details>\n    <details><summary>Q5. A lunatic executes a sale deed during a period of lucidity. Is it valid?</summary><div class=\"body\"><p><b>Yes.</b> Under S.12 a person usually of unsound mind may contract when of sound mind (lucid interval) — S.12 Ill. (a).</p></div></details>\n\n    <hr>\n    <div class=\"tip\"><b>Parting tip:</b> For a full 20-mark answer on a minor’s agreement, open by quoting Ss. 10 &amp; 11, then march through the three DU cases in order — <b>Mohori</b> (void ab initio; Ss. 64/65 inapplicable) → <b>Khan Gul</b> (no estoppel; equity may order restitution) → <b>Ajudhia Prasad</b> (prevailing law: no money-restitution against a minor-defendant). Close with S.68 (necessaries, against property only) and SRA § 33 (minor-plaintiff must do equity).</div>\n  </section>\n\n  <div class=\"footer\">\n    <p><b>DU LL.B. I-Term · Principles of Contract (General Principles) — LB-102 · Topic 5.</b> Primary source: <i>Cases Selected &amp; Edited, Faculty of Law, University of Delhi (Jan 2023)</i>, Topic 5 (Mohori Bibee, Khan Gul, Ajudhia Prasad) and <i>The Indian Contract Act, 1872</i> (Ss. 10, 11, 12, 64, 65, 68), the Specific Relief Act, 1963 (S.33) and the Indian Majority Act, 1875. Prepared as self-contained revision notes; cross-check statutes and case citations against your official handouts and report any needed corrections.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:4px\">Purpose: study aid only. Not a substitute for the DU casebook or the syllabus. Verify every citation before relying on it in the exam.</p>\n  </div>\n\n</div>"
