          "arguments": "<p><b>Appellant (Wife):</b> Sought restitution of conjugal rights to preserve the marriage; supported the constitutionality of Section 9 HMA as a means to promote reconciliation, not marital coercion.</p><p><b>Respondent (Husband):</b> Relied on the Andhra Pradesh High Court's ruling in <i>T. Sareetha</i> that Section 9 violates Articles 14 and 21 by coercing sexual consortium and infringing bodily autonomy.</p>",
          "principleEvolved": "<p><b>Constitutionality of Restitution of Conjugal Rights:</b> (1) Section 9 HMA is constitutional and does not violate Articles 14 and 21. (2) The decree serves a social purpose of reconciliation and consortium; the court does not coerce physical sexual intercourse, and enforcement under Order 21 Rule 32 CPC is restricted to financial attachment, not bodily arrest. (Overruling <i>T. Sareetha</i> and approving Delhi HC's <i>Harvinder Kaur</i>).</p>"
