          "facts": "Madras Bar Association challenged the constitutional validity of Chapter XXVII (Sections 407–434) of the Companies Act, 2013 establishing the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) and National Company Law Appellate Tribunal (NCLAT). The challenge attacked the transfer of company jurisdiction from High Courts to tribunals, executive dominance in the selection committee, and the eligibility qualifications for technical members.",
          "issues": "Constitutional validity of the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) and NCLAT provisions under Companies Act 2013.",
          "arguments": "Challenge based on separation of powers and independence of the judiciary.",
          "ratio": "A.K. Sikri J. for the 5-Judge Constitution Bench held that Parliament possesses legislative competence to establish specialist company tribunals. However, tribunalisation must satisfy judicial independence and separation of powers: (1) The selection committee cannot be executive-dominated and must be headed by the Chief Justice of India or nominee with a casting vote, (2) Technical members must possess specialized expertise and cannot sit without judicial members, and (3) Provisions allowing joint-secretaries as technical members violated judicial independence and were struck down.",
          "examTips": "Foundational 5-Judge Constitution Bench ruling on tribunalisation and judicial review."
