          "year": "DU LL.B. Past Paper",
          "marks": "10–20 Marks",
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "unit": "Topic 3: Company's Constitutional Documents (MOA & AOA)",
          "file": "sem 3/company/DU_LLB_Company_Law_Topic_3_Constitutional_Documents.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq-u3-16",
          "question": "6(a). “Articles constitute a contract between the company and the members and members inter se.” Discuss. Professional practice",
          "modelAnswer": "**Answer spine:** State § 10 and distinguish its legal fiction from ordinary bilateral contracting. The strongest proposition is company–member reciprocity in membership matters. A member may enforce an Article against the company and is bound by it in that capacity. Articles also organise member rights *inter se*, and courts may permit direct relief in a suitable membership dispute; but orthodox analysis resists treating every Article as an independent bilateral promise by each member to every other. Identify the right, identify the proper claimant/defendant, and apply the *qua*-member test. An employment, professional or supplier benefit calls for a separate contract, as *Eley* illustrates."
