          "question": "PYQ 1 — Q.2(a)/2019: A sues B for 2 years’ rent (B tenant). B admits tenancy, pleads paid without receipt. During trial B seeks to amend WS to deny tenancy. Decide.",
          "modelAnswer": "### ★ BEST MODEL ANSWER (10-mark)\n\n**Provision (2m):** Quote **O.6 R.17 + proviso** verbatim. General rule: material facts in pleadings, no decision outside pleadings, but illiteracy/counsel gaps necessitate amendment **with court permission**. Object: try **merits, real Q, complete justice, prevent multiplicity** (courts exist for justice not punishment).\n\n**Allow if (1m):** (1) necessary for real Q, (2) without injustice, (3) subserves ultimate justice/avoids further litigation.\n\n**Refusal Grounds — 5 Settled Principles (2m):** (1) unnecessary/technical, (2) **wholly displaces plaintiff’s suit**, (3) **takes accrued limitation right**, (4) **new/inconsistent/belated**, (5) **mala fide**.\n\n***Steward* (2m):** Tramway denied negligence, 6+ months later sought to shift liability to local authority (vs whom plaintiff now time-barred; earlier plea would have enabled timely suit) → **refused**. **The Court held:** *“Can defendants amend without placing plaintiff irrecoupably (costs/otherwise)? Action wholly displaced → refuse.”*\n\n**Admission rule (1m):** *Heeralal v. Kalyan Mal; Modi Spg.; BK Pillai:* **WS admission for plaintiff cannot be withdrawn if wholly displaces + irretrievable prejudice**; alternative plea allowed only if **no admission withdrawn**.\n\n**Present case (2m):** B’s **tenancy admission is foundation of A’s rent claim**; paid-without-receipt defence **presupposes tenancy**. Denying tenancy **withdraws admission, wholly displaces suit, inconsistent/mutually destructive (Dalip iii), belated trial-stage without diligence**. Cannot be cured by costs (entire basis shifts, limitation/evidence prejudice). **Amendment refused.** B may pursue **payment proof** (receipt secondary evidence, s.114 Illustration, witnesses) but not denial."
