          "examStrategy": "Master exact Section numbers, Orders, and Rules under CPC & Limitation Act for Limitation of Suits, Appeals & Applications. Structure answers using FIRAC/IRAC: Provision → Elements → Judicial Interpretation → Application to Facts. Cite landmark Supreme Court precedents to score top grades.",
          "caseMap": "R.B. Policies at Lloyd's v. Butler ((1949) 2 All ER 226 | King's Bench Divis) • Union of India v. West Coast Paper Mills Ltd. (AIR 2004 SC 1596 | Supreme Court of Indi) • Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha (AIR 1992 SC 1815 | Supreme Court of Indi) • Collector, Land Acquisition, Anantnag v. Mst. Katiji (AIR 1987 SC 1353 | (1987) 2 SCC 107 | Su) • State of Nagaland v. Lipok AO ((2005) 3 SCC 752 | Supreme Court of Indi) • State of Madhya Pradesh v. Bherulal ((2020) 10 SCC 654 | Supreme Court of Ind)",
          "htmlContent": "<section id=\"rev\">\n  <div class=\"sec-head\"><div class=\"sec-num\">&#9203;</div><h2 class=\"sec-title\">Quick Revision Sheet — One Last Look Before the Hall</h2></div>\n  <div class=\"sec-sub\">Sections 3–5 and the six cases, compressed</div>\n\n  <div class=\"grid2\">\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">The Sections in One Breath</div>\n      <b>s.3</b> — dismissal after the prescribed period, <b>even if limitation is not pleaded</b>; subject to ss.4–24; institution = plaint presented (pauper: application; winding-up: claim to liquidator); <b>set-off/counter-claim = separate suits</b>. <b>s.4</b> — last day closed → file on re-opening day (\"closed\" = closed during any part of normal working hours). <b>s.5</b> — appeals/applications only (never suits; never O.XXI; only \"courts\"); \"sufficient cause\"; Explanation — misled by HC order/practice/judgment.</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">The Vocabulary Trap</div>\n      <b>\"Period of limitation\"</b> = raw Schedule period. <b>\"Prescribed period\"</b> = period as computed under the Act (after ss.4–24). s.3's bar + s.5's extension both operate on the <b>prescribed</b> period. Special laws: s.29(2) — s.3 applies; ss.4–24 apply unless expressly excluded. \"Suit\" excludes appeal and application. Art. 58: \"right to sue <i>first</i> accrues\" ≠ Art. 113: \"right to sue accrues\" (<i>West Coast</i>).</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 25 — Butler (1949, KBD)</div>\n      Car stolen 27-6-1940 by unknown thief; found with innocent purchaser Jan 1947; writ 16-7-1947. <b>Held:</b> cause of action <b>accrued at the theft</b> — every ingredient existed; unknown identity of thief ≠ no accrual; s.26 (fraud) only postpones the <i>running</i>, never re-creates accrual; \"appalling results\" otherwise (the watch after 60 years). Policy: act of <b>peace</b>; stale claims = cruelty; protect innocent purchasers; sleepers get no help.</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 26 — West Coast (AIR 2004 SC 1596, 3J)</div>\n      Freight declared unreasonable by Railway Rates Tribunal (18-4-1966) → SC special leave (interim: no inflated distance; ₹2L bank guarantee) → SLP dismissed 14-10-1970 → writ refused (29-10-1973) → refund suits 1973/74. <b>Held:</b> merger (Kunhayammed; Rathore — tribunals equal courts; Mohammad Nooh) → refund suit's limitation ran from <b>SC's order</b>, not the Tribunal's; stay irrelevant once correctness \"in jeopardy\"; Art. 113, not 58; writ period excluded (ss.14–15); suits in time; <b>P.K. Kutty overruled</b>; s.3 to be construed \"broad based and liberal[ly]\".</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 27 — PNB (AIR 1992 SC 1815)</div>\n      Loan ₹15,000 (5-5-1984); guarantor's FDR ₹24,000 → ₹41,292; debtor defaults; debt time-barred 5-5-1987; bank adjusts ₹27,037.60 at maturity, credits balance. Complaint u/ss 409/109/114 IPC. <b>Held:</b> s.3 <b>bars remedy, not right</b> (s.27 sole exception); time-barred debt still due; adjustment from security in creditor's possession = lawful contract performance = <b>negation of s.405/409</b>; FDR entrusted as guarantor, not in banking course; no prima facie case; complaint quashed — process is not for oppression/vendetta.</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 28 — Katiji (AIR 1987 SC 1353)</div>\n      ₹14 lakh / 800% enhancement (₹1,000→₹8,000/kanal); State's appeal <b>4 days late</b>; HC refused condonation. <b>Held (Thakkar, J.):</b> \"To condone or not to condone is not the only question\"; sufficient cause is <b>\"adequately elastic\"</b>; six principles (no benefit in delay; refusal kills merits; condonation's worst case = merits; no pedantry — why not every hour/second?; substantial justice &gt; technicality; no presumption of deliberateness); <b>State ≠ litigant non grata</b> — equality, no step-motherly treatment; impersonal machinery, note-making/file-pushing/passing-the-buck — \"less difficult to understand though more difficult to approve\"; judiciary respected for <b>removing</b> injustice. Delay condoned; remitted to HC.</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 29 — Lipok AO ((2005) 3 SCC 752)</div>\n      Acquittal (18-12-2002) of police escort in shooting case (2 dead; post-mortem report missing → benefit of doubt); State's s.378(3) CrPC leave application <b>57 days late</b>: copy 15-1-2003 → file missing → traced 15-3-2003 → ADG 26-3-2003 → SPP → Secretary chases Addl. AG → filed 14-5-2003. HC refused. <b>Held (Pasayat, J.):</b> proof of cause = condition precedent; <b>sufficiency, not length</b>; liberal construction (N. Balakrishnan); no rigid rules (Shanti Misra); reasonable diligence (Brij Indar Singh); State gets \"a little play at the joints\" (Ramegowda); public interest suffers when State appeals default; legal cells + officer accountability; <b>57 days condoned; leave directed</b>.</div>\n    <div class=\"kbox\"><div class=\"kh\">Case 30 — Bherulal ((2020) 10 SCC 654)</div>\n      SLP <b>663 days late</b>; explanation: \"unavailability of documents\" + \"bureaucratic process... inadvertent\". <b>Held (Kaul, J.):</b> SC is not a place for governments \"to walk in when they choose\"; Katiji belonged to the pre-technology era; <i>Living Media</i>: red-tape explanations rejected — \"condonation of delay is an <b>exception</b> and should not be used as an <b>anticipated benefit</b>\"; \"law shelters everyone under the same light\"; merit is no answer — \"a bar of limitation can even shut out good cases\"; <b>\"certificate cases\"</b> to save officers' skin deprecated; <b>₹25,000 costs recoverable from the responsible officers</b>, certificate of recovery to court, copy to Chief Secretary, contempt on default; SLP dismissed as time-barred.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"card\">\n    <h3>Memory Hooks</h3>\n    <ul class=\"tick\">\n      <li><b>\"Dismiss–Deem–Don't-plead\"</b> — s.3: dismiss as time-barred even without the plea; deemed dates of institution in s.3(2).</li>\n      <li><b>\"Last-day grace\"</b> — s.4 cares only about the day the period <i>expires</i>; s.5 cares about the days <i>after</i> it.</li>\n      <li><b>\"A-A-A\"</b> — s.5 covers <b>A</b>ppeals and <b>A</b>pplications (not suits) made to a <b>A</b>court... minus O.XXI.</li>\n      <li><b>Case-number = delay-scale:</b> Katiji 4 days → Lipok 57 → Chandra Mani 109 → Bherulal <b>663!</b> The bigger the number, the harsher the Court.</li>\n      <li><b>Butler's watch:</b> if the owner wins, a 60-year-old claim on an innocent buyer succeeds — \"appalling results\"; hence accrual at theft.</li>\n      <li><b>PNB's ledger:</b> the right survived in the FDR — limitation killed the suit, not the security.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n</section>\n\n</div><!-- /page -->"
