          "question": "PYQ 1 — DU LL.B. II Term, LB-204, May/June 2025, Q.2(a) [10 marks]",
          "answer": "Introduction. Section 3 TPA defines “attested”: two or more witnesses, each of whom has seen the executant sign/affix his mark (or seen another sign by his direction) or received his personal acknowledgment, and each of whom has signed in the executant’s presence; no form is needed and the witnesses need not be present together. Attestation is mandatory for mortgages of Rs 100 and above (s.59) and gifts of immovable property (s.123). The definition, however, is silent as to who may attest — the question is answered by case law, principally Kumar Harish Chandra Singh Deo v. Bansidhar Mohanty , AIR 1965 SC 1738. Object of attestation. As Mudholkar J. stated, the object is “to protect the executant from being required to execute a document by the other party thereto by force, fraud or undue influence.” An attesting witness “bears witness to a fact” ( Abdul Jabbar ) — he is, in Lord Selborne’s words in Seal v. Claridge , one who “stands by but is not a party”. Party to the deed cannot attest. (i) Conceptually, one cannot be a witness to one’s own act; (ii) Freshfield v. Reed : “the party to an instrument cannot attest it”; (iii) statutorily, s.68 Evidence Act requires that a document required by law to be attested be proved by calling at least one attesting witness — the testimony of the parties cannot dispense with this; “inferentially, therefore, it debars a party from attesting” (Mudholkar J.). Indian cases: Peary Mohan Maiti , Sarur Jigar Begum (Cal), Gomathi Ammal (Mad). A person executing as agent/POA-holder of a party is in the same position. Party to the transaction but not to the deed can attest. In Kumar Harish Chandra a mortgage for Rs 15,000 (30.4.1945) was executed in favour of Jagannath Debata, but the money was advanced by Bansidhar Mohanty, in whose favour the deed was taken benami; Bansidhar was one of only two attesting witnesses. The mortgagor argued the attestation was invalid. The Supreme Court held: neither s.59 nor s.3 prescribes any qualification; the inferential bar from s.68 applies only to parties to the deed; “where a person is not a party to the deed there is no prohibition in law to the proof of the execution of the document by that person.” Lord Selborne’s reference to a “party to the transaction” was made in a case where the person was a party to the deed and cannot be read literally. The Court approved the Bombay decision in Balu Ravji Gharat v. Gopal Ravji (which analysed Swire v. Bell and Wickham v. Marquis of Bath ) and Durga Din v. Suraj Bakhsh (Oudh), and dismissed the appeal: the mortgage was validly attested. Rationale. Interest in the transaction affects the weight of the witness’s evidence, not his competence . The executant is sufficiently protected because the person can still be examined under s.68 as an attesting witness; the mischief (a party proving his own deed) does not arise. Related limits. Even a non-party is not an attesting witness unless he signed animo attestandi in the executant’s presence after seeing execution or receiving acknowledgment — the scribe, identifier and Sub-Registrar are prima facie not witnesses ( M.L. Abdul Jabbar Sahib v. Venkata Sastri , AIR 1969 SC 1147). And a pardanashin executant may be attested through a screen if the witnesses saw the act and identified her ( Padarath Halwai v. Ram Narain , AIR 1915 PC 21). Conclusion. The statement correctly states the law: the disqualification is of a party to the deed , deduced from the concept of witnessing and s.68 Evidence Act; a person merely interested in or party to the underlying transaction, who is not a party to the instrument, is a competent attesting witness ( Kumar Harish Chandra ).",
