          "unitNumber": 2,
          "question": "PYQ 2 — “What is attestation? Discuss the essentials of a valid attestation. Is the signature of the Sub-Registrar or of an identifying witness on a mortgage deed sufficient attestation?” [DU, recurring]",
          "answer": "Meaning. “To attest is to bear witness to a fact” (Bachawat J., Abdul Jabbar ). Section 3 TPA (inserted 1926, retrospective by Act 10 of 1927, to overrule Shamu Patter ) defines “attested”. Essentials. (1) Two or more witnesses; (2) each must have seen the executant sign or affix his mark, or seen another sign in his presence and by his direction, or received his personal acknowledgment ; (3) each must sign the instrument; (4) in the presence of the executant ; (5) with animus attestandi ; (6) attestation must follow execution; (7) witnesses need not be present together; (8) no particular form; (9) witness must be sui juris and not a party to the deed ( Kumar Harish Chandra ). Sub-Registrar / identifiers. In Abdul Jabbar a security bond charging immovable property (26.3.1953) was attested by one witness (Somnath Rao); the appellant relied on the signatures of the Sub-Registrar and two identifying witnesses on the registration endorsement, following Veerappa Chettiar . The Supreme Court held that under Registration Act ss.34(3), 35(1), 58 and 59 the registering officer signs in discharge of a statutory duty and identifiers sign to certify identity; “prima facie” such signatures are not attestation. They can count only if it is proved that the signatory (a) saw execution or received a personal acknowledgment, (b) signed in the executant’s presence, and (c) did so with the intention to attest. As none of this was proved, the bond was attested by one witness only. (Similarly the scribe — Abinash Chandra ; a legatee signing in token of consent — Shiam Sunder Singh ; identifier — Girja Datt Singh .) Consequence and exception. A mortgage or gift so attested is void for non-compliance with s.59/s.123; registration cannot cure the defect. But the instrument in Abdul Jabbar survived because a charge under s.100 does not require attestation; the 1929 amendment imports only the rights and liabilities of a simple mortgage ( Bapurao v. Narayan approved; Viswanadhan , Shiva Rao overruled). Conclusion. Signatures of the Sub-Registrar and identifiers are not, by themselves, attestation; they may be treated as such only on proof of perception, presence and animus attestandi.",
