          "unitNumber": 2,
          "question": "PYQ 3 — Problem: “A, a pardanashin lady, executed a mortgage deed of her house for Rs 5 lakh sitting behind a curtain. The two attesting witnesses, old family acquaintances, saw her hand signing through the curtain and recognised her voice, and then signed the deed while she remained behind the curtain. A’s heirs contend the mortgage is void for want of attestation as the witnesses never saw A. Decide.” [DU pattern]",
          "answer": "Issue. Whether the witnesses “saw the executant sign” within s.3 TPA, so that the mortgage complies with s.59 (principal ≥ Rs 100 — registration + signature + attestation by two witnesses required). Law. Padarath Halwai v. Ram Narain (PC 1915): two pardanashin ladies executed a mortgage (25.6.1892) behind an unlined chick ; the witnesses, who knew their voices, saw their hands sign and signed in their presence. The Privy Council held the deed duly attested — the requirement is that the witness see the act of execution and be able to identify the executant; seeing the face is not required. After 1926, s.3 additionally permits attestation on personal acknowledgment , so even if the witnesses had only heard A acknowledge her signature in a voice they recognised, and signed in her presence, attestation would be good. Contrast: an opaque tat with an unidentifiable voice — attestation fails. Application. The witnesses saw A’s hand sign, identified her by voice, and signed in her presence with intent to attest. All s.3 requirements are met. The heirs’ objection fails. Caveat. The mortgagee must separately be prepared to prove — the burden being on him — that the deed was explained to and understood by A and executed of her free will ( Farid-un-nisa v. Mukhtar Ahmad ; Kharbuja Kuer v. Jangbahadur Rai ); s.16 Contract Act and s.111 Evidence Act reinforce this. Assuming these are satisfied, the mortgage is valid .",
