          "unitNumber": 2,
          "question": "PYQ 4 — Short notes: (a) Animus attestandi; (b) Attestation vs Registration; (c) Documents requiring attestation under the TPA",
          "answer": "(a) Animus attestandi — the intention to bear witness to execution. A signature on the deed put for any other purpose (scribe, identifier, registrar, consenting legatee, explaining advocate) is not attestation ( Abdul Jabbar ; Girja Datt ; Shiam Sunder Singh ). Intention is a fact provable by evidence; the burden is on the propounder. Jarman: the Court must be satisfied that names were written animo attestandi . (b) Attestation vs Registration — attestation is by private witnesses at execution, testifying to the genuineness of execution; registration is by a public officer under the Registration Act, recording the document and the executant’s admission of execution and giving constructive notice. Both are required for mortgages ≥ Rs 100 and gifts of immovables; each is independent; registration does not supply attestation ( Abdul Jabbar ), and attestation does not dispense with registration. The proviso to s.68 Evidence Act relaxes proof for registered attested documents (other than wills) unless execution is specifically denied. (c) Documents requiring attestation under TPA — (i) mortgage deed where principal is Rs 100 or more, other than mortgage by deposit of title deeds (s.59); (ii) mortgage below Rs 100 if effected by instrument (s.59 second para); (iii) gift of immovable property (s.123). Not required: sale, lease, exchange, charge ( Abdul Jabbar ), transfer of actionable claim, relinquishment, partition, agreement to sell. Outside TPA: wills (Succession Act s.63).",
