          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "PYQ 1 — DU LL.B. II Term, LB-204, May/June 2025, Q.3",
          "answer": "Issues. (i) Can Suresh enforce his prior agreement against Amar, a subsequent purchaser? (ii) Is Amar a \"transferee for value who has paid his money in good faith and without notice of the original contract\" under s.19(b) SRA, 1963? (iii) Does Suresh's actual possession fix Amar with notice under Explanation II to s.3 TPA? Law. An agreement to sell creates no interest in land (s.54 TPA) but an obligation annexed to ownership enforceable against a transferee with notice or a gratuitous transferee (s.40, illustration — Sultanpur). s.19(b) SRA permits specific performance against a subsequent transferee \"except a transferee for value who has paid his money in good faith and without notice of the original contract\"; per Ram Niwas v. Bano (2000) 6 SCC 685 he must prove three things — value, payment in good faith, and absence of notice . \"Notice\" is defined in s.3 TPA (actual knowledge; wilful abstention; gross negligence) and Explanation II deems a person acquiring immovable property \"to have notice of the title, if any, of any person who is for the time being in actual possession thereof\" — codifying Lord Eldon's rule in Daniels v. Davison that a purchaser must inquire on what terms a tenant in possession holds. The Supreme Court held that \"notice\" is wider than \"knowledge\" , and that purchasers who \"relied upon the assertion of the vendor or on their own knowledge and abstained from making inquiry into the real nature of the possession of the tenant … cannot escape from the consequences of the deemed notice under Explanation II.\" H.N. Narayanaswamy Naidu v. Deveeramma (AIR 1981 Kant 93) likewise imputed notice to an assignee who failed to inquire from the person in actual possession, treating the omission as wilful abstention. Contrast Md. Mustafa v. Haji Md. Isa (AIR 1987 Pat 5) — where the claimant occupied only 1/7th of a multi-tenanted building, Explanation II was not attracted and a purchaser who had inquired from the owner was protected. Burden: the purchaser must plead and prove the s.19(b) exception, though the burden is light ( Md. Mustafa ). Application. Suresh is the tenant in actual possession of the very property sold — the Ram Niwas situation, not the Md. Mustafa fraction situation. Amar was bound to inquire from Suresh about the nature of his possession; had he done so he would have learnt of the 1 Jan agreement. Whether or not Amar actually knew of it, he is deemed to have notice of Suresh's title under Explanation II; reliance on Ravi's description of Suresh as \"merely a tenant\" is no answer. Amar therefore fails the third condition of s.19(b) and takes subject to Suresh's contract (s.40 TPA). Amar cannot recover possession from Suresh, whose possession is lawful both as tenant and (if he has performed or is willing to perform — s.53-A) as transferee in part performance; further, s.53-A's proviso protecting a transferee \"who has no notice of the contract or of the part performance\" does not aid Amar for the same reason. Suresh may sue for specific performance impleading both Ravi and Amar (s.19(a)–(b)); post-2018 specific performance is no longer discretionary (s.10 SRA as amended), subject to Suresh proving the agreement and his readiness and willingness (s.16(c)). The only caveats: (a) if the agreement is not genuine, Amar wins ( Ram Niwas remanded on this point); (b) if the agreement is registrable and unregistered, it remains admissible for specific performance (Registration Act s.49 proviso). Conclusion. Amar would not succeed. Being deemed to have notice of Suresh's agreement by virtue of Suresh's actual possession (Explanation II; Ram Niwas ; Naidu ), he is not a bona fide purchaser without notice under s.19(b) SRA; Suresh can enforce the agreement against him and retain possession.",
