          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "PYQ 4 — Problem: \"A owns a building with seven identical shops let to seven tenants. B, one tenant, claims that A agreed in writing to sell him the whole building and took Rs 7,000 in advance. A thereafter sells the building by registered deed to C, who inquired from A but not from any tenant. B sues C for specific performance, contending that his possession fixed C with notice. Decide.\" [DU pattern]",
          "answer": "Issue. Whether B's possession of one shop attracts Explanation II so as to deprive C of the s.19(b) SRA protection. Law. Explanation II deems a purchaser to have notice of the title of the person in actual possession of the property acquired ( Daniels v. Davison ). In Md. Mustafa v. Haji Md. Isa (AIR 1987 Pat 5), on facts identical to the problem (plaintiff tenant of one of seven katras ; registered sale to minors; no inquiry from tenants), the Patna High Court held, following its Full Bench in Hari Charan Kuar v. Kaula Rai and Kesharmull Agarwala , that the principle \"cannot be extended to a case in which the person basing his claim on the basis of a prior agreement is in possession of only a small fraction of the property\"; the purchaser is not bound to inquire from every tenant, and \"the deeming clause … cannot be attracted at all.\" The burden on the purchaser to prove want of notice is light; a sworn denial shifts the onus to the plaintiff. Contrast Ram Niwas v. Bano (SC 2000), where the tenant possessed the entire shop sold and Explanation II applied with full force. Application. B holds only 1/7th of the building; six other tenants hold identical portions; C inquired from the owner. Applying Md. Mustafa , Explanation II is not attracted; unless B proves C's actual knowledge (mere residence in the locality is insufficient), C is a bona fide purchaser for value without notice. B's remedy lies against A in damages (and refund of the advance, if the agreement is genuine). Decision. Suit against C fails. (Note the caveat: if B had been in possession of a substantial part or the whole, the Ram Niwas rule would bind C.)",
