          "unitNumber": 4,
          "question": "PYQ 1 — DU LL.B. II Term, LB-204, May/June 2025, Q.2(b)",
          "answer": "Issues. (i) Is the making of a Will a \"transfer\" or alienation of property? (ii) Does an order to maintain status quo regarding property prohibit a testamentary disposition, so that R is in contempt and the Will is void? Law. s.5 TPA defines \"transfer of property\" as \"an act by which a living person conveys property, in present or in future, to one or more other living persons, or to himself…\". The Act therefore deals only with transfers inter vivos ; testamentary succession is governed by the Indian Succession Act, 1925, s.2(h) of which defines a Will as \"the legal declaration of the intention of a testator with respect to his property which he desires to be carried into effect after his death\". In N. Ramaiah v. Nagaraj S. (AIR 2001 Kant 395) — on facts identical to the problem — a widow contesting probate proceedings was directed to \"maintain status quo\" regarding the disputed properties (on an application to restrain alienation/encumbrance); she then executed a registered Will of the properties in favour of her brother and died. The single Judge held the Will \"non est\" as executed in defiance of the order. The Division Bench (Raveendran J.) reversed, holding: (a) \"a transfer is a conveyance of an existing property by one living person to another … a Will does not involve any transfer … but is a legal expression of the wishes and intention of a person in regard to his properties which he desires to be carried into effect after his death\"; \"the concept of transfer by a living person is wholly alien to a Will\"; a transfer is irrevocable and operates at once or on a contingency, a Will is revocable and operates only on death; to treat a bequest as a \"transfer in future\" under s.5 \"is contrary to all known principles\"; (b) \"by execution of a Will, no right or title or interest is created in favour of any one during the life time\" of the testator; (c) \"no Court has the power to make an order, that too an interim order, restraining an individual from exercising his right to execute a will\"; (d) \"by making a will the testator neither changes title nor possession … nor alters the nature or situation of the property\"; hence making a Will \"does not violate an order of status quo … and consequently the testamentary disposition is neither void nor voidable\". The Delhi High Court in Kenneth Solomon v. Dan Singh Bawa (AIR 1986 Del 1) likewise recognised that the words of s.5 \"exclude transfer by will, for a will operates after the death of the testator\" and that no cause of action arises during the testator's life from the mere making of a Will, though it treated the eventual vesting of tenancy rights on death as \"parting with possession\" for the Delhi Rent Control Act — a rent-law concept, not a s.5 transfer. Application. The status quo order required R and G not to alter the existing state of affairs — title, possession, nature of the plot. By making a Will R conveyed nothing to anyone; the legatee acquires no interest during R's life; R may revoke it at any time (Succession Act s.62); title and possession remain exactly as before. There is therefore no disobedience of the order (CPC O.XXXIX r.2A) and no wilful breach amounting to civil contempt under s.2(b) Contempt of Courts Act, 1971. Contrast: had R executed a sale, gift or mortgage of the plot — a s.5 transfer altering title — he would be in breach and the alienation liable to be disregarded. Conclusion. R is not in contempt. A Will is not a transfer of property under s.5 TPA and does not violate a status quo order; the Will remains valid, and on R's death the legatee may step into R's shoes in the litigation ( N. Ramaiah v. Nagaraj S. ).",
