          "unitNumber": 4,
          "question": "PYQ 2 — \"Define 'transfer of property' under s.5 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 and analyse its essential elements. Is partition of joint Hindu family property a transfer? Refer to decided cases.\" [DU, recurring]",
          "answer": "Definition. s.5: \"an act by which a living person conveys property, in present or in future, to one or more other living persons, or to himself, or to himself and one or more other living persons\"; \"living person\" includes a company, association or body of individuals, incorporated or not (1929 amendment, which also added \"to himself\"). Elements. (1) An act — voluntary act of parties; transfers by operation of law (inheritance, insolvency, court sale) are excluded (s.2(d)). (2) Living person — the transferor must exist at the time of transfer; natural or juristic; competent under s.7; a Will, operating on death, is excluded ( Kenneth Solomon ; Ramaiah ). (3) Conveys — creates a new title or interest in the transferee which he did not have before; transactions that merely define, sever or extinguish existing rights (partition, release, surrender, family settlement, charge) are not transfers. (4) Property — every legal right except personal rights of status; includes incorporeal interests such as tenancy rights ( Kenneth Solomon ); subject to s.6 exclusions. (5) In present or in future — the conveyance may operate now or later, but the property must exist and belong to the transferor; future property/spes successionis cannot be conveyed (s.6(a)). (6) To other living persons or to himself — transferee must be in existence (unborn persons only through ss.13, 20); transfer to oneself in another capacity (trustee) permitted. Partition. In V.N. Sarin v. Ajit Kumar Poplai (AIR 1966 SC 432) tenanted premises of a joint family fell to a son on partition; the tenant pleaded s.14(6) DRC Act (five-year bar where the landlord \"acquired premises by transfer\"). Gajendragadkar C.J. held that coparcenary property is marked by community of interest and unity of possession; each coparcener has an antecedent title; partition merely converts joint enjoyment into enjoyment in severalty — \"that joint title is by partition transformed into separate titles\"; per Girja Bai v. Sadashiv Dhundiraj (PC 1916), partition \"does not give him a title or create a title in him\". A transfer under s.14(6) presupposes that title \"did not vest in him prior to such transfer\" — a stranger becoming landlord. Hence partition is not an acquisition by transfer; CIT v. Keshavlal Lallubhai Patel (AIR 1965 SC 866) to the same effect for income-tax. In Mohar Singh v. Devi Charan (AIR 1988 SC 1365) the Supreme Court extended the proposition to ordinary co-ownership: \"a partition is not actually a transfer of property but would only signify the surrender of a portion of a joint right in exchange for a similar right from the other co-sharer\", while applying the benefit of s.109 TPA to a co-owner allotted the tenanted shop on partition, on the principle of justice, equity and good conscience. Qualification. The Court in Sarin confined itself to s.14(6) and acknowledged that partition is treated as a \"transfer\" for s.53 TPA (fraudulent transfers) and s.17(1)(b) Registration Act (partition deeds registrable). The meaning is thus contextual. Conclusion. Partition of joint family property is not a \"transfer of property\" within s.5 because nothing new is conveyed — the sharer's pre-existing undefined interest is simply crystallised; but for anti-fraud and registration purposes the law treats it as a transfer.",
