          "unitNumber": 4,
          "question": "PYQ 3 — \"A Will is not a transfer of property within the meaning of s.5 of the Transfer of Property Act.\" Discuss with reference to Kenneth Solomon v. Dan Singh Bawa and N. Ramaiah v. Nagaraj . [DU pattern]",
          "answer": "Statement of law. s.5 requires an act of a living person conveying property inter vivos . A Will (Succession Act s.2(h)) is a declaration of intention to take effect after death; it is ambulatory and revocable (s.62); it vests nothing during life. It therefore fails the \"living person conveys\" test and is outside the TPA, which is confined to transfers by act of parties between living persons. Kenneth Solomon (Del 1986). Tenant Dr Sury bequeathed \"all my moveable and immoveable properties\" to her nephews; on her death the landlord sought eviction under DRC Act s.14(1)(b) (\"otherwise parted with possession\" without written consent). G.C. Jain J. held (i) tenancy rights are an interest in immovable property and passed under the residuary clause; (ii) on s.5: \"these words exclude transfer by will, for a will operates after the death of the testator. The act of making a will in itself would not attract … proviso (b). No landlord can claim eviction, during the lifetime of the tenant, on the ground that the tenant had made a will\"; yet (iii) the vesting on death is a volitional parting with possession by the tenant (following Nathu v. Devi Singh , and Anant Trimbak Sabnis — bequest is a transfer \"in its generic sense\"), unlike intestate devolution; eviction upheld. The case thus affirms that a Will is not a s.5 transfer while giving the rent statute's wider expression its own meaning. N. Ramaiah (Kant 2001). A widow under a status quo order made a Will of the disputed property; the respondent argued it was a \"transfer in future\" under s.5. Raveendran J. held the TPA inapplicable to testamentary succession; \"the concept of transfer by a living person is wholly alien to a Will\"; a transfer is an irrevocable conveyance of existing property, a Will a revocable regulation of succession; no right is created during the testator's life; a status quo order does not bar a Will and the Will is neither void nor voidable; the legatee was impleaded. Analysis. Both decisions concur on the core proposition; they diverge on the effect of the eventual vesting: Kenneth Solomon treats it as the maturing of a volitional act (for rent-control purposes), Ramaiah insists a Will creates no right whatever until death. The reconciliation is contextual — \"transfer\" and \"parting with possession\" are read according to each statute's object. Consequences of the rule: no stamp duty/compulsory registration for Wills; a Will of property under injunction is valid; a Will is not hit by s.6 restrictions or the rule against alienation in a tenancy; s.53 (fraudulent transfer) does not apply to Wills; but a Will cannot defeat rights already transferred inter vivos . Conclusion. The statement is correct: a Will operates by succession on death, not by conveyance between living persons, and is outside s.5 TPA.",
