          "unitNumber": 4,
          "question": "PYQ 4 — Problem: \"X and Y, co-owners, let two adjoining shops to T under one lease. They partition the property; shop 1 falls to X, who files for T's eviction from shop 1 for his bona fide need. T contends (a) the tenancy is indivisible and cannot be split without his consent, and (b) X acquired shop 1 by partition, which is not a 'transfer', so s.109 TPA does not help him. Decide.\" [DU pattern]",
          "answer": "Issues. (a) Applicability of the rule against splitting a tenancy; (b) whether s.109 TPA (rights of lessor's transferee) applies where severance of the reversion occurred by partition. Law. s.109: if the lessor transfers the property leased \"or any part thereof\", the transferee, absent contrary contract, \"shall possess all the rights … of the lessor as to the property or part transferred\". In Mohar Singh v. Devi Charan (AIR 1988 SC 1365), on identical facts, Venkatachaliah J. held: the rule that a landlord cannot split the unity and integrity of the tenancy binds the original lessor by his own contract, but s.109 is a statutory exception enabling an assignee of part of the reversion to exercise all the landlord's rights as to that part; attornment arises by operation of law; no consent of the tenant is needed ( Kannyan v. Alikutti , Mad FB). On the objection that partition is no transfer, the Court accepted that \"a partition is not actually a transfer of property but would only signify the surrender of a portion of a joint right in exchange for a similar right\", yet applied s.109 to partition following Ram Chandra Singh v. Ram Saran (All) — or, if the section does not apply in terms, its principle \"as embodying a rule of justice, equity and good conscience\". Application. (a) T's objection that the tenancy is indivisible fails: the reversion has been severed and X, as exclusive owner of shop 1, may enforce the lease as to shop 1 alone; T attorns to X by operation of law and pays apportioned rent (s.109, last para). (b) Although partition is not a s.5 transfer ( Sarin ; Mohar Singh ), the benefit of s.109 extends to X. Decision. X's petition is maintainable; subject to proof of bona fide need, T is liable to be evicted from shop 1 ( Mohar Singh ).",
