          "questionNumber": 2,
          "unitNumber": 5,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 1",
          "answer": "Model answer. Issue. Whether Bhavesh can claim field Y under s.43 TPA (feeding the grant by estoppel), or whether the sale of Y is void as a transfer of spes successionis under s.6(a). Law. s.6(a) prohibits transfer of \"the chance of an heir-apparent succeeding to an estate\". During his father's lifetime Akshay had no interest in Y — only a spes ( nemo est haeres viventis ). But s.43 provides that where a person \"fraudulently or erroneously represents that he is authorised to transfer certain immovable property and professes to transfer such property for consideration\", the transfer operates, at the transferee's option, on any interest the transferor acquires while the contract subsists. The illustration to s.43 is on all fours: A sells fields X, Y and Z, of which Z belongs to his father B; B dies, A inherits Z; C may require A to deliver Z. In Jumma Masjid v. Kodimaniandra Deviah (AIR 1962 SC 847) the Supreme Court held that s.6(a) (substantive prohibition on transfer of spes simpliciter ) and s.43 (rule of estoppel) operate in different fields; where the transferor represents a present title and the transferee acts on it for consideration, s.43 applies even though the transferor in fact had only a spes; the transferor's good or bad faith is immaterial; and the plea is under, not against, the statute. Kartar Singh v. Harbans Kaur ((1994) 4 SCC 730) lays down the two conditions: representation + transfer for consideration; subsequent acquisition while the contract subsists — and adds that the transferee must have been actually misled (no actual/constructive notice). Application. Akshay represented authority to transfer Y; Bhavesh paid consideration; Akshay has now acquired Y by inheritance; nothing shows the contract was rescinded. All conditions are met. Field X passed at once (s.8). As to Y, Bhavesh may, at his option, claim it; Akshay is estopped from asserting his after-acquired title. If Akshay has meanwhile sold Y to a bona fide purchaser for value without notice of Bhavesh's right, that purchaser is protected (s.43 para 2) and Bhavesh is left to damages (s.55(2)). Alternative. If Bhavesh knew Y belonged to the father, he was not misled; \"Section 43 would then have no application, and the transfer will fail under Section 6(a)\" ( Jumma Masjid , para 15; applied in Kartar Singh where the deed itself gave notice). The sale of Y would be void; Bhavesh could only recover the proportionate price under s.65 Contract Act. Conclusion: Bhavesh succeeds as to Y unless he had knowledge/notice of the father's title.",
