          "unitNumber": 5,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Model answer (outline). (1) State s.6(a) and its three limbs; rationale (public policy, protection of expectant heirs, speculation, nemo dat ); contracts to transfer spes also void — s.23 Contract Act, Ananda Mohan Roy . (2) State s.43 with illustration; doctrine of feeding the grant by estoppel; 1929 amendment inserting \"fraudulently or erroneously\"; two paragraphs. (3) The apparent conflict: an heir apparent's transfer is void under s.6(a), yet the illustration to s.43 validates the sale of field Z retained by the father — Sampath Naidu (Mad 1933) and Bindeshwari Singh (Oudh) held s.43 must yield to s.6(a); Alamanaya Kunigari and Shyam Narain held otherwise. (4) Reconciliation in Jumma Masjid — facts (reversioners' sale 1920; Gangamma d. 1933; release to mosque) and the six propositions: different subjects; substantive law v. estoppel; no implied exception (\"rewriting\"); estoppel under, not against, statute; illustration binding aid; test is whether transferee was misled, not the transferor's good faith. (5) Refinement in Kartar Singh : constructive notice and void transactions fall outside s.43. (6) Conclusion: the distinguishing factor is the knowledge/notice of the transferee — a professed transfer of expectancy (or one taken with notice) is void under s.6(a); a transfer on a representation of present title acted on in good faith for consideration is fed by s.43.",
