          "answer": "Model answer (outline). Definition — bare hope of succeeding; three limbs (heir apparent's chance; relation's chance of legacy; any other mere possibility of like nature) with examples (son during father's life; Hindu reversioner before 1956; legatee before testator's death; next cast of net). Reasons for non-transferability. Contracts — void under s.23 Contract Act ( Ananda Mohan Roy v. Gour Mohan Mullick , PC; Sri Jagannada Raju ); no specific performance; s.65 restitution. Distinction from contingent interest (s.21): a contingent interest is a present interest created by a transfer, dependent on an uncertain event, and is transferable (s.6 read with s.21, s.120 Succession Act), whereas a spes is not an interest in property at all; also distinguish from an undivided coparcenary share (transferable, s.44) and a Muslim heir's expectancy (void even with consent). Close with exception via s.43 ( Jumma Masjid ).",
