          "unitNumber": 5,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 5",
          "answer": "Model answer. This is Kartar Singh v. Harbans Kaur . Two conditions of s.43 (representation + consideration; subsequent acquisition while contract subsists) — both fail. (i) P was not misled: the deed itself disclosed that half the land was a minor's being sold by a guardian; a purchaser from a guardian must make reasonable and diligent inquiry as to authority (court permission under GWA ss.29–30 / HMGA s.8(2)) and necessity; P made none — constructive notice (s.3 TPA); \"Section 43 feeds its estoppel\" only when the transferee was misled ( Jumma Masjid para 15). (ii) The sale of S's share, made without sanction and avoided by decree, is non est ab initio — no subsisting contract to be fed. Result: P retains H's own half; S's half is not affected by s.43; P may sue H for proportionate refund/damages. Distinguish Jumma Masjid (deed asserted present title; purchaser had no notice).",
