          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 3",
          "answer": "Model answer (outline). (1) s.10 v. s.11 (Capsule): s.10 applies to any transfer (absolute or partial) and protects the power of alienation , voiding only absolute restraints; s.11 applies only to absolute transfers and protects the freedom of enjoyment , voiding any direction as to mode of enjoyment, save under para 2. Both leave the transfer intact. (2) Para 2 of s.11: direction on the transferred land for the beneficial enjoyment of the transferor's other immovable property is enforceable by him. (3) Covenants: negative (not to build/open windows/change user) — no expenditure; positive (repair drain, paint wall, maintain lawn) — financial burden; substance governs. (4) Running with land: at law burden does not run with freehold ( Keppell , Austerberry ); in equity Tulk v. Moxhay — facts (Leicester Square garden, 1808 covenant to keep open and unbuilt; Moxhay bought with notice, deed silent, intended to build); Lord Cottenham: question is not whether covenant runs but whether a purchaser with notice may use land inconsistently with his vendor's contract; otherwise a vendor could not sell part without rendering the rest worthless; inequitable to escape by resale; \"no one purchasing with notice of that equity can stand in a different situation from the party from whom he purchased\"; injunction upheld. Conditions: negative in substance; dominant land retained; notice. (5) s.40 TPA codifies: enforceable against a transferee with notice or a gratuitous transferee, not against a transferee for consideration without notice; illustration (Sultanpur contract); notice under s.3; remedies SRA ss.38–39, 19(b). (6) Example of the four covenants (Capsule).",
