          "unitNumber": 7,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 1",
          "answer": "Model answer. Issues. (i) Validity of the life interest in favour of B's unborn son; (ii) validity of the ultimate absolute interest in favour of X; (iii) devolution of the property. Law. An unborn person cannot be a direct transferee (s.5 — \"living persons\"), but s.13 permits an interest to be created for the benefit of an unborn person provided (a) it is preceded by a prior interest created by the same transfer in a living person and (b) it \"extends to the whole of the remaining interest of the transferor\" — i.e., is absolute . The illustration to s.13 (A → B and intended wife for life → eldest son of the marriage for life → A's second son) declares the eldest son's life interest void \"because it does not extend to the whole of A's remaining interest.\" s.14 further requires vesting within lives in being plus the beneficiary's minority. s.16 provides that where an interest fails under ss.13–14, \"any interest created in the same transaction and intended to take effect after or upon failure of such prior interest also fails.\" s.20 — a validly created unborn's interest vests at birth. Application. (1) B's life interest is valid — B is living. (2) The interest to B's unborn son is only a life interest ; it does not carry the whole remaining interest of A → void under s.13 . The Capsule's example is identical (\"a life estate for the benefit of B's unborn first child … would be void\"); Ram Newaz v. Nankoo (AIR 1926 All 283) struck down a reservation for the vendor's lineal descendants as \"a condition repugnant to the law\" because it created limited interests in unborn generations and might tie the land up \"for 100 or 200 years\" — the court applying the possible-events test (\"not what in fact happened\"). (3) X's interest, although absolute and in favour of a living person, is \"intended to take effect after\" the void life interest of the unborn son → fails under s.16 (as A's second son's interest fails in the illustration; as the vendee's interest failed in Ram Newaz ). (4) Result: B enjoys for life; on B's death the property reverts to A or A's heirs . How it could have been drafted validly. \"To B for life, then to B's first son absolutely\" (valid — vests at birth, s.20; Capsule ill. ); or \"to B for life, then to X absolutely\" (no unborn involved). Conclusion: the transfer is valid only as to B's life interest; the gifts to the unborn son and to X are void.",
