          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Model answer. Issue. Whether a lessee's option of (perpetual) renewal, which the lessor cannot object to, creates an interest that may vest beyond the period allowed by s.14 and is therefore void. Law. s.14: \"No transfer of property can operate to create an interest which is to take effect after the lifetime of one or more persons living at the date of such transfer, and the minority of some person…\" The rule \"falls within the branch of the law of property and its true object is to restrain the creation of future conditional interests in property … [it] is not concerned with contracts as such\" ( Ram Baran Prasad v. Ram Mohit Hazra , AIR 1967 SC 744 — pre-emption covenant unlimited in time held outside s.14 because a contract for sale creates no interest in land, ss.40, 54). s.105 defines a lease as a transfer of a right to enjoy property \"for a certain time … or in perpetuity \". The decided case. The clause is almost verbatim clauses 9–10 in R. Kempraj v. Burton Son & Co. (AIR 1970 SC 1872): lease of premises on M.G. Road, Bangalore, for 10 years from 1-11-1951 at Rs 450 p.m., with the lessee's right to renew \"for further similar periods … as long as they desire\", the lessor \"not [to] raise any objection whatsoever\". The lessor resisted the lessee's suit for specific performance of renewal on the ground of perpetuity. The Supreme Court (Grover J.) held: (i) the rule rests on the principle that \"the liberty of alienation shall not be exercised to its own destruction\"; (ii) s.14 \"is applicable only where there is transfer of property\"; the lease transferred an interest for ten years only, and \"the stipulation relating to renewal could not be regarded as transferring property or any rights therein\"; (iii) s.105 permits a lease in perpetuity, the reversion remaining with the lessor, so a perpetually renewable lease cannot offend the Act; (iv) Ganesh Sonar v. Purnendu Narayan Singha (AIR 1962 Pat 201) rightly distinguished Woodall v. Clifton because in India a contract concerning land creates no interest in it; (v) even in England perpetual renewal covenants are enforced — Muller v. Trafford (Farwell J.: \"free from any taint of perpetuity because it is annexed to the land\"), approved in Weg Motors v. Hales ; (vi) s.40 covenants running with land are not interests in land, the Act not recognising equitable estates; hence \"no interest in property has been created of the nature contemplated by [s.14]\". Appeal dismissed; renewal enforced. Conclusion. The clause does not offend the rule against perpetuity. It is a covenant for renewal, not a transfer creating a future interest; the lessee may obtain specific performance of a registered renewal deed (s.107 TPA; SRA s.10), subject to any rent-control law. (Contrast: an option to purchase the freehold would also be valid in India for the same reason — Ram Baran Prasad — though void in England under Gomm .)",
