          "answer": "Model answer (outline). State the proposition ( Ram Baran Prasad para 7). Explain why: s.14 speaks of a \"transfer of property\" creating an \"interest\"; s.54 — contract for sale creates no interest; s.40 para 2 — obligation annexed to ownership \"not amounting to an interest\", enforceable against transferees with notice; India does not recognise equitable estates (Mulla, via Kempraj ). Contrast England: contract creates equitable interest → option/pre-emption void if unlimited ( Gomm ; Woodall v. Clifton ); Indian pre-1882 cases followed this ( Tripoora Soonduree ; Allibhai ; Maharaj Bahadur Singh ) on the now-superseded Fati Chand doctrine. Facts/holding of Ram Baran Prasad (partition award 1940; blocks A–D; chain of sales; Rs 14,000; binding on assignees — Contract Act ss.37, 40; SRA ss.23(b), 27(b); vicinage). Facts/holding of Kempraj (10-year lease, perpetual renewal, s.105, Ganesh Sonar , Muller v. Trafford ). Personal covenants to pay money ( Walsh ; Witham v. Vane ). Limits: where the \"contract\" is in truth a transfer creating a future interest (e.g., a settlement) the rule applies; a covenant may still be defeated by a bona fide purchaser without notice. Conclusion.",
