          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 2,
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 1",
          "answer": "Model answer. Vested interest — s.19. Created (a) without specifying the time when it is to take effect, or (b) to take effect forthwith, or (c) on the happening of an event which must happen — unless a contrary intention appears. It is ownership : a present proprietary right, though enjoyment may be deferred (e.g., Rs 100 to B payable on C's death; to A for life, remainder to B). It is not defeated by the transferee's death before possession, and is transferable (s.6), heritable and attachable (CPC s.60). The Explanation says vesting is not negatived merely by postponement of enjoyment, a prior interest to another, a direction to accumulate income, or a gift over on a later event. Contingent interest — s.21. Created to take effect only on the happening, or non-happening, of a specified uncertain event (to B if he marries C; to B if C does not return within five years). The transferee has no present proprietary right — \"a chance of becoming an owner\" resting on an incomplete title; the interest becomes vested when the event happens (or, for a negative event, becomes impossible) and fails if the condition fails. It is transferable (it is more than a spes ) but not attachable ( Pestonjee Bhicajee v. Anderson , PC 1939), and generally not heritable if survival is implicit. Exception: an interest on attaining an age is not contingent if the intermediate income is given to the donee or applied for his benefit. Distinction in brief. (1) Certain v. uncertain event; (2) present ownership v. mere expectancy on an incomplete title; (3) condition precedent absent v. present; (4) death before possession — no effect v. usually fatal; (5) heritable v. depends on contingency; (6) attachable v. not attachable; (7) never offends s.14 v. must vest within the period; (8) construction — strong presumption in favour of vesting. Case: Rajesh Kanta Roy v. Shanti Debi (AIR 1957 SC 255) — sons' interest under a trust deed, to devolve on liquidation of debts and settlor's death, with monthly payments meanwhile and a clause in favour of heirs, held \"vested in title but restricted in enjoyment\" and therefore attachable in execution of the widow's maintenance decree; the question is one of intention gathered from the whole document, approached \"with a bias in favour of a vested interest unless the intention to the contrary is definite and clear.\"",
