          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Model answer (outline). Identify issue: vested (attachable, CPC s.60) or contingent (not attachable, Pestonjee Bhicajee ). Law: ss.19, 21 TPA; ss.119–120 ISA; Explanation to s.19; presumption of vesting; \"after payment of debts\" = charge (Jarman; ISA s.119 ill. (v)). Apply Rajesh Kanta Roy point by point: settlor's death certain; liquidation of debts — not intended as a condition of title because (i) sons receive income (monthly sums) meanwhile — application of income for their benefit, including the balance applied to debts they would themselves be bound to pay; (ii) heirs clause — contrary to the hallmark of contingency; (iii) surplus income follows corpus — present earmarking; (iv) settlor expected debts to be cleared (surplus contemplated, trusteeship for three lives only). Conclusion: \"vested in title but restricted in enjoyment\"; objection fails; attachment (subject to the trust and the debts) proceeds. Note the contrary case ( Bernard v. Mountague ) where a \"definite and clear\" intention postpones vesting.",
