          "answer": "Model answer (outline). Text of s.19 and its Explanation; the four situations with examples (payment at 25; life estate then remainder; accumulation; gift over); rationale — they concern enjoyment/divesting, not acquisition of title; condition precedent v. subsequent (ss.26, 29). Exception to s.21 — age + income given/applied = vested; English origin ( Hanson v. Graham ; Fox v. Fox ); ISA s.120 Exception. Rajesh Kanta Roy as the leading application: \"where the enjoyment of the property is postponed but the present income thereof is to be applied for the benefit of the donee the gift is vested and not contingent\" — even where only part of the income reaches the donee, if the rest is applied to his legal obligations (father's debts). Capsule adoption example (widow postponing adopted son's estate). Limits: clear contrary intention prevails ( Bernard v. Mountague ); \"if/when he attains\" without income → contingent (s.22).",
