          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Outline. Two theories (notice v. necessity) from 34 Am. Jur.; Bennett; Lord Cranworth in Bellamy v. Sabine ; PC in Faiyaz Husain Khan ; Capsule (\"based upon expediency … necessity for final adjudication … prevention of multiplicity\"). Consequences: notice/good faith/consideration irrelevant ( G.T. Girish para 99, contrasting s.19(1)(b) SRA; High Court's error in using constructive notice); the doctrine does not extend to periods when no competent court is seised even if the defendant knew of the plaintiff's claim ( Govinda Pillai ); the transfer is not void — valid inter partes, subject to decree; extension of res judicata ( Manjeshwara Krishnaya ). Note the Bombay/Gujarat registration model and England as alternatives based on notice. Conclude: the necessity basis explains both the rigour (binds innocent purchasers) and the limits (party, competent court, specific property) of the rule.",
