          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 3",
          "answer": "Answer. Facts mirror Supreme General Films Exchange v. Brijnath Singhji Deo (SC 1975). The mortgage suit (2018–2022) put a right to the hall directly and specifically in question; M is a party; the 2020 lease is a transfer pendente lite creating new rights: the 2005 unregistered lease (s.17 Registration Act; s.107 TPA) never created a valid interest beyond a tenancy from month to month and had expired in 2010; the 2012 letter and the 2019 compromise decree in a suit to which R was not a party cannot create an antecedent right binding R — the Supreme Court treated such a compromise as a \"device\" and rejected Bishan Singh v. Khazan Singh reliance. s.65A (mortgagor's leases limited to 3 years) does not displace s.52; if anything it too invalidates the lease as against R; s.64 CPC may also apply if the hall was attached. Result: the lease is subject to R's decree; the auction-purchaser takes free of it; T must vacate, with his remedy in damages against M.",
