          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 1",
          "answer": "Model answer. Meaning. A mortgage is a transfer of an interest in specific immovable property as security for a debt (s.58(a)). The mortgagor's right, on payment of the mortgage-money after it becomes due, to get back his documents, possession and a re-transfer is the right of redemption (s.60) — the Indian statutory counterpart of the English \"equity of redemption\", which Chancery evolved to relieve borrowers from the forfeiture of land on the \"law day\" ( Kreglinger ). Any stipulation in the mortgage which prevents, evades or hampers this right is a clog (fetter) on the equity of redemption and is void: \"once a mortgage, always a mortgage\" (Lindley M.R., Santley v. Wilde , adopted in Ganga Dhar v. Shankar Lal AIR 1958 SC 770). Statutory basis. Section 60 is \"unqualified in its terms, and contains no saving provision … in favour of contracts to the contrary\" ( Mohd. Sher Khan v. Swami Dayal , PC); the right may be extinguished only \"by act of the parties or by decree of a Court\" (proviso) — i.e., a subsequent independent contract, merger, or a final decree of foreclosure/sale ( Jayasingh Dnyanu Mhoprekar , followed in Shivdev Singh ). The doctrine applies only to terms forming part of the mortgage transaction, not to later variations. Rationale. \"Necessitous men are not, truly speaking, free men\" ( Vernon v. Bethell ); the court relieves where the bargain shows \"want of conscience\" — \"Was the mortgagor oppressed? Was he imposed upon?\" ( Ganga Dhar ). It is \"a rule of justice, equity and good conscience\" ( Pomal Kanji ). Illustrations of clogs. (i) Forfeiture/deemed sale on default — the \"redeem within six months after 85 years or the deed becomes a sale deed\" clause struck down in Ganga Dhar ; (ii) redemption made impossible or illusory — 99-year mortgages with interest payable only at the end and liberty to rebuild at the mortgagor's cost ( Pomal Kanji Govindji v. Vrajlal Karsandas Purohit AIR 1989 SC 436), or a 99-year possessory mortgage of 23 kanals for a meagre Rs 7,000 from a hard-pressed farmer ( Shivdev Singh v. Sucha Singh AIR 2000 SC 1935); (iii) penal interest from the date of the mortgage; (iv) redemption conditional on paying other debts; (v) option to purchase granted in the mortgage ( Samuel v. Jarrah Timber ); (vi) collateral advantages surviving redemption ( Noakes v. Rice ). Not clogs: a fair long term freely agreed ( Ganga Dhar — 85 years upheld; suit premature), reasonable notice before tender (s.60), necessary repairs on account, reasonable collateral advantages ending on redemption ( Kreglinger ), a later sale of the equity of redemption. Long-term mortgages — the test. Length of term is not by itself a clog ( Ganga Dhar ); whether it is depends on the totality of circumstances — period, circumstances of creation, financial position of the mortgagor and his relationship with the mortgagee, socio-economic conditions and price spiral, custom, interest and reconstruction clauses ( Pomal Kanji , para 27). In modern inflationary conditions \"long term, very long term, taken with other relevant factors, would create a presumption that it is a clog\"; it makes the mortgage \"illusory, though not decisive\" and is \"prima facie an indication\" ( Pomal Kanji ; Shivdev Singh ). \"Freedom of contract is permissible provided it does not lead to taking advantage of the oppressed or depressed people.\" Effect. The offending term is void and ignored; the rest of the deed stands (severability — Ganga Dhar ); the mortgagor may redeem at once (if the term itself is the clog) or after the term (if only the forfeiture clause is struck); the mortgagee's tenants must yield possession on redemption.",
