          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Outline. (1) Meaning and origin of the maxim (Parts 7.1–7.2). (2) s.60 — statutory right; no contract to the contrary; proviso. (3) Ganga Dhar in detail: facts (85 years + 6 months + deemed sale), two-tier analysis (total deprivation void per se; restriction relieved only for want of conscience), fact-based test, factors negating oppression (benefit of freeing the Kacheri; 47-year delay; smaller security; reasonable interest; repairs on account as a safeguard), co-extensive rights, severability; result — term upheld, suit premature. (4) Pomal Kanji in detail: 99 years + interest at end + rebuild + distress + only house; Ganga Dhar decided \"in the background of a sedate and fixed state of affairs\"; \"different conspectus\" in inflationary times; multi-factor test; presumption from very long term; \"freedom of contract … oppressed or depressed people\"; result — clog; tenants out. (5) Shivdev Singh as synthesis: applies both; 99 years for Rs 7,000 = clog. (6) Assessment: not a change of principle but of evidential emphasis — the burden has practically shifted to the mortgagee in very-long-term mortgages; the \"want of conscience\" test survives as the touchstone; length alone still not decisive. Conclude with policy: protection of necessitous borrowers v. sanctity of contract; parallels in Usurious Loans Act and s.16 Contract Act.",
