          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 3",
          "answer": "Answer. Clause (iii) is a forfeiture converting security into purchase — void per se ( Ganga Dhar ; Vernon v. Bethell ; s.60 proviso permits extinguishment only by subsequent act or decree). Clauses (i)–(ii) with the 99-year term replicate Pomal Kanji : interest on a part of the principal accumulating for 99 years (Rs 20,000 × 12% × 99 ≈ Rs 23.76 lakh simple interest) makes redemption \"practically impossible\"; unlimited reconstruction debited to the mortgagor in an inflationary era; distress; only residence — presumption of a clog; the term itself is struck, so the principal is due now and the suit is not premature . B's Ganga Dhar plea fails because, unlike that case, there is evidence of oppression and no benefit to the mortgagor; delay of 34 years does not cure a clog (the financial condition at 1990 is what matters — Pomal Kanji , para 36; Shivdev Singh , 26 years). Decree: preliminary decree for redemption (O.XXXIV r.7) on payment of Rs 50,000 with such interest as the court finds fair (the court may relieve against the accumulated interest as penal/unconscionable — Usurious Loans Act), and the cost of only necessary repairs (s.63A, s.72); B's tenants must deliver possession.",
