          "unitNumber": 11,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 1",
          "answer": "Q.1 (Essay). \"The dividing line between a lease and a licence is clear, though sometimes it becomes very thin or even blurred.\" Define lease and licence, distinguish between them, and discuss the tests laid down by the Supreme Court for determining whether a document creates a lease or a licence. (DU pattern; 20 marks) Model answer. Definitions. s.105 TPA: a lease is a transfer of a right to enjoy immovable property for a certain time (express/implied) or in perpetuity, for premium and/or rent, accepted by the transferee. s.52 Easements Act: a licence is a right granted to a person to do something in or upon the grantor's immovable property which would otherwise be unlawful, the right not amounting to an easement or an interest in the property. A lease is thus a transfer of an interest (a leasehold estate) carrying exclusive possession (s.108(b)); a licence is a personal permission that leaves legal possession with the owner and is merely a defence to trespass ( Thomas v. Sorrell ). Distinction (tabulate): interest v. permission; exclusive possession v. occupation; consideration essential v. not; transferable/heritable (s.108(j)) v. personal (s.56); binds transferee (s.109) v. not (s.59); determination only under s.111 with relief against forfeiture v. revocable (s.60) subject to two exceptions and deemed revocation (s.62); lessee can sue trespassers v. licensee cannot; registration (s.107) v. none; tenant protected by Rent Acts v. licensee not. Tests. (1) Older English exclusive-possession test ( Glenwood Lumber ). (2) Shift to intention ( Errington ; Cobb v. Lane ). (3) Associated Hotels v. R.N. Kapoor — Subba Rao J.'s four propositions: substance over form; intention is the real test; interest = lease, permission with possession in owner = licence; exclusive possession → prima facie tenant unless negativing circumstances. Applied to hold a \"deed of licence\" of hotel saloon rooms a lease (fixed payment irrespective of business, repair/meter covenants, transferability \"destructive of licence\"). (4) Sohan Lal Naraindas — exclusive possession not decisive; label and recital not decisive; crucial question is whether an interest was intended. (5) D'Souza — construction is a question of law; \"landlord\", covenant against sub-letting, renewal at occupier's will show a lease; Rent Acts construed to suppress the mischief; exclusive possession strong where no family/friendship. (6) Quality Cut Pieces — parties can genuinely contract for a licence; avoiding the Rent Act is not per se sham; store control, turnover commission and single gate → licence. (7) Delta International — six propositions: intention from the document; express negation of tenancy decisive unless camouflage pleaded and proved; without a document exclusive possession most relevant; non-party landlord not bound; grantor without power to lease → licence; lawful construction preferred. (8) Samir Kumar Chatterjee — where no document, onus on the party asserting licence. Conclusion. The line is conceptually clear (interest v. permission) but factually thin because possession can be handed over under both. Indian law resolves it by intention, read primarily from the document (with camouflage as the safety valve), and, failing a document, from conduct where exclusive possession is the leading pointer.",
