          "unitNumber": 11,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Q.2 (Problem). H, a hotel company, executes a \"deed of licence\" allowing K to use two rooms in the hotel for a hair-dressing saloon for one year at Rs 9,600 payable in advance \"whether business is done or not\". K must keep the rooms in good repair, pay for electricity and water through his own meters, make no alterations without consent, and may transfer the licence with H's consent; on default H may terminate without notice. K applies for fixation of standard rent under the Rent Act. H contends (i) K is a licensee, (ii) the rooms are \"rooms in a hotel\" exempt from the Act. Decide. Model answer. The facts are Associated Hotels of India v. R.N. Kapoor . (i) Lease or licence: apply the four propositions. The nomenclature \"licence\" is not decisive; K has exclusive possession of defined rooms for a term; the payment is a fixed sum irrespective of business (rent, not fee); the covenants to repair, to pay utilities through his own meters, not to alter, and forfeiture with interest are lease covenants; the power to transfer is \"destructive of any theory of licence\" (a licence is personal — s.56 Easements Act). Nothing (family, friendship, service, hotel-guest control) negatives the inference from exclusive possession. Hence K is a lessee/tenant (Subba Rao and S.K. Das JJ.). (ii) Exemption: on the 1947 Delhi Act, the majority (Sarkar J. — plain meaning: any room in a hotel building; S.K. Das J. — room physically in the hotel whose user is connected with the hotel's purpose, a saloon being an amenity for guests) held the rooms exempt, Subba Rao J. dissenting (exemption confined to rooms let as hotel accommodation). Result: the Rent Controller lacked jurisdiction to fix standard rent. Under the present Delhi Rent Control Act 1958, \"premises\" excludes \"a room in a hotel or lodging house\" (s.2(i)) and premises with rent above Rs 3,500 p.m. are outside the Act (s.3(c)), so today K would in any event not get standard rent; but as a lessee he retains all rights under Chapter V of the TPA (notice under s.106, relief against forfeiture, etc.).",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Statutory provision under Transfer of Property Act, 1882",
            "Application of judicial ratio from landmark DU precedents",
            "Structured legal conclusion and rights of parties"
          ]
