          "unitNumber": 11,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 3",
          "answer": "Q.3 (Problem). D, a monthly tenant of a petrol-pump site in Calcutta, cannot sub-let without the landlord's written consent. D executes a \"leave and licence\" with an oil company E: clause 12 states the document is not a lease and E will not claim tenancy; clauses 13–17 provide that if the landlord consents D will execute a sub-lease and E will buy the equipment for Rs 2.5 lakh; clause 18 fixes a \"licence fee\" of Rs 3,950 p.m. for the \"demised premises\"; E may run the pump in D's name; D bears all outgoings. Consent is never obtained. Fifteen years later D revokes and sues for possession. E pleads sub-tenancy relying on exclusive possession and the words \"demised premises\". Decide. Would the position differ if the head-landlord sued D for eviction on the ground of sub-letting? Model answer. Delta International v. Shyam Sunder Ganeriwalla . Apply the six propositions: intention from the document; cl. 12 expressly negatives tenancy and E has not pleaded/proved camouflage — between two companies with legal advice there is no exploitation, so D'Souza 's purposive approach does not apply; exclusive possession is \"one of the most relevant factors\" only in the absence of a clear document and \"loses its importance\" against express terms; \"demised premises\" is read in context — used only to compute a \"licence fee\" (had a lease been intended, \"rent\" would have been used) and it included plant and business; the business was run in D's name, D bore outgoings, revocation on default (cl. 9) is consistent with licence (s.62(c); Clubwala ); a sub-lease was expressly reserved for the future subject to consent and never materialised; and since both knew a sub-lease without consent was illegal (s.14(1) W.B. Act), the lawful construction is preferred. Held: licence ; D recovers possession. Second part: the head-landlord is not a party to the deed and is not bound by the \"licence\" recital (prop. 4; Rajbir Kaur para 32; Clubwala ); he may prove on the facts (exclusive possession for consideration; parting with possession) that D sub-let/parted with possession and seek eviction under the Rent Act. The label protects D against E but not against his landlord.",
