          "unitNumber": 11,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 4",
          "answer": "Q.4 (Problem). O lets P into his house under a \"leave and licence\" agreement for one year at Rs 350 p.m. \"compensation\". The document calls O \"landlord (hereinafter called the licensor)\", forbids P from \"sub-letting, under-letting or parting with possession\", and allows P to renew at his will on one month's notice. P stays for six years paying monthly. O sues P in the civil court for possession as a licensee. P contends he is a tenant and the civil court has no jurisdiction under the Rent Act. Decide. Model answer. B.V. D'Souza v. Antonio Fausto Fernandes . The construction of the document is a question of law. Applying Associated Hotels : substance over form; the document itself reveals a tenancy — \"landlord\"; a covenant against sub-letting is appropriate only to a lease (a licensee cannot sub-let at all); renewal at the occupier's will confers an interest; the monthly \"compensation\" is rent; P has exclusive possession and the transaction is purely commercial with no friendship or family element to negative tenancy ( Facchini v. Bryson ). The Rent Act is construed to advance its object; the landlord cannot defeat it by words of his choice. The one-year term having expired and payments having been accepted for years, P is a month-to-month tenant by holding over (ss.116, 106). The civil suit is barred (s.56 Goa Act / s.50 DRC Act) and must be dismissed; O must proceed before the Rent Controller on a statutory ground. Distinguish Delta : here O is the owner-landlord with power to lease and superior bargaining position, and the document's internal terms contradict the label.",
