          "questionNumber": 6,
          "unitNumber": 11,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 5",
          "answer": "Q.5 (Problem). T, a tenant of a house, sues S for eviction alleging that in 1979 he permitted S, out of compassion, to occupy one room gratuitously for four months and S has not left. S pleads that his father and he lived there since the 1960s, that he is a sub-tenant at Rs 20 p.m. having paid Rs 2,000 advance, and occupies a room, kitchen and toilet exclusively; he produces a 1965 school certificate and postal receipts with the address. Both courts decree the suit, holding that S failed to prove tenancy and disbelieving his witnesses. In second appeal, advise S. Model answer. Samir Kumar Chatterjee v. Hirendra Nath Ghosh . (1) Onus : where the plaintiff sues on the footing of licence and the defendant pleads tenancy, the initial burden is on the plaintiff to prove induction as a licensee (ss.101–102 Evidence Act); the courts below misplaced the onus — an error of law justifying interference under s.100 CPC despite concurrent findings ( Dilbagrai Punjabi ). (2) Evidence : the plea and documents of residence since 1965 destroy the story of a 1979 induction and were ignored; the plaintiff's witnesses are interested; his rent counterfoils only prove his own payments and the appellate court's own signature comparison (s.73) is hazardous; the absence of receipts is explained by T's fear of exposing an unauthorised sub-letting; O.XLI r.31 CPC was not complied with. (3) Nature of occupation : absent a document, intention is gathered from conduct — exclusive possession of self-contained accommodation for years with payment is a tenancy, not a four-month gratuitous licence ( Delta prop. 3). Advice: the second appeal should succeed and the suit be dismissed; S is a sub-tenant and can be evicted only under the Rent Act (and the head-landlord, not T, may have a ground against T for sub-letting).",
