          "year": "2024",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 3,
          "unitNumber": 12,
          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 2",
          "answer": "Q.2 (Problem — DU pattern). M, a widow, executes a registered gift deed of her lands in favour of her daughter-in-law D at the time of D's marriage to her son. The deed makes D \"full owner in possession from today\" and thereafter states that D shall render service to M, maintain her for life and perform her funeral rites, and shall not sell the land during M's lifetime. After the son's death D, being neglected, goes to her father's house and remarries. M executes a registered deed of cancellation, pleading that the gift was conditional on service and maintenance, and that D's remarriage defeats it. D sues for declaration and possession. Decide. Would your answer differ if the deed had opened with the words \"in order to provide for my maintenance\" and provided that on D's failure to maintain the gift shall stand revoked? Model answer. The problem is Tila Bewa v. Mana Bewa . (1) Completion: the gift satisfies ss.122–123 (registered, attested, accepted — D took possession); the donor's continued presence or absence of mutation is irrelevant. (2) Revocation — s.126: a gift may be revoked only (a) on an agreed specified event not dependent on the donor's will, or (b) on grounds on which a contract may be rescinded, excluding want or failure of consideration; \"save as aforesaid, a gift cannot be revoked.\" Here (a) there is no agreement/defeasance clause that failure of service or maintenance shall revoke the gift; (b) failure to maintain is at most failure of consideration, which s.126 expressly excludes — the remedy is a suit for maintenance. (3) Construction: the deed makes an absolute transfer in its operative portion and the directions to serve, maintain and perform funeral rites follow it; per Barman J. such directions are \"pious wishes\", not conditions. The restraint on sale during M's life is at most a partial restraint, not a ground of revocation. (4) Remarriage and M's apprehension of waste are legally irrelevant. (5) The deed of cancellation is a nullity — a donor cannot revoke \"at will and pleasure\" but must get the deed set aside by a court on a valid ground; M pleaded no undue influence or fraud with issues framed. Decree for D: declaration of title and immediate possession (the HC even set aside an appellate condition postponing possession to M's death). Alternative deed: if the deed opened with the maintenance purpose followed by the operative clause, the gift would be \"subject to the liability to maintain\"; and if it further provided that on default the gift shall stand revoked, that is a valid agreement under s.126 para 1 — an event (D's default) not dependent on M's will — so M could revoke on proof of default (subject to bona fide purchasers for value without notice). Today, if M is a senior citizen, s.23 of the 2007 Act would also allow the Tribunal to declare such a conditional transfer void ( Sudesh Chhikara requires the condition to be part of the transfer).",
