          "question": "DU Semester Examination Question 3",
          "answer": "Q.3 (Problem). B, an ailing illiterate widow aged about 70, lives with her only daughter K, a poor widow who manages B's land. While K is away, R and S, remote collaterals of B's late husband, take B to town saying she will be shown to a doctor; there a gift deed of B's entire land in their favour, reciting past services, is scribed by R's scribe, attested by R's men and registered the same day. B on return tells K she was made to sign \"some paper\"; both lodge a police complaint; B dies four days later. R and S take possession. K sues to set aside the deed and for possession. The first appellate court dismisses the suit because K did not prove fraud with particulars, did not cross-examine the scribe about reading over, and the Sub-Registrar's endorsement shows admission. Advise K in second appeal. Model answer. Kartari v. Kewal Krishan . (1) Law: a gift must be made \"voluntarily\" (s.122) and may be set aside on any ground on which a contract may be rescinded (s.126 para 2) — undue influence under s.16 Contract Act. The three-stage test ( Subhas Chandra Das Mushib ): position to dominate; use of it for unfair advantage; if the transaction is unconscionable, burden on the donee (s.16(3)). (2) Position to dominate: age ~70, illness, illiteracy, isolation from her natural adviser (daughter deliberately kept out), physical custody of the collaterals under a pretext. (3) Unfair advantage: the donees engaged the scribe, procured witnesses and registration on the same day — the beneficiary's leading part \"by itself is sufficient\" ( Vellasawmy Servai suspicious-circumstances rule applied to gifts); natural heir wholly excluded; recital of \"services\" unproved. (4) Unconscionability: the donor gave her entire property, kept nothing for maintenance and disinherited a dependent widowed daughter — she \"would not have agreed to such a transaction\". Hence the burden shifted to R and S. (5) Pardanashin rule: B being illiterate and ignorant, R and S had to prove that the deed was read over, explained and substantially understood — that the \"mental act\" coincided with the \"physical act\" ( Farid-un-nisa ; Chinta Dasya extending the rule beyond pardanashin women). They led no such proof; the scribe's claim that he wrote what she dictated is incredible given the legal language. (6) Appellate court's errors: failure to plead particulars of fraud is fatal only to fraud, not to undue influence; no cross-examination on \"another document\" was needed since K set up none; the Sub-Registrar's endorsement is a routine, rebuttable presumption; the scribe's register entry of the police complaint (thumb-marked, kept in course of business) is primary evidence and shows B's contemporaneous objection and ignorance of the contents; the court misplaced the burden on K — an error of law under s.100 CPC. Advice: the second appeal should succeed; the deed be set aside as obtained by undue influence; K, as natural heir (Hindu Succession Act s.15 — daughter is Class I heir of a female Hindu), gets possession with costs.",
