          "answer": "Q.4 (Essay). \"Save as aforesaid, a gift cannot be revoked.\" Explain the circumstances in which a gift may be suspended or revoked under the Transfer of Property Act, with the help of illustrations and decided cases. Model answer (outline). Introduce the irrevocability principle and its rationale (a completed transfer vests title; the donee's security; contrast Muslim law). Door 1 — agreed condition (para 1): requirements — agreement of both parties; specified event; not dependent on the donor's will; valid condition (ss.10–12, 25, 31–34); Illustration (a) (reverter on failure of donee's line); Illustration (b) (revocable at pleasure → void pro tanto); suspension v. revocation. Door 2 — contractual rescission (para 2): coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation (ss.15–19A Contract Act); mistake; Kartari in detail (three-stage test, burden, pardanashin rule); exclusion of \"want or failure of consideration\" — Tila Bewa (maintenance direction as pious wish; need for defeasance clause; remedy is maintenance suit; cancellation deed a nullity). Para 3 — no revocation for ingratitude, remarriage, changed circumstances, apprehension of waste. Para 4 — bona fide transferees for value without notice protected. Procedure — revocation for vitiated consent requires a suit within three years (Art. 59 Limitation Act); statutory addition — s.23 Senior Citizens Act 2007 ( Sudesh Chhikara ; Urmila Dixit ). Conclude with the flowchart logic: defeasance clause or vitiated consent — nothing else.",
