          "anchorId": "roadmap",
          "facts": "Count Folke Bernadotte , a Swedish national, was appointed United Nations Mediator in Palestine. On 17 September 1948 , while engaged in the performance of his duties in Jerusalem, he was assassinated; a French observer, Colonel André Sérot, was killed in the same attack. The persons responsible were never apprehended by the authorities of the State concerned (Israel, then not yet a UN Member). The UN believed reparation was due and, more importantly, wanted a legal answer about who could claim — it or the national State. On 3 December 1948 the General Assembly adopted Resolution 258 (III) . Its preamble records that the series of tragic events which had befallen agents of the United Nations \"raises, with greater urgency than ever, the question of the arrangements to be made by the United Nations with a view to ensuring to its agents the fullest measure of protection in the future and ensuring that reparation be made for the injuries suffered\"; and that \"it is highly desirable that the Secretary-General should be able to act without question as efficaciously as possible with a view to obtaining any reparation due.\" By that Resolution the GA submitted two legal questions to the Court for an advisory opinion. The two questions, verbatim Question I: \"In the event of an agent of the United Nations in the performance of his duties suffering injury in circumstances involving the responsibility of a State, has the United Nations, as an Organization, the capacity to bring an international claim against the responsible de jure or de facto government with a view to obtaining the reparation due in respect of the damage caused (a) to the United Nations, (b) to the victim or to persons entitled through him?\" Question II: \"In the event of an affirmative reply on point 1 (b), how is action by the United Nations to be reconciled with such rights as may be possessed by the State of which the victim is a national?\"",
          "issues": "Does the UN possess international personality? The Charter nowhere says so expressly. If so, does personality include the capacity to bring an international claim (a) for damage to the Organisation itself, and (b) for damage to its agent? Can the claim be brought against a State that is not a Member of the United Nations? How is the Organisation's \"functional protection\" to be reconciled with the national State's right of diplomatic protection? Does the fact that the agent bears the nationality of the defendant State bar the claim?",
          "arguments": "Submissions of applicant State versus respondent State before the International Court of Justice.",
          "ratio": "Functional necessity / implied powers: an international organisation possesses the powers \"conferred upon it by necessary implication as being essential to the performance of its duties,\" even where the constituent instrument is silent. Relative, not uniform, personality: subjects of law are not identical in nature or extent of rights; personality depends on the needs of the community. The UN is an international person but is neither a State nor a super-State. Objective international personality: an organisation created by a vast majority of the international community possesses personality erga omnes , and not merely as against its own members. Functional protection: an independent right of the Organisation, parallel to but distinct from diplomatic protection, arising by necessary intendment from the Charter and justified by the agent's independence and by Article 100. Reparation as a general principle: the breach of an international engagement entails an obligation to make reparation in adequate form ( Factory at Chorzów ). Primacy of function over form: the identity of the claimant depends on whose right has been breached, not on the identity or nationality of the person injured.",
          "examTips": "Functional necessity / implied powers: an international organisation possesses the powers \"conferred upon it by necessary implication as being essential to the performance of its duties,\" even where the constituent instrument is silent. Relative, not uniform, personality: subjects of law are not identical in nature or extent of rights; personality depends on the needs of the community. The UN is an international person but is neither a State nor a super-State. Objective international personality: an organisation created by a vast majority of the international community possesses personality erga omnes , and not merely as against its own members. Functional protection: an independent right of the Organisation, parallel to but distinct from diplomatic protection, arising by necessary intendment from the Charter and justified by the agent's independence and by Article 100. Reparation as a general principle: the breach of an international engagement entails an obligation to make reparation in adequate form ( Factory at Chorzów ). Primacy of function over form: the identity of the claimant depends on whose right has been breached, not on the identity or nationality of the person injured."
