          "anchorId": "case2",
          "facts": "Background. The proceedings arose out of events from the spring of 2014 onwards: the annexation of Crimea by the Russian Federation and the armed conflict in eastern Ukraine (Donetsk and Luhansk regions), where Ukraine alleged that Russia was supporting, arming and financing armed groups (the self-styled \"DPR\" and \"LPR\"). 16 January 2017: Ukraine filed an Application in the Registry of the Court, invoking as the basis of jurisdiction Article 24(1) of the ICSFT 1999 (for the events in eastern Ukraine) and Article 22 of CERD 1965 (for the situation in Crimea). Both are \"compromissory clauses\" — treaty provisions conferring jurisdiction on the ICJ for disputes relating to the interpretation or application of the treaty. 19 April 2017: the Court made an Order on provisional measures, finding that the conditions for the indication of measures were met only in part and only under CERD . It did not find the mens rea element of terrorism-financing offences to be \"plausible.\" It ordered Russia, pending the final decision: (i) to refrain from maintaining or imposing measures that prevent the Crimean Tatar community from conducting its own representative institutions, including the Mejlis; (ii) to ensure the availability of Ukrainian-language education; and (iii) both Parties to refrain from any action aggravating the dispute. 12 September 2018: the Russian Federation raised preliminary objections to the jurisdiction of the Court and to the admissibility of the Application. Hearings were held in June 2019. Judgment on the objections was delivered on 8 November 2019 .",
          "issues": "Core legal questions regarding international obligations and state responsibility.",
          "arguments": "Submissions of applicant State versus respondent State before the International Court of Justice.",
          "ratio": "Treaty crimes and \"any person\": a criminal-law suppression convention may reach conduct by State agents as \"persons,\" even though the State itself is not made responsible for the offence — the responsibility is for the State's failure to take measures and to cooperate (Art. 9(1)). State financing of terrorism lies outside the ICSFT — a limiting interpretation of a suppression convention, with the express saving that such conduct is not thereby lawful (governed by the Charter's non-intervention and non-use-of-force rules). Jurisdiction ratione materiae vs. merits: questions of plausibility and mens rea belong to the merits and should not generally be examined at the jurisdictional stage — a clarification of the Oil Platforms / Genocide jurisprudence. Compromissory-clause preconditions: negotiation and arbitration requirements in suppression conventions are justiciable procedural preconditions; \"genuine attempt\" is a flexible, fact-sensitive test; futility or deadlock suffices. CERD Art. 22's preconditions are alternative, not cumulative — a purposive, \"effective and prompt\" reading of an anti-discrimination treaty's dispute-settlement clause. Exhaustion of local remedies is inapplicable where a State challenges a pattern of conduct rather than espousing the cause of identified nationals — an important refinement of the diplomatic-protection analogy. For the law of subjects: the case demonstrates that individuals (including State officials) may be the direct addressees of international obligations under treaty regimes, while State responsibility under such treaties is engaged only through the State's own ancillary obligations — the modern, graduated picture of individual and State responsibility side by side.",
          "examTips": "Treaty crimes and \"any person\": a criminal-law suppression convention may reach conduct by State agents as \"persons,\" even though the State itself is not made responsible for the offence — the responsibility is for the State's failure to take measures and to cooperate (Art. 9(1)). State financing of terrorism lies outside the ICSFT — a limiting interpretation of a suppression convention, with the express saving that such conduct is not thereby lawful (governed by the Charter's non-intervention and non-use-of-force rules). Jurisdiction ratione materiae vs. merits: questions of plausibility and mens rea belong to the merits and should not generally be examined at the jurisdictional stage — a clarification of the Oil Platforms / Genocide jurisprudence. Compromissory-clause preconditions: negotiation and arbitration requirements in suppression conventions are justiciable procedural preconditions; \"genuine attempt\" is a flexible, fact-sensitive test; futility or deadlock suffices. CERD Art. 22's preconditions are alternative, not cumulative — a purposive, \"effective and prompt\" reading of an anti-discrimination treaty's dispute-settlement clause. Exhaustion of local remedies is inapplicable where a State challenges a pattern of conduct rather than espousing the cause of identified nationals — an important refinement of the diplomatic-protection analogy. For the law of subjects: the case demonstrates that individuals (including State officials) may be the direct addressees of international obligations under treaty regimes, while State responsibility under such treaties is engaged only through the State's own ancillary obligations — the modern, graduated picture of individual and State responsibility side by side."
