          "name": "MAP How to Use These Notes Primary source. These notes are built on the DU Faculty of Law LB-205 case material for 2022 ( Public International Law — Cases Selected and Edited ), which prints the syllabus at pp. iii–v and the full text of six Topic-2 materials at pp. 7–36 (Right of Passage, Asylum, Effect of Awards, Preah Vihear, Nuclear Weapons AO, Kosovo AO) and of the North Sea Continental Shelf cases at pp. 127–139. To that base the notes add: the DU classroom notes (PIL Notes, Stanzin Chostak), the LED Exam Capsule, and S.K. Verma, An Introduction to Public International Law (2nd ed.) , whose Chapter on &ldquo;Sources of Modern International Law&rdquo; is the prescribed-text spine of this topic. Where a judgement is prescribed but not printed in the DU volume (Lotus, Island of Palmas, Namibia, Western Sahara, Chagos), the notes say so and use the judgment itself and the official ICJ summaries. 1. Learn the skeleton first Every question on this topic is answered out of Article 38(1) . Memorise its four clauses verbatim (this document, §2.1 A) and the five-head map (§2.1 E). Then learn one paragraph of detail under each head. That alone secures a pass mark. 2. Then learn the two work-horses Custom (§2.1.2) and General Principles (§2.1.3) carry more marks than anything else in Topic 2 — twenty-eight of the recorded questions from 1991 to 2025 are on these two heads. Give them the most revision time. 3. Learn the cases by their &ldquo;one line&rdquo; Lotus = opinio juris cannot be inferred from abstention . North Sea = the seven tests for treaty-to-custom . Asylum = regional custom must be constant and uniform . Right of Passage = local custom between only two States . Preah Vihear = acquiescence and estoppel . Effect of Awards = res judicata . Nuclear Weapons = the normative value of GA resolutions . Namibia, Western Sahara, Kosovo and Chagos = the law-creating weight of GA and SC resolutions and of advisory opinions . The syllabus, mapped Table 2.1 — Topic 2 as the syllabus states it, and where each head is treated Syllabus head (LB-205, p. iii–iv) Prescribed case(s) Where in these notes 2.1 Statute of the ICJ, 1945 (Article 38) — (the Article itself) §2.0, §2.1 2.1.1 International Treaties and Conventions — §2.1.1 2.1.2 International Custom 3. Lotus (PCIJ 1927) · 4. North Sea Continental Shelf (ICJ 1969) · 5. Right of Passage over Indian Territory (ICJ 1960) · 6. Asylum (ICJ 1950) §2.1.2; Cases 1–4 2.1.3 General Principles of Law Recognised by Civilised Nations 7. Effect of Awards of Compensation (ICJ 1954) · 8. Island of Palmas (PCA 1928) · 9. Temple of Preah Vihear (ICJ 1962) §2.1.3; Cases 5–7 2.1.4 Judicial Decisions, Juristic Opinion — §2.1.4 2.1.5 Ex aequo et bono — (Art. 38(2)) §2.1.5 2.2.1 Resolutions of General Assembly 10. Legality of the Threat or Use of Nuclear Weapons (AO 1996) §2.2.1; Case 8 2.2.2 Resolutions of Security Council 11. Namibia (AO 1971) · 12. Western Sahara (AO 1975) · 13. Kosovo (AO 2010) §2.2.2; Cases 9–11 2.2.3 Advisory Opinions of PCIJ and ICJ 14. Chagos Archipelago (AO 2019) §2.2.3; Case 12 The one structural point to hold on to. The syllabus divides this topic into two blocks. 2.1 is the &ldquo;classical&rdquo; list of Article 38(1) — the sources the Court must apply. 2.2 is the &ldquo;modern&rdquo; or &ldquo;other&rdquo; block — resolutions of the General Assembly, resolutions of the Security Council, and advisory opinions. The modern block is not in Article 38 at all, and the whole of your answer on 2.2 is therefore an argument about whether, and how, materials that Article 38 omits can still make law. Sections §2.2.1–2.2.3 below give you that argument, case by case. 2.0 The Concept of &ldquo;Sources&rdquo; of International Law",
