          "id": "pil-c-u2-5",
          "name": "North Sea Continental Shelf Cases (Federal Republic of Germany / Denmark; Federal Republic of Germany / Netherlands)",
          "citation": "ICJ Reports / Landmark International Precedent",
          "unitNumber": 2,
          "unit": "Topic 2: Sources of International Law (Treaties, Custom, General Principles)",
          "file": "sem 2/PIL/Topic2_Sources_of_International_Law_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case2",
          "facts": "By two Special Agreements concluded between the Federal Republic of Germany and Denmark, and between the Federal Republic and the Netherlands, the parties asked the Court to determine the principles and rules of international law applicable to the delimitation, as between each pair of States, of the areas of the continental shelf in the North Sea which appertained to each of them. The North Sea is shallow: the whole seabed is continental shelf at a depth of less than 200 metres except for the Norwegian Trough. Much of it had already been divided by agreements between the United Kingdom, Norway, Denmark and the Netherlands, all drawn on the basis of median lines between &ldquo;opposite&rdquo; States. The geography mattered: whereas Denmark's and the Netherlands' coastlines are convex in relation to the North Sea, the German coast is concave , so that an equidistance line drawn between the three States would cut Germany off from a large part of the shelf directly in front of it. Germany, which had signed but not ratified the 1958 Geneva Convention on the Continental Shelf, argued that each State should get a &ldquo;just and equitable share&rdquo; of the shelf in proportion to the length of its sea frontage.",
          "issues": "Was Article 6(2) of the 1958 Geneva Convention on the Continental Shelf — the &ldquo;equidistance–special circumstances&rdquo; rule — applicable to Germany, which was not a party to the Convention? Did Article 6 either declare, or crystallise, a pre-existing or emergent rule of customary international law binding on Germany? Had the equidistance principle, through the Convention's own impact and subsequent State practice, since become a rule of customary international law binding on all States, including Germany? If not, what principles and rules of law governed the delimitation of the shelf between the parties?",
          "arguments": "Submissions of applicant State versus respondent State before the International Court of Justice.",
          "ratio": "The Court held that the Geneva Convention &ldquo; did not embody or crystallize any pre-existing or emergent customary rule of international law , according to which the delimitation of continental shelf areas between adjacent States must, unless the Parties otherwise agree, be carried out on an equidistance–special circumstance basis&rdquo; (para. 69). It held further that the Convention's subsequent effect had not been constitutive of such a rule, and that State practice to date had been insufficient (para. 81). Equidistance, therefore, could not be invoked against Germany as custom. The Court laid down instead the principles of equitable delimitation (paras. 85–101): delimitation is to be effected by agreement in accordance with equitable principles and taking account of all relevant circumstances, so as to leave as much as possible to each party the parts of the shelf that constitute the natural prolongation of its land territory into and under the sea without encroaching on the natural prolongation of the other. Where the application leaves overlapping areas, these are to be divided in agreed proportions, or failing agreement equally, or by joint jurisdiction, user or exploitation. The factors to be taken into account include the general configuration of the coasts and any special or unusual features; the physical and geological structure and natural resources so far as known; and a reasonable degree of proportionality between the extent of the shelf appertaining to each State and the length of its coast.",
          "examTips": "The cumulative conditions for the passage of a treaty rule into custom — norm-creating character; widespread and representative participation including specially affected States; extensive and virtually uniform practice; opinio juris ; and the reservations-clause test. Custom is not to be inferred from mere uniformity of practice ; the subjective element must be shown ( Lotus applied). Distinction between opposite and adjacent coasts in maritime delimitation; and the recognition that equidistance is a method, not a rule. Equitable principles as law ( infra legem ), and the express denial that this amounts to a decision ex aequo et bono . A reasonable degree of proportionality between shelf area and coastal length as a factor in delimitation — a rule the Court invented and which has been applied ever since ( Tunisia/Libya , 1982; Libya/Malta , 1985; the Bay of Bengal arbitration, 2014, where India and Bangladesh argued proportionality, with the Tribunal adjusting the line at the &ldquo;concavity&rdquo; of the Bangladesh coast)."
