          "facts": "(a) Partition and the Radcliffe Award. On 20 February 1947 the British Government announced its intention to transfer power in British India by June 1948; on 3 June 1947 it issued a statement on the method of transfer; and on 18 July 1947 the British Parliament passed the Indian Independence Act, 1947 , which came into force on the &ldquo;appointed day&rdquo;, 15 August 1947, setting up the two independent Dominions of India and Pakistan. Section 2 defined the territories of each Dominion; section 3(1) provided that the Province of Bengal should cease to exist and two new Provinces, East Bengal and West Bengal, should be constituted in its place; section 3(3) provided that the boundaries of the new Provinces &ldquo;shall be such as may be determined... by the award of a boundary commission&rdquo;, and that until then the Bengal districts specified in the First Schedule should be treated as East Bengal and the remainder as West Bengal; section 3(4) defined &ldquo;award&rdquo; as the decision of the Chairman of the commission contained in his report to the Governor-General. On 30 June 1947 the Governor-General announced that Bengal and Punjab would be partitioned. A boundary commission was appointed for Bengal, consisting of four judges of High Courts and a Chairman to be appointed later — and Sir Cyril Radcliffe was appointed Chairman. Its terms of reference required the demarcation of the boundaries of the two parts of Bengal &ldquo;on the basis of ascertaining the contiguous areas of Muslims and non-Muslims&rdquo;, while also taking into account &ldquo;other factors&rdquo;. The Commission made its award on 12 August 1947 — three days before the appointed day — known as the Radcliffe Award . (b) Berubari Union No. 12. The Union with which the case is concerned &ldquo;has an area of 8.75 sq miles and a population of ten to twelve thousand residents&rdquo;. It is situated in Police Station Jalpaiguri, District Jalpaiguri, then part of the Rajshahi Division. It was not specified in the First Schedule to the Independence Act — so, on the face of that Schedule, it would have been part of West Bengal. As the Court explained, however, &ldquo;the First Schedule to the Independence Act did not really come into operation at all&rdquo;, because the Radcliffe Award was made before the appointed day. (c) The Bagge Tribunal. Boundary disputes having arisen, the two Dominions agreed at the Inter-Dominion Conference held at New Delhi on 14 December 1948 to set up a tribunal &ldquo;without delay and in any case not later than 31-1-1949&rdquo; for their adjudication and final decision. This was the Indo-Pakistan Boundaries Disputes Tribunal , presided over by Lord Justice Algot Bagge , whose award — the Bagge Award — was made on 26 January 1950. Critically for the case: &ldquo;no issue was raised about the Berubari Union. In fact no reference was made to the District of Jalpaiguri at all in the proceedings before the Tribunal.&rdquo; (d) The dispute and the Agreement. It was two years later, in 1952, that Pakistan raised the question of Berubari Union for the first time, alleging that under the award Berubari should really have formed part of East Bengal and had been wrongly treated as part of West Bengal. Throughout that period &ldquo;the Berubari Union continued to be in the possession of the Indian Union and was governed as a part of West Bengal&rdquo;. Correspondence between the Prime Ministers followed and the dispute remained alive until 1958, when the two Prime Ministers reached the Agreement of 10 September 1958 (recorded in a joint note of the Commonwealth Secretary, Ministry of External Affairs, India and the Foreign Secretary, Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Pakistan). The two items material to the Reference were: Item 3 (Berubari Union 12): &ldquo;This will be so divided as to give half the area to Pakistan, the other half adjacent to India being retained by India. The Division of Berubari Union 12 will be horizontal, starting from the north east corner of Debiganj Thana. The division should be made in such a manner that the Cooch-Behar Enclaves between Pachagar Thana of East Pakistan and Berubari Union 12 of Jalpaiguri Thana of West Bengal will remain connected as at present with Indian territory and will remain with India. The Cooch-Behar Enclaves lower down between Boda Thana of East Pakistan and Berubari Union 12 will be exchanged along with the general exchange of enclaves and will go to Pakistan.&rdquo; Item 10 (Exchange of Enclaves): &ldquo;Exchange of old Cooch-Behar Enclaves in Pakistan and Pakistan Enclaves in India without claim to compensation for extra area going to Pakistan, is agreed to.&rdquo;",
          "issues": "Was the trial Judge in error in holding that the map (Ext. A-1) on which the appellants relied to show that a horizontal division from the north-east corner of Debiganj Thana would leave no part of Berubari Union No. 12 in Pakistan was &ldquo;neither relevant nor reliable&rdquo;? Was the proposed transfer of the 512 acres of Chilahati constitutional, given (a) the history of the Radcliffe Award, (b) the appellants' reading of Entry 13 of the First Schedule (&ldquo;administered as if...&rdquo;) and (c) their plea of adverse possession? Whether the Ninth Amendment Act's failure to prescribe the precise manner of division made it defective.",
          "arguments": "Submissions of applicant State versus respondent State before the International Court of Justice.",
          "ratio": "Table 3.15 — The Supreme Court's answers in Berubari (I) Question Answer Q.1 (Berubari Union) Yes — legislative action is necessary for the implementation of the Agreement. Q.2 (If so, which law?) (a) A law of Parliament relatable to Article 3 would be incompetent ; (b) a law relatable to Article 368 is competent and necessary ; (c) a law relatable to both Article 368 and Article 3 would be necessary only if Parliament chooses first to amend Article 3; in that case Parliament may pass a law under Article 368 and then follow it up with a law relatable to the amended Article 3 to implement the Agreement. Q.3 (Exchange of Enclaves) Same as (a), (b) and (c) of the answer to Question 2 — because the exchange too involves cession of Indian territory.",
