          "id": "pil-c-u4-1",
          "name": "MAP How to Use These Notes What this topic is. Topic 3 asked what happens when international law and municipal law meet inside a State's courts. Topic 4 asks the prior and larger question: what happens when a State breaks international law? The answer is the law of State responsibility — the body of rules that decides when a State is responsible, for whose conduct, and with what consequences. It is the topic on which the International Court of Justice has done more of its work than on any other, and it is the topic around which seven of the eight hours of this part of the syllabus are built. What the syllabus requires. Six heads: 4.1 the basis of international responsibility (with Corfu Channel , Barcelona Traction , Tehran Hostages and Nicaragua ); 4.2 the constituent elements (damage theory, fault theory, absolute liability and risk theory); 4.3 the Act of State — the rules of attribution (with LaGrand ); 4.4 the forms of reparation (restitution, indemnity, satisfaction, guarantee against repetition, with Costa Rica v. Nicaragua and DRC v. Uganda ); 4.5 the rule of exhaustion of local remedies ; and 4.6 the ILC Draft Code on Responsibility of States for Internationally Wrongful Acts, 2001 . All six heads are treated in that order below. How each head is treated. Every head is built in four layers: (i) the text — the relevant articles of the ILC Articles, quoted, with the ILC's own commentary where it matters; (ii) the doctrine — S.K. Verma's Chapter 11 and the prescribed texts, which supply the theory; (iii) the cases — the seven prescribed judgments in full detail (facts, issues, arguments of both sides, decision, ratio), together with the supporting authorities (Chorzów Factory, Alabama Claims, Caire, Youmans, Tadić, Avena, Bosnia v. Serbia, Gabčíkovo–Nagymaros, Diallo, Pulp Mills, Italy v. Germany and the rest); and (iv) the extra material the syllabus does not print — related conventions, statutes and orders, added at the point where they belong, with real examples (Bhopal, Chernobyl, Cosmos 954, Enrica Lexie, Kulbhushan Jadhav, the Indian nuclear-liability statute). Why the case-list matters here. The seven prescribed cases are not seven illustrations of one rule; they are the seven load-bearing joints of the topic. Corfu Channel establishes that responsibility can be founded on omission and proved by circumstantial evidence, and that a judicial declaration is itself satisfaction. Tehran Hostages separates responsibility for what a State's own organs do from responsibility for what private persons do, and shows how adoption converts the second into the first. Nicaragua fixes the threshold of \"effective control\" for attributing the conduct of irregular forces. Barcelona Traction is about who may invoke responsibility, and contains the modern statement of obligations owed erga omnes . LaGrand is the case on individual rights under a treaty, on the binding character of provisional measures, and on assurances of non-repetition. Costa Rica v. Nicaragua is the case in which the Court first awarded compensation for damage to the environment as such. DRC v. Uganda is the case in which the Court worked out how reparation is quantified on a mass scale, and how compensation relates to satisfaction. Together they cover the whole syllabus. A note on sources. The case material prints the judgments of Nicaragua , LaGrand , DRC v. Uganda and the full text of the ILC Articles, and prints Corfu Channel and Tehran Hostages elsewhere in the volume (in the chapters on the law of the sea and diplomatic immunities). Barcelona Traction and Costa Rica v. Nicaragua are listed in the syllabus but not printed in the case material; they are set out below from the judgments themselves and from S.K. Verma's treatment, and the provenance is stated at the head of each case. ↑ top 4.0 The Problem Stated A. Why This Topic Matters International law has no police force, no bailiff and no prison. What it has instead is the law of State responsibility: the set of rules that says that a breach of an international obligation entails legal responsibility, that responsibility produces legal consequences (cessation, reparation, and in defined circumstances countermeasures), and that the consequences can be demanded of the responsible State before courts, tribunals and political organs. Strip this topic out and the rest of international law becomes advice. As the International Law Commission put it when it sent its Articles to the General Assembly in 2001, the Articles seek \"to promote the peaceful settlement of disputes by making available to States a body of rules which will enable them to determine whether a particular act or omission is wrongful, and if so, what the consequences should be\". Three things give the topic its present shape. First , it is secondary law: unlike the rules that prohibit the use of force, protect diplomats or allocate the continental shelf, the law of responsibility does not tell States what to do; it tells them what follows when they have not done it. That is why the subject can be stated in fifty-nine articles, and why those articles apply indifferently to a breached treaty, a violated boundary, an injured diplomat or a poisoned river. Second , it has moved from doctrine to codification: the subject on which Grotius, Vattel and Anzilotti wrote essays is now governed by a text — the ILC Articles of 2001 — which the International Court of Justice has repeatedly described as reflecting customary international law, and which national courts and investment tribunals cite as a matter of course. Third , it has been renewed by human rights, environmental and investment law, each of which tests the classical categories: can a State be responsible for failing to prevent a private wrong? Is damage to the environment compensable in itself? Are there obligations owed to the international community as a whole — and if so, who may invoke them? Real examples — where this topic shows up India and consular access. In Kulbhushan Jadhav (ICJ, 2019) India obtained a finding that Pakistan had breached Article 36 of the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, 1963 by denying consular access to its national, and an order of \"effective review and reconsideration\" of his conviction and sentence. The reasoning is the reasoning of LaGrand and Avena (Case 27 below). India's remedy was not money but a declaration, review — and a guarantee of non-repetition. Bhopal. The Union Carbide disaster of 1984 is the classic case in which a weak domestic liability regime and a foreign parent company met the international law of State responsibility for injury to aliens, and produced both the Union Carbide settlement (US$470 million, 1989) and India's later statutory response — the Public Liability Insurance Act, 1991 and the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986's section 15 (with the National Green Tribunal's compensation regimes). Enrica Lexie. The killing of two Indian fishermen off Kerala in 2012 by Italian marines, and the arbitral award of 2020, turned in part on Italy's responsibility for the conduct of its armed forces and on India's counter-claims — a live example of the law of attribution and reparation in a maritime setting. Chernobyl, Cosmos 954 and nuclear liability. The 1986 reactor disaster and the crash of a Soviet nuclear-powered satellite in Canada in 1978 are the standard examples of the \"risk\" or \"absolute liability\" theory (§4.2 C): lawful activities that carry serious risk, governed by special conventions imposing liability without proof of fault — a model India adopted in the Civil Liability for Nuclear Damage Act, 2010 and the Nuclear Damages Rules, 2011. B. The Vocabulary You Must Control",
