          "id": "pil-c-u5-1",
          "name": "MAP How to Use These Notes What this topic is. Every other topic in this paper deals with States on land. Topic 5 deals with the 70.8 per cent of the earth's surface that is water, and with the oldest question in the law of nations after war: how much of the sea may a State call its own, and on what terms may it use the rest? The answer, today, is a single instrument — the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, 1982 (\"UNCLOS\"), signed at Montego Bay on 10 December 1982, in force since 16 November 1994, with 320 articles, 17 Parts and 9 Annexes — read together with the four Geneva Conventions of 1958, the 1994 Implementing Agreement on Part XI, the 1995 Fish Stocks Agreement and the 2023 BBNJ Agreement. The whole topic is the working-out of one idea, expressed by the International Court in the North Sea Continental Shelf cases and repeated ever since: \"the land dominates the sea\" — the land territorial situation of a State is the starting point for determining its maritime rights. What the syllabus requires. Six heads, in the order of the syllabus image: 5.1 maritime zones — territorial sea, contiguous zone, continental shelf, exclusive economic zone, high seas; 5.2 delimitation of adjacent and opposite maritime boundaries; 5.3 the concept of the \"common heritage of mankind\" relating to the resources of the international seabed Area; 5.4 international seabed mining — the parallel system of mining; 5.5 Indian maritime interests, policy and law; 5.6 the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea. Seven cases carry the topic: 30. Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries Case (ICJ Rep. 1951, p. 116); 31. Corfu Channel Case (ICJ Rep. 1949, p. 4); 32. North Sea Continental Shelf Cases (ICJ Rep. 1969, p. 3); 33. Libya v. Tunisia Continental Shelf Case (ICJ Rep. 1982, p. 17); 34. Republic of Italy v. Union of India (2013) 4 SCC 721; 35. In the Matter of the Bay of Bengal Maritime Boundary Arbitration (Bangladesh v. India) , PCA, 2014; 36. Maritime Delimitation in the Indian Ocean (Somalia v. Kenya) , ICJ Rep. 2021. All seven are set out below in the seven-part format required by the case material: (1) why the case is in the syllabus, (2) facts, (3) issues, (4) arguments of both sides, (5) decision, (6) principle evolved, (7) examination angle. How each head is treated. Four layers, as in the earlier topics. (i) The text: the governing articles are quoted — UNCLOS articles first, then the corresponding 1958 articles, then the Indian statutory provision, because that is the order in which an examiner expects a coastal State's powers to be justified. (ii) The doctrine: S.K. Verma's Chapter 14 (\"Law of the Sea\") supplies the theory, history and structure; the DU case material supplies the cases and the examination vocabulary; the class notes and the exam capsule supply the Indian framing (\"the land dominates the sea\", the zone first-principles). (iii) The cases: the seven prescribed cases in full, plus thirty supporting authorities — Lotus , I'm Alone , Red Crusader , M/V Saiga , M/V Virginia G , Norstar , Bangladesh/Myanmar , Ghana/Côte d'Ivoire , the 2011 and 2024 Seabed Disputes Chamber opinions, and the rest. (iv) The material the syllabus does not print: every related order, rule, notification and statute is inserted at the point where it belongs — the 1958 Conventions, the 1994 Implementation Agreement, the Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act 1878, the Territorial Waters Act 1956, the Maritime Zones Act 1976 and the notification SO 67/E of 1981, the Maritime Zones of India (Regulation of Fishing by Foreign Vessels) Act 1981, the SUA Act 2002, the Coast Guard Act 1978, the Admiralty Act 2017, the Maritime Anti-Piracy Act 2022, the CLCS and the ISA Regulations — with real examples from Indian and world practice. The two items the teacher highlighted. On the syllabus image two entries are marked in green, and both are treated at disproportionate length below: case 34 , Republic of Italy v. Union of India (2013) 4 SCC 721 — the Enrica Lexie reference, the only Indian case in the syllabus and the meeting point of the contiguous zone, the EEZ, the Maritime Zones Act and the Constitution (§ 5.1.2 , § 5.1.4 , case 34); and head 5.6 , the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea — which is treated with the whole of its Statute, its jurisdiction, its case-law and its Indian dimension (§ 5.6 ). The handwritten notes on the image (\"baseline → low tide line\", \"Sec-2 Art-3\", \"Sec-4 Art-1\", \"500 NM E.E.Z\" style marks, \"High Seas\" underlined) are answered at the corresponding places, and Appendix F collects them in one table. A note on sources and provenance. The DU LB-205 case material (2022) prints, in its Law-of-the-Sea block: Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries ; Corfu Channel ; North Sea Continental Shelf ; Tunisia/Libya ; Republic of Italy v. Union of India ; and Somalia v. Kenya . The facts, pleadings, reasoning and operative paragraphs of those six are taken from the case material and from the judgments themselves. The Bay of Bengal award (case 35) is listed but not printed in the case material; it is set out below from the Award of 7 July 2014 and from the published commentary, and the provenance is stated at the head of the case. Verma's Chapter 14 is the doctrinal spine throughout. Where a figure, date or instrument has been verified against an external official source (the ICJ, ITLOS, the PCA, the ISA, the Ministry of Earth Sciences, the International Seabed Authority) rather than the course material, the text says so. ↑ top 5.0 The Problem Stated",
