          "id": "pil-c-u5-2",
          "name": "5.1 Maritime Zones The first head of the syllabus is a study of belts. UNCLOS does not treat \"the sea\" as one thing: it slices it, from the coast outwards, into spaces in which the coastal State's competence decreases band by band, and alongside that horizontal slicing it separates the airspace , the water column and the seabed and subsoil , because different States may hold rights in different layers at the same spot. Two principles give the structure its rigour. First, everything is measured from the baseline (Art. 5, and exceptionally Arts. 7, 47); change the baseline and every zone moves. Second, the character of the coastal State's competence changes with the belt: sovereignty in internal waters and the territorial sea; control in the contiguous zone; sovereign rights and jurisdiction in the EEZ; sovereign rights over resources only on the continental shelf; and no State competence at all on the high seas and in the Area, where the Convention substitutes the flag State, the international community, or the Authority. The table below is the single most useful object in this topic: an examiner's favourite question is a comparison, and this table contains every comparison worth writing. Table 5.3 — The maritime zones compared: distance, legal character, resources, enforcement, and the governing articles Feature Internal waters Territorial sea Contiguous zone EEZ Continental shelf Distance from baseline Landward of the baseline 0–12 nm (Art. 3) 12–24 nm (Art. 33(2)) 12–200 nm (Art. 57) To the outer edge of the margin, or 200 nm; up to 350 nm in Art. 76 terms Legal character Sovereignty (Art. 8) Sovereignty (Art. 2(1)) Control, not sovereignty (Art. 33) Sovereign rights + jurisdiction (Art. 56) — not sovereignty Sovereign rights for resource purposes (Art. 77) — not sovereignty Layers covered Water, seabed, subsoil, airspace Water, seabed, subsoil, airspace (Art. 2(2)) Water column and seabed, but no resource rights; no airspace rights Water column, seabed and subsoil for economic purposes; airspace not included Seabed and subsoil only; the superjacent water is unaffected (Art. 78) Right of innocent passage None (Art. 8(1)); exception for straight-baseline enclosures (Art. 8(2)) Yes (Art. 17) Passage is the high-seas freedom of navigation exercised under Art. 58 Yes, as freedom of navigation under Art. 58(1); not \"innocent passage\" as such Not applicable (the shelf is about the seabed; navigation is governed by the water-column régime) Resource rights Complete Complete (subject to the passage right) None; only the four enforcement competences All natural resources, living and non-living; energy from water, currents and winds; Art. 56(1)(a) Mineral and other non-living resources of the seabed and subsoil, and sedentary living organisms (Art. 77(4)) Rights of the coastal State \"whether claimed or not\" Not applicable Not applicable Exists only if declared (Art. 33) Exists only if declared (Arts. 55, 57) — but the shelf right exists ipso jure Yes — inherent and automatically vested (Art. 77(3)) Enforcement powers All laws All laws, with the Art. 27 and 28 limits for ships in passage Prevention and punishment of customs, fiscal, immigration and sanitary breaches only (Art. 33(1)) All laws adopted consistently with the Convention, including boarding, inspection, arrest and judicial proceedings (Art. 73), subject to prompt-release and bond All laws for exploring and exploiting the shelf: drilling, installations, safety zones, and no interference with navigation (Arts. 60, 78, 80, 81) Military activities Regulated by the coastal State, subject to treaties Innocent passage cannot be used for any military activity listed in Art. 19(2), e.g. weapons exercise, intelligence gathering (19(2)(a)–(c)); submarines must navigate on the surface (Art. 20) No separate power; the Art. 58 freedoms apply beyond the territorial sea Contested: the coastal State has no general power to restrict foreign naval activity; Art. 58 preserves high-seas freedoms, and Art. 88 confines the EEZ to peaceful purposes Peaceful purposes only in the Area beyond national jurisdiction; on the shelf, Art. 78 and installations rules Delimitation provision Governed by the boundary of the land, unless historic title Art. 15: median/equidistance unless special circumstances justify otherwise Follows the territorial sea and EEZ delimitation (i.e. Art. 15 to 24 nm, then Arts. 74/83 beyond) Art. 74: agreement on the basis of international law to achieve an equitable solution Art. 83: same standard; plus Art. 76 limits and Art. 76(10) (the shelf boundary does not affect the superjacent water boundary) The distinction that decides cases Sovereignty vs sovereign rights. In the territorial sea the coastal State has sovereignty — the verb is \"sovereignty is exercised\". In the EEZ and on the shelf it has sovereign rights — \"rights\" for defined purposes only, and the Convention's draftsman chose the words deliberately. In Italy v. Union of India (case 34) the Supreme Court drew exactly this distinction: the Union's power in the EEZ is a power to legislate for the \"exclusive economic zone\", not to exercise sovereignty, and the notification SO 67/E of 1981 extending the Indian Penal Code and the Code of Criminal Procedure to the EEZ therefore rests on Article 297 of the Constitution read with sections 6 and 7 of the Maritime Zones Act, 1976 — not on ordinary territorial jurisdiction. Get the vocabulary wrong and the whole answer becomes wrong. 5.1.1 The Territorial Sea",
