          "id": "pil-c-u6-1",
          "name": "☰ How to Use These Notes Topic 6 is the shortest head in the syllabus — five hours — but it is the head that quietly supplies the grammar of every other topic. State responsibility asks whose conduct it is; the law of the sea asks whose law governs the ship; diplomatic relations ask whose courts are excluded; international criminal law asks who may prosecute. Every one of those questions is a jurisdiction question. The topic is therefore best learned not as a list of five heads but as one instrument with two dials: which basis of jurisdiction is being invoked, and which power — to prescribe or to enforce — is being exercised. The red-ink annotation on the syllabus, \"Civil\" and \"criminal\" with arrows to head 6.4, is the teacher telling you that extra-territoriality must be dealt with on both sides: the criminal side (does India's penal law reach conduct abroad?) and the civil side (may an Indian court take a dispute with a foreign element, and what happens to a foreign judgment?). What is inside Every sub-head of 6.1 to 6.5 is treated in full. Each of the three prescribed cases — Arrest Warrant (case 37), Abu Salem (case 38) and Savarkar (case 39) — is given facts, issues, the arguments of both sides, the decision and the principle evolved, as the standing instruction for this project requires. The classic supporting authorities — Lotus , The Franconia , Eichmann , Pinochet , Joyce , Yunis , Rauscher , Castioni , Soering , the Indian cases from Mubarak Ali to Surinder Kaur Sandhu — are digested in the same way, though more briefly. What is added Because the instruction is to place every related order, rule and Act at the point where it arises, the notes carry the Indian Extradition Act, 1962 section by section; the Foreigners Act, 1946; the Extradition (India–Portugal) notification, GSR 822(E) of 13 December 2002; the 1957 European Convention on Extradition and the 2002 European Arrest Warrant; the Commonwealth scheme; the Genocide Convention's Article VII and the torture and hijacking conventions; the treaty practice on double criminality and on assurances against the death penalty; and India's own extra-territorial provisions in the penal, tax, competition, company and information-technology statutes. Real examples are given wherever the law has actually been used. A note on the three prescribed cases The DU case material for Topic 6 prints Abu Salem (casebook pp. 177–185) and Arrest and Return of Savarkar (pp. 186–189, including the text of the United Kingdom–France Arbitration Agreement of 25 October 1910 and the award). It does not print a digest of case 37, Arrest Warrant of 11 April 2000 (Democratic Republic of the Congo v. Belgium) , which appears in the syllabus by citation only (I.C.J. Reports 2002, p. 3). That case is therefore set out in §6.3 G from the judgment and the Court's own official summary, with a provenance note; nothing in it is inferred. The same convention is followed where a modern development is reported from press or institutional sources rather than from a judgment: the source is named in the text or in Appendix E . 6.0 The Problem Stated — What Jurisdiction Is",
