          "unit": "Topic 6: State Jurisdiction & Extradition",
          "file": "sem 2/PIL/Topic6_State_Jurisdiction_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "s62",
          "facts": "(a) The legislation. By the Law of 16 June 1993 , as amended by the Law of 19 February 1999 , Belgium made punishable under its own law grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions of 1949 and of the Additional Protocols, and serious violations of international humanitarian law, including crimes against humanity. Article 7 of the Law conferred on the Belgian courts jurisdiction over such offences \"wheresoever they may have been committed\" — the widest statutory assertion of universal jurisdiction then in force in any country. Article 5 of the Law provided that the immunity attaching to a person's official capacity under international law would not prevent its application. (b) The warrant. On 11 April 2000 an investigating judge of the Brussels tribunal de première instance issued an \"international arrest warrant in absentia\" against Mr Abdulaye Yerodia Ndombasi , charging him, as perpetrator or co-perpetrator, with offences constituting grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions and the Additional Protocols and with crimes against humanity. The alleged acts lay in speeches made by Mr Yerodia in 1998 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo which, in the view of the Belgian authorities, incited racial hatred and led to attacks on persons of Tutsi origin. At the time the warrant was issued Mr Yerodia was the Minister for Foreign Affairs of the Congo . The warrant was transmitted to the Congolese authorities and was circulated internationally through Interpol with a request that he be located and provisionally arrested with a view to extradition to Belgium. The warrant contained an exception for the case of an official visit by Mr Yerodia to Belgium, and he never in fact suffered arrest there. (c) The proceedings. On 17 October 2000 the Congo filed an Application instituting proceedings against Belgium, relying on the Parties' declarations under Article 36(2) of the Statute (Belgium's of 17 June 1958; the Congo's of 8 February 1989), and asking the Court to declare that Belgium must annul the warrant. A request for provisional measures was rejected by Order of 8 December 2000, in which the Court nonetheless held that the issues should be determined as soon as possible. After the Application, Mr Yerodia ceased to be Minister for Foreign Affairs and then ceased to hold any ministerial office. Written pleadings were filed in 2001 (the Congo's Memorial, then Belgium's Counter-Memorial; a second round of pleadings was dispensed with by agreement) and public hearings were held from 15 to 19 October 2001.",
          "issues": "Did the Court have jurisdiction, or had the case become moot with Mr Yerodia's departure from office? Had the dispute been transformed into an action of diplomatic protection, so that the local-remedies rule applied? Assuming Belgium had jurisdiction to issue the warrant, did the issue and international circulation of the warrant violate the immunity from criminal jurisdiction and the inviolability which the Congo's incumbent Minister for Foreign Affairs enjoyed under international law? What were the consequences — was the Congo entitled to an order requiring Belgium to cancel the warrant? And — the question the Court did not decide — was Belgium's exercise of universal jurisdiction over offences committed abroad by a non-national against non-nationals consistent with international law?",
          "arguments": "Submissions of applicant State versus respondent State before the International Court of Justice.",
          "ratio": "By fifteen votes to one — the Court rejected Belgium's objections relating to jurisdiction, mootness and admissibility; found that it had jurisdiction to entertain the Application filed on 17 October 2000; found that the Application was not without object and that the case was not moot; and found the Application admissible. (Judge Oda dissented on all four.) By thirteen votes to three — the Court found that the issue against Mr Yerodia of the arrest warrant of 11 April 2000, and its international circulation, \"constituted violations of a legal obligation of the Kingdom of Belgium towards the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in that they failed to respect the immunity from criminal jurisdiction and the inviolability which the incumbent Minister for Foreign Affairs of the Democratic Republic of the Congo enjoyed under international law\". (Judges Oda and Al-Khasawneh and Judge ad hoc Van den Wyngaert dissented.) By ten votes to six — the Court found that Belgium \"must, by means of its own choosing, cancel the arrest warrant of 11 April 2000 and so inform the authorities to whom that warrant was circulated\". Belgium complied: the warrant was cancelled and the authorities concerned were informed. Belgium also amended its legislation in 2003, narrowing the reach of universal jurisdiction in Belgian law — a legislative response which is itself part of the history of the case.",
          "examTips": "Essential ICJ / PCIJ landmark ruling to cite in DU LL.B. semester exams."
