          "id": "pil-c-u6-4",
          "name": "6.4 Extra-territorial Jurisdiction of States — Civil and Criminal The teacher's red-ink note, and what it requires On the syllabus image the words Civil and criminal are written in red, with arrows, beside head 6.4 (\"Extra-territorial Jurisdiction of State\"). The instruction implicit in the annotation is that extra-territoriality must be prepared on both sides: the criminal side — the reach of penal law to conduct abroad, and the means by which an offender outside the territory becomes triable within it; and the civil side — the power of a State's courts to adjudicate disputes with a foreign element, the recognition and enforcement of foreign judgments, and the limits which immunity places upon both. The class notes take the same view, and cite K.T.M.S. Abdul Cader for the proposition that the expression \"extra-territorial operation\" is used in two senses in Indian constitutional practice. This section follows that structure: first the concept and the two powers, then the criminal doctrines, then the civil rules, then the management of conflict between States. A. What \"Extra-territorial\" Means The expression is used in three different ways, and an examination answer should say which one it is using. Extra-territorial operation of a law — a statute which, by its own terms, governs conduct, persons or things outside the territory of the enacting State. The class notes quote the Madras High Court in K.T.M.S. Abdul Cader v. Union of India , AIR 1977 Mad. 386, for the two senses in which the expression is normally used: \"firstly, laws in respect of acts and events which take place inside the State but have operation outside, and secondly, laws with reference to the nationals of a State in respect of their acts outside.\" To these must be added the two modern statutory techniques: laws which attach to conduct abroad on the basis of effects within the State (Competition Act, s. 32; IT Act, s. 75; Income-tax Act, s. 9), and laws which implement treaty obligations to try or extradite (the Anti-Hijacking Act, 2016; the SUA Act, 2002; the MAPA 2022). Extra-territorial jurisdiction over persons or property — the competence of a State's courts or executive to deal with persons, acts and property situated outside its territory. This is the sense in which the phrase is used in the present head of the syllabus. Extra-territorial enforcement — the actual exercise of State authority abroad: arrest, seizure, service of process, investigation, execution of judgments. This is the sense in which the phrase most often appears in disputes, because it is here that international law imposes its clearest prohibition. The constitutional question is settled in India by Article 245(2) of the Constitution, which provides that no law made by Parliament shall be deemed invalid on the ground that it would have extra-territorial operation. The question of international law is not settled by that provision at all: a statute may be valid under the Constitution and still amount to a breach of international law if the jurisdiction it claims is not one that international law recognises, or if its enforcement abroad violates the sovereignty of another State. B. The Prescribe/Enforce Divide Restated Everything in this section follows from two propositions of the Lotus judgment: \"…the first and foremost restriction imposed by international law upon a State is that — failing the existence of a permissive rule to the contrary — it may not exercise its power in any form in the territory of another State. In this sense jurisdiction is certainly territorial… It does not, however, follow that international law prohibits a State from exercising jurisdiction in its own territory, in respect of any case which relates to acts which have taken place abroad… Far from laying down a general prohibition…, it leaves them in this respect a wide measure of discretion, which is only limited in certain cases by prohibitive rules…\" The first proposition is the rule of enforcement jurisdiction : it is a prohibition, and it is not displaced merely because the State has a good prescriptive title. The second is the rule of prescriptive jurisdiction : it is a freedom, qualified by prohibitive rules (immunity, the treaty clauses, the rule on collisions which became Article 97 of UNCLOS, and, on the modern view, the requirement of a genuine link). Three practical consequences follow, and they should be carried through the whole of this section: A State may not enforce abroad through its own officials. Police officers, tax inspectors, competition investigators and process-servers may not operate in another State; nor may a State seize property there. Hence the machinery of mutual legal assistance, letters rogatory (s. 166A of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 and its successor provisions), the service of foreign process through diplomatic channels (Order V Rule 25–26 of the Code of Civil Procedure), and the treaty networks for the recognition of judgments and arbitral awards. Where a State disregards the prohibition — as in abductions — it commits an internationally wrongful act and owes reparation, even though its courts may then be competent to try the accused ( Eichmann , Yunis , Alvarez-Machain , and Savarkar , case 39). Prescription abroad is effective only through custody. A prescriptive claim is a claim about what law applies; a prosecution, however, requires the person. The whole of head 6.5 — extradition, deportation, rendition, asylum — is the machinery by which presence is obtained or refused. That is the link between the two halves of this topic. Conflict between two prescriptive claims is resolved by the rules of cooperation and restraint, not by hierarchy. Concurrent jurisdiction is lawful; what is unlawful is the enforcement of one State's claim within another State's territory without consent. The two States must therefore negotiate, extradite, cooperate or tolerate; section H below sets out the techniques they use. C. Criminal Extra-territoriality — the Doctrines",
