          "id": "pil-c-u6-6",
          "name": "6.6 Limits and Bridges — Immunity, Concurrency and Cyberspace The five earlier heads describe how a State acquires jurisdiction. This section describes what happens next: the three things that stop a State from exercising a jurisdiction it lawfully has (immunity, the priority of another State's claim, and the practical impossibility of enforcing abroad), and the bridges of cooperation by which the system turns the impossibility into an ordinary administrative process. A. Immunity — Jurisdiction Without Power to Exercise It The single most important proposition of this topic is the one the International Court of Justice stated in the Arrest Warrant case: \"the rules governing the jurisdiction of national courts must be carefully distinguished from those governing jurisdictional immunities: jurisdiction does not imply absence of immunity, while absence of immunity does not imply jurisdiction.\" Four immunities have to be kept apart, and each has its own instrument, its own extent in time, and its own waiver rule. Immunity of the State and of its property. A State may not be made a defendant before the courts of another State without its consent. India's law on the subject is still the colonial-era rule in the Code of Civil Procedure: sections 86 and 87 require the consent of the Central Government, certified in writing by a Secretary to the Government, before a foreign State may be sued, and s. 86(2) lists the exceptions in which such consent may be given (the foreign State has itself instituted the suit; the proceeding relates to immovable property in India in its possession or enjoyment; the matter is commercial; and so on); waiver of the privilege must be shown by conduct amounting to submission to the jurisdiction. The modern statement of the law is the United Nations Convention on Jurisdictional Immunities of States and Their Property, 2004 (General Assembly resolution 59/38 of 2 December 2004), which codifies the restrictive theory: immunity for acts jure imperii but no immunity for commercial transactions (Art. 10), contracts of employment (Art. 11), personal injury and damage to property in the forum State (Art. 12), ownership and possession of immovable property in the forum State (Art. 13), industrial and intellectual property (Art. 14), and participation in companies (Art. 15), with a separate régime in Arts. 18–21 for immunity from measures of constraint (pre-judgment and post-judgment). The Convention is not yet in force; India has not ratified it; but its scheme is the standard formulation and is worth citing. The leading decision is Jurisdictional Immunities of the State (Germany v. Italy: Greece intervening) , I.C.J. Reports 2012, p. 99 , in which the Court held that State immunity is a procedural bar attaching to the exercise of jurisdiction and is not affected by the gravity of the alleged violations of international law; that there is no exception for war crimes or crimes against humanity; and that Italy had violated its obligations both by permitting civil claims against Germany in its courts and by enforcing Greek judgments against it. The corollary, stated repeatedly in the Court's judgment to answer the \"last resort\" argument: the bar of immunity is independent of whether an alternative remedy exists. Immunity of the troika — Head of State, Head of Government, Foreign Minister. An incumbent of one of these three offices enjoys full immunity from the criminal jurisdiction of foreign courts and inviolability, ratione personae , while in office, without distinction between acts done in an official or a private capacity and without any exception for international crimes (case 37). The immunity ceases with the office, but the former official continues to enjoy immunity ratione materiae for acts performed in an official capacity (the point on which the Court in the Arrest Warrant case, para. 61, said it could not decide without more information, and which the House of Lords and the French Court of Cassation had addressed in the other direction in the Pinochet litigation for the specific offence of torture). Diplomatic and consular immunity. The Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961 (Topic 7) confers on the diplomatic agent inviolability of person, premises, papers and correspondence and immunity from the criminal, civil and administrative jurisdiction of the receiving State (Art. 31), subject to the three exceptions in Art. 31(1)(a)–(c) and to waiver by the sending State (Art. 32); the members of the diplomatic mission enjoy a lesser immunity (Art. 37); the mission's premises are inviolable (Art. 22). Consular officers under the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, 1963, enjoy immunity in respect of acts performed in the exercise of consular functions (Art. 43 and Art. 41 for inviolability), which is a functional and not a personal immunity — the VCCR's Art. 36 notification requirement being the point which produced the LaGrand and Avena judgments (Topic 7's material). Special missions are governed by the 1969 Convention; and the officials of international organisations by the general instruments and headquarters agreements. Waiver and the consequences of the distinction. Immunity is a right of the State, not a privilege of the individual, and it may be waived — expressly (Art. 32 VCDR for diplomats; CPC s. 86 in India) or by conduct amounting to submission. The consequences of the distinction, all of them examinable, are: (a) a foreign State may itself prosecute its own official, and the official may be immune abroad and liable at home; (b) an international tribunal may enjoy a jurisdiction which national courts lack (the very point made by the ICJ in case 37 for the ICC); (c) immunity from jurisdiction may be lost without immunity from execution being lost, which is why judgments against States remain paper (the second half of the Germany v. Italy judgment); and (d) waiver must be clear — the taking of a step in the proceedings by a State's lawyer may amount to waiver, while a plea of immunity made at the first opportunity preserves it. B. Concurrency — Two or More States, One Set of Facts",
