          "id": "pil-c-u7-1",
          "name": "☰ How to Use These Notes Topic 7 asks one question in three costumes. The question is: when may a State, its envoys and its agents be made answerable in the courts or to the police of another State? The first costume is sovereign (State) immunity — the immunity of the foreign State itself, together with its property, from the jurisdiction of the forum State's courts; its central battleground is the distinction between acts done in the exercise of sovereign authority ( acta jure imperii ) and acts of a private, commercial or managerial character ( acta jure gestionis ). The second is diplomatic immunity — the near-total inviolability and jurisdictional immunity of a diplomatic mission and of diplomatic agents under the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961. The third is consular immunity — the narrower, function-based protection of consular posts and consular officers under the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, 1963. All three rest on the same bedrock propositions: the sovereign equality of States ( par in parem non habet imperium — an equal has no authority over an equal), the absolute territorial jurisdiction of the State over its own territory, and the functional necessity of leaving the channels of inter-State communication unblocked. Marshall C.J. stated both halves of that paradox in 1812 in The Schooner Exchange v. McFaddon : the jurisdiction of a nation within its own territory is \"necessarily exclusive and absolute\", and yet every sovereign is understood, by implication, to have waived a part of that jurisdiction in favour of the foreign sovereign, his ships, his envoys and their missions. What is inside Every sub-head of 7.1 to 7.5 is treated in full: the types of diplomatic agents (VCDR Article 1 definitions, the three classes of heads of mission in Article 14, the older four-class classification of the Congress of Vienna, and the five categories of mission personnel); consuls (the four classes of heads of consular post, the exequatur, Article 5 functions, and the diplomat–consul comparison); sovereign and non-sovereign acts (the absolute and restrictive theories, the nature and purpose tests, the UN Convention of 2004, the US and UK statutes, and CPC sections 86–87); diplomatic immunity, personal and property (Articles 20–47 of the VCDR, article by article); and consular privileges and immunities (Articles 28–57 of the VCCR, article by article). Each of the four prescribed cases — Schooner Exchange (40), Tehran Hostages (41), Germany v. Italy (42) and Jadhav (43) — is given its facts, issues, the arguments of both sides, the decision and the principle evolved, as the standing instruction for this project requires. What is added Because the instruction is to place every related order, rule and Act at the point where it arises, the notes carry the Vienna Conventions of 1961 and 1963 article by article; the Optional Protocols on the compulsory settlement of disputes (which gave the ICJ jurisdiction in Tehran and in Jadhav ); the UN Convention on Jurisdictional Immunities of States and Their Property, 2004 ; the European Convention on State Immunity, 1972 ; the US Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act, 1976 (including the Tate Letter of 1952) and the UK State Immunity Act, 1978 ; the Diplomatic Relations (Vienna Convention) Act, 1972 of India; sections 86–87 CPC ; the Convention on Special Missions, 1969 ; the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of Crimes against Internationally Protected Persons, 1973 ; the International Organisations (Privileges and Immunities) Act, 1948 ; Articles 104–105 of the UN Charter; the ILC's 1989 Draft Articles on the Diplomatic Courier and Bag ; the Pakistan Army Act provisions used in Jadhav ; and the diplomatic-law incidents that illustrate every rule (Tehran 1979, the Iraqi Embassy raid in Pakistan 1973, the Libyan People's Bureau 1984, the Dikko affair 1984, the Kuwait 1990 embassy seizures, the Chinese Embassy in Belgrade 1999, the Devyani Khobragade arrest 2013, and the India–Canada expulsions of 2024). Real examples, diagrams and comparison tables are given at every stage. The standing instruction and how it is honoured here The instruction for this project is that the notes be built primarily on the DU LB-205 course material (\"maximum portion based on DU case material\"). For Topic 7 that instruction has to be read carefully, because the course material prints only two of the four prescribed cases. The casebook contains the Tehran Hostages judgment (case 41, printed pages 189–207) and the Jadhav judgment (case 43, printed pages 208–219). It does not print The Schooner Exchange v. McFaddon (case 40) or Jurisdictional Immunities of the State (Germany v. Italy) (case 42): those two appear in the syllabus list by citation only, without page numbers. Cases 41 and 43 are therefore digested here from the casebook text itself — with the casebook's own paragraph numbers, its own quotations and its own structure preserved — and cases 40 and 42 are digested from the sources themselves (the report of Marshall C.J.'s opinion of 1812 and the ICJ's judgment and official summary of 3 February 2012), each under an express provenance note. Nothing taken from outside the casebook is ever presented as casebook material. 7.0 The Problem Stated — Three Immunities, One Idea",
