          "id": "pil-c-u7-8",
          "name": "7.5 Consular Privileges and Immunities Consular law is the working end of diplomacy: visas, passports, the welfare of nationals, ships and cargo, births, deaths, marriages, notarial acts, the protection of a national who has been arrested. Its governing instrument is the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, 1963 — \"VCCR\" in these notes — which India acceded to in November 1977 and which gave the Court its jurisdiction in case 43. The privileges and immunities of consular officers are narrower than those of diplomatic agents in almost every respect, and the reason is functional: a consul lives and works among the local population, deals daily with the local authorities, and cannot discharge those functions if he is entirely outside local law. Everything in this head follows from that single premise — and the whole of case 43 ( Jadhav ) is an application of it. A. The Two Chapters of the VCCR — Career and Honorary Posts",
          "citation": "ICJ Reports / Landmark International Precedent",
