          "question": "Model answer — 2025, Q.1 (Subjects / personality + the UN claim hypothetical), 20 marks (i) Opening — the test. An entity is a subject of international law if it possesses international legal personality: \"capable of possessing international rights and duties, and … the capacity to maintain its rights by bringing international claims\" — Reparation for Injuries (1949). Legal personality comprises four capacities: to make claims; to conclude treaties; to enjoy privileges and immunities; and to be the bearer of obligations (and responsibility for their breach). (ii) Theories. ① States only — Oppenheim (1905), Sir Frederick Smith; Art. 34(1) ICJ Statute. ② Individuals only — Kelsen. ③ The modern, functional view — States remain primary, but organisations, individuals and certain non-State entities hold graded personality. Cite Reparation (Organsiation); Barcelona Traction (States and companies); LaGrand / Jadhav (individuals). (iii) The subject categories. • States — Montevideo criteria 1933; sovereignty, equality (GA Res. 2625(XXV)); recognition (declaratory vs. constitutive, Lauterpacht; Haile Selassie (1939), Arantzazu Mendi (1939)). • International organisations — established by treaty; personality inferred from functions (\"implied powers\"); objective personality ( Reparation ); privileges and immunities (1946 General Convention); limits ( WHO Nuclear Weapons AO, 1996); UNAT Awards AO (1954). • Individuals — as beneficiaries of rights (UDHR 1948; ICCPR OP1; ECHR Art. 34; VCCR Art. 36 — LaGrand , Avena , Jadhav ) and bearers of duties (piracy; Nuremberg Principle I; Genocide Art. IV; Rome Statute Arts. 25, 27, 28) — but no general treaty-making power and no standing before the ICJ. • Non-State entities — insurgents, national liberation movements, peoples (self-determination), the Holy See, the ICRC, indigenous peoples (UNDRIP 2007), MNCs (ICSID). (iv) The hypothetical. A UN employee of nationality X is murdered by a secret service agency of State Y; the UN claims against Y. Applying Reparation : Maintainability ratione personae: the UN is an international person with objective personality, so it may bring a claim even if Y is a non-Member (\"fifty States, representing the vast majority of the international community, had the power … to bring into being an entity possessing objective international personality\"). Question I(a): the UN may claim for damage to itself — its administrative machine, property, assets and \"interests of which it is the guardian.\" Question I(b): the UN may also claim reparation for the damage suffered by the victim or persons entitled through him — because it \"does not represent the agent, but is asserting its own right\" that obligations due to it be respected; the nationality of the agent (even that of the defendant State) is \"not pertinent to the admissibility of the claim,\" since the claim rests on the agent's status , not his nationality. Agency: the Court requires the injured person to be an \"agent\" of the Organisation, performing duties, with the damage arising \"in circumstances involving the responsibility of a State.\" The employment relationship is the effective test of agency — his nationality is irrelevant. Question II: where the national State also has a right of diplomatic protection, \"there is no rule of law which assigns priority to the one or to the other\"; the two rights may be exercised independently, and Art. 100 independence justifies functional protection. Attribution: the acts must be attributable to Y (a secret service agency is a State organ — ILC ARSIWA Art. 4) and must constitute the breach of an international obligation owed to the UN; responsibility towards the UN rather than towards State X is the basis. (v) Conclusion. Maintainable: the UN may bring a claim for both heads of damage; if Y is the State of nationality of the agent, the claim is still admissible because the UN asserts its own right. This is exactly the situation in which Reparation arose — Bernadotte, a Swedish national, was assassinated while serving as UN Mediator in Palestine.",
