          "question": "Answer 2 — 2015 Q.2(a) and (b): constitutional provisions on treaty making and implementation; the role of the Indian judiciary Question (a). Discuss the constitutional provisions on treaty making and its implementation in India. (b) Discuss the role of the Indian judiciary in the effectuation of international legal obligations. (2015, Q.2, 20 marks) (a) Treaty-making. Explain that the word &ldquo;treaty&rdquo; is not defined in the Constitution; that Article 53(1) vests the executive power of the Union in the President; that Article 73(1)(a) extends it to matters on which Parliament may legislate (read with Entry 10, List I, &ldquo;foreign affairs&rdquo;, and Entry 14, &ldquo;entering into treaties and agreements... and implementing of treaties&rdquo;), while Article 73(1)(b) extends it to the exercise of rights, authority and jurisdiction exercisable by virtue of any treaty; that Article 77 prescribes the form of executive action; and that Article 246 read with Entry 14 gives Parliament the legislative competence. Conclusion: the executive may bind India internationally without prior parliamentary approval — Ram Jawaya Kapur ; Manmull Jain ; Nirmal Bose ; the Privy Council in A.G. Canada v. A.G. Ontario ; and there is no analogue of the United States Senate's two-thirds consent. Add the qualifications: implementation requires legislation; money obligations require legislative sanction; cession requires a constitutional amendment ( Berubari ); and the 100th Amendment of 2015 shows the sequence in practice (1974 Agreement → 2011 Protocol → constitutional amendment). (b) Treaty-implementation and the judiciary's role. Set out the four judicial techniques: (i) enforcement after legislation — the ordinary case; (ii) harmonious construction — the presumption that Parliament does not intend to breach international law, used in Jolly George to read section 51 CPC down and in Gramophone to read the Copyright Act in the setting of the Berne Convention; (iii) reception of custom — Vellore (precautionary and polluter pays principles as law of the land), PUCL , Pratap Singh ; and (iv) interpretation of the Constitution in the light of conventions — Vishaka (CEDAW), NALSA , Puttaswamy (UDHR and ICCPR). Add the counterweights: the courts will not enforce an unlegislated treaty against a statute; they will not apply international law against the Constitution ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ); they leave delicate questions of foreign policy and recognition to the executive; and where territory is involved they insist on the constitutional forms ( Berubari ; Sukumar Sengupta ). Conclude that the judiciary's role is interpretive and gap-filling, not displacing : it effectuates international obligations through Indian law rather than above it — the &ldquo;monism in interpretation, dualism in hierarchy&rdquo; formula.",
          "answer": "Question (a). Discuss the constitutional provisions on treaty making and its implementation in India. (b) Discuss the role of the Indian judiciary in the effectuation of international legal obligations. (2015, Q.2, 20 marks) (a) Treaty-making. Explain that the word &ldquo;treaty&rdquo; is not defined in the Constitution; that Article 53(1) vests the executive power of the Union in the President; that Article 73(1)(a) extends it to matters on which Parliament may legislate (read with Entry 10, List I, &ldquo;foreign affairs&rdquo;, and Entry 14, &ldquo;entering into treaties and agreements... and implementing of treaties&rdquo;), while Article 73(1)(b) extends it to the exercise of rights, authority and jurisdiction exercisable by virtue of any treaty; that Article 77 prescribes the form of executive action; and that Article 246 read with Entry 14 gives Parliament the legislative competence. Conclusion: the executive may bind India internationally without prior parliamentary approval — Ram Jawaya Kapur ; Manmull Jain ; Nirmal Bose ; the Privy Council in A.G. Canada v. A.G. Ontario ; and there is no analogue of the United States Senate's two-thirds consent. Add the qualifications: implementation requires legislation; money obligations require legislative sanction; cession requires a constitutional amendment ( Berubari ); and the 100th Amendment of 2015 shows the sequence in practice (1974 Agreement → 2011 Protocol → constitutional amendment). (b) Treaty-implementation and the judiciary's role. Set out the four judicial techniques: (i) enforcement after legislation — the ordinary case; (ii) harmonious construction — the presumption that Parliament does not intend to breach international law, used in Jolly George to read section 51 CPC down and in Gramophone to read the Copyright Act in the setting of the Berne Convention; (iii) reception of custom — Vellore (precautionary and polluter pays principles as law of the land), PUCL , Pratap Singh ; and (iv) interpretation of the Constitution in the light of conventions — Vishaka (CEDAW), NALSA , Puttaswamy (UDHR and ICCPR). Add the counterweights: the courts will not enforce an unlegislated treaty against a statute; they will not apply international law against the Constitution ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ); they leave delicate questions of foreign policy and recognition to the executive; and where territory is involved they insist on the constitutional forms ( Berubari ; Sukumar Sengupta ). Conclude that the judiciary's role is interpretive and gap-filling, not displacing : it effectuates international obligations through Indian law rather than above it — the &ldquo;monism in interpretation, dualism in hierarchy&rdquo; formula.",
