          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 3 — 2013 Q.4: Indian practice on the adoption and implementation of international treaties Question. Discuss the Indian practice on adoption and implementation of international treaties. (2013, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer under six heads. 1. Adoption (conclusion and ratification): executive act under Articles 53, 73, 77 and Entry 14 — Manmull Jain , Nirmal Bose ; no prior parliamentary consent; India follows the dualist approach and &ldquo;has not yet legislated&rdquo; a general statute on treaties, so that practice is to be gathered from the Constitution and the cases. 2. International effect : the treaty binds India at once ( A.G. Canada v. A.G. Ontario ); internal law cannot be pleaded against it (VCLT Arts. 27, 46; ARSIWA Arts. 3, 32; the Alabama Claims, 1872). 3. Domestic effect — the general rule : legislation is required where the treaty (i) affects the rights of citizens, (ii) modifies the law of the State, or (iii) creates financial obligations — Maganbhai (Shah J.) — and the preponderant view is that legislation is required generally: Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , Jolly George , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee (the Gleneagles Accord). 4. The minority view and its partial acceptance : the self-executing/non-self-executing dichotomy imported into Indian law by Shah J. in Maganbhai , and the self-executing character of the Kutch Award; Rosaline George (1994). 5. Cession and territory : Berubari (Art. 368), Ram Kishore Sen (boundary — no amendment), Sukumar Sengupta (servitude — no amendment), the Ninth Amendment, and the 100th Amendment of 2015. 6. The interpretive practice : Jolly George (ICCPR Art. 11 and s. 51 CPC), Gramophone (Berne Convention and &ldquo;import&rdquo;), Vellore (custom), Vishaka (CEDAW; Art. 141 guidelines; POSH Act 2013), Puttaswamy (UDHR, ICCPR, Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993). Conclude with the prescribed text's own five-point summary of Indian treaty practice (see Table 3.13) and with the observation that although judicial authority predominantly supports the need for legislation, &ldquo;more recently, the Supreme Court has resorted to international treaties, which are ratified by India but not legislated, in the interpretation of the constitutional and statutory provisions, or in the absence of contrary legislative provisions, and thus making them part of the domestic law.&rdquo;",
          "answer": "Question. Discuss the Indian practice on adoption and implementation of international treaties. (2013, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer under six heads. 1. Adoption (conclusion and ratification): executive act under Articles 53, 73, 77 and Entry 14 — Manmull Jain , Nirmal Bose ; no prior parliamentary consent; India follows the dualist approach and &ldquo;has not yet legislated&rdquo; a general statute on treaties, so that practice is to be gathered from the Constitution and the cases. 2. International effect : the treaty binds India at once ( A.G. Canada v. A.G. Ontario ); internal law cannot be pleaded against it (VCLT Arts. 27, 46; ARSIWA Arts. 3, 32; the Alabama Claims, 1872). 3. Domestic effect — the general rule : legislation is required where the treaty (i) affects the rights of citizens, (ii) modifies the law of the State, or (iii) creates financial obligations — Maganbhai (Shah J.) — and the preponderant view is that legislation is required generally: Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , Jolly George , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee (the Gleneagles Accord). 4. The minority view and its partial acceptance : the self-executing/non-self-executing dichotomy imported into Indian law by Shah J. in Maganbhai , and the self-executing character of the Kutch Award; Rosaline George (1994). 5. Cession and territory : Berubari (Art. 368), Ram Kishore Sen (boundary — no amendment), Sukumar Sengupta (servitude — no amendment), the Ninth Amendment, and the 100th Amendment of 2015. 6. The interpretive practice : Jolly George (ICCPR Art. 11 and s. 51 CPC), Gramophone (Berne Convention and &ldquo;import&rdquo;), Vellore (custom), Vishaka (CEDAW; Art. 141 guidelines; POSH Act 2013), Puttaswamy (UDHR, ICCPR, Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993). Conclude with the prescribed text's own five-point summary of Indian treaty practice (see Table 3.13) and with the observation that although judicial authority predominantly supports the need for legislation, &ldquo;more recently, the Supreme Court has resorted to international treaties, which are ratified by India but not legislated, in the interpretation of the constitutional and statutory provisions, or in the absence of contrary legislative provisions, and thus making them part of the domestic law.&rdquo;",
