          "id": "pil-pyq-u3-4",
          "year": "2013",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 5,
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 4 — Short note: the Blackstonian doctrine (2013 Q.8(c); 2011 Q.(iv)) Question. Write a short note on the applicability of the Blackstonian doctrine. (2013, Q.8(c), 10 marks; 2011, Q.(iv), 10 marks) Short-note content (five paragraphs). (1) What it is : Blackstone's proposition that &ldquo;the law of nations... is adopted in its full extent by the common law, and is held to be part of the law of the land&rdquo; — the incorporation doctrine , the practical expression of monism. (2) Its development in England : applied in Buvot v. Barbuit , Triquet v. Bath , Emperor of Austria v. Day ; qualified in West Rand Central Gold Mining Co. v. R. (1905) and Chung Chi Cheung (1939); temporarily displaced by R. v. Keyn (1876) and restored by the Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878; refreshed by Trendtex (1977) and Rayner (1990). (3) Its two qualifications : no inconsistency with statute; no inconsistency with a rule already declared by the highest court. (4) Its applicability in India : received through Article 372(1) (the pre-1950 law continues); applied in Krishna Sharma (1954), Gramophone (1984: &ldquo;unless they are in conflict with an Act of Parliament&rdquo;), PUCL (1997) and Vellore (1996: precautionary and polluter pays principles as part of the law of the land); limited by A.D.M. Jabalpur (no enforcement against the Constitution). (5) Its limits and its opposite : the doctrine applies to custom , not to treaties , for which India follows the transformation (dualist) approach — Jolly George ; and where a treaty and a statute conflict, the statute prevails. Mention that the questions on the doctrine have been asked repeatedly (2011, 2013, 2015, 2025) — a sign that the examiners expect the Blackstone–Keyn–West Rand–Chung Chi Cheung–Trendtex sequence and the Indian cases.",
          "answer": "Question. Write a short note on the applicability of the Blackstonian doctrine. (2013, Q.8(c), 10 marks; 2011, Q.(iv), 10 marks) Short-note content (five paragraphs). (1) What it is : Blackstone's proposition that &ldquo;the law of nations... is adopted in its full extent by the common law, and is held to be part of the law of the land&rdquo; — the incorporation doctrine , the practical expression of monism. (2) Its development in England : applied in Buvot v. Barbuit , Triquet v. Bath , Emperor of Austria v. Day ; qualified in West Rand Central Gold Mining Co. v. R. (1905) and Chung Chi Cheung (1939); temporarily displaced by R. v. Keyn (1876) and restored by the Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878; refreshed by Trendtex (1977) and Rayner (1990). (3) Its two qualifications : no inconsistency with statute; no inconsistency with a rule already declared by the highest court. (4) Its applicability in India : received through Article 372(1) (the pre-1950 law continues); applied in Krishna Sharma (1954), Gramophone (1984: &ldquo;unless they are in conflict with an Act of Parliament&rdquo;), PUCL (1997) and Vellore (1996: precautionary and polluter pays principles as part of the law of the land); limited by A.D.M. Jabalpur (no enforcement against the Constitution). (5) Its limits and its opposite : the doctrine applies to custom , not to treaties , for which India follows the transformation (dualist) approach — Jolly George ; and where a treaty and a statute conflict, the statute prevails. Mention that the questions on the doctrine have been asked repeatedly (2011, 2013, 2015, 2025) — a sign that the examiners expect the Blackstone–Keyn–West Rand–Chung Chi Cheung–Trendtex sequence and the Indian cases.",
