          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 6 — 2011 Q.4: how far do international treaties bind Indian courts? Question. How far do international treaties bind Indian courts? Discuss in detail the Indian practice of implementation of treaties in the municipal legal system. (2011, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer. Begin with the proposition that a treaty binds the State (and therefore India as an international person) from the moment it is concluded — Articles 53, 73, Entry 14, List I; Manmull Jain ; Nirmal Bose — irrespective of parliamentary approval, and that no internal law can be pleaded against it (VCLT Arts. 27, 46). Then answer the question actually asked, in four propositions. An unlegislated treaty does not bind Indian courts as law. &ldquo;Treaties which are part of international law do not form part of the law of the land unless expressly made so by the legislative authority&rdquo; ( Birma , AIR 1951 Raj 127); &ldquo;In India, treaties do not have the force of law and consequently obligations arising therefrom will not be enforceable in municipal courts unless backed by legislation&rdquo; ( Shiv Kumar Sharma , AIR 1968 Del 643); the Covenant &ldquo;does not automatically make... an enforceable part of the corpus juris of India&rdquo; ( Jolly George , AIR 1980 SC 470); G.G. Menon (extradition under the 1881 Act refused); Civil Rights Vigilance Committee (Gleneagles Accord not enforceable at the instance of citizens). But an unlegislated treaty binds the courts in interpretation. The settled canon: where a statute is capable of two constructions, the court adopts the construction consistent with India's international obligations — because the legislature is presumed not to intend a breach — Jolly George ; Gramophone (the Copyright Act read in the setting of the Berne Convention and the Transit Trade Convention); Krishna Sharma ; and, in the UK, Brind . Note that this is not enforcement of the treaty but interpretation of the statute. Where there is a legislative vacuum, a convention may be read into the constitutional guarantees — Vishaka (CEDAW into Articles 14, 15, 19(1)(g) and 21; guidelines binding under Article 141 until legislation; now the POSH Act, 2013); PUCL ; NALSA ; Puttaswamy (UDHR Art. 12 and ICCPR Art. 17; the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993). Custom, by contrast, is received directly as law where no statute or constitutional provision is against it — Vellore , PUCL . But a treaty never binds a court against a statute or the Constitution. Conflict with a statute: the statute prevails — Jolly George ; Gramophone (&ldquo;National courts cannot say &lsquo;yes&rsquo; if Parliament has said &lsquo;no&rsquo;&rdquo;); Puttaswamy ¶133. Conflict with the Constitution: the treaty is unenforceable — A.D.M. Jabalpur ; the Article 13 inference (a treaty with Part III is &ldquo;null and void&rdquo;). Conflict over territory: the constitutional forms govern — Berubari (amendment), Ram Kishore Sen and Sukumar Sengupta (no amendment needed). Conclusion with the five-point summary of Indian treaty practice from the prescribed text, and the contemporary statement that &ldquo;the courts in India may enforce treaties which are not inconsistent with the Indian law or when there is a gap in the law&rdquo; — the interpretive turn of the last three decades.",
          "answer": "Question. How far do international treaties bind Indian courts? Discuss in detail the Indian practice of implementation of treaties in the municipal legal system. (2011, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer. Begin with the proposition that a treaty binds the State (and therefore India as an international person) from the moment it is concluded — Articles 53, 73, Entry 14, List I; Manmull Jain ; Nirmal Bose — irrespective of parliamentary approval, and that no internal law can be pleaded against it (VCLT Arts. 27, 46). Then answer the question actually asked, in four propositions. An unlegislated treaty does not bind Indian courts as law. &ldquo;Treaties which are part of international law do not form part of the law of the land unless expressly made so by the legislative authority&rdquo; ( Birma , AIR 1951 Raj 127); &ldquo;In India, treaties do not have the force of law and consequently obligations arising therefrom will not be enforceable in municipal courts unless backed by legislation&rdquo; ( Shiv Kumar Sharma , AIR 1968 Del 643); the Covenant &ldquo;does not automatically make... an enforceable part of the corpus juris of India&rdquo; ( Jolly George , AIR 1980 SC 470); G.G. Menon (extradition under the 1881 Act refused); Civil Rights Vigilance Committee (Gleneagles Accord not enforceable at the instance of citizens). But an unlegislated treaty binds the courts in interpretation. The settled canon: where a statute is capable of two constructions, the court adopts the construction consistent with India's international obligations — because the legislature is presumed not to intend a breach — Jolly George ; Gramophone (the Copyright Act read in the setting of the Berne Convention and the Transit Trade Convention); Krishna Sharma ; and, in the UK, Brind . Note that this is not enforcement of the treaty but interpretation of the statute. Where there is a legislative vacuum, a convention may be read into the constitutional guarantees — Vishaka (CEDAW into Articles 14, 15, 19(1)(g) and 21; guidelines binding under Article 141 until legislation; now the POSH Act, 2013); PUCL ; NALSA ; Puttaswamy (UDHR Art. 12 and ICCPR Art. 17; the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993). Custom, by contrast, is received directly as law where no statute or constitutional provision is against it — Vellore , PUCL . But a treaty never binds a court against a statute or the Constitution. Conflict with a statute: the statute prevails — Jolly George ; Gramophone (&ldquo;National courts cannot say &lsquo;yes&rsquo; if Parliament has said &lsquo;no&rsquo;&rdquo;); Puttaswamy ¶133. Conflict with the Constitution: the treaty is unenforceable — A.D.M. Jabalpur ; the Article 13 inference (a treaty with Part III is &ldquo;null and void&rdquo;). Conflict over territory: the constitutional forms govern — Berubari (amendment), Ram Kishore Sen and Sukumar Sengupta (no amendment needed). Conclusion with the five-point summary of Indian treaty practice from the prescribed text, and the contemporary statement that &ldquo;the courts in India may enforce treaties which are not inconsistent with the Indian law or when there is a gap in the law&rdquo; — the interpretive turn of the last three decades.",
