          "id": "pil-pyq-u3-6",
          "year": "2002",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 7,
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 1 — 2002 Q.4(a): the theoretical relationship between national and international law Question. &ldquo;It is important to clear various underlying assumptions in the relation between national and international law.&rdquo; Explain theoretically the relationship between national and international law, keeping in view the above. (2002, Q.4(a), part of a 20-mark question) Answer plan. (1) Identify the assumptions that must be cleared: that international law and national law are comparable; that one must be subordinate to the other; that a State is either &ldquo;monist&rdquo; or &ldquo;dualist&rdquo;; and that the resolution of a conflict in a national court settles the international position. (2) Monism : one legal order, common origin, the individual at the base of all law — Kelsen's hierarchy and Grundnorm ; Lauterpacht; Fitzmaurice; Starke's &ldquo;international constitutional law&rdquo;. Criticism: States do not behave monistically; unity of legal science does not yield a rule of priority. (3) Dualism : two systems, different subjects, sources and juridical origin — Triepel's trio of differences and Anzilotti's postulates (obedience v. pacta sunt servanda ). Criticism: individuals and organisations are now subjects; &ldquo;State will&rdquo; reduces to the will of the people; custom binds without consent; the theory cannot explain general international law. (4) The theories of application : transformation/specific adoption (positivist) and delegation (monist) — Verma's point that they are &ldquo;the manifestation of the theories of dualism and monism&rdquo;. (5) Primacy : dualists to municipal law; monists divided; Kelsen to State law on non-scientific grounds and the two reasons his choice fails (the anarchy of plural primacies; the survival of international law through revolutions and the repeal of constitutions); Starke to international law and Verma's three objections. (6) The cleared position : the controversy is &ldquo;more academic than real&rdquo;; the two systems have their own spheres; the supremacy of international law in the international sphere is unchallenged, as is that of municipal law in State matters; what international law requires is that a State in breach &ldquo;cannot shelter itself behind domestic law by way of absolution&rdquo;. (7) Illustrate with the Indian material: Berubari (the treaty binds India; the Constitution channels the implementation), Jolly George (the Covenant binds India; the statute governs the court), Vishaka (the treaty informs the Constitution). Close with Verma's &ldquo;mixture&rdquo; conclusion.",
          "answer": "Question. &ldquo;It is important to clear various underlying assumptions in the relation between national and international law.&rdquo; Explain theoretically the relationship between national and international law, keeping in view the above. (2002, Q.4(a), part of a 20-mark question) Answer plan. (1) Identify the assumptions that must be cleared: that international law and national law are comparable; that one must be subordinate to the other; that a State is either &ldquo;monist&rdquo; or &ldquo;dualist&rdquo;; and that the resolution of a conflict in a national court settles the international position. (2) Monism : one legal order, common origin, the individual at the base of all law — Kelsen's hierarchy and Grundnorm ; Lauterpacht; Fitzmaurice; Starke's &ldquo;international constitutional law&rdquo;. Criticism: States do not behave monistically; unity of legal science does not yield a rule of priority. (3) Dualism : two systems, different subjects, sources and juridical origin — Triepel's trio of differences and Anzilotti's postulates (obedience v. pacta sunt servanda ). Criticism: individuals and organisations are now subjects; &ldquo;State will&rdquo; reduces to the will of the people; custom binds without consent; the theory cannot explain general international law. (4) The theories of application : transformation/specific adoption (positivist) and delegation (monist) — Verma's point that they are &ldquo;the manifestation of the theories of dualism and monism&rdquo;. (5) Primacy : dualists to municipal law; monists divided; Kelsen to State law on non-scientific grounds and the two reasons his choice fails (the anarchy of plural primacies; the survival of international law through revolutions and the repeal of constitutions); Starke to international law and Verma's three objections. (6) The cleared position : the controversy is &ldquo;more academic than real&rdquo;; the two systems have their own spheres; the supremacy of international law in the international sphere is unchallenged, as is that of municipal law in State matters; what international law requires is that a State in breach &ldquo;cannot shelter itself behind domestic law by way of absolution&rdquo;. (7) Illustrate with the Indian material: Berubari (the treaty binds India; the Constitution channels the implementation), Jolly George (the Covenant binds India; the statute governs the court), Vishaka (the treaty informs the Constitution). Close with Verma's &ldquo;mixture&rdquo; conclusion.",
