          "question": "Answer 4 — 1999 Q.4: the criterion adopted by municipal courts Question. &ldquo;The relationship between international law and municipal law has posed a difficult problem to municipal courts, namely, to what extent may such courts give effect within the municipal sphere to rules of international law.&rdquo; Clearly examine the criterion which has been adopted by municipal courts to resolve this issue. Refer to settled judicial precedents. (1999, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer. Present the criterion as a hierarchy of four questions , which is the cleanest way to answer. What kind of rule is it — custom or treaty? Custom is generally received without legislation ( The Paquete Habana , 175 U.S. 677: &ldquo;international law is part of our law... where there is no treaty and no controlling executive or legislative act or judicial decision&rdquo;; Trendtex ; Chung Chi Cheung ; in India Krishna Sharma , Gramophone , Vellore ). Treaties, in the UK and India, require legislation ( The Parlement Belge ; Maclaine Watson ; Jolly George ); in the US they require it only if not self-executing ( Sei Fujii ). Is there a statute on the point? If the statute is clear, it prevails over custom and over an unlegislated treaty ( Mortensen v. Peters , 1906; Gramophone ; Jolly George ; Puttaswamy ¶133). If the statute is ambiguous, it is construed in conformity with international law ( Salomon ; Brind ; Gramophone ). Is there a constitutional provision? The Constitution always prevails ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ; Tag v. Rogers ; the German Basic Law's fundamental provisions; Article 54 of the French Constitution), although a reception clause may give custom or treaties rank above ordinary statutes (Article 25 German Basic Law; Articles 93–94 Dutch Constitution; Article 28(1) Greek Constitution; Article 15(4) Russian Constitution). Is the rule directly effective (self-executing) as regards individuals? If yes, the individual may rely on it ( Paquete Habana ; Trendtex ; Bennett v. Horseferry Magistrates' Court , 1993 — the abduction case; Vishaka , where the convention supplied the content of the rights). If no, the individual's remedy is only through interpretation ( Jolly George ) or through the political organs ( Xavier v. Canara Bank : &ldquo;the individual cannot come to Court but may complain to the Human Rights Committee&rdquo;). Add the criterion's twin : the criterion applied by international tribunals is the reverse and flat — internal law is no excuse (VCLT Art. 27; ARSIWA Arts. 3, 32; the Alabama Claims; LaGrand ). Close with the observation that the criterion is not a single rule but a technique of accommodation , and that its direction of travel in India since 1980 has been towards greater use of international norms.",
          "answer": "Question. &ldquo;The relationship between international law and municipal law has posed a difficult problem to municipal courts, namely, to what extent may such courts give effect within the municipal sphere to rules of international law.&rdquo; Clearly examine the criterion which has been adopted by municipal courts to resolve this issue. Refer to settled judicial precedents. (1999, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer. Present the criterion as a hierarchy of four questions , which is the cleanest way to answer. What kind of rule is it — custom or treaty? Custom is generally received without legislation ( The Paquete Habana , 175 U.S. 677: &ldquo;international law is part of our law... where there is no treaty and no controlling executive or legislative act or judicial decision&rdquo;; Trendtex ; Chung Chi Cheung ; in India Krishna Sharma , Gramophone , Vellore ). Treaties, in the UK and India, require legislation ( The Parlement Belge ; Maclaine Watson ; Jolly George ); in the US they require it only if not self-executing ( Sei Fujii ). Is there a statute on the point? If the statute is clear, it prevails over custom and over an unlegislated treaty ( Mortensen v. Peters , 1906; Gramophone ; Jolly George ; Puttaswamy ¶133). If the statute is ambiguous, it is construed in conformity with international law ( Salomon ; Brind ; Gramophone ). Is there a constitutional provision? The Constitution always prevails ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ; Tag v. Rogers ; the German Basic Law's fundamental provisions; Article 54 of the French Constitution), although a reception clause may give custom or treaties rank above ordinary statutes (Article 25 German Basic Law; Articles 93–94 Dutch Constitution; Article 28(1) Greek Constitution; Article 15(4) Russian Constitution). Is the rule directly effective (self-executing) as regards individuals? If yes, the individual may rely on it ( Paquete Habana ; Trendtex ; Bennett v. Horseferry Magistrates' Court , 1993 — the abduction case; Vishaka , where the convention supplied the content of the rights). If no, the individual's remedy is only through interpretation ( Jolly George ) or through the political organs ( Xavier v. Canara Bank : &ldquo;the individual cannot come to Court but may complain to the Human Rights Committee&rdquo;). Add the criterion's twin : the criterion applied by international tribunals is the reverse and flat — internal law is no excuse (VCLT Art. 27; ARSIWA Arts. 3, 32; the Alabama Claims; LaGrand ). Close with the observation that the criterion is not a single rule but a technique of accommodation , and that its direction of travel in India since 1980 has been towards greater use of international norms.",
