          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 5 — 1998 Q.4(a): applicability of customary international rules in the practice of India and the USA Question. &ldquo;Two questions arise, first, whether international law is of its own force, drawn into the law of the land without the aid of a municipal statute and, second, whether so drawn, it overrides municipal law in case of conflict&rdquo; ( Gramophone Co. of India v. B. B. Pandey , AIR 1984 SC 667). Discuss with reference to the applicability of customary international rules in the practice of India and the U.S.A. (1998, Q.4(a), part of a 20-mark question) Answer. Take the two questions separately, and answer each for both countries. Question 1 — is custom drawn in without a statute? India : yes, through the common-law doctrine of incorporation continued by Article 372(1) — Krishna Sharma (1954) (courts apply international law so far as not inconsistent with statute; they cannot override a statute), PUCL (1997) (&ldquo;almost accepted proposition of law&rdquo;), Vellore (1996) (sustainable development, precautionary principle and polluter pays principle accepted as part of the domestic law), Pratap Singh (2005) (international law as statutory implication). USA : yes, custom is part of federal law — The Paquete Habana (1900): &ldquo;International law is part of our law, and must be ascertained and administered by the Courts of Justice of appropriate jurisdiction... where there is no treaty and no controlling executive or legislative act or judicial decision&rdquo;; its determination by the federal courts binds State courts (Restatement (Third), 1987). Question 2 — does it override municipal law in conflict? India : no. Gramophone itself supplies the answer — the rule is incorporated &ldquo;unless [it is] in conflict with an Act of Parliament&rdquo;; &ldquo;municipal law must prevail in case of conflict&rdquo;; the remedy is interpretation so as to avoid confrontation. A.D.M. Jabalpur adds the constitutional limit; Vellore itself accepts the principles only because they do not conflict with Indian law. USA : no, and with three exceptions carved into the Paquete Habana formula itself — no treaty , no controlling executive or legislative act , no judicial decision . Hence in Garcia-Mir v. Meese (1986) an Attorney-General's decision (a &ldquo;controlling executive act&rdquo;) displaced customary law on the detention of aliens; a clear statute prevails ( The Over the Top , 1925); and the Constitution prevails ( Tag v. Rogers , 1959). Where no such act exists, the courts harmonise — the Charming Betsy canon (1804): &ldquo;an act of Congress ought never to be construed to violate the law of nations if any other possible construction remains&rdquo;. Conclusion. In both countries the answer to the first question is &ldquo;yes&rdquo; and to the second &ldquo;no&rdquo; — a qualified incorporation : custom is received as a rule of decision subject to the supremacy of the legislature and the constitution. India's practice is the more generous as regards the content of custom ( Vellore ), the USA's the more institutionalised as regards its federal character, and neither permits custom to defeat a clear statute. Note the further difference in treaty practice: India requires legislation generally, while the USA gives self-executing treaties the rank of supreme law.",
          "answer": "Question. &ldquo;Two questions arise, first, whether international law is of its own force, drawn into the law of the land without the aid of a municipal statute and, second, whether so drawn, it overrides municipal law in case of conflict&rdquo; ( Gramophone Co. of India v. B. B. Pandey , AIR 1984 SC 667). Discuss with reference to the applicability of customary international rules in the practice of India and the U.S.A. (1998, Q.4(a), part of a 20-mark question) Answer. Take the two questions separately, and answer each for both countries. Question 1 — is custom drawn in without a statute? India : yes, through the common-law doctrine of incorporation continued by Article 372(1) — Krishna Sharma (1954) (courts apply international law so far as not inconsistent with statute; they cannot override a statute), PUCL (1997) (&ldquo;almost accepted proposition of law&rdquo;), Vellore (1996) (sustainable development, precautionary principle and polluter pays principle accepted as part of the domestic law), Pratap Singh (2005) (international law as statutory implication). USA : yes, custom is part of federal law — The Paquete Habana (1900): &ldquo;International law is part of our law, and must be ascertained and administered by the Courts of Justice of appropriate jurisdiction... where there is no treaty and no controlling executive or legislative act or judicial decision&rdquo;; its determination by the federal courts binds State courts (Restatement (Third), 1987). Question 2 — does it override municipal law in conflict? India : no. Gramophone itself supplies the answer — the rule is incorporated &ldquo;unless [it is] in conflict with an Act of Parliament&rdquo;; &ldquo;municipal law must prevail in case of conflict&rdquo;; the remedy is interpretation so as to avoid confrontation. A.D.M. Jabalpur adds the constitutional limit; Vellore itself accepts the principles only because they do not conflict with Indian law. USA : no, and with three exceptions carved into the Paquete Habana formula itself — no treaty , no controlling executive or legislative act , no judicial decision . Hence in Garcia-Mir v. Meese (1986) an Attorney-General's decision (a &ldquo;controlling executive act&rdquo;) displaced customary law on the detention of aliens; a clear statute prevails ( The Over the Top , 1925); and the Constitution prevails ( Tag v. Rogers , 1959). Where no such act exists, the courts harmonise — the Charming Betsy canon (1804): &ldquo;an act of Congress ought never to be construed to violate the law of nations if any other possible construction remains&rdquo;. Conclusion. In both countries the answer to the first question is &ldquo;yes&rdquo; and to the second &ldquo;no&rdquo; — a qualified incorporation : custom is received as a rule of decision subject to the supremacy of the legislature and the constitution. India's practice is the more generous as regards the content of custom ( Vellore ), the USA's the more institutionalised as regards its federal character, and neither permits custom to defeat a clear statute. Note the further difference in treaty practice: India requires legislation generally, while the USA gives self-executing treaties the rank of supreme law.",
