          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 6 — 1995 Q.3: Fitzmaurice, the unreal controversy, and the treaty practice of India and the USA Question. According to Fitzmaurice, the entire monist–dualist controversy on the subject of international law and municipal law is unreal and does not exist. Both simultaneously have their spheres of activity. However, occasions come when they clash. Discuss the State practice relating to treaties in India and the United States of America. Which theory, on the relationship between international law and municipal law, is supported by their practice? (1995, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Begin with Fitzmaurice's position as stated in the prescribed text: the two systems &ldquo;operate without any conflict in their assigned spheres of action&rdquo;; accordingly &ldquo;the question of superiority or primacy of one system over the other does not arise&rdquo;, and where a State organ fails to apply international law, that is &ldquo;a manifestation of its weakness, but does not invalidate the theory, since the State will incur international responsibility&rdquo;. (Recall also that Fitzmaurice considered Anzilotti to have been wrongly labelled a dualist — a nice point of scholarship.) Then give the treaty practice of the two States, and finally answer the &ldquo;which theory&rdquo; question. India. Making: executive act (Arts. 53, 73, Entry 14) — Manmull Jain , Nirmal Bose . International effect at once; internal effect only on legislation (Art. 253) — Jolly George , Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee . Self-execution recognised in a minority view ( Maganbhai , Shah J.; the Kutch Award). Cession requires an amendment ( Berubari ); boundary settlement and servitudes do not ( Ram Kishore Sen ; Sukumar Sengupta ). Statutes prevail on conflict; conventions inform interpretation and fill vacuums ( Gramophone , Vishaka , Puttaswamy ). United States. Making: President with the advice and consent of two-thirds of the Senate (Art. II(2)); effect: &ldquo;supreme Law of the Land&rdquo; (Art. VI(2)) — but only if self-executing ( Sei Fujii ; Medellín ); later-in-time rule as against federal statutes, with the presumption against congressional override ( Edye ; Cook ; US v. PLO ); treaties prevail over State law ( Missouri v. Holland ); the Constitution prevails over treaties ( Tag v. Rogers ); executive agreements have equivalent force ( Belmont ; Pink ); custom is federal law, subject to controlling acts ( Paquete Habana ; Garcia-Mir ). Which theory is supported by their practice? Neither exclusively — and that is the answer the question is fishing for. On paper the United States looks monist (treaties are supreme law) but behaves dualistically (self-execution, later-in-time, Medellín ); India looks dualist (Art. 253; Jolly George ) but behaves monistically in interpretation ( Vishaka , Vellore , Puttaswamy ). Both are &ldquo;dualist in hierarchy, monist in interpretation&rdquo;, and each illustrates Fitzmaurice's point that the controversy is unreal in the sense that the systems rarely collide in an irreconcilable way — and that when they do, each answers within its own sphere, with the State bearing the international responsibility.",
          "answer": "Question. According to Fitzmaurice, the entire monist–dualist controversy on the subject of international law and municipal law is unreal and does not exist. Both simultaneously have their spheres of activity. However, occasions come when they clash. Discuss the State practice relating to treaties in India and the United States of America. Which theory, on the relationship between international law and municipal law, is supported by their practice? (1995, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Begin with Fitzmaurice's position as stated in the prescribed text: the two systems &ldquo;operate without any conflict in their assigned spheres of action&rdquo;; accordingly &ldquo;the question of superiority or primacy of one system over the other does not arise&rdquo;, and where a State organ fails to apply international law, that is &ldquo;a manifestation of its weakness, but does not invalidate the theory, since the State will incur international responsibility&rdquo;. (Recall also that Fitzmaurice considered Anzilotti to have been wrongly labelled a dualist — a nice point of scholarship.) Then give the treaty practice of the two States, and finally answer the &ldquo;which theory&rdquo; question. India. Making: executive act (Arts. 53, 73, Entry 14) — Manmull Jain , Nirmal Bose . International effect at once; internal effect only on legislation (Art. 253) — Jolly George , Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee . Self-execution recognised in a minority view ( Maganbhai , Shah J.; the Kutch Award). Cession requires an amendment ( Berubari ); boundary settlement and servitudes do not ( Ram Kishore Sen ; Sukumar Sengupta ). Statutes prevail on conflict; conventions inform interpretation and fill vacuums ( Gramophone , Vishaka , Puttaswamy ). United States. Making: President with the advice and consent of two-thirds of the Senate (Art. II(2)); effect: &ldquo;supreme Law of the Land&rdquo; (Art. VI(2)) — but only if self-executing ( Sei Fujii ; Medellín ); later-in-time rule as against federal statutes, with the presumption against congressional override ( Edye ; Cook ; US v. PLO ); treaties prevail over State law ( Missouri v. Holland ); the Constitution prevails over treaties ( Tag v. Rogers ); executive agreements have equivalent force ( Belmont ; Pink ); custom is federal law, subject to controlling acts ( Paquete Habana ; Garcia-Mir ). Which theory is supported by their practice? Neither exclusively — and that is the answer the question is fishing for. On paper the United States looks monist (treaties are supreme law) but behaves dualistically (self-execution, later-in-time, Medellín ); India looks dualist (Art. 253; Jolly George ) but behaves monistically in interpretation ( Vishaka , Vellore , Puttaswamy ). Both are &ldquo;dualist in hierarchy, monist in interpretation&rdquo;, and each illustrates Fitzmaurice's point that the controversy is unreal in the sense that the systems rarely collide in an irreconcilable way — and that when they do, each answers within its own sphere, with the State bearing the international responsibility.",
