          "id": "pil-pyq-u3-11",
          "year": "1994",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 12,
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 7 — 1994 Q.2: the customary practices of India, the UK and the USA compared Question. In fact there is no reason to consider [the] relationship between international law and municipal law [a problem], as both the laws act in their respective spheres and there is never any clash between them. However, States do have different practices in this regard... Discuss, therefore, the customary practices of India, the UK and the USA in this regard to bring home the differences in their approach. (1994, Q.2, 20 marks) Answer. This question is confined to custom (contrast Answer 6, which is about treaties) — a distinction worth stating expressly. United Kingdom : the Blackstonian incorporation doctrine (Blackstone; Buvot v. Barbuit ; Triquet v. Bath ), qualified by West Rand (1905) and Chung Chi Cheung (1939), interrupted by R. v. Keyn (1876) and restored by statute (Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878), modernised by Trendtex (1977) and Rayner (1990); two qualifications (no inconsistency with statute; no inconsistency with a declared rule), plus the exceptions of Acts of State and the Crown's prerogative (executive certificates); harmonious construction ( ex p. Salam ) and judicial notice; prize courts apply international law even against an Order in Council ( The Zamora ). United States : custom is part of federal law ( Paquete Habana ), subject to three exceptions read into the doctrine itself (treaty, controlling executive or legislative act, judicial decision); federal determination binds State courts (Restatement (Third)); executive certificates on recognition and immunity; the Charming Betsy canon; the Constitution prevails ( Tag v. Rogers ). India : incorporation through Article 372(1) ( T.K. Varred ); custom applied so far as consistent with statute ( Krishna Sharma , Gramophone , PUCL , Vellore , Pratap Singh ); not enforceable against the Constitution ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ); the executive's statements accepted in matters of recognition and the extent of territory; no judicial authority yet on custom where there is no controlling statute, executive decision or treaty. Differences in approach — draw them out: the UK's is doctrinal (a common-law rule with judicial qualifications); the USA's is constitutional-federal (custom as federal law, subject to the political branches); India's is received and subordinate (via Article 372, but with no reception clause and a marked reluctance to let custom displace anything written). Conclusion : the &ldquo;spheres&rdquo; theory holds for custom more often than for treaties, because custom is generally consistent with domestic law; where it is not, all three systems prefer the domestic rule, and the State answers internationally.",
          "answer": "Question. In fact there is no reason to consider [the] relationship between international law and municipal law [a problem], as both the laws act in their respective spheres and there is never any clash between them. However, States do have different practices in this regard... Discuss, therefore, the customary practices of India, the UK and the USA in this regard to bring home the differences in their approach. (1994, Q.2, 20 marks) Answer. This question is confined to custom (contrast Answer 6, which is about treaties) — a distinction worth stating expressly. United Kingdom : the Blackstonian incorporation doctrine (Blackstone; Buvot v. Barbuit ; Triquet v. Bath ), qualified by West Rand (1905) and Chung Chi Cheung (1939), interrupted by R. v. Keyn (1876) and restored by statute (Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878), modernised by Trendtex (1977) and Rayner (1990); two qualifications (no inconsistency with statute; no inconsistency with a declared rule), plus the exceptions of Acts of State and the Crown's prerogative (executive certificates); harmonious construction ( ex p. Salam ) and judicial notice; prize courts apply international law even against an Order in Council ( The Zamora ). United States : custom is part of federal law ( Paquete Habana ), subject to three exceptions read into the doctrine itself (treaty, controlling executive or legislative act, judicial decision); federal determination binds State courts (Restatement (Third)); executive certificates on recognition and immunity; the Charming Betsy canon; the Constitution prevails ( Tag v. Rogers ). India : incorporation through Article 372(1) ( T.K. Varred ); custom applied so far as consistent with statute ( Krishna Sharma , Gramophone , PUCL , Vellore , Pratap Singh ); not enforceable against the Constitution ( A.D.M. Jabalpur ); the executive's statements accepted in matters of recognition and the extent of territory; no judicial authority yet on custom where there is no controlling statute, executive decision or treaty. Differences in approach — draw them out: the UK's is doctrinal (a common-law rule with judicial qualifications); the USA's is constitutional-federal (custom as federal law, subject to the political branches); India's is received and subordinate (via Article 372, but with no reception clause and a marked reluctance to let custom displace anything written). Conclusion : the &ldquo;spheres&rdquo; theory holds for custom more often than for treaties, because custom is generally consistent with domestic law; where it is not, all three systems prefer the domestic rule, and the State answers internationally.",
