          "id": "pil-pyq-u3-12",
          "year": "1993",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 13,
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 8 — 1993 Q.3: no theory reflects State practice — examine the treaty practices of India, the USA and the UK Question. None of the theories which explain the relationship between international law and municipal law reflects the correct position of State practices in this regard. We can know the exact position only by examining the State practices. Examine the treaty practices of India, the USA and the UK to know the position of their law vis-à-vis the law of nations. (1993, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Accept the premise and then test it in the three systems. India : executive conclusion (Arts. 53, 73, Entry 14); legislation for implementation (Art. 253) — Jolly George , Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee ; self-execution in a minority view ( Maganbhai ); cession by amendment ( Berubari ), boundary and servitude without ( Ram Kishore Sen , Sukumar Sengupta ); statutes prevail, but conventions guide interpretation ( Gramophone ) and fill vacuums ( Vishaka ). United States : Art. II(2) and Art. VI(2); the self-executing doctrine ( Sei Fujii , Medellín ); later-in-time ( Edye , Cook ); the Constitution prevails; executive agreements ( Belmont , Pink , and the Case–Zablocki reporting requirement, 1972); the PLO Mission episode and the ICJ's advisory opinion (1988) showing international accountability. United Kingdom : the Crown's prerogative to make treaties; &ldquo;treaties are not self-executing&rdquo; ( Maclaine Watson ; Rayner ; The Parlement Belge ); the five categories requiring legislation; cession requiring a statute; unincorporated treaties as an aid to interpretation ( Brind ); the Human Rights Act 1998 and the EU (Withdrawal) Act 2018 showing internal effect to be a matter of legislative choice. Evaluation : (i) all three are dualist for treaties in structure and rather more open in interpretation; (ii) all three distinguish between what binds the State and what binds its courts; (iii) all three have found ways to avoid the clash (self-execution; legislation; harmonious construction; the &ldquo;no conflict&rdquo; finding as in Gramophone and Vishaka ); (iv) none matches either theory completely — so the premise stands, and the correct method is the two-question method of &sect;3.0 C: application and supremacy, answered separately for custom, treaty and constitution. Close with Verma's conclusion that &ldquo;each situation must be analysed by itself&rdquo;.",
          "answer": "Question. None of the theories which explain the relationship between international law and municipal law reflects the correct position of State practices in this regard. We can know the exact position only by examining the State practices. Examine the treaty practices of India, the USA and the UK to know the position of their law vis-à-vis the law of nations. (1993, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Accept the premise and then test it in the three systems. India : executive conclusion (Arts. 53, 73, Entry 14); legislation for implementation (Art. 253) — Jolly George , Birma , Shiv Kumar Sharma , G.G. Menon , Civil Rights Vigilance Committee ; self-execution in a minority view ( Maganbhai ); cession by amendment ( Berubari ), boundary and servitude without ( Ram Kishore Sen , Sukumar Sengupta ); statutes prevail, but conventions guide interpretation ( Gramophone ) and fill vacuums ( Vishaka ). United States : Art. II(2) and Art. VI(2); the self-executing doctrine ( Sei Fujii , Medellín ); later-in-time ( Edye , Cook ); the Constitution prevails; executive agreements ( Belmont , Pink , and the Case–Zablocki reporting requirement, 1972); the PLO Mission episode and the ICJ's advisory opinion (1988) showing international accountability. United Kingdom : the Crown's prerogative to make treaties; &ldquo;treaties are not self-executing&rdquo; ( Maclaine Watson ; Rayner ; The Parlement Belge ); the five categories requiring legislation; cession requiring a statute; unincorporated treaties as an aid to interpretation ( Brind ); the Human Rights Act 1998 and the EU (Withdrawal) Act 2018 showing internal effect to be a matter of legislative choice. Evaluation : (i) all three are dualist for treaties in structure and rather more open in interpretation; (ii) all three distinguish between what binds the State and what binds its courts; (iii) all three have found ways to avoid the clash (self-execution; legislation; harmonious construction; the &ldquo;no conflict&rdquo; finding as in Gramophone and Vishaka ); (iv) none matches either theory completely — so the premise stands, and the correct method is the two-question method of &sect;3.0 C: application and supremacy, answered separately for custom, treaty and constitution. Close with Verma's conclusion that &ldquo;each situation must be analysed by itself&rdquo;.",
