          "id": "pil-pyq-u3-13",
          "year": "1992",
          "term": "II Term",
          "questionNumber": 14,
          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Answer 9 — 1992 Q.4: the theories, State practice, and which is nearer to reality Question. State the various theories regarding the relationship between International law and Municipal law. Discuss them with the help of State practice, stating clearly which is nearer to reality. (1992, Q.4, 20 marks) Answer. This is the classic question of the topic, and the model answer is set out in full at the end of &sect;3.1 (see the boxed skeleton after Fig. 3.3). In outline: (1) the two questions — application and supremacy; (2) monism — Kelsen, Lauterpacht, Fitzmaurice, Starke, Duguit, Wright; consequences; criticism; (3) dualism — Triepel's three differences, Anzilotti's postulates; consequences; criticism; (4) the theories of application — transformation/specific adoption and delegation; (5) primacy — dualists to municipal law, Kelsen's reluctant choice of State law and the reasons it fails, Starke's international constitutional law and Verma's objections, and the settled &ldquo;spheres&rdquo; conclusion; (6) State practice — UK (Blackstone to Trendtex for custom; Maclaine Watson for treaties), USA ( Paquete Habana for custom; Art. VI(2) with self-execution for treaties), India (Arts. 372, 51(c), 253 with Gramophone , Jolly George , Vellore , Vishaka , Puttaswamy ), and the continental clauses (Germany Art. 25; Netherlands 93–94; France 55; Greece 28(1); Russia 15(4)); (7) the verdict — neither theory is nearer to reality as a universal description ; practice is &ldquo;a mixture of international law supremacy, municipal law supremacy and coordination&rdquo;, and States cannot be &ldquo;compartmentalise[d]&rdquo; into monist and dualist groups; the nearest to reality is the coordination position of the prescribed text, with the qualification that the State remains internationally responsible whatever its courts decide.",
