          "question": "Answer 10 — 1991 Q.3: each supreme in its own sphere — customary practices of the USA, the UK and India Question. In fact International law and Municipal law are each supreme in their respective spheres. Nevertheless State practices vary. Discuss the State practices in International customary law of the USA, U.K. and India. (1991, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Begin by explaining the &ldquo;sphere&rdquo; thesis: as the prescribed text puts it, &ldquo;international law and municipal law are not comparable since both have their own sphere of operation and neither can be termed as subordinate to the other. The supremacy of international law in the international sphere is unchallenged in the same way as of municipal law in the State matters.&rdquo; Then give the three practices as in Answer 7, with emphasis on custom : the USA ( Paquete Habana 's formula and its three exceptions; Garcia-Mir ; the Charming Betsy canon; federal character of custom); the UK (Blackstone; West Rand ; Chung Chi Cheung ; Trendtex ; the two qualifications and the exceptions for Acts of State and the prerogative); India ( Krishna Sharma ; Gramophone ; PUCL ; Vellore ; Pratap Singh ; the constitutional limit in A.D.M. Jabalpur ; the unresolved question of custom where no statute, executive act or treaty exists). Then make the three comparisons which earn the marks: (i) all three have a common ancestor (the common law of incorporation), so the differences are differences of qualification , not of principle; (ii) the qualifications differ in origin — judicial (UK), constitutional-federal (USA), constitutional-imported-through-Article 372 (India); (iii) the consequences of a conflict are the same — the domestic rule prevails, and the State remains internationally responsible. Conclude with the practical corollary that custom is the channel through which international law most often enters domestic law without legislation, and that the Indian examples ( Vellore 's precautionary and polluter pays principles) show it doing real work.",
          "answer": "Question. In fact International law and Municipal law are each supreme in their respective spheres. Nevertheless State practices vary. Discuss the State practices in International customary law of the USA, U.K. and India. (1991, Q.3, 20 marks) Answer. Begin by explaining the &ldquo;sphere&rdquo; thesis: as the prescribed text puts it, &ldquo;international law and municipal law are not comparable since both have their own sphere of operation and neither can be termed as subordinate to the other. The supremacy of international law in the international sphere is unchallenged in the same way as of municipal law in the State matters.&rdquo; Then give the three practices as in Answer 7, with emphasis on custom : the USA ( Paquete Habana 's formula and its three exceptions; Garcia-Mir ; the Charming Betsy canon; federal character of custom); the UK (Blackstone; West Rand ; Chung Chi Cheung ; Trendtex ; the two qualifications and the exceptions for Acts of State and the prerogative); India ( Krishna Sharma ; Gramophone ; PUCL ; Vellore ; Pratap Singh ; the constitutional limit in A.D.M. Jabalpur ; the unresolved question of custom where no statute, executive act or treaty exists). Then make the three comparisons which earn the marks: (i) all three have a common ancestor (the common law of incorporation), so the differences are differences of qualification , not of principle; (ii) the qualifications differ in origin — judicial (UK), constitutional-federal (USA), constitutional-imported-through-Article 372 (India); (iii) the consequences of a conflict are the same — the domestic rule prevails, and the State remains internationally responsible. Conclude with the practical corollary that custom is the channel through which international law most often enters domestic law without legislation, and that the Indian examples ( Vellore 's precautionary and polluter pays principles) show it doing real work.",
