          "unitNumber": 3,
          "question": "Problem 1 — 1992 Q.5: the Danish trawler and the British statute Problem. The British Parliament passed a law prohibiting fishing in an area of the British maritime belt. A Danish trawler was captured while fishing in this area and was put on trial. The Captain of the trawler pleaded that International Law permitted it. Decide. (1992, Q.5) Model answer. The problem is the classic Mortensen v. Peters situation, and it is decided by the rule that a clear statute prevails over customary international law within the municipal sphere . Facts of the real case: in Mortensen v. Peters , (1906) 8 F (J) 93, a Danish subject, Mortensen, master of the Norwegian steamer Rio Tinto , was convicted by the Court of Justiciary of Scotland of otter-trawling in the Moray Firth within an area covered by the Sea Fisheries Regulation (Scotland) Act, 1895. The Act prohibited trawling within an area extending more than seventy miles from the coast in the Moray Firth, whereas customary international law then recognised only a ten-mile (or, for the territorial sea proper, a three-mile) limit for bays and estuaries. Mortensen pleaded that international law permitted the fishing. Held: the statute was clear and unambiguous, and it was applied; the plea of international law failed; the appellant was liable. Reasoning: (i) the Blackstonian incorporation doctrine is subject to the qualification that custom must not be inconsistent with a British statute, whether earlier or later ( West Rand ; Chung Chi Cheung ; the qualification identified in the prescribed text); (ii) where the words of a statute are clear, the court must give effect to them, even though the statute is in breach of international law ( cf. Polites v. Commonwealth (1945) 70 CLR 60); the presumption that Parliament does not intend to legislate against international law operates only where the statute is ambiguous ( ex p. Salam ; Salomon ; Brind ); (iii) it is not for the court to correct the legislature: as the Indian Supreme Court was to put it much later, municipal law must prevail in case of conflict and &ldquo;national courts cannot say &lsquo;yes&rsquo; if Parliament has said &lsquo;no&rsquo; to a principle of international law&rdquo; ( Gramophone , AIR 1984 SC 667). Consequence for the United Kingdom: the United Kingdom remained internationally responsible for any resulting breach — Article 27 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties and Article 3 of the Articles on State Responsibility — and international law would look to Parliament, not to the Court of Justiciary, for a remedy (as Parliament had done by the Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878 after R. v. Keyn , and as it did in the twentieth century by the Territorial Sea Act, 1987 establishing a twelve-nautical-mile territorial sea consistently with Article 3 of UNCLOS, 1982). Answer to the \"decide\" instruction: convict the captain; the plea of international law is no defence against a clear statute, but the State may not invoke that statute to excuse the breach internationally. If the same problem arose in India, the result would be the same, on the authority of Krishna Sharma , Gramophone and Puttaswamy ¶133 — municipal law prevails on a real conflict.",
          "answer": "Problem. The British Parliament passed a law prohibiting fishing in an area of the British maritime belt. A Danish trawler was captured while fishing in this area and was put on trial. The Captain of the trawler pleaded that International Law permitted it. Decide. (1992, Q.5) Model answer. The problem is the classic Mortensen v. Peters situation, and it is decided by the rule that a clear statute prevails over customary international law within the municipal sphere . Facts of the real case: in Mortensen v. Peters , (1906) 8 F (J) 93, a Danish subject, Mortensen, master of the Norwegian steamer Rio Tinto , was convicted by the Court of Justiciary of Scotland of otter-trawling in the Moray Firth within an area covered by the Sea Fisheries Regulation (Scotland) Act, 1895. The Act prohibited trawling within an area extending more than seventy miles from the coast in the Moray Firth, whereas customary international law then recognised only a ten-mile (or, for the territorial sea proper, a three-mile) limit for bays and estuaries. Mortensen pleaded that international law permitted the fishing. Held: the statute was clear and unambiguous, and it was applied; the plea of international law failed; the appellant was liable. Reasoning: (i) the Blackstonian incorporation doctrine is subject to the qualification that custom must not be inconsistent with a British statute, whether earlier or later ( West Rand ; Chung Chi Cheung ; the qualification identified in the prescribed text); (ii) where the words of a statute are clear, the court must give effect to them, even though the statute is in breach of international law ( cf. Polites v. Commonwealth (1945) 70 CLR 60); the presumption that Parliament does not intend to legislate against international law operates only where the statute is ambiguous ( ex p. Salam ; Salomon ; Brind ); (iii) it is not for the court to correct the legislature: as the Indian Supreme Court was to put it much later, municipal law must prevail in case of conflict and &ldquo;national courts cannot say &lsquo;yes&rsquo; if Parliament has said &lsquo;no&rsquo; to a principle of international law&rdquo; ( Gramophone , AIR 1984 SC 667). Consequence for the United Kingdom: the United Kingdom remained internationally responsible for any resulting breach — Article 27 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties and Article 3 of the Articles on State Responsibility — and international law would look to Parliament, not to the Court of Justiciary, for a remedy (as Parliament had done by the Territorial Waters Jurisdiction Act, 1878 after R. v. Keyn , and as it did in the twentieth century by the Territorial Sea Act, 1987 establishing a twelve-nautical-mile territorial sea consistently with Article 3 of UNCLOS, 1982). Answer to the \"decide\" instruction: convict the captain; the plea of international law is no defence against a clear statute, but the State may not invoke that statute to excuse the breach internationally. If the same problem arose in India, the result would be the same, on the authority of Krishna Sharma , Gramophone and Puttaswamy ¶133 — municipal law prevails on a real conflict.",
