          "question": "Question bank with answer plans Table 4.21 · Expected questions and one-line answer plans Question Answer plan Define State responsibility. What is its legal basis? Art. 1 + Chorzów + sovereign equality + Ago's primary/secondary rule distinction + Art. 3 no excuse of internal law. Distinguish between primary and secondary rules of international law. Ago's reports; primary rules create obligations; secondary rules (the ILC Articles) govern the consequences of breach; illustrate with the VCDR (primary) and Articles 30–37 (secondary). \"No damage, no responsibility.\" Discuss the damage theory. No general requirement; depends on the primary obligation (uniform law example); damage re-enters as \"injury\" (Art. 31(2)); standing (Arts. 42, 48); Trail Smelter and Gabčíkovo contrasted with embassy inviolability. Is fault a necessary element of State responsibility? Objective responsibility in principle; fault returns through diligence obligations ( Caire ; Corfu ; Tehran ); Verma's \"no clear rule either way\". Explain the theory of absolute liability and the risk theory. Treaty-based strict liability: 1972 Space Objects; nuclear 1960/1963; oil 1969/1971; channelling, insurance, caps, funds; Art. 55; Cosmos 954 ; M.C. Mehta ; 1991 Act. What is meant by \"attribution\" or \"imputability\"? Discuss Articles 4–11. Definition (legal fiction connecting the individual's act to the State); each article with a case (see §4.3). \"A State is not responsible for the acts of private persons.\" Examine. True as to attribution (Art. 8's limits; Nicaragua ¶115); but responsibility for the State's own failure ( Corfu ; Tehran ), adoption (Art. 11), insurrection (Art. 10), complicity (Arts. 16–18). Discuss the rule in the Corfu Channel case. Facts; two Special Agreement questions; knowledge inferred; general principles; Operation Retail and satisfaction; £843,947; the standard of proof (\"liberal recourse to inferences of fact\"). Explain the principle of Barcelona Traction . Separate legal personality; shareholders' State lacks jus standi ; exceptions; erga omnes dictum and its descent (Art. 48; Gambia v. Myanmar ). What did Tehran Hostages decide about attribution? Two phases; ¶58 (no initial attribution); ¶61–63 (failure to protect); ¶74 (adoption → agents of the State); self-contained régime (¶86); deferred reparation (¶90). Discuss \"effective control\" with reference to Nicaragua and Tadić . Nicaragua ¶115; Tadić overall control; Genocide 2007 rejecting Tadić for State responsibility; practical difference; Art. 8. \"An apology is enough.\" Discuss with reference to LaGrand . ¶123 (apology insufficient); ¶124 (programme as assurance); ¶125 (review and reconsideration); Art. 37 satisfaction and Art. 30(b). Are provisional measures of the ICJ binding? LaGrand ¶¶92–110 (Art. 41; Art. 94 Charter; Sofia order); consequences — breach engages responsibility ( DRC v. Uganda , 2000). Discuss the forms of reparation with the ILC Articles. See the model answer at 2015/2013/2011 above: Arts. 31, 34–39 with cases. What is restitutio in integrum ? When may it be refused? Art. 35; material impossibility; disproportionate burden; Chorzów ; Temple of Preah Vihear ; Rainbow Warrior ; Gabcíkovo . Are punitive damages available in international law? No: Lusitania ; I'm Alone ; DRC v. Uganda ¶102; individual criminal responsibility is the proper channel (Art. 58). Explain satisfaction and its limits. Art. 37; declaration, regret, apology; proportionality; no humiliation; Corfu Channel ; Rainbow Warrior ; Genocide 2007. Discuss guarantees of non-repetition. Art. 30(b) (and the older classification as a form of reparation); LaGrand ; Nicaragua 's order to cease; human-rights practice; the 2005 UN Basic Principles. What is the rule of exhaustion of local remedies? What are its exceptions? Art. 44(b); rationale; Ambatielos ; Finnish Ships ; Interhandel ; ELSI ; Diallo ; Art. 15 Diplomatic Protection Articles. Distinguish nationality of claims from exhaustion of local remedies. Art. 44(a) and (b); Nottebohm (genuine link); Barcelona Traction (corporations); continuance of nationality; the two are cumulative conditions of admissibility. Discuss the circumstances precluding wrongfulness. Arts. 20–27 with cases (see §4.3(H) and the defences section). What are countermeasures? State their conditions. Arts. 22, 49–54; Naulilaa ; Air Services ; Gabčíkovo ; retorsion distinguished; human-rights limits. When may a State plead necessity? Art. 25; cumulative conditions; Gabčíkovo ¶¶49–58; Wall AO; Argentinian cases; Art. 27(a). What is the legal status of the ILC Articles, 2001? Not a treaty; annexed to GA res. 56/83; applied as customary law ( DRC v. Uganda , 2022; Genocide , 2007); lex specialis (Art. 55); the question of a future convention. Write short notes on: (i) erga omnes ; (ii) serious breaches of peremptory norms; (iii) plurality of responsible States; (iv) interest; (v) contribution to the injury. (i) Barcelona Traction ¶33; Arts. 42, 48. (ii) Arts. 40–41; non-recognition; Iraq/Kuwait. (iii) Art. 47; Art. 19; recourse. (iv) Art. 38; 4%/6% splits. (v) Art. 39; Gabčíkovo .",
