          "question": "Answer 3 · \"Write an essay on the Freedom of the Open Sea\" (question bank, Q. 9(1) — 20 marks) I. Introduction. The freedom of the seas is the oldest principle of international law and the one that most directly shaped the modern world. Its classical statement is Grotius's Mare Liberum (1609), written for the Dutch East India Company: the sea, like the air, is incapable of occupation and is therefore res communis — accessible to all nations and belonging to none. Selden's Mare Clausum defended the contrary claims of the English crown, and Bynkershoek, writing a century later, gave the principle its practical settlement: the land's sovereignty ends where the range of its cannon ends, which is why the coastal belt came to be measured in miles while the rest of the ocean remained free. The modern law is the product of that compromise: the high seas are free, the belt is sovereign, and the boundary between them has been pushed outwards steadily for four centuries. II. The high seas defined. Article 86 of UNCLOS defines the high seas as all parts of the sea that are not included in the exclusive economic zone, in the territorial sea or in the internal waters of a State, or in the archipelagic waters of an archipelagic State. The definition is negative and its effect is to make the high seas the residue of the ocean: what is not zoned is free. The high seas are open to all States, whether coastal or land-locked, and no State may validly purport to subject any part of them to its sovereignty (Art. 89). The high seas are reserved for peaceful purposes (Art. 88). III. The freedoms. Article 87(1) lists the freedoms of the high seas: navigation; overflight; the laying of submarine cables and pipelines; the construction of artificial islands and other installations permitted by international law; fishing; and scientific research. The list is not exhaustive — it is followed by the words \"and other freedoms recognised by international law\" — but every freedom must be exercised with due regard to the interests of other States (Art. 87(2)), and the freedoms are qualified by the provisions of the Convention on fisheries (Arts 116–119), on the environment (Part XII), on marine scientific research (Part XIII) and on the Area (Part XI). The 1958 High Seas Convention contained only four freedoms (navigation, fishing, cables and pipelines, overflight) and provided that they were to be exercised with reasonable regard to the interests of other States (Art. 2). IV. The legal order of the high seas. Freedom is not anarchy, and the high seas are governed by five doctrines which the Convention expresses: Nationality of ships and the genuine link — ships have the nationality of the State whose flag they are entitled to fly (Art. 91), and there must exist a \"genuine link\" between the State and the ship; but the genuineness of the link does not affect the ship's nationality for the purpose of the recognition of flag-State rights (the ITLOS judgment in the Saiga (No. 2) case, 1999). The exclusive jurisdiction of the flag State — a ship on the high seas is subject to the exclusive jurisdiction of its flag State (Art. 92), and every State must effectively exercise its jurisdiction and control over ships flying its flag in administrative, technical and social matters (Art. 94). The classic formulation of the flag-State principle is the Lotus case (1927), where the Permanent Court held that a State may not exercise jurisdiction over a foreign vessel on the high seas; the exception recognised in Lotus for collisions — the flag State and the State of the offender — is now codified in Article 97 for collisions on the high seas. The exceptions: piracy, slave trade, unauthorised broadcasting, drugs, the right of visit — the freedom of the seas yields to the common interest in suppressing piracy (Arts 100–107: every State may seize a pirate ship on the high seas and try the pirates in its own courts, whatever the flag), the slave trade (Art. 99), unauthorised broadcasting (Art. 109), and the traffic in narcotic drugs (Art. 108). Article 110 confers the right of visit — the boarding of a foreign ship suspected of piracy, slave trading or unauthorised broadcasting, or of being without nationality — which is the most important exception to flag-State exclusivity, and Article 111 provides for hot pursuit. Warship immunity — warships and government ships operated for non-commercial purposes have complete immunity on the high seas (Arts 95–96), which is why an ITLOS order — in ARA Libertad (2012) — could require the release of a naval training ship detained at the instance of a domestic court. Duties of the flag State — the freedom carries the duty to protect the marine environment (Arts 192, 194, 209), to conserve living resources (Arts 116–119, including the duty to cooperate in regional fisheries organisations), to render assistance to persons in distress (Art. 98), and to protect cables and pipelines (Art. 113). V. The erosion of the freedom. The essay should confront the criticism that freedom of the high seas has been hollowed out. The movement of the outer limit from 3 to 12 to 24 to 200 nautical miles; the recognition of the exclusive economic zone, which makes two-thirds of the ocean's productive capacity subject to coastal-State rights; the régime of the Area, which removes the deep seabed from the operation of the freedom altogether; the proliferation of international fisheries regulation and of regional fisheries management organisations; the BBNJ Agreement of 2023, which for the first time authorises the establishment of marine protected areas on the high seas with binding effect; the climate litigation in the ITLOS advisory opinion of 2024; and the increasing regulation of shipping by the International Maritime Organization — all qualify the classical freedom. Two rival readings are possible: that the freedom has been transformed from a right of liberty into a régime of regulated use, or that it has been refined so that each user must respect the interests of the others — freedom with due regard, and not freedom with impunity. VI. Conclusion. The freedom of the open sea remains the foundational principle of the law of the sea: it is the reason no State has been able to claim sovereignty over the ocean, the reason the flag State is the primary guardian of the law of the sea, and the reason the Convention could be drafted as a single package covering the ocean as a whole. What has changed is its content. The freedom of the present century is a freedom of international cooperation — restrained by the common interest in resources, environment and security — a freedom whose limits are the limits set by the common heritage principle, the environmental duty and the rights of other users. That transformation is precisely the history of the law of the sea. Detail: §5.1.5 (part 07), Arts 86–120, Tables 5.12, Fig. 5.6; case 31 for Corfu ; §5.1.1 for the belt.",
