          "question": "Answer 4 · \"Discuss the concept of the Continental Shelf with reference to recent developments\" (question bank, Q. 9(2) — 20 marks) I. The physical and legal idea. The continental shelf is the natural seaward extension of the land: geologically it consists of the shelf proper (a gentle slope), the continental slope, and the continental rise, together forming the continental margin, and it is rich in oil, gas and minerals. The legal concept comes from the Truman Proclamation of 28 September 1945, in which the United States claimed the resources of the shelf appurtenant to its coasts on the ground that it was a natural prolongation of the land. The 1958 Geneva Convention on the Continental Shelf adopted both the natural prolongation idea and the exploitability criterion : the shelf extended to a depth of 200 metres or, beyond that limit, to where the depth of the superjacent waters admitted the exploitation of the natural resources (Art. 1). The criterion was indeterminate, and it was replaced by Article 76 of UNCLOS. II. The modern definition — Article 76. Article 76(1) defines the continental shelf as comprising the seabed and subsoil of the submarine areas that extend beyond the territorial sea throughout the natural prolongation of the land territory to the outer edge of the continental margin, or to a distance of 200 nautical miles from the baselines where the outer edge does not extend that far. The outer edge of the margin beyond 200 nm is measured by the two tests in Article 76(4): the thickness of sedimentary rocks (a point where the distance from the foot of the continental slope to the point where the thickness of sedimentary rocks is at least 1 per cent of the shortest distance to the foot of the slope — the Gardiner formula), or a distance from the foot of the slope not exceeding 60 nautical miles. Those limits are then capped by Article 76(5)–(6): 350 nautical miles from the baseline, or 100 nautical miles beyond the 2,500-metre isobath, and, on submarine ridges, 350 nm. The coastal State establishes the outer limits on the basis of the recommendations of the Commission on the Limits of the Continental Shelf (Art. 76(8); Annex II) — the Commission being the scientific body of 21 elected members to which submissions are made. III. The rights of the coastal State. Article 77 vests in the coastal State sovereign rights for the purpose of exploring the shelf and exploiting its natural resources — mineral and other non-living resources of the seabed and subsoil, together with living organisms belonging to sedentary species — and provides that those rights are exclusive in the sense that, if the coastal State does not explore or exploit the shelf, no one else may do so without its express consent. The rights are ipso facto and ab initio , and do not depend upon occupation or proclamation (Art. 77(3)) — the holding of the Court in the North Sea Continental Shelf cases, from which the doctrine derives. Articles 78–81 complete the régime: the legal status of the superjacent waters and airspace is unaffected; the coastal State may not interfere unjustifiably with navigation, cables, pipelines or the other rights of third States; it may construct and operate installations on the shelf and may establish 500-metre safety zones around them; it may authorise drilling for all purposes (Art. 81); it may lay cables and pipelines (Art. 79); and Article 82 requires payments or contributions to the International Seabed Authority for the exploitation of the shelf beyond 200 nm — the only provision that carries the benefit-sharing idea of the common heritage into areas of national jurisdiction. IV. Delimitation between States. Delimitation is governed by Article 83, which requires an agreement \"in order to achieve an equitable solution\" and, failing agreement, recourse to the Part XV procedures — the standard which the North Sea cases (case 32) and Tunisia/Libya (case 33) created. The modern method is the three-stage approach discussed at §5.2: a provisional equidistance line, adjusted for relevant circumstances, and then tested for gross disproportion; and the same three stages apply to the shelf beyond 200 nm ( Bangladesh/Myanmar 2012, the Bay of Bengal award 2014, Somalia v. Kenya 2021). V. \"Recent developments\". Four developments deserve emphasis. The CLCS and the handling of disputes. The Commission's workload (over eighty submissions) has produced three important practical rules: the ten-year period (decided by the Meeting of States Parties in 2001, which allowed States whose submissions were due by 2009 an extended period), the rule that where a land or maritime dispute exists the Commission makes no recommendation on the disputed portion absent the parties' consent, and the practice of \" partial \" or amended submissions — as in India's revised Central Arabian Sea submission of 2025, which followed Pakistan's objection to the 2009 submission. The delimitation of the shelf beyond 200 nm. Bangladesh/Myanmar (ITLOS 2012) and the Bay of Bengal arbitration (2014) established that a court or tribunal may delimit the outer shelf before the CLCS has acted, and the concept of the grey area — where the shelf of one State lies under the EEZ of another — emerged from the 2014 award. Somalia v. Kenya (2021) extended the same line beyond 200 nm. These decisions have given Article 76 a jurisprudential substance which the 1958 Convention never had. The relationship with the EEZ. Because coastal States now claim an EEZ of 200 nm, and because the shelf beyond 200 nm corresponds to areas of deeper water and lesser present value, the two régimes have converged. The concept of the \" single maritime boundary \" — one line delimiting the seabed and the water column together — has made the distinction between shelf and EEZ less important in practice than it was in 1969, though it remains decisive in the grey area and in the CLCS procedure. The Area and the common heritage. The outer limit of the shelf is also the boundary of the common heritage of mankind: everything beyond it is the Area, administered by the Seabed Authority under Part XI (§§5.3–5.4). The extended shelf claim is thus a claim to remove territory from the common heritage. VI. Conclusion. The continental shelf has moved from a physical description (the 200-metre line) to a legal entitlement (natural prolongation to the outer edge of the margin) to a regime of scientific delimitation conducted through the CLCS; and in the process it has generated the most sophisticated body of delimitation law in international law — the three-stage method, the relevant circumstances and the disproportionality test, all of which were built on the shelf cases. Detail: §5.1.3 (part 05), Art. 76 in full, Tables 5.10–5.11, Fig. 5.5; cases 32, 33, 35 and 36.",
