          "question": "Answer 7 · \"State the facts and the decision in: Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries, Corfu Channel, North Sea Continental Shelf\" (question bank, Q. 21) Table 5.30 · The three cases in the compressed form in which they should be recalled Case Facts in three lines Decision in three lines The principle Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries (UK v. Norway), ICJ Rep. 1951, p. 116 Norway's coast is deeply indented and fringed by the skjærgaard; by a decree of 16 July 1935 it delimited its fisheries zone by straight baselines drawn through 48 fixed points on the mainland, islands and rocks; the United Kingdom challenged both the system of straight baselines and the ten-mile rule for bays. The Court upheld the decree: the straight baseline method was lawful for this coast; the lines did not depart appreciably from the general direction of the coast and the enclosed waters were sufficiently linked to the land; the ten-mile rule was not binding on Norway, which had consistently opposed it; the validity of the delimitation depended upon international law and not on the consent of other States. Where the coast is deeply indented or fringed by islands, straight baselines may be drawn — the origin of Article 7 of UNCLOS (with Article 8(2) on historic passage rights). Corfu Channel (UK v. Albania), ICJ Rep. 1949, p. 4 British warships passed through the North Corfu Channel in May and October 1946; on 22 October two destroyers struck mines, with loss of life; Albania had not warned of the mines; the United Kingdom then swept the channel without Albania's consent (Operation Retail). Albania was responsible for the explosions on 22 October (knowledge of the minefield and failure to warn) and for firing upon the ships; the Channel was a strait used for international navigation; the British passage of the warships was not unlawful; Operation Retail was unlawful as a violation of Albanian sovereignty, and the Court's declaration of illegality constituted satisfaction; compensation of £843,947 was awarded. Warships may pass through international straits in peacetime; the coastal State may not prohibit such passage; a court's declaratory judgment may itself be satisfaction for a breach; the case is also the foundation of the modern rules on straits and on the duty to warn of dangers. North Sea Continental Shelf (Germany v. Denmark; Germany v. Netherlands), ICJ Rep. 1969, p. 3 A dispute over the delimitation of the North Sea shelf; Denmark and the Netherlands relied on the equidistance rule of Article 6 of the 1958 Geneva Convention; Germany, which had not ratified the Convention, argued that the rule was not customary and that its concave coast would be cut off by equidistance. Article 6 was not opposable to Germany; the equidistance rule was not customary international law (it lacked a norm-creating character, it was subject to a reservation clause in Article 12 of the Convention, State practice was not uniform, and there was no opinio juris ); delimitation must be by agreement on equitable principles, taking account of the coastal geography, and in this configuration produced lines which the parties later implemented by agreement in 1971. Delimitation of the continental shelf is to be effected by agreement in accordance with equitable principles, taking account of all relevant circumstances — the foundation of Articles 74(1) and 83(1) and of the three-stage method. Full seven-part digests: case 30, case 31, case 32.",
          "answer": "Table 5.30 · The three cases in the compressed form in which they should be recalled Case Facts in three lines Decision in three lines The principle Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries (UK v. Norway), ICJ Rep. 1951, p. 116 Norway's coast is deeply indented and fringed by the skjærgaard; by a decree of 16 July 1935 it delimited its fisheries zone by straight baselines drawn through 48 fixed points on the mainland, islands and rocks; the United Kingdom challenged both the system of straight baselines and the ten-mile rule for bays. The Court upheld the decree: the straight baseline method was lawful for this coast; the lines did not depart appreciably from the general direction of the coast and the enclosed waters were sufficiently linked to the land; the ten-mile rule was not binding on Norway, which had consistently opposed it; the validity of the delimitation depended upon international law and not on the consent of other States. Where the coast is deeply indented or fringed by islands, straight baselines may be drawn — the origin of Article 7 of UNCLOS (with Article 8(2) on historic passage rights). Corfu Channel (UK v. Albania), ICJ Rep. 1949, p. 4 British warships passed through the North Corfu Channel in May and October 1946; on 22 October two destroyers struck mines, with loss of life; Albania had not warned of the mines; the United Kingdom then swept the channel without Albania's consent (Operation Retail). Albania was responsible for the explosions on 22 October (knowledge of the minefield and failure to warn) and for firing upon the ships; the Channel was a strait used for international navigation; the British passage of the warships was not unlawful; Operation Retail was unlawful as a violation of Albanian sovereignty, and the Court's declaration of illegality constituted satisfaction; compensation of £843,947 was awarded. Warships may pass through international straits in peacetime; the coastal State may not prohibit such passage; a court's declaratory judgment may itself be satisfaction for a breach; the case is also the foundation of the modern rules on straits and on the duty to warn of dangers. North Sea Continental Shelf (Germany v. Denmark; Germany v. Netherlands), ICJ Rep. 1969, p. 3 A dispute over the delimitation of the North Sea shelf; Denmark and the Netherlands relied on the equidistance rule of Article 6 of the 1958 Geneva Convention; Germany, which had not ratified the Convention, argued that the rule was not customary and that its concave coast would be cut off by equidistance. Article 6 was not opposable to Germany; the equidistance rule was not customary international law (it lacked a norm-creating character, it was subject to a reservation clause in Article 12 of the Convention, State practice was not uniform, and there was no opinio juris ); delimitation must be by agreement on equitable principles, taking account of the coastal geography, and in this configuration produced lines which the parties later implemented by agreement in 1971. Delimitation of the continental shelf is to be effected by agreement in accordance with equitable principles, taking account of all relevant circumstances — the foundation of Articles 74(1) and 83(1) and of the three-stage method. Full seven-part digests: case 30, case 31, case 32.",
