          "question": "5. The first question — does Article 36 apply at all? (the three arguments rejected) (a) No espionage exception in the text. \"Neither Article 36 nor any other provision of the Vienna Convention contains a reference to cases of espionage.\" Reading Article 36 in its context and in the light of the object and purpose of the Convention — whose preamble speaks of contributing \"to the development of friendly relations among nations\" and whose Article 36(1) opens with the purpose of \"facilitating the exercise of consular functions relating to nationals of the sending State\" — the Court held that consular officers may in all cases exercise the rights of access, and that \"it would run counter to the purpose of that provision if the rights it provides could be disregarded when the receiving State alleges that a foreign national in its custody was involved in acts of espionage\". (b) The travaux préparatoires confirm the reading. The Court examined the ILC discussions of 1960 on \"consular intercourse and immunities\" and the records of the Vienna Conference of 1963; they confirmed that Article 36 does not exclude classes of persons such as those suspected of espionage. The single point for which Pakistan found support in the travaux — the proposal of the ILC's Special Rapporteur that notification might be deferred — went no further than to allow a short delay, and was not adopted as an exception. (This is the point which students should keep: the negotiation history supports \"delay\", not \"denial\".) (c) Custom cannot supply an exception where the treaty speaks. Article 36 \"expressly regulates the question of consular access to, and communication with, nationals of the sending State and makes no exception with regard to cases of espionage\", so that Article 36, and not customary international law, governed the matter in the relations between the parties. Having so held, the Court found it unnecessary to determine whether the customary rule alleged by Pakistan had existed in 1963. (d) The 2008 Agreement does not displace Article 36. Point (vi) of the India–Pakistan Agreement on Consular Access of 21 May 2008 provides: \"In case of arrest, detention or sentence made on political or security grounds, each side may examine the case on its merits.\" The Court held that point (vi) \"cannot be read as denying consular access\" in such cases: had the parties intended to restrict the rights guaranteed by Article 36, \"one would expect such an intention to be unequivocally reflected in the provisions of the Agreement\"; and any derogation from Article 36 for political or security reasons \"may render the right related to consular access meaningless as it would give the receiving State the possibility of denying such access\". Turning to Article 73(2) of the Convention — which preserves the freedom of States to conclude agreements \"confirming or supplementing or extending or amplifying\" its provisions — the Court held that the paragraph contemplates only agreements of that character, that the 2008 Agreement had been negotiated by the parties in full awareness of Article 73(2), and that it was a subsequent agreement intended to \"confirm, supplement, extend or amplify\" the Convention. Consequently point (vi) does not displace the obligations under Article 36. The conclusion of the whole section: \"the Vienna Convention is applicable in the present case, regardless of the allegations that Mr. Jadhav was engaged in espionage activities.\"",
          "answer": "(a) No espionage exception in the text. \"Neither Article 36 nor any other provision of the Vienna Convention contains a reference to cases of espionage.\" Reading Article 36 in its context and in the light of the object and purpose of the Convention — whose preamble speaks of contributing \"to the development of friendly relations among nations\" and whose Article 36(1) opens with the purpose of \"facilitating the exercise of consular functions relating to nationals of the sending State\" — the Court held that consular officers may in all cases exercise the rights of access, and that \"it would run counter to the purpose of that provision if the rights it provides could be disregarded when the receiving State alleges that a foreign national in its custody was involved in acts of espionage\". (b) The travaux préparatoires confirm the reading. The Court examined the ILC discussions of 1960 on \"consular intercourse and immunities\" and the records of the Vienna Conference of 1963; they confirmed that Article 36 does not exclude classes of persons such as those suspected of espionage. The single point for which Pakistan found support in the travaux — the proposal of the ILC's Special Rapporteur that notification might be deferred — went no further than to allow a short delay, and was not adopted as an exception. (This is the point which students should keep: the negotiation history supports \"delay\", not \"denial\".) (c) Custom cannot supply an exception where the treaty speaks. Article 36 \"expressly regulates the question of consular access to, and communication with, nationals of the sending State and makes no exception with regard to cases of espionage\", so that Article 36, and not customary international law, governed the matter in the relations between the parties. Having so held, the Court found it unnecessary to determine whether the customary rule alleged by Pakistan had existed in 1963. (d) The 2008 Agreement does not displace Article 36. Point (vi) of the India–Pakistan Agreement on Consular Access of 21 May 2008 provides: \"In case of arrest, detention or sentence made on political or security grounds, each side may examine the case on its merits.\" The Court held that point (vi) \"cannot be read as denying consular access\" in such cases: had the parties intended to restrict the rights guaranteed by Article 36, \"one would expect such an intention to be unequivocally reflected in the provisions of the Agreement\"; and any derogation from Article 36 for political or security reasons \"may render the right related to consular access meaningless as it would give the receiving State the possibility of denying such access\". Turning to Article 73(2) of the Convention — which preserves the freedom of States to conclude agreements \"confirming or supplementing or extending or amplifying\" its provisions — the Court held that the paragraph contemplates only agreements of that character, that the 2008 Agreement had been negotiated by the parties in full awareness of Article 73(2), and that it was a subsequent agreement intended to \"confirm, supplement, extend or amplify\" the Convention. Consequently point (vi) does not displace the obligations under Article 36. The conclusion of the whole section: \"the Vienna Convention is applicable in the present case, regardless of the allegations that Mr. Jadhav was engaged in espionage activities.\"",
