          "unitNumber": 7,
          "question": "6. The second question — what Article 36 required, and what was breached Table 7.16 — The three alleged breaches, as found by the Court Obligation under Article 36(1) What the Court found Result (b), first limb — to inform the detained national of his rights Pakistan never claimed to have informed Mr. Jadhav of his Article 36 rights; on the contrary, it maintained throughout that the Convention did not apply to a person suspected of espionage. The Court inferred from that position that the information had not been given. Breach of Article 36(1)(b) (operative point 3: 15-1). (b), second limb — to inform the consular post of the sending State \"without delay\", if the national so requests Notification was in fact given on 25 March 2016 (and enabled India to request access the same day). But there were \"sufficient grounds at the time of the arrest on 3 March 2016 or shortly thereafter for Pakistan to conclude that the person was, or was likely to be, an Indian national\"; the delay of some three weeks was not \"without delay\". The Court held that \"without delay\" is not to be read as \"immediately upon arrest and before interrogation\", but also that the circumstances here made three weeks excessive. Breach of Article 36(1)(b) — the notification limb (operative point 4: 15-1). (a) and (c) — the right of consular officers to visit, converse and correspond with the national, and to arrange his legal representation No consular access was ever granted. Pakistan's condition — that access would be considered in the light of India's co-operation in the investigation — was no answer: India's alleged failure to co-operate \"does not relieve Pakistan of its obligation to grant consular access\". Nor did the appointment of a defending officer for Mr. Jadhav dispense with the consular officers' right to arrange legal representation, which presupposes conversation and correspondence with the detained person. Breach of Article 36(1)(a) and (c) (operative point 5: 15-1). Three structural points from this section should be memorised. First, the Court treated Article 36(1) as creating individual rights — of the detained person — which, by Article I of the Optional Protocol, may be invoked before the Court by the national State (the line of reasoning begun in LaGrand and Avena ). Secondly, the duty to notify the consular post is implied by Article 36(1)(c) as well as by (b): the rights of consular officers to visit and to arrange representation presuppose that they know of the detention. Thirdly, the duty is not conditional on reciprocity or on the sending State's conduct : a receiving State cannot make consular access a bargaining counter in an investigation.",
          "answer": "Table 7.16 — The three alleged breaches, as found by the Court Obligation under Article 36(1) What the Court found Result (b), first limb — to inform the detained national of his rights Pakistan never claimed to have informed Mr. Jadhav of his Article 36 rights; on the contrary, it maintained throughout that the Convention did not apply to a person suspected of espionage. The Court inferred from that position that the information had not been given. Breach of Article 36(1)(b) (operative point 3: 15-1). (b), second limb — to inform the consular post of the sending State \"without delay\", if the national so requests Notification was in fact given on 25 March 2016 (and enabled India to request access the same day). But there were \"sufficient grounds at the time of the arrest on 3 March 2016 or shortly thereafter for Pakistan to conclude that the person was, or was likely to be, an Indian national\"; the delay of some three weeks was not \"without delay\". The Court held that \"without delay\" is not to be read as \"immediately upon arrest and before interrogation\", but also that the circumstances here made three weeks excessive. Breach of Article 36(1)(b) — the notification limb (operative point 4: 15-1). (a) and (c) — the right of consular officers to visit, converse and correspond with the national, and to arrange his legal representation No consular access was ever granted. Pakistan's condition — that access would be considered in the light of India's co-operation in the investigation — was no answer: India's alleged failure to co-operate \"does not relieve Pakistan of its obligation to grant consular access\". Nor did the appointment of a defending officer for Mr. Jadhav dispense with the consular officers' right to arrange legal representation, which presupposes conversation and correspondence with the detained person. Breach of Article 36(1)(a) and (c) (operative point 5: 15-1). Three structural points from this section should be memorised. First, the Court treated Article 36(1) as creating individual rights — of the detained person — which, by Article I of the Optional Protocol, may be invoked before the Court by the national State (the line of reasoning begun in LaGrand and Avena ). Secondly, the duty to notify the consular post is implied by Article 36(1)(c) as well as by (b): the rights of consular officers to visit and to arrange representation presuppose that they know of the detention. Thirdly, the duty is not conditional on reciprocity or on the sending State's conduct : a receiving State cannot make consular access a bargaining counter in an investigation.",
