            "MAP How to Use These Notes This document reconstructs Topic 1 of the LB-205 (Public International Law) syllabus, Faculty of Law, University of Delhi — the paper whose prescribed reading list opens with the words \"Topic 1: Nature and Development of International Law (10 hours)\" , followed by sub-topics 1.1 to 1.6 and the two prescribed cases Reparation for Injuries Suffered in the Service of the United Nations (ICJ Rep. 1949, p. 174) and Application of ICSFT and CERD (Ukraine v. Russian Federation) (ICJ Rep. 2019). Method of Construction Every proposition below is anchored to one of the following, in descending order of priority — exactly the hierarchy DU examiners mark on: Tier 1 — DU Statutory Material: the LB-205 Case Material, LL.B. IInd Term, Faculty of Law, DU (May 2022) — the \"Cases Selected and Edited By\" volume, and the syllabus printed in it. Tier 2 — Prescribed Books: S.K. Verma, An Introduction to Public International Law (2nd ed., Satyam Law International) — the DU-prescribed text; S.K. Kapoor, International Law and Human Rights ; Malcolm N. Shaw; Brownlie/Crawford; Gurdip Singh; V.K. Ahuja; Harris & Sivakumaran Cases & Materials . Tier 3 — DU classroom material in the project folder: PIL Notes — Dr. Stanzin Chostak (DU Faculty of Law) ; Exam Capsule (LED) — DU LLB semester exam material ; Public International Law — Smart Notes . Tier 4 — Primary instruments: UN Charter (full text), ICJ Statute, VCDR 1961, VCCR 1963, ILC Draft Articles on State Responsibility 2001 (all in the project folder). Tier 5 — Juristic classics in the project folder: Oppenheim (1908), Scott (1905 & 1907), Sherman (1921), Goodhart (1936), MacKenzie (1939), Hurst (1944). Tier 6 — Recorded DU question papers (1992–2025) used only for the PYQ section. Syllabus map at a glance Table 0.1 — The six sub-topics of Topic 1 and where each is answered below Sub-topic Prescribed wording Core legal material it needs 1.1 Definition of International Law Oppenheim 1905 → 8th/9th ed.; Austin/Holland/Bentham critique; Starke, Brierly, Hart, Fitzmaurice; natural law & positivism; Kelsen's coercive order 1.2 Development — need for international peace and security, establishment of the UN, development of different generations of human rights UN Charter Arts. 1, 2(4), 33, 39–42, 51, 55, 68; UDHR 1948; ICCPR & ICESCR 1966; three generations of rights 1.3 Codification: work of the International Law Commission Charter Art. 13(1)(a); GA Res. 174(II) 1947; ILC Statute Arts. 1, 2, 8, 15–24; outputs 1.4 Sanctions — Security Council, General Assembly, ICJ, Human Rights Council, ITLOS, ICC, PCA, sanctions under human rights treaties Charter Ch. VI, VII, Arts. 5, 6, 13, 94; GA Res. 377(V); ICJ Statute Arts. 36, 59, 94; Rome Statute; UNCLOS Part XV; ILC ARSIWA Arts. 22, 49–54 1.5 Subjects — States, International Organisations, Individuals Reparation (1949); Ukraine v. Russia (2019); VCCR Art. 36; Barcelona Traction ; Nuremberg 1.6 Third World and International Law Decolonisation; GA Res. 1514(XV), 1803(XVII), 3201(S-VI), 3281(XXIX); UNCLOS Part XI; TWAIL literature Fig. 0.1 — Concept map of Topic 1 and the two prescribed authorities 1.1 Definition of International Law Every DU answer on Topic 1 should open by conceding a methodological point: there is no single definition of international law , because the discipline has changed faster than its definitions. As the DU course material puts it, \"the impact of the 'context' and the social milieu on the definition and development of international law\" is what matters. 1 The definitional history runs from a State-only, European, war-centred law, to a universal, welfare-oriented, organisation-and-individual-inclusive law. A. Oppenheim's Classical Definition (1905) \"Law of Nations or International Law is the name for the body of customary and conventional rules which are considered legally binding by civilised states in their intercourse with each other .\" L. Oppenheim, International Law: A Treatise , Vol. I (1st ed., 1905), p. 1 — the definition printed in the DU classroom material. Oppenheim and his successors built the definition on four load-bearing elements, each of which later came under attack: Table 1.1 — Anatomy of Oppenheim's definition and what each element excludes Element What Oppenheim meant What modern law says now \"body of rules\" Rules, not standards, principles or policies; a static corpus. Law is dynamic — \"continuously reinterpreted and reshaped in the very process of its application\"; general principles (Art. 38(1)(c) ICJ Statute) and soft law add fluids. \"customary and conventional\" Only two sources: custom + treaties. Art. 38(1) ICJ Statute adds general principles, judicial decisions and publicists; SC resolutions (Art. 25), GA resolutions, unilateral acts, jus cogens . \"civilised states\" A Euro-Christian \"standard of civilisation\"; China's 5,000-year culture did not qualify. Deleted from the 8th edition onwards; the \"standard of civilisation\" is now treated as the ideological mask of colonialism (see §1.6 ). \"states in their intercourse with each other\" States are the only subjects; individuals are objects. International organisations, individuals, peoples, insurgents, ICRC, Holy See, multinationals hold some personality — the determinative test is functional (see §1.5 ). B. The Five-fold Criticism of Oppenheim (the standard DU answer) The DU-syllabus material identifies five defects — \"every important element in it can now be challenged.\" International organisations are omitted. \"It is now generally recognised that, not only 'states' but public international organisations, have rights and duties under International Law, even though they may not have all the rights and duties that states have.\" In fact \"the future of International law is one with the future of international organisation.\" Authority: Reparation for Injuries (1949) — the UN is an international person; Effect of Awards of the UN Administrative Tribunal (1954). \"Civilised states\" is a discriminatory phrase. The criterion of \"civilisation\" was neither long history nor culture — it was Christian-European identity. Hence the 8th edition re-wrote the definition: \"Law of Nations or International Law is the name for the body of customary and treaty rules which are considered legally binding by States in their intercourse with each other.\" Individuals and private persons are omitted. \"More controversial but no longer untenable is the view that even individuals and other private persons may have some such rights and duties.\" Authority: UDHR 1948; ICCPR and its First Optional Protocol (individual petitions to the Human Rights Committee); European Convention on Human Rights 1950, Art. 34 (individual applications to the European Court). Wilfred Jenks' famous formulation: contemporary international law \"must be regarded as the common law of mankind in an early stage of development.\" Sources are understated. International law consists \"not only of customary and conventional rules but also of 'General Principles of Law'\" — Art. 38(1)(c) ICJ Statute. \"Body of rules\" is static. The conception of international law as a fixed body of rules \"now stands changed as static and inadequate.\" Law must be \"flexible, adaptable and changeable.\" Examiner's favourite quote Sir Henry Maine: \"The greatest function of the 'law of nature' was discharged in giving birth to modern International Law.\" Jenks: \"Contemporary International Law … must be regarded as the common law of mankind in an early stage of development.\" Fenwick (quoted in DU material): international law is \"the body of general principles and specific rules which are binding upon the members of the international community in their mutual relations.\" C. Modern Definitions — a Comparative Table Table 1.2 — Definitions of international law across the classical–modern spectrum Author / Text Definition Diagnostic value Oppenheim (1905) Body of customary and conventional rules considered legally binding by civilised states in their intercourse with each other. Classical, State-centric, European. Oppenheim (8th ed.) Body of customary and treaty rules considered legally binding by States in their intercourse with each other. \"Civilised\" dropped; source base widened. Oppenheim (9th ed.) \"International law is the body of rules which are legally binding on States in their intercourse with each other. These rules are primarily those which govern the relations of States, but States are not the only subjects of international law. International organisations and, to some extent, also individuals may be subjects of rights conferred and duties imposed by international law.\" Best single modern definition; note \"primarily\" and \"to some extent\" — hierarchy among subjects survives. J.L. Brierly The body of rules and principles of action which are binding upon civilised States in their relations with one another (later editions: \"the law of nations … is the body of binding rules which the general body of States accepts as governing their mutual relations\"). Stresses acceptance rather than command. C. Wilfred Jenks \"Common law of mankind in an early stage of development.\" Functional, inclusive, teleological. Kelsen The totality of legal norms regulating the mutual behaviour of States (primacy of international law; individual is the ultimate addressee). Presupposes a basic norm (Grundnorm) ; the sanction is the essence of law. S.K. Verma (DU text) Public international law is the body of legal rules governing the conduct of States and other subjects of international law in their relations inter se , rooted in the community of States. Preferred formulation for India-specific answers. Gurdip Singh (DU reading list) International law as \"the body of rules which are legally binding on States …\" with legal personality attaching to entities endowed with rights, duties and capacity to take action on the international plane. Links definition to personality. Answer skeleton — \"Define international law. How far is the traditional definition still valid?\" Oppenheim 1905 verbatim + the four elements. Five criticisms, each with an authority and, where available, a case. Modern definition (Oppenheim 9th ed. / Jenks / Verma); one line on Fenwick and Brierly. Conclude: definition must be functional, not formal — international law today is the law of an international community , not merely of \"civilised States\"; but the State remains the \"primary\" subject, so the traditional definition is incomplete rather than wholly wrong. D. Public International Law Distinguished from Private International Law Table 1.3 — Public vs. Private International Law (a recurring 2-mark question) Basis Public International Law Private International Law (Conflict of Laws) Parties States, international organisations, and (partially) individuals. Private individuals and corporations. Source Treaties, custom, general principles (Art. 38, ICJ Statute). Primarily the municipal law of each State. Subject-matter Rights and duties of States inter se : war, peace, territory, diplomacy, human rights. Which law/forum applies to a private dispute with a foreign element (jurisdiction, choice of law, enforcement of foreign judgments). Forum ICJ, ITLOS, ICC, arbitral tribunals. Municipal courts (e.g., Indian courts applying lex loci , lex situs ). Sanction Weak, decentralised — Chapter VII, countermeasures, reciprocity. Strong: a municipal decree enforceable by the State's coercive apparatus. Nature of law One system for all States — uniform . Varies with each State — not uniform . Also called The Law of Nations; droit des gens (Fr.); Völkerrecht (Ger.). Conflict of Laws. Link between the two: the two systems touch at the point of jurisdiction . When an Indian court refuses to enforce a foreign confiscatory law, or applies the doctrine of act of State , it is in truth applying the public policy of international law through private international law. S.K. Verma's Chapter 1, §A treats this at pp. 4–5. E. Is International Law \"True Law\"? — the Austinian Challenge 1. The denial: Austin, Hobbes, Pufendorf, Holland, Bentham John Austin's command theory holds that law properly so-called has two necessary features: (i) it is a command of a determinate sovereign , and (ii) it is enforced by a sanction . Because international law lacks both a world legislature and a coercive enforcement agency, Austin classed it as \" positive international morality \", analogous to \"rules binding a club or society.\" Hobbes (man is \"nasty, brutish and violent\") and Pufendorf agreed; Holland called it \"the vanishing point of jurisprudence\"; Bentham too denied it the name of law. Their six supporting arguments (as listed in the DU material): No determinate superior political authority exists in international law; No effective legislative machinery comparable to a legislature; Sanction is an essential element of law, and international law lacks it; No executive power enforces ICJ decisions or treaty obligations; No potent judiciary with compulsory jurisdiction; Hence some writers call it \"quasi-law.\" 2. The defence: Starke's four arguments and the modern consensus Professor Starke's four main arguments (printed in the DU material): Formal legislative authority is not essential to law. Many primitive communities had law without a legislature — Sir Henry Maine's historical school established this by research. International legislation exists. Law-making treaties and conventions — the Hague and Geneva Conventions, the UN Charter, the Vienna Conventions — are binding although they do not emanate from a sovereign. Authoritative agencies treat it as law. States themselves plead treaties, precedent and jurists, not morality; \"there is no such thing as an International Lawlessness.\" The United Nations and the ICJ are based on the true legality of international law : Art. 38 ICJ Statute directs the Court to decide \"in accordance with international law\"; Art. 59 makes decisions binding; Art. 94 makes them enforceable. Additional modern arguments: customary rules are treated as law (English common law itself is customary); Paquete Habana (1900) — \"International law is a part of our law, and must be ascertained and administered by the courts of justice of appropriate jurisdiction\"; Hart — \"international law is law because States regard it as law. Nothing need be further proved\"; Fitzmaurice — enforceability exists \"even if only in a rough and rudimentary form\"; Kelsen — in a decentralised society enforcement occurs through self-help , and international law makes reprisals and the use of force lawful as countermeasures while unlawful otherwise; hence it is true law. The frequency-of-violation fallacy. Violations prove weakness of machinery, not absence of law — municipal law is violated daily too. Oppenheim conceded that \"while the frequency of the violations of international law may strain its legal force to breaking point, the formal (though often cynical) affirmation of its binding nature is not without significance.\" Brierly: international law is \"more honoured in the observance than in the breaches.\" \"The difference of origin and of the stages through which law and treaty pass is merged in the result… If the principle is binding at all — that is, if nations regard that a principle binds them — it is of no great moment whether the force is moral, ethical or physical. It seems … unscientific, if not positively absurd, to make the nature of a thing depend upon a mere form of punishment.\" James Brown Scott, \"The Legal Nature of International Law,\" 1905 — one of the classic essays supplied in your project folder. A pupil's caution — Zimmern's charge Sir Alfred Zimmern wrote that for anyone trained in the British tradition the term 'International Law' \"is never law as we understand it, and it often … comes dangerously near to being an imposture, a simulacrum of law, an attorney's mantle artfully displayed on the shoulders of arbitrary power.\" Goodhart (1936) and MacKenzie (1939) both devoted their Grotius/AJIL papers to answering this charge; MacKenzie's reply was that international law is the \"law of the international community\" and its function is conflict-resolution and value-allocation within that community. Cite this to show reading beyond the textbook. Fig. 1.1 — The \"true law\" debate as a decision-flow (also a ready-made answer structure) F. The Basis of International Law — Jurisprudential Theories \"Basis\" asks a different question from \"definition\": not what the rules are, but why are they binding — from where does international law derive its obligatory force? Two principal schools, each with sub-branches. I. Naturalist School Law of Nature Core: international law is part of the law of nature; States obey it because it is natural law applied to States. Origins: Greek Stoics → Roman jus naturale and jus gentium (the law applied to foreigners in Rome, as against jus civile ) → medieval divine law → 16th–17th century secularisation by Vittoria, Suarez, Gentilis → Grotius , who separated jus naturale from jus gentium and based the law of nations on nature, custom and treaties ; natural law became \"the dictate of right reason\". Sub-schools after Grotius: Naturalists (Pufendorf — natural law exclusively); Positivists (Bynkershoek — State consent/practice); Grotians or Eclectics (Wolff, Vattel — both). Vattel: \"We use the term 'necessary Law of Nations' for that law which results from applying the natural law to nations. It is necessary, because nations are absolutely bound to observe it.\" II. Positivist School & Consent Theory Will of the State Core: law is law as it is , not as it ought to be; the binding force comes from the consent / will of States , evidenced in custom and treaties. Exponents: Bynkershoek → Zorn (international law as a branch of State law) → Triepel (obligatory force stems from agreement between States — Vereinbarung ) → Anzilotti ( pacta sunt servanda as the fundamental norm) → Jellinek and the auto-limitation / self-limitation theory; the \"will of the State\" idea traces to Hegel. Auto-limitation: sovereign will is supreme; international law binds only to the extent the State has voluntarily restricted its own sovereignty. Oppenheim's own view: common consent of States is the basis. Weakness: easy for treaties, hard for custom (a new State is bound by pre-existing custom without having consented — see §1.6 ); and it cannot explain jus cogens or obligations erga omnes . Also, the Lotus presumption (restrictions on State independence are not presumed) is the purest judicial statement of positivism. The Doctrine of Fundamental Rights (a naturalist offshoot) Deduced from the \"essential nature\" of the State, every State — like man — possesses certain fundamental or natural rights: self-preservation, independence, equality, respect, and intercourse . Brierly called it \"merely the old doctrine of natural rights of man transferred to the State.\" It influenced the American Declaration of Independence and the classic rules on non-intervention. Criticism (for the answer): (i) it confuses the State with the individual; (ii) a legal right presupposes a legal system — rights cannot pre-exist the system that validates them; (iii) it denies the possibility of growth by positing a fixed \"order of nature\"; (iv) it is subjective and detached from State practice, hence its decline. But note its revival: human rights and international criminal law are the modern heirs of natural-law thinking. Other theories worth one paragraph each Table 1.4 — Supplementary theories of the basis of obligation Theory Chief exponent(s) Gist One-line critique Sociological / \"social solidarity\" Léon Duguit, Georges Scelle Law rests on social interdependence ( solidarité sociale ); the rule of law binds because society requires it. Underplays the State's formal role. Policy-oriented / New Haven McDougal, Lasswell, Reisman Law is a continuous process of authoritative decision serving community values; not a fixed \"body of rules\". Accused of dissolving law into politics. Pure theory of law Hans Kelsen Validity rests on a hierarchy of norms culminating in the Grundnorm ; law is a \"coercive order\". Formal; the Grundnorm is a presupposition, not a proof. Consent / auto-limitation Triepel, Anzilotti, Jellinek, Oppenheim Will of the State, express or tacit. Cannot explain custom binding on new States or jus cogens . Ubi societas ibi jus Brierly, Fitzmaurice, Verma \"As the society, so is the law\": obligation flows from the existence of an international community and the \"law habit\" of States. Best synthesis; accept and use it in conclusions. G. Sanctions and the Legal Character — Kelsen, Starke and the \"law habit\" Kelsen's test: the distinguishing feature of law is that it is a coercive order — rules of law must be connected with a sanction, and force must be either a sanction or a delinquency. In the international field sanctions exist as war and reprisal (historically) and, today, as enforcement action, countermeasures and collective measures. The difference between municipal and international law is only one of degree of centralisation : \"within the State, force is monopolised by the community; in the society of States a legitimate use of force may be made by the subjects of law — the individual States.\" Hence, international law is true law but a weak law. Starke's three principal sanctions (as reproduced in the DU class material): Chapter VII of the UN Charter — if there is a threat to the peace or an act of aggression, the Security Council may take necessary action. Article 94 of the Charter — decisions of the ICJ are binding, and a party may have recourse to the Security Council for enforcement. Article 2(4) read with Article 51 — members have undertaken not to use force; the only permitted unilateral use is self-defence, and even that is subject to Security Council supervision. Plus the informal sanctions: adverse public opinion (Rousseau's \"the strongest fear\"), economic sanctions, severance of diplomatic relations, suspension or expulsion from international organisations (Art. 5 and 6 UN Charter), and punishment of war crimes. Verma's list adds: fear of being branded a human-rights violator by NGOs such as Amnesty International, Asia Watch and Greenpeace. Critical note (use this to lift the answer above average) Sanctions are not co-extensive with international law . The Charter sanctions protect \"international peace and security\" — only a part of international law. There is no general machinery for breach of, say, a trade or environmental treaty; the only general remedy remains countermeasures (self-help), governed by the ILC Articles on State Responsibility 2001, Arts. 49–54. Also note the selectivity problem: Certain Expenses of the United Nations (ICJ Rep. 1962, p. 151) is cited in Verma to show that \"political pressure may sometimes prevent the Security Council from applying such sanctions.\" ↑ back to contents 1.2 Development of International Law \"In the long march of mankind from the cave to the computer a central role has always been played by the idea of law, the idea that order is necessary and chaos inimical to a just and stable existence. If it is applicable to a legal system, it is so equally with international law.\" Opening line of DU PIL Question Paper, 2000 — the examiner's own framing of this sub-topic. The syllabus prescribes development under three heads: (a) the need for international peace and security ; (b) the establishment of the United Nations ; and (c) the development of different generations of human rights . Each is a separate exam question; the historical narrative below supplies the common backdrop. A. Historical Development — a Five-Stage Timeline Fig. 1.2 — Five stages in the development of international law (use this spine for any \"development\" essay) Stage I — Antiquity: the classical roots International law did not begin in 1648 or 1625. Every civilisation that dealt with other political communities produced something like it: India: the dharmaśāstra tradition — the Ramayana, Mahabharata, Manusmriti and Kautilya's Arthashastra contain rules on the conduct of war (prohibition of poisoned weapons, protection of the wounded and of non-combatants, inviolability of envoys), treaties ( sandhi ) and neutrality. Ashoka's inscriptions record diplomatic missions to Hellenic kings. These are moral-religious rules, but the concept of a law binding even the king is genuinely Indian. Greece: the amphictyonic leagues, the inviolability of heralds, the tradition of arbitration between city-States — and the Stoic doctrine of a universal natural law binding all men. Rome: the jus fetiale (priestly rules on declaring war and concluding treaties) and, crucially, the jus gentium — the law applied to foreigners, later identified with jus naturale , \"the principles which ought to control human conduct … founded in the very nature of man as a rational and social being.\" Stage II — Grotius and Westphalia Hugo Grotius (1583–1645), in De Jure Belli ac Pacis (1625), produced the first systematic framework of the modern science. He was influenced by Gentilis and by Vittoria and Suarez. He secularised natural law — \"the dictate of right reason instead of divine prescriptions\" — and based the law of nations on nature, custom and treaties . His enduring doctrines: just and unjust war; qualified neutrality; freedom of the seas (in Mare Liberum , against Selden's Mare Clausum ); restitution of harm done by a State; and the value of periodic conferences. The Peace of Westphalia (1648) is the political counterpart: it ended the Thirty Years' War, recognised the territorial sovereignty and independence of States, and gave birth to the Westphalian model — a society of formally equal, sovereign States acknowledging no superior. Almost every modern \"Third World\" critique (see §1.6 ) attacks the legacy of this model's exclusions. Stage III — The nineteenth century: from usages to conferences Before the 19th century, development was \"effected mainly through usages, practices and writings of jurists.\" In the 19th century States began to legislate collectively: Table 1.5 — Landmarks of the 19th-century development (S.K. Verma, Chapter 1, §III) Instrument / Event Contribution Congress of Vienna, 1815 First European Assembly; rules for navigation of international rivers; classification of diplomatic ranks (later codified in VCDR 1961). Neutrality treaties (Switzerland 1815; Belgium 1831) Permanent neutralisation as a legal status. River Commissions (Rhine 1831; Danube 1856) Creation of international administrative unions — the ancestor of the international organisation. Declaration of Paris, 1856 Codified maritime warfare — abolition of privateering. Geneva Convention, 1864 Red Cross / ICRC; amelioration of the condition of the sick and wounded — birth of international humanitarian law. St. Petersburg Declaration, 1868 Prohibited use of explosive bullets — first prohibition of a weapon for humanitarian reasons. Alabama Claims Arbitration, 1872 US–UK; proved arbitration could settle great-power disputes. Hague Conventions 1899 & 1907 Laws of war and neutrality; Permanent Court of Arbitration (1899) , functioning from 1900; Arts. 37–90 of the 1907 Convention on pacific settlement — the framework for all later arbitration. Suez (1888) & Panama (1901) Canal regimes Permanent neutralised waterways under an international regime. The limitation: \"the law could still be described as European Public Law applicable among the Christian nations .\" Turkey became the first non-European subject only in the mid-nineteenth century; elsewhere the \"system of capitulations\" subjected Western nationals to their own consular courts in China, Iran and Turkey. Stage IV — The League experiment and its failure The League of Nations (1920) was \"the first international organisation designed to maintain international peace and promote cooperation among nations.\" Membership was open to \"any\" State — the first universalising move. It was joined by the Permanent Court of International Justice (1921) and by the ILO. The Kellogg–Briand Pact (1928) renounced war as an instrument of national policy but provided no machinery. The Hague Codification Conference (1930) failed on all three of its subjects. The system collapsed over Manchuria (1931) and Abyssinia (1935). The lesson drawn by the framers of the UN Charter: a prohibition of force without centralised enforcement is a paper prohibition . B. The Need for International Peace and Security The Preamble of the UN Charter opens with the determination \"to save succeeding generations from the scourge of war, which twice in our lifetime has brought untold sorrow to mankind.\" Article 1(1) makes the maintenance of international peace and security the Organisation's first purpose . The Charter's peace architecture has five layers — memorise them as a ladder: Fig. 1.3 — The Charter's graduated response to threats to the peace Real examples — the peace-and-security machinery in action Collective security (Art. 42): Korea 1950 (SC Res. 83/84, in the absence of the USSR); Kuwait 1990–91 (Res. 678 authorising \"all necessary means\"). Comprehensive sanctions (Art. 41): Southern Rhodesia (Res. 232, 1966 — the first mandatory sanctions); Iraq (Res. 661, 1990); Libya (Res. 1970 & 1973, 2011); DPRK (Res. 1718, 2006 and successors); Iran (Res. 1929, 2010). \"Smart\" or targeted sanctions: asset freezes, travel bans, arms embargoes on named individuals and entities — the post-1999 model (Res. 1267 Al-Qaida/Taliban Committee; Res. 1373 counter-terrorism; Res. 1540 WMD non-proliferation). Peacekeeping: not mentioned in the Charter — an invention of the General Assembly (\"Chapter VI½\"), upheld in Certain Expenses (1962) as an expense of the Organisation. Uniting for Peace: GA Res. 377(V), 1950 — if the SC is deadlocked by the veto, the GA may take up the matter and recommend collective measures (used for Suez 1956, Hungary 1956, and invoked for Ukraine in 2022 → GA Res. ES-11/1). C. Establishment of the United Nations 1. Genesis The name \"United Nations\" was coined in the Declaration by United Nations of 1 January 1942 (Washington), signed by 26 States. The institutional blueprint was agreed at Dumbarton Oaks (1944) and at Yalta (1945, on the veto), and the Charter was signed at San Francisco on 26 June 1945 , entering into force on 24 October 1945 (now United Nations Day). The ICJ was established in June 1945 and began work in April 1946; the first General Assembly met in London in January 1946. 2. Purposes and principles Article 1 — Purposes To maintain international peace and security — collective measures for prevention and removal of threats, and adjustment or settlement of disputes by peaceful means and in conformity with justice and international law; To develop friendly relations among nations based on respect for the principle of equal rights and self-determination of peoples ; To achieve international co-operation in solving international problems of an economic, social, cultural or humanitarian character, and in promoting and encouraging respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms for all without distinction as to race, sex, language or religion; To be a centre for harmonizing the actions of nations in the attainment of these common ends. Article 2 — Principles Sovereign equality of all Members; Fulfilment in good faith of Charter obligations; Peaceful settlement of disputes; Refrain from threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any State; Give the UN every assistance in any action it takes; refrain from giving assistance to any State against which the UN is taking preventive or enforcement action; Non-intervention in matters essentially within the domestic jurisdiction of any State (subject to Chapter VII enforcement measures); Charter obligations prevail over conflicting obligations under any other international agreement (Art. 103). 3. Principal organs (Article 7) and membership Table 1.6 — The six principal organs and their relevance to Topic 1 Organ Composition / Note Topic-1 relevance General Assembly All 193 members (Arts. 9–22); one State, one vote; \"Town meeting of the world.\" Source of soft law; sanctions: Arts. 5, 6, 19; Uniting for Peace; budget power (Art. 17) used to enforce compliance. Security Council 15 members — 5 permanent (China, France, Russia, UK, USA) with veto under Art. 27(3), and 10 elected for two years. The enforcement organ: Chapter VII, Arts. 5, 6, 94(2); decisions binding under Art. 25 ( Namibia , 1971). Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC) 54 members; coordinators of the specialised agencies; created the Commission on Human Rights in Feb. 1946 under Art. 68. Human-rights machinery; specialist bodies; NGO consultative status (Art. 71). Trusteeship Council Suspended operations 1 Nov. 1994 (Palau); no remaining trust territories. Historical: decolonisation and self-determination — the Third World's first legal victory. International Court of Justice 15 judges, 9-year terms, The Hague; principal judicial organ (Art. 92). Contentious and advisory jurisdiction; Art. 94(2) enforcement; source of the definitional cases of Topic 1. Secretariat Headed by the Secretary-General (Art. 97); Art. 99 power to bring matters to the SC; Art. 100 independence of staff. Art. 100 is the textual root of the Reparation case's \"functional protection\". Membership: original members (Art. 3), admission by GA on SC recommendation (Art. 4 — \"peace-loving States\"), suspension (Art. 5), expulsion (Art. 6). Growth from 51 founders (1945) to 193 members , with South Sudan (2011) the most recent. India was a founder member. D. Development of Different Generations of Human Rights The syllabus phrase \"different generations of human rights\" tracks Karel Vašák's classification (1977, UNESCO), adopted in the DU material as follows: the two Covenants of 1966 together represent the first two generations; the third generation emerged in the 1970s with the predominant support of developing countries. Fig. 1.4 — The three generations of human rights and the instruments that embody them Key instrument facts (quote these in the exam) Universal Declaration of Human Rights, 1948 — GA Res. 217A(III), adopted 10 December 1948 by 48 votes to none with 8 abstentions; technically not a treaty, but now treated as an authoritative interpretation of the Charter's human-rights clauses and, in substantial part, as customary law. The ICJ referred to it in United States Diplomatic and Consular Staff in Tehran (1980) ICJ Rep. 3, para. 91; the Supreme Court of India used it in Jolly George Varghese v. Bank of Cochin , AIR 1980 SC 470; the European Court of Justice used it in Golder , 57 ILR 201. The International Bill of Rights = UDHR + ICCPR + ICESCR + Optional Protocols. ICCPR, 1966 — adopted 16 Dec. 1966 (106–0); in force 23 March 1976; monitored by the Human Rights Committee (18 experts); procedures: State reports (Art. 40), inter-State communications (Art. 41), individual communications under the First Optional Protocol (adopted 66–2–38; in force 23 March 1976); Second Optional Protocol (1989) on abolition of the death penalty. India is a party to the ICCPR and to the First Optional Protocol (reservation on Art. 1 self-determination). ICESCR, 1966 — in force 3 January 1976; monitored by the Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights; individual communications under its Optional Protocol (2008). Ratification figures in the DU text: 166 parties to ICCPR; 160 to ICESCR (2009 figures). Critique of the third generation: \"Many western scholars are skeptical of labelling such group rights as human rights since they do not vest in, nor are exercised by, an individual.\" Realisation is also harder — the negative duty not to impede development is easy, but the positive duty to assist development \"involves a higher level of commitment which may not be easily forthcoming, as it has happened with … the New International Economic Order.\" Two-markers on this sub-topic Q: \"Are human rights part of international law?\" — Yes: (i) Charter Arts. 1(3), 55, 56, 68; (ii) the International Bill of Rights; (iii) treaty-based complaint machinery; (iv) individual standing (ECHR Art. 34; ICCPR OP1); (v) jus cogens prohibitions (genocide, torture, slavery); (vi) obligations erga omnes — Barcelona Traction (1970) para. 34; (vii) individual criminal responsibility (Nuremberg; Rome Statute). ↑ back to contents 1.3 Codification of International Law: The Work of the International Law Commission \"The codification of international law aims at putting together the rules of law on a given subject in a systematic manner making its provisions clearer by removing all lacunas, and also modifying the rules in accordance with the changed conditions.\" S.K. Verma, An Introduction to Public International Law (2nd ed.), Ch. 1, §V — the DU-prescribed text. A. Meaning and the Codification–Progressive Development Distinction Because international law is largely unwritten and generated by the practice of nearly two hundred States, its defects are notorious: uncertainty, lacunae, conflicting State practice, and the absence of an authoritative text . Codification is the cure. The word itself derives from Bentham's \"codification\" — the reduction of law into a code. The two operating concepts are defined by Article 15 of the Statute of the International Law Commission : Table 1.7 — Codification vs. Progressive Development (Art. 15, ILC Statute) Basis Codification Progressive Development Statutory definition \"The more precise formulation and systematisation of rules of international law in fields where there already has been extensive State practice, precedent and doctrine.\" \"The preparation of draft conventions on subjects which have not yet been regulated by international law or in regard to which the law has not yet been sufficiently developed in the practice of States.\" Nature of the exercise Declaratory — recording lex lata (the law as it is). Legislative — creating lex ferenda (the law as it should be). Typical subjects Diplomatic relations; consular relations; law of treaties; law of the sea. State responsibility; jurisdictional immunities; nationality of natural persons; climate-related obligations. The overlap — judge Sorensen's warning (quote it) \"Although theoretically clear and distinguishable, the two notions tend in practice to overlap or to leave between them an intermediate area in which it is not possible to indicate precisely where codification ends and progressive development begins.\" Judge Max Sorensen, dissenting opinion, North Sea Continental Shelf Cases (1969) ICJ Rep. 3, at pp. 242–43. The Commission itself follows the same procedure regardless of which label applies — a single set of draft articles, sent to Governments, revised, and then either adopted by a conference or \"taken note of\" by the General Assembly. B. The Codification Movement Before 1947 Table 1.8 — Pre-UN codification attempts Phase Event Outcome Scholarly Bentham's proposal of a comprehensive code (1789); Institut de Droit International (1873) ; International Law Association (1873); the Harvard Research drafts. Private, unofficial drafts — influential but not binding. Diplomatic (1899–1907) Hague Peace Conferences. \"The most notable achievement before the First World War was the Hague Conventions of 1899 and 1907, relating to the laws of war and neutrality.\" Partial codification of the laws of war; creation of the Permanent Court of Arbitration . League of Nations (1930) Hague Codification Conference — three subjects: law of nationality, territorial waters, State responsibility . A big disappointment. Agreement only on a few minor points of nationality (ratified by very few States); \"sharp disagreements\" on everything else, \"even on those matters which, till then were generally regarded as established rules.\" C. The International Law Commission — Constitution and Method 1. Legal basis Charter, Article 13(1)(a): \"The General Assembly shall initiate studies and make recommendations for the purpose of … encouraging the progressive development of international law and its codification.\" GA Resolution 174(II) of 21 November 1947 established the ILC by a Statute \"for the promotion of the progressive development of international law and its codification.\" The Commission held its first session in 1949 and in that year drew up a provisional list of 14 subjects for codification. 2. Composition 34 members (originally 15, then 21, then 25; raised to 34 in 1981), elected by the General Assembly for five years , \"having competence in international law\", \"representing the main forms of civilisation and the principal legal systems of the world\" (Arts. 2, 3 and 8 of the Statute). Geographic distribution (per Verma's footnote to Arts. 2, 3 and 8): Africa 8; Latin America and the Caribbean 6; Asia 7; Eastern Europe 3; Western Europe and Other States 8; plus two rotating seats. Members sit in their individual capacity — not as representatives of Governments. Verma's sharp observation: \"increasingly the professional diplomats from the respective States now are donning the Commission, which belies the apolitical character of the Commission.\" An Indian, Sir Benegal Narsing Rau , was a member of the Commission and of the 1958 Law of the Sea Conference; India has been consistently represented (e.g., Special Rapporteurs on State responsibility such as Roberto Ago (Italy) and James Crawford (Australia)). 3. Working method — the six-step cycle Fig. 1.5 — The ILC's six-step codification cycle D. The ILC's Outputs — the Evidence of Success Table 1.9 — Conventions produced through the ILC (the classic list from Verma, updated) Year Instrument Topic-1 relevance 1958 Four Geneva Conventions on the Law of the Sea — Territorial Sea & Contiguous Zone; Continental Shelf; High Seas; Fishing & Conservation of Living Resources Superseded but foundational for UNCLOS 1982. 1961 Convention on the Reduction of Statelessness Individual & nationality. 1961 Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations (in the project folder as VCDR.pdf) Diplomatic immunity; invoked in Tehran Hostages (1980). 1963 Vienna Convention on Consular Relations (in the folder as VCCR.pdf) Art. 36 → LaGrand (2001), Avena (2004), Jadhav (2019): individual rights . 1969 Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (\"the treaty on treaties\") The first law-making treaty in which the new nations participated fully; jus cogens Arts. 53, 64. 1969 Convention on Special Missions Ad hoc diplomacy. 1978 Vienna Convention on Succession of States in Respect of Treaties Decolonisation (\"clean slate\" rule, Art. 16). 1983 Vienna Convention on Succession of States in respect of State Property, Archives and Debts Statehood. 1986 Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties between States and International Organisations (not in force) International organisations as treaty-makers — the Reparation personality confirmed textually. 1997 Convention on the Law of the Non-Navigational Uses of International Watercourses (GA, 21 May 1997) Shared resources. 1998 Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court Individual criminal responsibility (§1.5). 2001 Draft Articles on Responsibility of States for Internationally Wrongful Acts (ARSIWA) — in the folder as 9_6_2001.pdf Secondary rules of responsibility; attribution; reparation. 2001 Draft Articles on Prevention of Transboundary Harm from Hazardous Activities Environmental risk. 2004 UN Convention on Jurisdictional Immunities of States and their Property (not yet in force) Sovereign immunity; Germany v. Italy (2012). 2006 Draft Articles on Diplomatic Protection Nationality of claims; exhaustion of local remedies. 2018 Draft Conclusions on Identification of Customary International Law The modern authority on custom's two elements. 2022 Draft Conclusions on Peremptory Norms of General International Law ( jus cogens ) Hierarchy of norms. Other ILC products (past requests of the GA): the 1949 Draft Declaration on Rights and Duties of States; the 1950 Principles of International Law recognised in the Nuremberg Charter and Judgment; the 1952 Report on Reservations to Multilateral Conventions; the 1954 Draft Code of Offences against the Peace and Security of Mankind; the 1958 Model Rules on Arbitral Procedure; and the 1974 Definition of Aggression (GA Res. 3314 (XXIX), 14 Dec. 1974). Current and recent work programme: jurisdictional immunities; protection of persons in the event of disasters; shared natural resources (oil and gas); responsibility of international organisations; expulsion of aliens; protection of the environment in relation to armed conflicts; sea-level rise in relation to international law; general principles of law (2023). Critical appraisal — the marks-winning paragraph Success: the ILC has given the international community its most important law-making conventions; it has made customary law visible and citable; and its drafts shape State conduct even when un-adopted (the ARSIWA is treated by the ICJ as reflecting custom). Delay: the State-responsibility project took from 1949 to 2001 — over fifty years; some topics have been on the agenda for decades. Overload: \"the Commission is already saddled with too many subjects and is confining itself mainly to traditional subjects where there exists considerable amount of non-controversial State practice\" — hence the trend towards special codification bodies (UNCLOS III, the Rome Conference, the ad hoc committees on terrorism and on jurisdictional immunities). Third World view (see §1.6): the new States regard the ILC as too slow, too Western in composition and too conservative in its choice of topics; they prefer the General Assembly and special conferences where their \"number power\" counts. On the other side \"the western countries are wary of the 'terror of majority' unleashed by new nations\". Structural limitation: the ILC can only draft. Whether a rule becomes law depends on ratification — e.g., the 1986 Vienna Convention and the 2004 Jurisdictional Immunities Convention are still not in force. Ready answer — \"Discuss the work of the International Law Commission in the codification of international law.\" ① Meaning & need of codification → ② Art. 15 distinction (codification v. progressive development) + Sorensen's overlap warning → ③ Legal basis: Charter Art. 13(1)(a) + GA Res. 174(II), 1947 → ④ Constitution: 34 members, individual capacity, equitable geographical distribution → ⑤ Six-step method (flowchart) → ⑥ List of major conventions (table) → ⑦ Why even non-treaty drafts matter (ARSIWA before the ICJ) → ⑧ Critique: delay, overload, composition, Third World dissatisfaction, dependence on ratification → ⑨ Conclusion: the ILC is the \"legislative draftsman\" of a decentralised system; it does not legislate, but it clarifies , and clarification is the closest thing international law has to legislation. ↑ back to contents 1.4 Sanctions of International Law The syllabus lists eight categories: Security Council, General Assembly, ICJ, Human Rights Council, ITLOS, ICC, PCA, and sanctions under the different human rights treaties . Write the answer as an institutional survey — one heading per institution, each answering: (i) what is the legal basis? (ii) what sanction can it impose? (iii) is the sanction binding? (iv) illustration. A. The Concept of Sanction and Its Classification A \"sanction\" in international law is the consequence attached to a breach of an international obligation, designed to induce compliance. Two preliminary points must be made: Sanctions are not co-extensive with international law. Chapter VII sanctions protect \"international peace and security\", which is only a part of international law. For the rest, the system relies on countermeasures , reciprocity , reputation and reciprocal treaty withdrawal . Sanctions are decentralised. There is no world police. Kelsen's insight: in a decentralised legal order, the subjects of law themselves execute the law through self-help, and international law's contribution is to make such self-help lawful in defined circumstances and unlawful otherwise. Table 1.10 — A working classification of sanctions Category Description Examples Centralised / institutional Applied by an organ of the international community under a treaty regime. SC Chapter VII (Art. 41, 42); UNCLOS Part XV; WTO Dispute Settlement Body suspension of concessions; ICAO & IAEA referrals. Judicial Binding adjudication with an enforcement hook. ICJ Art. 94(2); ITLOS binding judgments; ICC arrest warrants; ECtHR judgments supervised by the Committee of Ministers. Horizontal / self-help Applied by an injured State directly against the wrongdoer. Retorsion (unfriendly but lawful acts) and countermeasures (an otherwise unlawful act rendered lawful by the prior breach) — ILC ARSIWA Arts. 22, 49–54. Membership-based Suspension, expulsion, loss of voting rights in an organisation. UN Charter Arts. 5, 6, 19; Yugoslavia suspended from ECOSOC (1992); Russia suspended from the Human Rights Council (2022). Non-institutional / informal \"Sanctions of public opinion.\" Adverse publicity by Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch, Greenpeace; media exposure; naming and shaming; FATF grey-listing. Fig. 1.6 — The plural, decentralised sanctions system B. The Security Council — Chapter VII (the strongest sanction) The DU material's first sanction is Chapter VII: \"if there is threat to the international peace and security or an aggression has taken place, the Security Council can take necessary action to maintain or restore international peace and security.\" Examine the machinery article by article: Table 1.11 — Chapter VII (and related) provisions in operation Article Text / content Effect Art. 24 Members confer on the SC \"primary responsibility for the maintenance of international peace and security\". Source of the SC's constitutional primacy. Art. 25 Members \"agree to accept and carry out the decisions of the Security Council\". Binding force of SC decisions — held in Legal Consequences for States of the Continued Presence of South Africa in Namibia (South West Africa) , ICJ Rep. 1971, p. 16, para. 113, to extend to \"decisions\" (Art. 25) and not merely recommendations. Art. 27(3) Substantive decisions require 9 affirmative votes \"including the concurring votes of the permanent members\". The veto — the single most criticised feature of the system; the practice of abstention being treated as concurrence ( Namibia para. 22). Art. 39 \"The Security Council shall determine the existence of any threat to the peace, breach of the peace, or act of aggression…\" The determination is the gateway; judicial review of it is not available in practice. Art. 40 Provisional measures (cease-fire, withdrawal) without prejudice to rights/claims. Binding provisional orders; e.g. Res. 660 (1990) on Iraq. Art. 41 Measures not involving armed force: \"complete or partial interruption of economic relations and of rail, sea, air, postal, telegraphic, radio, and other means of communication, and the severance of diplomatic relations.\" Full-embargo sanctions, arms embargoes, travel bans, asset freezes; the platform for all \"sanctions committees\". Art. 42 If Art. 41 measures \"would be inadequate or have proved to be inadequate\", action \"by air, sea, or land forces … demonstrations, blockade, and other operations\". Military enforcement; used in Korea (1950) and Kuwait (1990–91) and Libya (2011). Arts. 43–47 Special agreements to place armed forces at the SC's disposal; Military Staff Committee. Never implemented — the \"missing teeth\" of collective security; hence ad hoc coalitions and peacekeeping. Arts. 48–50 Implementation by all or some Members; mutual assistance; consultation for States with \"special economic problems\". Art. 50 is the legal answer to the \"collateral damage\" objection to sanctions (used by India in the Iraq sanctions debates). Arts. 5 & 6 Suspension of the rights and privileges of membership (Art. 5); expulsion (Art. 6) — both by the GA on the SC's recommendation. Membership sanctions. Real examples — the Security Council actually sanctioning Southern Rhodesia (1966): SC Res. 232 imposed the first-ever mandatory economic sanctions under Art. 41, after the Unilateral Declaration of Independence; upheld in the Namibia advisory opinion context. South Africa: an arms embargo (Res. 418, 1977) plus a voluntary oil embargo — the model of a sanctions campaign against apartheid; complemented by the 1976 UN sports boycott and the 1977 Gleneagles Agreement. Iraq (1990–2003): Res. 661 (comprehensive economic sanctions), 678 (\"all necessary means\"), 687 (cease-fire terms, disarmament, the basis of the Oil-for-Food Programme) — the most-studied case, also criticised for its humanitarian consequences. Libya (2011): Res. 1970 (referral to the ICC, arms embargo, asset freeze, travel ban) and Res. 1973 (authorising \"all necessary measures\" to protect civilians) — the controversial authorisation that produced the NATO air campaign. Counter-terrorism: Res. 1267 (1999) Al-Qaida/Taliban Committee; Res. 1373 (2001) — a general, quasi-legislative obligation on all States; Res. 1540 (2004) on weapons of mass destruction. Non-proliferation: Res. 1718 (2006) and successors on the DPRK; Res. 1929 (2010) on Iran. Iran–US: the ICJ in Alleged Violations of the 1955 Treaty (2023) held that US \"snapback\" of sanctions breached the Treaty of Amity. Veto inaction: Syria (multiple vetoes), Crimea 2014, and the 2022 invasion of Ukraine — where the SC was deadlocked and the GA took over via the \"Uniting for Peace\" route. This is the standard criticism of the SC as a sanctions organ. Two doctrines to cite: Art. 103 — hierarchy: Charter obligations prevail over any other international agreement; SC sanctions thus override conflicting treaty rights (the basis on which the ECtHR in Al-Jedda and Nada v. Switzerland reconciled the 1267 regime with the ECHR). Art. 2(7) — the limit: nothing in the Charter authorises intervention \"in matters which are essentially within the domestic jurisdiction of any State\", but \"this principle shall not prejudice the application of enforcement measures under Chapter VII\". Human-rights situations are therefore sanctionable if the SC makes an Art. 39 determination. C. The General Assembly The GA has no coercive power; it has legitimacy, voice, budget and membership powers . Four mechanisms: Table 1.12 — General Assembly as a sanctions actor Mechanism Basis & Content Illustration Recommendations Arts. 10–14. Generally recommendatory, but may declare or crystallise legal rules; may \"make recommendations with a view to … the peaceful adjustment of any situation\". Nuclear Weapons AO, ICJ Rep. 1996 (I), para. 70: GA resolutions \"can, in certain circumstances, provide evidence important for establishing the existence of a rule or the emergence of an opinio juris .\" Also Nicaragua (1986) paras. 188, 191 on Res. 2625 (XXV). Uniting for Peace GA Res. 377(V), 3 Nov. 1950 — if the SC fails because of the veto, the GA may be convened within 24 hours and may \"recommend collective measures\". Suez & Hungary 1956; Congo 1960; and invoked in Feb.–Mar. 2022 for Ukraine (GA Res. ES-11/1 and ES-11/2). Membership & credentials Art. 5 (suspension), Art. 6 (expulsion) on SC recommendation; credentials and representation disputes (Art. 21 rules of procedure). GA Res. 47/1 (1992) on the FRY's participation; South Africa's credentials rejected repeatedly during apartheid after 1974. Art. 19 — loss of vote A member in arrears equal to two years' contributions loses its vote in the GA (unless the failure is due to conditions beyond its control). Used against South Africa (1986), Haiti (1993–96, extended) and Somalia (1994); the \" lapsed States \" jurisprudence. D. The International Court of Justice The ICJ is the only international court of general jurisdiction, but its sanctions are indirect. Structure the answer around four points: Contentious jurisdiction. Under Art. 36(1) — treaty-based compromissory clauses: under Art. 36(2) — the \"Optional Clause\" declarations, of which India's (still in force, with reservations excluding Commonwealth States and multilateral-treaty disputes where the other party accepted jurisdiction only within 12 months before the dispute) is a famous example. Over 70 States have made declarations. Binding force and enforcement. Art. 59 (decisions bind the parties and only in respect of that case); Art. 60 (final, without appeal); Art. 61 (revision). Art. 94(1) UN Charter : \"Each Member of the United Nations undertakes to comply with the decision of the International Court of Justice in any case to which it is a party.\" Art. 94(2) : if a party fails to perform its obligations, the other party may \"have recourse to the Security Council, which may, if it deems necessary, make recommendations or decide upon measures to be taken to give effect to the judgment.\" This is the DU material's second sanction. Provisional measures (Art. 41). Long thought non-binding; now settled as binding in LaGrand (Germany v. United States) , ICJ Rep. 2001, p. 466: \"the object and purpose of the Statute … implies that [provisional measures] should be binding\"; confirmed in Armed Activities (DRC v. Uganda) 2000 and applied in the 2017 Ukraine v. Russia Order. Notice also the paradox — a party that violates provisional measures is in breach twice over. Advisory jurisdiction (Art. 65). Not binding as such, but \"the Court's advisory opinions carry great weight\" ( Tehran Hostages , 1980, para. 53); they are often the vehicle for the most important legal pronouncements — Reparation (1949), Reservations to the Genocide Convention (1951), Namibia (1971), Western Sahara (1975), Nuclear Weapons (1996), Wall in the Occupied Palestinian Territory (2004), Kosovo (2010), Chagos (2019), Climate Change (2024). The weaknesses of the ICJ as a sanction — say all four (i) Jurisdiction is consensual — no State can be brought to the Court without its consent; (ii) the Court has no enforcement machinery of its own — Art. 94(2) returns the matter to the Security Council, where the veto applies; (iii) compliance has been uneven — the US withdrew its acceptance of compulsory jurisdiction after Nicaragua , and withdrew from the Optional Protocol to the VCCR after Avena ; (iv) apart from Nicaragua , \"there has been hardly any case when the aggrieved State has approached the Security Council under Art. 94\" (Verma). But note the counter-example of successful compliance : Corfu Channel (UK paid £843,947 to Albania in 1992), Temple of Preah Vihear (Thailand returned the temple in 1963), Jadhav (Pakistan allowed consular access and review), Chagos (partially implemented). ↑ back to contents E. The Human Rights Council Genesis and status. The UN Commission on Human Rights (CHR) was established by ECOSOC in February 1946 under Art. 68 of the Charter, assisted by the Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights (UNHCHR). It \"had been criticised for allowing countries with poor human rights records to be members.\" By GA Res. A/RES/60/251 of 15 March 2006 the Commission was replaced by the Human Rights Council , a subsidiary organ of the General Assembly (whereas the CHR reported to ECOSOC), based at Geneva. Composition: 47 members elected by the GA — 13 from Africa, 13 from Asia, 6 from Eastern Europe, 8 from Latin America and the Caribbean, 7 from Western European and Other States; members serve three-year terms, may be suspended by a two-thirds vote of the GA for gross and systematic violations (this happened to Libya in 2011). The Council's four sanctions-relevant instruments: Table 1.13 — The Human Rights Council's machinery Mechanism Legal basis Sanction-value & example Universal Periodic Review (UPR) Institution-building package, HRC Res. 5/1 (18 June 2007) Every UN member's human-rights record is peer-reviewed every ~4.5 years. A reputational sanction. India has undergone four UPR cycles (2008, 2012, 2017, 2022). Special Procedures (Special Rapporteurs, Working Groups, Independent Experts) HRC resolutions; country and thematic mandates Country visits, urgent appeals, communications to Governments, annual reports. E.g., the Special Rapporteur on torture; the Working Group on Arbitrary Detention. Complaint Procedure HRC Res. 5/1 — Working Group on Communications + Working Group on Situations Confidential, for \"consistent patterns of gross and reliably attested violations\". The old \"1503 procedure\" re-designed. Commissions of Inquiry / Fact-finding missions HRC special sessions (one-third of members may request) COI on Syria (2011–), on the DPRK (2013), on Myanmar (2017–), on Ukraine (2022–). Reports feed into ICC referrals and sanctions designations. Caveat for the answer: the Council has no enforcement power. Its output is recommendatory and reputational ; the actual legal consequences come from other institutions (the SC, the ICC, treaty bodies, or regional courts). F. The International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea (ITLOS) Basis: UNCLOS 1982, Annex VI (Statute of ITLOS). Seat: Hamburg. 21 judges elected for nine years by the States Parties. Functioning since 1996. Jurisdiction: disputes submitted under Part XV; compulsory jurisdiction over (i) Part XI (deep seabed \"Area\") disputes — where the Seabed Disputes Chamber is compulsory for States Parties, the Authority and contractors; (ii) prompt release of vessels and crews (Art. 292 — a unique \"summary\" jurisdiction); (iii) provisional measures (Art. 290); (iv) any dispute where States have chosen ITLOS under Art. 287. Enforcement: UNCLOS Art. 296 — decisions are final and binding; Art. 279–280 (peaceful means); Art. 298 (opt-out for maritime delimitation, military activities, SC law-enforcement action). Landmark cases: M/V Saiga (No. 2) (1999 — prompt release; reparation); Bangladesh/Myanmar (2012 — first ITLOS maritime boundary case, applied the \"equidistance/relevant circumstances\" method); Arctic Sunrise (2013 — provisional measures against Russia, which did not appear yet partly complied); Enrica Lexie (Italy v. India) ended in PCA arbitration (2020); the 2024 Advisory Opinion on Climate Change and the Oceans — a major ITLOS statement that States must take measures to prevent marine pollution and protect the marine environment from climate impacts. India connection: Dr. Neeru Chadha has served as an ITLOS judge since 2017 (first Indian woman elected to an international court); India took the Enrica Lexie dispute to arbitration under UNCLOS and in Republic of Italy v. Union of India , (2013) 4 SCC 721 the Indian Supreme Court held that the dispute was arbitrable and India's courts should await the arbitral award (the \"Italian Marines\" case, dropping the criminal proceedings against the marines under the 2014 Agreement). G. The International Criminal Court (ICC) Basis: the Rome Statute, adopted 17 July 1998 (120 votes in favour, 7 against, 21 abstentions); in force 1 July 2002; 125 States Parties (India, China, the USA, Russia, Israel and Pakistan are not parties). Seat: The Hague. Jurisdiction (Art. 5): genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, and the crime of aggression (activated 2018). Trigger mechanisms (Arts. 12–14): (i) State referral; (ii) SC referral under Chapter VII — used for Darfur (Res. 1593, 2005) and Libya (Res. 1970, 2011); (iii) proprio motu investigation by the Prosecutor with Pre-Trial Chamber authorisation (e.g., Kenya, Georgia, Bangladesh/Myanmar, Ukraine). Complementarity (Arts. 1, 17): the ICC acts only where national courts are unwilling or unable — a jurisdictional concession to sovereignty. Individual responsibility (Arts. 25, 27, 28): jurisdiction over natural persons; official capacity is irrelevant (Art. 27) — no immunity even for a serving Head of State; superior responsibility (Art. 28). Sanction: arrest warrants, prosecution, imprisonment up to 30 years, or life imprisonment; reparations to victims (Art. 75) and the Trust Fund for Victims. Landmark: Lubanga (2012 — first conviction); Ntaganda (2019); Ongwen (2021); the arrest warrant against President Omar al-Bashir (2009–10) — which triggered the immunity debate revisited in Jordan Referral re Al-Bashir (Appeals Chamber, 2019); the 2023 warrants against Russian officials (including the sitting Head of State) following the SC's inability to act on Ukraine; the 2024 warrants in the Gaza situation. India's position (a favourite question): India voted against the Rome Statute in 1998 and has not signed. Reasons: (i) the crime of aggression and the definition of war crimes were too broad; (ii) Art. 12 gives the Court jurisdiction without State consent (non-consensual jurisdiction); (iii) the pre-trial role of the Security Council and the veto; (iv) the possibility of politicised prosecutions; (v) India insisted on the inclusion of nuclear weapons and terrorism as crimes. India does, however, participate in the ICC's Assembly as an observer and supported the SC referral of Darfur. H. The Permanent Court of Arbitration (PCA) Basis: Convention for the Pacific Settlement of International Disputes, adopted at the First Hague Peace Conference ( 1899 ), revised in 1907. The PCA began functioning in 1900 — the oldest global dispute-settlement institution. Nature: despite the name, the PCA is not a court ; it is an intergovernmental organisation providing registry and administrative support to arbitral tribunals, conciliation commissions and fact-finding commissions chosen by the parties. It maintains the \"panel\" of potential arbitrators (each State nominates up to four) and a list of standing \"members of the Court\" (Max Huber's famous 1928 Island of Palmas award was rendered by a PCA tribunal). Consent as the basis: jurisdiction requires an arbitration agreement — either a compromis , a compromissory clause (e.g., UNCLOS Art. 287), or a general arbitration treaty (the 1899/1907 Conventions, or bilateral investment treaties). Sanction-value: awards are binding on the parties, final, and not subject to appeal (a limited annulment is available only under ICSID/UNCITRAL rules); enforcement relies on the parties' good faith, and pecuniary awards are often paid to avoid commercial and reputational consequences. Landmark modern cases: Bay of Bengal Maritime Boundary Arbitration (Bangladesh/India) , PCA 2014 — India participated and complied; South China Sea Arbitration (Philippines v. China) , 2016 — China refused to participate and rejected the award; Enrica Lexie (Italy v. India) , 2020; Dispute concerning Coastal State Rights in the Black Sea (Ukraine v. Russia) , 2020; Investment arbitrations against India (e.g., Devas v. India ; Vodafone v. India ) — all PCA-administered. I. Sanctions under Human Rights Treaties The \"sanctions\" here are monitoring, reporting and complaint procedures — soft, but real: they produce findings of violation which carry reputational and political consequences and which municipal courts increasingly cite. Table 1.14 — Treaty-based human rights enforcement machinery Treaty Body Procedures ICCPR, 1966 Human Rights Committee (18 members) State reports (Art. 40); inter-State communications (Art. 41 — never used); individual communications under the First Optional Protocol — the Committee's \"views\" are not formally binding but are complied with in the majority of cases (e.g., Toonen v. Australia , 1994 — Tasmania's sodomy laws repealed). ICESCR, 1966 Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights State reports; individual communications under the 2008 Optional Protocol; inquiries. CAT, 1984 Committee against Torture Individual communications (Art. 22); confidential inquiry into systematic torture (Art. 20); State-to-State (Art. 21). Landmark: Gafgen v. Germany before the ECtHR; the Committee's decisions in Agiza v. Sweden — non-refoulement to torture. CEDAW, 1979 CEDAW Committee Individual communications and inquiry under the 1999 Optional Protocol (India is a party to CEDAW but not to its Optional Protocol). CRC, 1989 / CRPD, 2006 CRC Committee / CRPD Committee Reporting; individual communications (CRC OP3; CRPD OP). Regional: ECHR, 1950 European Court of Human Rights The strongest of all. Art. 34: individual applications (a true international remedy for individuals); binding judgments; execution supervised by the Committee of Ministers (Art. 46); just satisfaction (Art. 41); pilot judgments. Example: Ireland v. UK (1978); Al-Skeini (2011). Regional: ACHR, 1969 Inter-American Commission and Court Individual petitions; binding judgments; reparations orders. Regional: ACHPR, 1981 African Commission and Court Communications; the landmark Ogoni decision (SERAC v. Nigeria, 2001). The honest concession to make: \"Under most of these instruments, an individual does not have any direct recourse to any international organ to enforce its rights, and in certain cases, where such machinery exists … non-binding recommendations are generally made. The net result of all this is that the United Nations so far has not proved very effective in the prevention of flagrant violations of human rights.\" (Verma, Ch. on Human Rights.) See also the Genocide Convention 1948 — which contained no implementing machinery at all until Art. IX was invoked before the ICJ in Bosnia v. Serbia (2007). J. Horizontal Sanctions — Retorsion and Countermeasures Where no institution acts, international law permits the injured State to act itself. The governing text is the ILC Articles on State Responsibility, 2001 (in the project folder): Table 1.15 — Retorsion, countermeasures and SC sanctions compared Feature Retorsion Countermeasure (ARSIWA) SC sanction Definition An unfriendly but lawful act. An act that would be unlawful but for the prior wrongful act of the target State (Art. 22). A decision of the SC under Chapter VII. Trigger Any disagreement. An internationally wrongful act of the target State (Art. 49(1)) — not a mere \"unfriendly\" act. An Art. 39 determination of threat/breach/aggression. Purpose Pressure / signal. \"To induce that State to comply with its obligations\" — not punishment (Art. 49(1)). To maintain or restore international peace and security. Limit None beyond general law. Proportionality (Art. 51) and the prohibition of countermeasures involving force (Art. 50(1)(a)); no derogation from fundamental human rights or humanitarian obligations; suspension of countermeasures if the wrongful act ceases (Art. 52(3)); must be terminated if the dispute is pending before a court with binding powers (Art. 52(3)(b)). Must respect jus cogens ; Art. 50 obligations continue. Authority Inherent. Naulilaa (1928) 2 RIAA 1012 (the classic reprisal case); Air Services Agreement (France v. US) (1978) 18 RIAA 416 — proportionality; Gabčíkovo–Nagymaros (1997) — Hungary could not rely on a wrongfully terminated countermeasure. Art. 25, Art. 41/42; Namibia (1971). Real examples of horizontal measures US sanctions on Iran after the 1979 hostage crisis: asset freezes and a trade embargo — held lawful in part by the ICJ in Tehran Hostages (1980) as response to Iran's breach of diplomatic immunity. EU and US \"Magnitsky Acts\" — targeted visa bans and asset freezes on named officials for human-rights abuses; the Global Magnitsky Act 2016. India–Pakistan: India's revocation of Pakistan's MFN status and tariff hikes after Pulwama (2019) — retorsion/countermeasures within the WTO framework. Russia 2022: unprecedented Western sanctions (SWIFT exclusion, central-bank asset immobilisation, oil price cap) plus the GA's suspension of Russia from the Human Rights Council (7 April 2022, GA Res. ES-11/3) and the expulsion of Russia from the Council of Europe. Suspensions/expulsions from organisations: Cuba expelled from the OAS (1962); Yugoslavia suspended from ECOSOC and barred from the GA (1992); Egypt suspended from the Arab League (1979–89); Russia expelled from the G8 (2014). K. A Composite Assessment — \"Do sanctions determine the effectiveness of international law?\" This is literally a recorded DU question (2015). Build the answer on four moves: Yes, in part: sanctions give the law teeth. Without Chapter VII, Art. 2(4) would be exhortation; without the ICC, individual criminal responsibility would be a policy ideal; without ITLOS, UNCLOS disputes would go unresolved. The existence of a sanction is also Kelsen's defining feature of a legal order. But sanction is neither necessary nor sufficient. Customary and common law systems functioned without a sovereign enforcer; conversely, the SC's enforcement record is selective — Korea (1950) but not Tibet (1950–51), Kuwait (1990) but not Chechnya (1994–99). Veto and politics distort enforcement. The permanent members' veto means the strongest sanction is unavailable against the strongest States — the critique famously implied in Certain Expenses (1962) and demonstrated in Syria after 2011 and Ukraine in 2022. Compliance does not depend only on coercion. States obey international law because of the \"law habit\" (Fitzmaurice), reciprocity, reputation, domestic implementation and self-interest. Brierly: international law \"is more honoured in the observance than in the breaches.\" Sanctions are the visible edge of a much larger structure of compliance. Model short note — \"Sanctions under International Law\" (10 marks) Intro: sanction = consequence of breach; in a decentralised order sanctions are plural and uneven. → Centralised: SC Chapter VII (Arts. 39–42; examples Rhodesia, Iraq, Libya, DPRK; the veto problem), GA (Arts. 5, 6, 19; Res. 377(V); Res. 265(III)/2625), Art. 94(2) ICJ judgments, HRC (UPR, Special Procedures, COIs), ITLOS (Art. 296; prompt release), ICC (Art. 27; SC referrals), PCA (binding awards preserved by consent). → Treaty-based: ICCPR OP1 individual communications, CAT Art. 20 inquiries, ECHR Art. 34/46. → Horizontal: retorsion and countermeasures (ARSIWA Arts. 22, 49–54; Naulilaa ; Air Services Agreement ). → Non-legal: public opinion, reciprocity, \"law habit\". → Conclusion: sanctions are increasingly targeted and legalised (smart sanctions, judicialised accountability) but remain politically selective; they supplement, and do not replace, the ordinary mechanisms of compliance. ↑ back to contents 1.5 Subjects of International Law — States, International Organisations and Individuals The syllabus puts this sub-topic in three layers: (a) the position of States; (b) international organisations — their establishment and personality; (c) individuals — the development of human rights and the position of individuals. The two prescribed cases both live here: Reparation (organisations) and Ukraine v. Russia (individuals). A. Legal Personality — the Governing Test The definition to memorise \"The subjects of law in any legal system are not necessarily identical in their nature or in the extent of their rights, and their nature depends upon the needs of the community.\" — Reparation for Injuries , ICJ Rep. 1949, p. 174, at 178. \"An entity is a subject of international law … capable of possessing international rights and duties, and … has the capacity to maintain its rights by bringing international claims.\" — ibid. , at 179. The DU classroom material (citing Gurdip Singh) defines personality as \"the capacity to be a bearer of rights and duties under international law\". Four constituents of legal capacity are traditionally tested: Capacity to make claims in respect of breaches of international law (procedural capacity / locus standi ); Capacity to enter into treaties and other international agreements (treaty-making power); Capacity to enjoy privileges and immunities from the jurisdiction of national courts; Capacity to be subject to obligations and to be held responsible for their breach — the \"duty\" side of personality (e.g., individual criminal responsibility). To this modern writers add the capacity to espouse claims on behalf of others (functional protection), and the capacity to be a party before international tribunals. Fig. 1.7 — Subjects of international law: the full map, with the three competing theories B. The Three Theories Stated in the DU Material Table 1.16 — The three theories with their exponents, arguments and refutation Theory Chief exponents & argument Refutation 1. Only States are subjects Oppenheim: \"the law of nations is primarily a law of international conduct of States and not of their citizens.\" Sir Frederick Smith: \"States and States alone enjoy a locus standi in the law of nations and … they are the only bearers of international personality.\" Textual support: Art. 34(1) ICJ Statute. Ignores the UN and other organisations ( Reparation ); ignores individual rights and duties; Art. 34(1) governs only the ICJ, not international law; the theory is descriptive of the 19th century, not of today. 2. Only individuals are subjects Kelsen: the State \"is a technical legal concept and includes the rules of law applicable on the persons living in a definite territory\"; the duties of States are ultimately the duties of individuals; there is no real distinction between State law and international law. Practically untenable: individuals have no general treaty-making capacity, no standing before the ICJ, and no general capacity to make claims; their personality is derivative and exceptional. The theory \"tries to create a monopoly of an individual.\" 3. States, individuals and certain non-State entities (the modern view) \"This view undoubtedly appears to be far better than the first two views.\" States remain the primary subjects, but personality is graduated: organisations enjoy it functionally; individuals enjoy rights (ICCPR OP1, ECHR Art. 34, VCCR Art. 36) and bear duties (Nuremberg; Rome Statute); non-State entities enjoy it in specific contexts. Its only weakness is that it states no single criterion. Answer: the criterion is functional — the capacities actually conferred by the international community for the discharge of particular functions. The synthesis paragraph to write: \"The typical legal subject of international law is the State. Ordinarily, international law deals with the rights and duties of States, but other entities may also be considered subjects to the extent they can enter into legal relations and exercise certain rights on the international plane. The practice of the last half-century has clearly discarded the old dogmatic approach that the State is the only subject of international law.\" (From the DU class material.) C. States — the Primary Subject 1. Criteria of Statehood The Montevideo Convention on the Rights and Duties of States, 1933, Article 1 — the standard four criteria: Permanent population; Defined territory (not necessarily settled or fully delimited — North Sea Continental Shelf ; Israel/India cases); Government — effective control, though \"failed States\" and Governments-in-exile complicate this ( Aaland Islands 1920); Capacity to enter into relations with other States — i.e., independence and the absence of control by another State (hence the rejection of \"puppet\" claims; contrast the condominium of the New Hebrides/Vanuatu). The Montevideo criteria are complemented by two modern additions: respect for self-determination and legality of the entity's creation (the \"unlawful creation\" doctrine applied to Northern Cyprus — Loizidou cases; Southern Rhodesia; and to the Bantustans). 2. Recognition — declaratory and constitutive theories Table 1.17 — Recognition of States and Governments Doctrine Content Authority Constitutive The act of recognition creates the State's international personality; an unrecognised entity is not a subject. Positivist writers; practical support in Arantzazu Mendi (1939); the \"collective recognition\" technique (EEC Declaration on Yugoslavia, 1991). Declaratory Statehood exists as a matter of fact and law once the Montevideo criteria are met; recognition is merely evidence . Lauterpacht : \"To recognise a political community as a State is to declare that it fulfils the conditions of statehood as required by international law. If these conditions are present, the existing States are under a duty to grant recognition.\" Opened in Deutsche Continental Gas-Gesellschaft v. Polish State (1929); the EU Badinter Commission (1991–93); Chagos (2019) para. 177 (note the ICJ's caution). De facto Provisional recognition, where effective control exists but permanence is doubtful — looks backward to facts. Legal effect is retroactive to the date of effective control ; a de facto recognised Government may be sued in the recognising State's courts (see the classic English cases Haile Selassie v. Cable & Wireless Ltd. (1939) and The Arantzazu Mendi (1939) from the DU past papers). De jure Definitive recognition, once all criteria are satisfied — looks forward and is permanent. Requires the entity's acts to be given full effect; retroactivity nunc pro tunc to the date the entity came into existence (Luther v. Sagor (1921)). The recognition questions appear mostly in other topics, but they arise here because recognition is the jurisdictional gate through which an entity becomes a \"State\" for the purposes of the rights and immunities of States . 3. Sovereign equality and its nine corollaries UNGA Res. 2625 (XXV), 1970 — Declaration on Principles of International Law concerning Friendly Relations — elaborates the principle that all States enjoy sovereign equality, meaning: (i) juridical equality; (ii) the rights inherent in full sovereignty; (iii) the duty to respect the personality of other States; (iv) territorial integrity and political independence; (v) the right freely to choose and develop political, social, economic and cultural systems; (vi) the duty to comply fully and in good faith with international obligations and to live in peace. An illustration you can add — act of State and sovereign immunity A dramatic practical consequence of \"the State as a subject\" is the doctrine of the act of State and State immunity. In Kuwait Airways Corp. v. Iraqi Airways Co. [1995] 3 All ER 694 (in your folder), the House of Lords held that Iraq's seizure of KAC aircraft and their transfer to Iraqi Airways under Revolutionary Command Council Resolution 369 were acts of a sovereign character ( acta jure imperii ); accordingly, under s. 14(2)(a) of the State Immunity Act 1978 the airline could not claim immunity for them — because s. 14(2) withdraws immunity where a State entity is engaged in a commercial transaction — and the Court could look behind the State's own legislation; but the subsequent incorporation of the aircraft into the fleet was commercial use. Service of process on the Iraqi embassy was held invalid under s. 12(1) of the Act. The case captures perfectly the modern lesson: the State's personality confers immunity, but only for genuinely governmental acts . D. International Organisations as Subjects 1. Establishment and types Organisations began as nineteenth-century administrative unions — the Rhine and Danube river commissions, the International Telegraph Union (1865), the Universal Postal Union (1874). The 20th century produced the institutional generation of the League (1919), the ILO (1919) and finally the UN (1945), followed by the specialised agencies and a dense network of bodies: ILO, FAO, UNESCO, WHO, IMF, IBRD (World Bank), ICAO, IMO, ITU, WMO, WIPO, IFAD, UNIDO, IAEA (which reports to the GA and, where relevant, the SC), the WTO (1995), the ICC and ITLOS (created by treaty). 2. Personality: conferred, implied and objective No express Charter provision creates UN personality. The Court in 1949 built one out of the Organisation's characteristics: a centre with organs (Art. 7), special tasks, membership duties (Art. 2(5)), capacity to make recommendations, legal capacity and privileges and immunities (Arts. 104, 105), and the power to conclude agreements (Art. 63). Implied powers: an organisation \"must be deemed to have those powers which, though not expressly provided in the Charter, are conferred upon it by necessary implication as being essential to the performance of its duties.\" The doctrine was solidified in Certain Expenses of the United Nations (1962) ICJ Rep. 151, at 168, and restated in Legality of the Use by a State of Nuclear Weapons in Armed Conflict (WHO Advisory Opinion, 1996) — where the Court refused to imply a WHO competence to address the legality of nuclear weapons because it was not necessary for WHO's constitutional functions. Use both cases together: the doctrine is powerful but bounded by functional necessity . Objective personality: because 50 States representing the vast majority of the international community created the UN, it possesses personality erga omnes , \"and not merely personality recognized by them alone\" — so the UN may claim even against non-Member States. Absolute or relative? Brownlie's caution (worth stating in an answer): the practice of the UN and other organisations remains essentially functional and limited — organisations do not enjoy sovereignty, territory, or general jurisdictional competence. The correct statement is that personality is derivative, functional and relative . 3. Consequences of organisational personality Treaty-making capacity — the 1986 Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties between States and International Organisations (not yet in force) codifies it. Privileges and immunities — the General Convention on the Privileges and Immunities of the United Nations, 1946 (s. 35 is expressly relied on in Reparation ); the ICJ's Applicability of the Obligation to Arbitrate under Section 21 of the UN Headquarters Agreement (1988) — the PLO observer mission case. Capacity to bring claims — the holding of Reparation ; also the Organisation's right to functional protection of its agents. Responsibility of organisations — the ILC's Draft Articles on the Responsibility of International Organizations (2011), mirroring the State-responsibility Articles. Limits — an organisation cannot be a party to contentious proceedings before the ICJ (Art. 34(1) ICJ Statute); its personality is not the same as a State's, \"still less … a super-State.\" Prepared answer — \"Does the United Nations possess legal personality? Discuss with special reference to the Reparation for Injuries Case (1949).\" ① The question: definitional silence of the Charter → ② Facts of the opinion (GA Res. 258(III), 3 Dec. 1948; questions I and II) → ③ Court's reasoning: subjects need not be identical; the Charter's characteristics; the UN is a centre with organs and tasks; practice (1946 Convention s. 35; treaty-making) → ④ Holding: the UN is an international person but not a State and not a super-State; it may bring international claims for (a) damage to itself and (b) damage to its agents (functional protection); the nationality of the agent, even if that of the defendant State, is no bar → ⑤ Objective personality: 50 States representing the vast majority of the international community → ⑥ Follow-up authorities: Effect of Awards of the UNAT (1954); Certain Expenses (1962); Namibia (1971); WHO Nuclear Weapons (1996) for the limits → ⑦ Modern application: peacekeeping claims, the 1994 Safety of UN Personnel Convention, ILC 2011 Articles → ⑧ Conclusion: implied powers + functional necessity = the organisation's charter of personality. E. Individuals as Subjects — Rights, Duties and Standing 1. The old orthodoxy: the individual as object Under classical law the individual was an object of international law: diplomatic protection belonged to the State, piracy and slave-trading were suppressed through the medium of State responsibility, and no individual could appear before an international tribunal. The DU material records the traditional view with its textual support — Art. 34(1) of the ICJ Statute: \"only States may be parties in cases before the Court.\" 2. The transformation — five milestones Table 1.18 — How the individual moved from object to subject Milestone Instrument / Event Legal effect for the individual 1919 — Versailles Treaty of Versailles, Arts. 297 & 304 (Mixed Arbitral Tribunals); the ILO Constitution; the Minorities Treaties. Individuals could appear before mixed arbitral tribunals against States; minorities received treaty rights. (This is the \"beginning with the Treaty of Versailles\" of the 1992 DU question.) 1945 — Nuremberg London Charter, Art. 6; the Nuremberg and Tokyo judgments (1946). Individuals bear direct criminal responsibility : \"crimes against international law are committed by men, not by abstract entities, and only by punishing individuals who commit such crimes can the provisions of international law be enforced.\" Official position is no defence (Art. 7); superior orders no excuse (Art. 8). 1948 — Human Rights UDHR 1948 (GA Res. 217A(III)); the Genocide Convention 1948 (Art. IV — punishable rulers, public officials and private individuals). The individual becomes the beneficiary of rights declared at the international plane. 1966/1950 — Petitions ECHR 1950, Art. 34 — individual applications to the European Court; ICCPR 1966, Optional Protocol I — individual communications to the Human Rights Committee. Procedural capacity : the individual may initiate an international claim — the single most decisive step toward subjecthood. 1963 onward — Consular rights & criminal law VCCR 1963, Art. 36(1)(b); the Genocide, Torture, Hostage-taking and Terrorist-financing Conventions; the ICTY (1993) and ICTR (1994); the Rome Statute (1998). Individual rights enforceable before the ICJ through the national State ( LaGrand , Avena , Jadhav ) and individual liability before international criminal courts. 3. Individual rights — the consular trilogy (know these three) Table 1.19 — VCCR Article 36(1)(b) and the three ICJ cases Case Facts Holding LaGrand (Germany v. USA) ICJ Rep. 2001, p. 466 Two German brothers arrested, tried and executed in Arizona without consular notification. Art. 36(1) VCCR \"creates individual rights \" for the detained person; these may be invoked by the national State; provisional measures under Art. 41 are binding ; the US's procedural default rule could not defeat the obligation; \"review and reconsideration\" of conviction and sentence required. Avena (Mexico v. USA) ICJ Rep. 2004, p. 12 52 Mexican nationals sentenced to death in US courts without consular notification. Reaffirmed LaGrand ; the US must provide review and reconsideration of conviction and sentence, and the obligation applies notwithstanding US procedural rules. Jadhav (India v. Pakistan) ICJ Rep. 2019, p. 418 Kulbhushan Jadhav, an Indian national, arrested in Pakistan, sentenced to death by a military court; consular access denied for over three years; Pakistan did not inform India as required by Art. 36(1)(b) and Art. 36(1)(c). Pakistan breached Art. 36 ; the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations was not displaced by the 2008 India–Pakistan bilateral agreement; Pakistan must provide effective review and reconsideration of the conviction and sentence, by means of its own choosing, including consular access and consideration of India's assistance; the Court declined to order annulment/release or to direct acquittal (India's other pleas rejected). India's use of the case: India invoked the Optional Protocol to the VCCR conferring jurisdiction on the ICJ; the Provisional Measures Order of 18 May 2017 (India's first successful provisional-measures request) restrained the execution of the sentence, and the final judgment of 17 July 2019 was implemented through Pakistan's ordinance of 2020 creating a review mechanism — a live example of the ICJ's compliance pull even without enforcement. 4. Individual duties — international criminal responsibility Nuremberg Principles 1950 (formulated by the ILC at the GA's request) — Principle I: any person who commits an act which constitutes a crime under international law is responsible therefor and liable to punishment. Genocide Convention 1948, Art. IV — \"persons committing genocide … shall be punished, whether they are constitutionally responsible rulers, public officials or private individuals.\" Statutes of the ICTY (1993) / ICTR (1994) / Rome Statute (1998) — Art. 25 (individual criminal responsibility), Art. 27 (irrelevance of official capacity), Art. 28 (command responsibility). Immunity: the incumbent Head of State, Head of Government or Foreign Minister enjoys immunity ratione personae before foreign municipal courts — Arrest Warrant of 11 April 2000 (DRC v. Belgium) , ICJ Rep. 2002, p. 3 — but not before international criminal tribunals (Art. 27 Rome Statute; Al-Bashir referral). The 2024 ICJ judgment in Immunity of a Head of State (Equatorial Guinea v. France) confirmed that the immunity accrues to the State, not merely the official. 5. How much subjecthood? The balanced formulation The maximalist claim \"The rights and duties of States are in the final analysis rights and duties of individuals who compose the States and, therefore, individuals alone are the subjects of international law\" (Kelsen). Support: piracy; Nuremberg; individual petitions; the \"human rights revolution\"; obligations erga omnes and jus cogens ; investment arbitration (individuals and companies may sue States directly under ICSID — the 1965 Convention on the Settlement of Investment Disputes, cited in the DU material). The balanced (and correct) claim \"There is no general rule that the individual cannot be a 'subject of international law', and in particular contexts he appears as a legal person on the international plane\" — Ian Brownlie (the exact quotation in the 2015 DU paper). Limits: no general personality; no general treaty-making power; no standing before the ICJ (Art. 34(1)); rights are derivative, and are available only where a treaty confers them; nationality remains the principal connecting factor; enforcement still largely requires States. Real-world illustrations of individual subjecthood Diplomatic protection vs. functional protection: the classic contrast from Reparation — the State protects its national (diplomatic protection, with its prerequisites of continuous nationality and exhaustion of local remedies: Nottebohm (1955), Barcelona Traction (1970)); the organisation protects its agent , irrespective of nationality. Refugees and stateless persons: the 1951 Refugee Convention (Art. 33 non-refoulement) and the 1961 Statelessness Convention give individuals directly invocable status; the ILC's Draft Articles on Diplomatic Protection, 2006, keep the rule that a State may protect only nationals. Barcelona Traction (1970) para. 33: \"an essential distinction should be drawn between the obligations of a State towards the international community as a whole, and those arising vis-à-vis another State… By their very nature the former are the concern of all States … they are obligations erga omnes .\" This is the doctrinal bridge from the individual-in-State to the individual-in-the-international-community. India-context: Jadhav ; Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) (CEDAW read into Arts. 14/15/19/21); Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India (1996) (customary international environmental principles incorporated) — the individual's international rights working through municipal courts. F. Certain Non-State Entities Table 1.20 — Non-State entities and the nature of their personality Entity Basis of personality Limits / example Insurgents and belligerents Recognition of belligerency or insurgency; the laws of war apply; they may be held to humanitarian obligations. Antonio Cassese's argument; the Ilasq and the Spanish Civil War cases; Common Article 3 of the Geneva Conventions 1949 applies to \"parties to the conflict\". National liberation movements GA Res. 2621 (XXV), 1970 and the \"Additional Protocol I\" to the Geneva Conventions (Art. 96(3)) — they may declare their willingness to apply the Conventions. PLO, SWAPO, ANC — observer status; not full subjects. Peoples The right of self-determination — Charter Arts. 1(2), 55; GA Res. 1514(XV) 1960, 1541(XV) 1960, 2625(XXV) 1970; common Art. 1 ICCPR/ICESCR; the ICJ's *Chagos Advisory Opinion* (2019) — a staple of the 1.6 Third World topic. The right is held by \"peoples,\" not by individuals; secession is confined by the \"salt-water\" and remedial-secession debates ( Kosovo 2010; Quebec Reference , 1998). Holy See / Vatican City Historic sovereign personality; party to the Lateran Treaty 1929; member of the UN as a permanent observer. Party to many treaties (VCDR, VCLT, the Chemical Weapons Convention). Sovereign Order of Malta Historic personality; diplomatic relations with over 100 States; observer at the UN. No territory; personality retained by recognition. ICRC The 1949 Geneva Conventions (Arts. 9/9/9/10) confer a mandate to protect victims; observer status at the UN; has concluded headquarters agreements. A hybrid — a Swiss private association with international mandates; its role shows personality can be functional and partial. Multinational corporations Treaty-based: bilateral investment treaties; ICSID arbitration (the 1965 Convention cited in the DU material); multilateral: the Energy Charter Treaty; the Ruggie/UN Guiding Principles (2011) as soft law. Cannot be a party before the ICJ; cannot claim sovereign immunities; still not \"subjects\" in the classical sense — Barcelona Traction (1970) treats them through the shareholder/nationality lens. Indigenous peoples UNDRIP 2007 (GA Res. 61/295); ILO Convention 169 (1989); decisions of the Inter-American Court ( Awas Tingni , 2001). Collective rights recognised; consent-based consultation rights emerge in customary practice. Individuals before municipal courts — Taiwan, Northern Cyprus (Note them as \"unresolved entities\" for contrast.) Recognition disputes continue to define the boundary of subjecthood. Model answer structure — \"Is the individual a subject of international law?\" (a question asked in 2015, 2013, 2012, 2005, 2001, 1999, 1998, 1995, 1994, 1993, 1992 — memorise this structure) Definition of subject/personality — Reparation test; four constituents of legal capacity. The classical objection — Oppenheim, Sir Frederick Smith; Art. 34(1) ICJ Statute; the individual as object. The transformation thesis — Treaty of Versailles 1919 → Nuremberg 1945–46 → UDHR 1948 → ECHR Art. 34 (1950) → ICCPR OP1 (1966) → VCCR Art. 36 (1963) → Genocide/Torture/Terrorism Conventions → ICTY/ICTR/ICC. Specific rights — LaGrand , Avena , Jadhav ; individual petitions; ICSID; human-rights tourism before regional courts. Specific duties — piracy, Nuremberg Principle I, Genocide Art. IV, Rome Statute Arts. 25, 27; Arrest Warrant and its limits. Balance — Brownlie: \"no general rule that the individual cannot be a subject… in particular contexts he appears as a legal person on the international plane.\" No general treaty-making power; no ICJ standing; nationality still the key; personality is partial, derivative and functional . Conclusion — the individual is a subject in specific contexts ; international law has moved from a law about States to a law for the benefit of human beings, but the State remains the primary subject — the two are complementary, not exclusive. ↑ back to contents 1.6 Third World and International Law The last line of the syllabus — and one that DU examiners love because it separates a textbook answer from a thinking one. The 2025 paper's question paper even highlights this head in red ink. The treatment below follows S.K. Verma's Chapter 1 (§IV \"New States and International Law\", pp. 19–23) and adds the TWAIL literature. A. Who are the \"Third World\"? Definitions and Terminology \" Third World \" — coined in the Cold War as a description of the non-aligned group (India, Yugoslavia, Egypt, Indonesia, Ghana), and used today to describe the developing States of Asia, Africa and Latin America, of which the great majority won independence after 1945. \" New States \" — the term Verma uses, with a caveat that it \"is considered a misnomer\"; R.L. Friedheim and J.J.G. Syatauw suggest the more accurate term \" dissatisfied States \": States that accept much of the existing law, but reject the parts which were procured against them or in their absence. \" TWAIL \" — Third World Approaches to International Law — a scholarly movement (R.P. Anand, B.S. Chimni, Upendra Baxi, Antony Anghie, Makau Mutua, James Gathii, Obiora Okafor) which argues that international law is not a neutral, benign body of rules, but a system whose categories (sovereignty, territory, statehood, the \"standard of civilisation\") were forged in, and continue to reproduce, colonial relations. B. The Historical Grievance — Law as Colonial Instrument Exclusion from the \"club.\" Oppenheim's definition — \"civilised states\" — illustrates the point. As Verma notes, the law was \"European Public Law applicable among the Christian nations\"; \"even though China had 5,000 years of old culture, she was not included in the group of civilised states.\" Turkey became the first non-European subject only in the mid-nineteenth century; the League of Nations (1920) marked the \"beginning of a new era\" of universality. Doctrine as instrument. The critical narratives: terra nullius and the denial of sovereignty to indigenous polities (a doctrine the ICJ rejected, for the purposes of the Western Sahara advisory opinion, in the Sahul pleadings and finally in Western Sahara (1975); and which Australia repudiated in Mabo v. Queensland (No. 2) (1992)); the \"standard of civilisation\"; the \"civilising mission\"; the Berlin Conference (1884–85) and the \"effective occupation\" test; the uti possidetis juris doctrine; and, most vividly, the system of capitulations \"by which their nationals were subject to their national consular courts rather than to the national law of the capitulating State, such as in China, Iran, Turkey.\" Colonial \"consent.\" Because most Asian and African territories were not sovereign members of the international community in 1945, they were bound by the existing corpus of custom without having participated in its formation — a fact TWAIL writers say is fatal to the positivist account of consent, and which the law conceals by the technique of \"tacit acquiescence.\" C. The Third World's Positive Contribution — Six Reorientations Table 1.21 — Third World interventions in the substance of international law Zone of law The Third World's move Instrument / authority 1. Decolonisation & self-determination Constitutionalised the right of peoples to self-determination and made colonial rule illegitimate. \"Their support to the principle of self-determination saw the end of colonialism.\" Charter Arts. 1(2), 55; GA Res. 1514(XV) , Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples, 14 Dec. 1960; GA Res. 1541(XV) (principles for determining when non-self-governing territory has reached full self-government); ICJ Western Sahara (1975) and Chagos (2019). 2. Permanent sovereignty over natural resources Asserted the right of a State to control, nationalise and dispose of its resources and to exclude foreign capital. GA Res. 1803(XVII), 1962 — Permanent Sovereignty over Natural Resources; GA Res. 3171(XXVIII), 1973; NIEO declarations 1974. 3. New International Economic Order (NIEO) Demanded a restructuring of global economic relations: preferential and non-reciprocal trade benefits, technology transfer, commodity price stabilisation, sovereign debt relief and the right to regulate multinational corporations. GA Res. 3201 (S-VI) , Declaration on the Establishment of a NIEO, 1 May 1974; GA Res. 3281(XXIX) , Charter of Economic Rights and Duties of States, 12 Dec. 1974 (adopted 120–6–10, against the Western States). 4. Common heritage of mankind Extended the idea of shared management to the deep seabed, the moon and outer space, and to the atmosphere. Arvid Pardo's 1967 Malta proposal; the UNCLOS 1982 Part XI and the Parallel System of seabed mining; the Moon Treaty 1979; the Antarctic Treaty 1959 (and the criticism of its \"club\" character); the 1994 Implementation Agreement (Part XI) as a compromise with the industrialised States. 5. Human rights — the third generation Pushed collective rights (development, environment, peace, self-determination) onto the human rights agenda and argued that the two Covenants of 1966 are indivisible . Declaration on the Right to Development 1986; UDHR + ICCPR + ICESCR as the International Bill of Rights; the Vienna Declaration 1993; Group of 77 and the Non-Aligned Movement positions in the Human Rights Council. 6. Institutional reform Demanded equitable representation: more ILC seats; more judges from Asia, Africa and Latin America on the ICJ; expansion of the Security Council; an end to weighted voting in the IMF and World Bank. UN Charter Art. 23 (SC) proposed reform — the \"G4\" (India, Brazil, Germany, Japan) and the African \"Ezulwini Consensus\"; Verma's observation that \"they also challenged the Bretton Woods Agreement, which has the provision for weighted voting in the International Monetary Fund.\" Fig. 1.8 — Third World & international law: grievance, instruments, counter-push and today's agenda D. The Structural Problem the Third World's Numbers Created Verma presents the dialectic fairly — state both sides for full marks: The Third World's gains \"They are closely associated in the codification and formulation of new norms of international conduct for States by their participation in the deliberations of the International Law Commission.\" They \"have helped in giving new directions to the law on State responsibility by not confining it only to the treatment of aliens, but also to other breaches of international law as well as affecting the rules concerning the use of force.\" They obtained an equitable representation of \"the principal civilisations of the world\" on the ICJ (Africa 3, Asia 3, Latin America 2, Eastern Europe 2, Western Europe and others 5). The enduring difficulty \"The explosion of new States and their unified stand on many issues … is making it increasingly difficult to adopt new rules.\" \"The western countries are wary of the 'terror of majority' unleashed by new nations.\" \"The first major casualty of this new phenomenon is the principle of unanimity for the adoption of the rules of international law.\" It \"not only makes difficult new customary rules to be evolved, but also the adoption and implementation of multilateral treaties.\" Possible outcomes: agreement on the lowest common denominator — \"some general, imprecise or ambiguous norms\"; or a shift from universality to regional law (as in Latin America and the EU). Verma's own synthesis — memorise this as the conclusion: \"As States engage in varied transnational activities, some commonality of interest, howsoever limited, will always remain and that can become the basis for framing and evolving international law with universal application. With increasing interdependence among nations and the global effects of new technological developments affecting outer space, sea-bed and the environment, there has been a growing awareness about the need for universal law or the 'common law of mankind', which should transcend the national borders and should have within its ambit individuals, corporations, international institutions, States and other groups.\" E. India's Contribution — a Ready-Made Section for Any Answer Founding member of the UN (1945) and an early and active participant in the ILC; Sir Benegal Narsing Rau was a member of the ILC and the principal architect of the draft that became the Genocide Convention and of the 1950 report on reservations. Non-alignment and peaceful co-existence — the Panchsheel principles (1954) and the NAM (Belgrade, 1961) as a Third-World alternative to bloc politics; Jawaharlal Nehru's advocacy of peaceful settlement and decolonisation. Goa 1961 — the Third-World argument in action (self-determination against colonial sovereignty), not condemned as aggression by the SC or the GA. Nuclear disarmament — the Rajiv Gandhi Action Plan (1988); India's refusal to sign the NPT as discriminatory; the 1996 ICJ Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion, where India argued for an obligation to negotiate in good faith. Trade and development — the GATT/WTO Special and Differential Treatment; the TRIPS and public-health debate; India's TRIPS waiver proposal (2020) with South Africa. Judicial contribution — Radhabinod Pal's dissenting opinion at the Tokyo Tribunal (1946–48); Judge Nagendra Singh (President of the ICJ, 1985–88); Judge Dalveer Bhandari (ICJ, since 2012); Dr. Neeru Chadha (ITLOS, since 2017); Justice A.S. Anand and others in the Human Rights Committee. Municipal reception — Constitution Arts. 51(c) and 253; Berubari (1960), Jolly George Varghese (1980), Gramophone Co. (1984), Vellore (1996), Vishaka (1997), Puttaswamy (2017). ↑ back to contents ★ Case Laws Prescribed for Topic 1 — Full Treatment The DU LB-205 syllabus prescribes exactly two authorities at the end of Topic 1. Both are treated below in the format examiners expect: Facts → Issues → Arguments of both sides → Decision → Principles evolved → Commentary and subsequent application. A third sub-section collects the twenty-one supporting authorities that appear throughout Topic 1. CASE 1 — Reparation for Injuries Suffered in the Service of the United Nations (Advisory Opinion, ICJ Reports 1949, p. 174 (11 April 1949).)",
            "Application of the International Convention for the Suppression of the Financing of Terrorism and of the International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination (Ukraine v. Russian Federation) (ICJ Reports (2019). The judgment prescribed is the Judgment on Preliminary Objections of 8 November 2019 . Related stages: Provisional Measures Order of 19 April 2017 ; Merits Judgment of 31 January 2024 .)",
            "Supporting Authorities — the twenty-one cases that carry Topic 1 These are the authorities that DU answers are expected to weave in. Each entry gives the proportion for which the case is cited. Table 1.25 — Supporting authorities, proportion and short facts Case Citation What it decides — and where to use it Paquete Habana (1900) 175 US 677 \"International law is a part of our law\" — Justice Gray. \"Is international law true law?\" and monism in the US. SS Lotus (France v. Turkey) PCIJ Ser. A, No. 10 (1927) Restrictions on the independence of States are not to be presumed; consent-based jurisdiction; opinio juris. Positivism; listed in DU Topic 2. North Sea Continental Shelf ICJ Rep. 1969, p. 3 The two-element test of custom; a treaty provision may generate custom if of a \"fundamentally norm-creating character\"; Judge Sorensen's dissent on codification/progressive development. 1.3 & the \"true law\" debate. Effect of Awards of Compensation made by the UN Administrative Tribunal ICJ Rep. 1954, p. 47 The GA cannot refuse to give effect to a UNAT award; the Tribunal's competence derives from the Organisation's implied powers; award has res judicata effect. 1.5 — personality of the UN. Certain Expenses of the United Nations ICJ Rep. 1962, p. 151 Peacekeeping expenses are \"expenses of the Organization\"; broad reading of implied powers; note Verma's remark that political pressure can also block sanctions. 1.2/1.4. Namibia (South West Africa) ICJ Rep. 1971, p. 16 SC decisions under Art. 25 are binding (para. 113); abstention by a permanent member does not defeat a resolution (para. 22); the Mandate was lawfully terminated; South Africa's presence illegal. 1.4 SC sanctions. Western Sahara ICJ Rep. 1975, p. 12 No terra nullius for organised peoples; self-determination and the wishes of the inhabitants; the \"AOU\" (Agreement on Oil) cases. 1.5/1.6. United States Diplomatic and Consular Staff in Tehran (USA v. Iran) ICJ Rep. 1980, p. 3 Inviolability of diplomatic premises and personnel (VCDR 1961); Iran responsible even for the acts of private militants once it acquiesced; the UDHR relied upon at para. 91. 1.4/1.5. Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. USA) ICJ Rep. 1986, p. 14 Custom identical to treaty rules does not \"supervene\"; GA Res. 2625/3314 and opinio juris; US breach of non-intervention and non-use of force; and the award that the US refused to obey. 1.4 (compliance) & 1.2. Barcelona Traction (Belgium v. Spain) ICJ Rep. 1970, p. 3 Erga omnes obligations (para. 33–34); the company as an international actor only through the State of nationality; diplomatic protection. 1.5 Subjects. LaGrand (Germany v. USA) ICJ Rep. 2001, p. 466 VCCR Art. 36(1) creates individual rights ; provisional measures under Art. 41 are binding . 1.4/1.5. Avena (Mexico v. USA) ICJ Rep. 2004, p. 12 Reaffirms LaGrand ; obligation of review and reconsideration applies notwithstanding domestic procedural rules. 1.5. Arrest Warrant of 11 April 2000 (DRC v. Belgium) ICJ Rep. 2002, p. 3 Incumbent Head of State/Government/Foreign Minister enjoy immunity ratione personae before foreign courts — even for international crimes; but international tribunals are unaffected. 1.5 individuals. Legality of the Threat or Use of Nuclear Weapons (AO) ICJ Rep. 1996 (I), p. 226 GA resolutions as evidence of opinio juris (para. 70); \"the threat or use of nuclear weapons would generally be contrary to the rules of international law applicable in armed conflict\"; the famous non liquet on extreme circumstances. 1.2/1.4. Legality of the Use by a State of Nuclear Weapons (WHO AO) ICJ Rep. 1996, p. 66 Implied powers are limited by functional necessity — WHO lacks competence to address the legality of nuclear weapons. 1.5 limits on organisation personality. Gabčíkovo–Nagymaros (Hungary/Slovakia) ICJ Rep. 1997, p. 7 Treaty termination and countermeasures; the consequences of a State's own wrongful conduct; reparation and restitution. 1.4 countermeasures. Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro (Genocide) ICJ Rep. 2007, p. 43 State responsibility for failure to prevent genocide; Art. IX jurisdiction; individual responsibility distinguished from State responsibility. 1.5/1.4. Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo (AO) ICJ Rep. 2010, p. 403 The Court declined to decide whether Kosovo was a State; general international law contains no prohibition of declarations of independence. 1.5/1.6. Legal Consequences of the Separation of the Chagos Archipelago (Mauritius) (AO) ICJ Rep. 2019, p. 169 Self-determination is an obligation erga omnes ; the UK's detachment of the Chagos Archipelago was unlawful; the \"Mauritius\" opinion underlies the 2024 UK–Mauritius agreement and the UNGA resolution of May 2024. 1.6. Jadhav (India v. Pakistan) ICJ Rep. 2019, p. 418 VCCR Art. 36 breach; effective review and reconsideration; India's leading contemporary ICJ case. 1.4/1.5. Kuwait Airways Corp. v. Iraqi Airways Co. [1995] 3 All ER 694 (HL) Act of State and State immunity (acta jure imperii vs gestionis); s. 14(2) State Immunity Act 1978; service on an embassy under s. 12(1) invalid. 1.5 — the State as a subject. One-line \"principle\" chestnuts (memorise for the case-proportion questions) Reparation → implied powers, functional protection, objective personality. Ukraine v. Russia → \"any person\" includes State agents; State financing outside ICSFT; alternative preconditions under CERD Art. 22. North Sea → two elements of custom; treaty provisions of \"norm-creating character\". Nicaragua → custom survives treaty; GA resolutions as opinio juris; US non-compliance. LaGrand → individual rights; provisional measures are binding. Jadhav → consular notification is an individual right; effective review and reconsideration. ↑ back to contents ★ Previous Year Questions (DU Semester Exams) with Model Answers About this section — read the provenance note The May/June 2025 LB-205 paper reproduced below is the official paper supplied in your project folder. The PYQ bank for 1992–2015 is drawn from a widely used DU LL.B. student archive of past question papers on Public International Law. Older papers are reproduced in the condensed form in which they appear in that archive; where the archive's text was garbled, the question has been normalised and marked. Questions are grouped by theme because the same question recurs in different years with near-identical wording — learn the theme, not the year. A. The Most Recent Paper — DU LL.B. II/IV/VI Semester Examination, May/June 2025 (Paper Code LB-205) Duration / Marks 3 Hours · Maximum Marks 100 · Answer any five questions out of eight (all questions carry 20 marks) Medium English or Hindi (uniform throughout) Table 2.1 — The 2025 paper at a glance, and which questions belong to Topic 1 Q. Subject-matter Topic Topic-1 link 1 Subjects of international law / \"international legal personality\"; plus a hypothetical : a UN employee of nationality X murdered by a secret service agency of country Y — the UN brings a claim. Discuss maintainability with decided cases. 1.5 + 1.1 Direct — Reparation (1949) 2 State of Tarkin funds a rebel group in Yoda; Yoda sues before the ICJ — decide responsibility. (Nicaragua-style) 4 (State responsibility) Indirect 3 Extradition as a matter of courtesy; principles; role of municipal law; extradition without a treaty 6 (Jurisdiction/Extradition) — 4 Short notes on any two: (1) Warships and rights of innocent passage; (2) Sanctions under International Law ; (3) General Assembly Resolution as a source of international law; (4) Treaties as a source of international law. 5 / 1.4 / 2 Direct on 4(2) 5 Blackstonian doctrine and the relationship of international law with municipal law; Indian practice with cases 3 — 6 Rights and duties of coastal States in territorial waters and contiguous zone; measurement where the coastline is deeply indented with a fringe of islands 5 — 7 Diplomats vs. consuls; the \"UFO embassy in Zedex\" hostage scenario under the VCDR 1961 7 — 8 \"General principles of law recognised by civilised nations\" — Art. 38(1)(c); Tunkin's objection; your opinion with decided cases 2 — Model answer — 2025, Q.1 (Subjects / personality + the UN claim hypothetical), 20 marks (i) Opening — the test. An entity is a subject of international law if it possesses international legal personality: \"capable of possessing international rights and duties, and … the capacity to maintain its rights by bringing international claims\" — Reparation for Injuries (1949). Legal personality comprises four capacities: to make claims; to conclude treaties; to enjoy privileges and immunities; and to be the bearer of obligations (and responsibility for their breach). (ii) Theories. ① States only — Oppenheim (1905), Sir Frederick Smith; Art. 34(1) ICJ Statute. ② Individuals only — Kelsen. ③ The modern, functional view — States remain primary, but organisations, individuals and certain non-State entities hold graded personality. Cite Reparation (Organsiation); Barcelona Traction (States and companies); LaGrand / Jadhav (individuals). (iii) The subject categories. • States — Montevideo criteria 1933; sovereignty, equality (GA Res. 2625(XXV)); recognition (declaratory vs. constitutive, Lauterpacht; Haile Selassie (1939), Arantzazu Mendi (1939)). • International organisations — established by treaty; personality inferred from functions (\"implied powers\"); objective personality ( Reparation ); privileges and immunities (1946 General Convention); limits ( WHO Nuclear Weapons AO, 1996); UNAT Awards AO (1954). • Individuals — as beneficiaries of rights (UDHR 1948; ICCPR OP1; ECHR Art. 34; VCCR Art. 36 — LaGrand , Avena , Jadhav ) and bearers of duties (piracy; Nuremberg Principle I; Genocide Art. IV; Rome Statute Arts. 25, 27, 28) — but no general treaty-making power and no standing before the ICJ. • Non-State entities — insurgents, national liberation movements, peoples (self-determination), the Holy See, the ICRC, indigenous peoples (UNDRIP 2007), MNCs (ICSID). (iv) The hypothetical. A UN employee of nationality X is murdered by a secret service agency of State Y; the UN claims against Y. Applying Reparation : Maintainability ratione personae: the UN is an international person with objective personality, so it may bring a claim even if Y is a non-Member (\"fifty States, representing the vast majority of the international community, had the power … to bring into being an entity possessing objective international personality\"). Question I(a): the UN may claim for damage to itself — its administrative machine, property, assets and \"interests of which it is the guardian.\" Question I(b): the UN may also claim reparation for the damage suffered by the victim or persons entitled through him — because it \"does not represent the agent, but is asserting its own right\" that obligations due to it be respected; the nationality of the agent (even that of the defendant State) is \"not pertinent to the admissibility of the claim,\" since the claim rests on the agent's status , not his nationality. Agency: the Court requires the injured person to be an \"agent\" of the Organisation, performing duties, with the damage arising \"in circumstances involving the responsibility of a State.\" The employment relationship is the effective test of agency — his nationality is irrelevant. Question II: where the national State also has a right of diplomatic protection, \"there is no rule of law which assigns priority to the one or to the other\"; the two rights may be exercised independently, and Art. 100 independence justifies functional protection. Attribution: the acts must be attributable to Y (a secret service agency is a State organ — ILC ARSIWA Art. 4) and must constitute the breach of an international obligation owed to the UN; responsibility towards the UN rather than towards State X is the basis. (v) Conclusion. Maintainable: the UN may bring a claim for both heads of damage; if Y is the State of nationality of the agent, the claim is still admissible because the UN asserts its own right. This is exactly the situation in which Reparation arose — Bernadotte, a Swedish national, was assassinated while serving as UN Mediator in Palestine. B. Topic-1 PYQ Bank (1992–2015) — Thematic Classification Table 2.2 — Topic-1 questions classified by theme, with the years in which they appeared # Theme Years Model answer 1 Is international law \"true law\"? Austin's \"positive morality\" thesis; sanctions 2011, 2000, 1998, 1995, 1994, 1993, 1992 → below, §B.1 2 Traditional and modern definitions of international law 1999, 2002 → §B.2 3 Basis of international law — jurisprudential theories 2015, 1992 → §B.3 4 Development of international law; has it fulfilled its object? 2000, 2002 → §B.4 5 Three generations of human rights; implementation of ICCPR/ICESCR 2015, 2013, 2012, 2011 → §B.5 6 Sanctions as a mode for enforcement; \"sanctions determine the effectiveness of international law\" 2015, 2013, 1999; 2025 Q4(2) → §B.6 7 Role, functions and powers of the UN Human Rights Council 2015 → §B.7 8 Is the individual a subject of international law? 2015 (×2), 2013, 2012, 2002, 2001, 1999, 1998, 1995, 1994, 1993, 1992 → §B.8 9 UN legal personality / Reparation case; international organisations as subjects 2015, 2000, 2001, 1998 → §B.9 10 Tests/constituents of legal capacity and personality 2002, 1999, 1998 → §B.10 11 Taylor-made scenario questions using fictional States (Tarkin/Yoda; Slavania/Paktonia; Gabriel/Inkowa; Timbakto) 2025, 2002, 1999, 1995, 1994, 1993, 1992 → §B.11 B.1 Model answer — \"'International law is not true law but positive international morality.' Discuss critically this statement of Austin and spell out the sanctions of international law in detail.\" 2011 1998 1995 1994 1993 1992 2000 1. Introduction. The question is not whether international law exists, but whether it satisfies the definition of \"law\". Austin's command theory (following Hobbes, Bentham, Pufendorf and Holland) holds that law is a command of a determinate sovereign enforced by sanction; international law lacks both a legislature and an enforcement agency, and is therefore only \"positive international morality\" — for Austin it is \"law improperly so-called,\" analogous to rules binding a club. 2. Austin's six reasons. (i) no determinate superior political authority; (ii) no effective legislative machinery; (iii) no sanction; (iv) no executive to enforce ICJ decisions or treaties; (v) no potent judiciary with compulsory jurisdiction; (vi) hence it is \"quasi-law\". 3. Foundations of the command theory criticised. (a) Austin's own admission that \"the essential part of constitutional law is nothing more than positive morality\" (Goodhart, 1936) shows the theory over-excludes: British constitutional law, the basic norm of the British Constitution, and the common law itself cannot satisfy the test. (b) Sir Henry Maine's historical jurisprudence proved that in primitive societies there were laws without a sovereign. (c) Modern jurisprudence (Hart, Kelsen, Fitzmaurice) does not require a sovereign command — law is a system of rules recognised by officials, and in a decentralised order, sanctions operate through self-help. 4. Starke's four answers. (i) formal legislative authority is not essential to law (primitive communities; customary systems); (ii) international legislation exists — law-making treaties (Charter, Geneva, Hague, Vienna Conventions); (iii) authoritative agencies do not treat it as mere morality — States plead treaties and precedent; (iv) the UN and the ICJ are founded on the true legality of international law — Art. 38 ICJ Statute, Art. 59, Art. 94 UN Charter. 5. Further answers. Paquete Habana (1900) — \"International law is a part of our law\"; Hart — \"international law is law because States regard it as law. Nothing need be further proved\"; Kelsen — international law is a \"coercive order\" whose sanctions are reprisals and war, lawful only as countermeasures; Brierly — it is \"more honoured in the observance than in the breaches\"; and the frequency of violations no more disproves its legality than municipal crime disproves municipal law (Oppenheim: violations \"may strain its legal force to breaking point; the formal … affirmation of its binding nature is not without significance\"). 6. Sanctions in detail — the second half of the question. Structure it in three layers: (a) Centralised sanctions: Chapter VII of the Charter — Art. 39 determination, Art. 40 provisional measures, Art. 41 (non-military: interruption of economic relations and of rail, sea, air, postal, telegraphic, radio and other means of communication, and severance of diplomatic relations), Art. 42 (military action by air, sea and land forces); Art. 25 makes decisions binding ( Namibia , 1971). Examples: Rhodesia (Res. 232, 1966 — the first mandatory sanctions), Iraq (Res. 661, 1990), Libya (1970, 1973), DPRK (1718), the targeted regimes 1267/1373/1540. (b) Judicial and institutional sanctions: Art. 94(1) and (2) — ICJ judgments and recourse to the SC; Art. 41 ICJ Statute (provisional measures, now binding per LaGrand ); GA Arts. 5, 6 and 19 (suspension, expulsion, loss of vote — the South Africa, Haiti and Somalia precedents); GA Res. 377(V) \"Uniting for Peace\"; HRC UPR and special procedures; ITLOS Art. 296; ICC arrest warrants (Art. 27) with SC referrals in Darfur and Libya; PCA awards enforced by consent and reputation. (c) Horizontal and informal sanctions: retorsion; countermeasures under ARSIWA Arts. 22, 49–54 — must be proportionate (Art. 51; Air Services Agreement (1978); cf. Naulilaa (1928)) and may not involve force or breach fundamental human rights or humanitarian obligations (Art. 50); and adverse public opinion, by which \"no State would like to be branded as the violator of human rights\" (Verma) — the sanctions of any organised community that lacks a police. 7. Conclusion. Austin's theory rests on a definition of law that cannot survive the actual plurality of legal systems. International law is law — but law of a special, decentralised kind: as Brierly put it, it \"operates in a decentralised political system. There is no world legislature, no international police, and no international court with compulsory jurisdiction.\" Hence Starke's conclusion: it is \"weak law\" — not no law. B.2 Model answer — \"A sharp contrast exists between the traditional and modern definitions of international law, yet they share some common features. Mark out the distinctions … and the way they have reoriented international law.\" 1999 (cf. 2002 ) Structure. (i) Oppenheim 1905 as the traditional definition; (ii) five criticisms (organisations; \"civilised\"; individuals; general principles; static conception); (iii) the modern definitions — Oppenheim 8th and 9th editions, Fenwick, Jenks, Brierly, Kelsen; (iv) common features retained: a body of rules , binding , governing relations between distinct political communities, backed by the possibility of sanction; (v) the reorientation: from a European law of coexistence to a universal law of cooperation; from a State-only law to a law of a community of States and peoples; from a law about war to a law about peace and welfare; (vi) conclusion — the definition has followed the community, and the community has become universal (\" ubi societas ibi jus \"). Table to draw (one-liner): Traditional (State-centric; sources = custom and treaty; subjects = States; content = coexistence and war; geography = European; sanction = war and reprisal) vs. Modern (multi-actor; sources = + general principles, resolutions, unilateral acts; subjects = States, organisations, individuals; content = cooperation, human rights, development, environment; geography = universal 193 States; sanction = institutions, countermeasures and opinion). B.3 Model answer — \"Explain the basis of international law with the help of jurisprudential theories.\" 2015 1992 1. The question. \"Basis\" = the source of the binding force of international law; it is distinct from the question of sources (Art. 38). Three broad families of answers have been given: nature, will, and society. 2. Natural law school. Greek Stoics and Roman jus naturale/jus gentium ; divine law in the medieval period; secularisation by Vittoria, Suarez, Gentilis and above all Grotius, for whom natural law was \"the dictate of right reason\". Sub-schools after Grotius: naturalists (Pufendorf), positivists (Bynkershoek) and Grotians (Wolff, Vattel — the \"necessary Law of Nations\"). The offshoot doctrine of fundamental rights — self-preservation, independence, equality, respect, intercourse — with Brierly's criticism that a right presupposes a legal system and that the doctrine denies growth. Maine's tribute: \"the greatest function of the 'law of nature' was discharged in giving birth to modern International Law.\" Twentieth-century revival: human rights and international criminal law. 3. Positivism and consent. Law as it is ; the will of the State as the source — Zorn (a branch of State law), Triepel (Vereinbarung/agreement), Anzilotti (pacta sunt servanda), Jellinek (auto-limitation), Oppenheim (common consent). Judicial expression: Lotus (1927) — restrictions on the independence of States cannot be presumed. Criticisms: cannot explain custom binding on new States (see the Third World problem, §1.6) or jus cogens ; and \"consent\" is often a fiction (tacit acquiescence). 4. Sociological and policy schools. Duguit and Scelle (social solidarity); McDougal and Lasswell (authoritative decision-making for community values); Kelsen's pure theory (the hierarchy of norms and the Grundnorm ; law as a coercive order operating through self-help). 5. The modern synthesis. Ubi societas ibi jus : the basis of obligation is the existence of an international community , whose members recognise the law as binding and habitually comply — the \"law habit\" (Fitzmaurice); Brierly's observation that violations are few compared to the vast body of observance; and the practical answer that the basis of obligation is a combination of consent (for treaties), practice and opinio juris (for custom), and the necessities of coexistence and cooperation for everything else. 6. Conclusion. No single theory explains the whole. The naturalists explain why States ought to obey; the positivists explain what they have agreed to; the sociological school explains why they do, in fact, obey. Together they describe a legal order that is real, binding, and decentralised. B.4 Model answer — \"In the long march of mankind from the cave to the computer a central role has always been played by the idea of law … explain the main reasons responsible for a systematic development of international law. How far do you agree that international law has by and large fulfilled its objective?\" 2000 (cf. 2002 ) Part A — reasons for systematic development (use the five-stage timeline of Fig. 1.2): (i) needs of commerce and communication (river commissions, IMO, ICAO, ITU, UPU); (ii) the destructiveness of war — Geneva 1864, St. Petersburg 1868, the Hague Conventions, the League, the Kellogg–Briand Pact, the UN Charter; (iii) the expansion of the international community beyond Europe (Turkey, Latin America, decolonisation; the League's \"any State\" and the UN's \"peace-loving States\"); (iv) institutionalisation — the League, PCIJ, UN, ICJ, specialised agencies, ICC, ITLOS, WTO; (v) the emergence of the individual as a subject and the human-rights movement (Versailles 1919, Nuremberg 1945, UDHR 1948, the Covenants 1966); (vi) new domains — air law 1919/1944, outer space 1967, the seabed and UNCLOS 1982, the environment (Stockholm 1972, UNCLOS, UNFCCC 1992, Paris 2015), cyber and climate; (vii) codification through the ILC and diplomatic conferences. Part B — evaluation (\"has it fulfilled its object?\"): Successes — 193 States living under a common Charter; the prohibition of force in Art. 2(4) and the near-universal renunciation of conquest; decolonisation achieved largely through law and institutions; the human-rights corpus; the prohibition of genocide and the growth of individual criminal accountability; the settlement of thousands of boundary disputes; the law of the sea; a functioning criminal court. Failures — the veto and paralysed collective security (Syria, Crimea, Ukraine 2022); selectivity in enforcement; humanitarian catastrophes under sanctions (Iraq); failure to prevent genocide (Rwanda 1994, Srebrenica 1995); climate inaction; inequality of economic institutions; the persistence of the Third World's grievance (§1.6). Conclusion. International law has not abolished war or injustice, but it has made violence costly, disputes justiciable and accountability possible (Nuremberg, ICTY, ICTR, ICC). Its object is not the elimination of conflict but its regulation. As Verma puts it: \"Legal institutions can never guarantee lasting peace and security … what matters is that law responds to the changing needs of society.\" B.5 Model answer — \"What are the three generations of human rights? Discuss in detail the implementation of the human rights recognised by the ICCPR 1966 and the ICESCR 1966.\" 2015 2013 2012 2011 Part A — three generations (Vašák's classification): ① First generation — civil and political rights (life, liberty, fair trial, expression, religion, vote); negative rights; roots in the 18th-century natural-rights philosophy of Locke and Rousseau; UDHR Arts. 3–21; ICCPR 1966 and its two Optional Protocols. ② Second generation — economic, social and cultural rights (work, fair wages, social security, health, education, food, housing, cultural participation); positive rights; roots in socialism and the Mexican (1917) and Soviet (1918) constitutions; UDHR Arts. 22–27; ICESCR 1966 and its Optional Protocol 2008. ③ Third generation — collective or solidarity rights (development, healthy environment, peace, self-determination, communication, humanitarian assistance); emerged in the 1970s with \"the predominant support of developing countries\"; the Declaration on the Right to Development 1986; Stockholm/Rio. Note the critique — many Western scholars doubt that group rights are human rights because they vest in no individual; and the positive duty aspects are hard to realise, as the fate of the NIEO shows. The two Covenants together with the UDHR and the protocols form the International Bill of Rights . Part B — implementation of the ICCPR. (i) Reporting: State Parties report to the Human Rights Committee (18 independent experts) under Art. 40; (ii) Inter-State communications under Art. 41 (declaration-based; never used in practice); (iii) Individual communications under the First Optional Protocol — the Committee expresses \"views\" (adopted 66–2–38; in force 23 March 1976); (iv) General Comments interpreting the Covenant (e.g., GC 34 on freedom of expression); (v) the Second Optional Protocol on abolition of the death penalty (1989). India is party to the Covenant and to OP1, with a reservation on Art. 1 (self-determination). Illustrations of impact: Toonen v. Australia (1994) — decriminalisation of homosexuality in Tasmania; the Committee's jurisprudence on arbitrary detention, torture and the death penalty. Part C — implementation of the ICESCR. (i) Reporting to the Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (since 1987); (ii) the Committee's General Comments (e.g., GC 14 on the right to health; GC 15 on water; GC 24 on business and human rights); (iii) the Optional Protocol (2008) allowing individual and inter-State communications and inquiries; (iv) \"progressive realisation\" and the minimum core (Art. 2(1)); (v) the special role of the softer machinery, and of national courts (India: Vishaka 1997 for workplace harassment; Puttaswamy 2017 for privacy; the right to food litigation under Art. 21, the National Food Security Act 2013). Part D — assessment. The Covenant machinery is treaty-based, not coercive: \"most of these instruments provide the machinery and procedure, albeit not so perfect, for the implementation and monitoring compliance … an individual does not have any direct recourse to any international organ to enforce its rights, and in certain cases … non-binding recommendations are generally made.\" Contrast the European Convention 1950, whose Art. 34 individual application and Art. 46 supervision by the Committee of Ministers give the individual a real remedy — \"except under the European Convention, a similar situation exists under other regional treaties as well.\" ↑ back to contents B.6 Model answer — \"Discuss sanctions as a mode for enforcement of international law.\" 2013 / \"Sanctions determine the effectiveness of international law. Comment.\" 2015 / \"Sanctions in international law\" (short note) 1999 1. Concept. A sanction is the consequence attached to a breach of an international obligation, intended to induce compliance. Because international society is decentralised, sanctions are plural: some are institutional, some judicial, some horizontal, some purely reputational. Kelsen's premise: the distinguishing feature of law is that it is a coercive order — in a decentralised society the sanction takes the form of self-help, which international law makes lawful only as a countermeasure. 2. The eight institutional sanctions of the syllabus. Treat them in the order set out in the syllabus itself (this is what earns full marks): ① Security Council — Chapter VII: Art. 39 determination, Art. 40 provisional measures, Art. 41 (economic, communications and diplomatic sanctions), Art. 42 (military enforcement); binding under Art. 25 ( Namibia 1971); Art. 5/6 suspension and expulsion; Art. 94(2) enforcement of ICJ judgments. Examples: Rhodesia 1966, South Africa 1977, Iraq 1990, Libya 2011, DPRK, 1267/1373/1540. Critique: the veto produces paralysis and selectivity (Syria, Crimea, Ukraine 2022). ② General Assembly — Arts. 10–14 recommendations; Art. 19 loss of vote (South Africa 1986; Haiti; Somalia); Arts. 5 and 6 on SC recommendation; Res. 377(V) Uniting for Peace (Suez 1956; Ukraine 2022, ES-11/1); GA resolutions as evidence of custom ( Nuclear Weapons AO 1996, para. 70; Nicaragua 1986). ③ ICJ — binding judgments (Arts. 59, 60); Art. 94(1) and (2); provisional measures binding ( LaGrand , 2001); advisory opinions as authoritative pronouncements ( Reparation , Namibia , Chagos ). Limits: consensual jurisdiction; no enforcement of its own; uneven compliance ( Nicaragua ; Avena ; but note Corfu Channel , Temple of Preah Vihear and Jadhav as compliance successes). ④ Human Rights Council — UPR (Res. 5/1, 2007), Special Procedures, complaint procedure, commissions of inquiry; no enforcement, but reputational and evidentiary effects. ⑤ ITLOS — UNCLOS Art. 296; Seabed Disputes Chamber; prompt release (Art. 292); provisional measures (Art. 290); M/V Saiga (No. 2) ; Bangladesh/Myanmar (2012); advisory opinion on climate change (2024). ⑥ ICC — Rome Statute Arts. 5, 12–14, 17, 25, 27; SC referrals (Darfur 2005, Libya 2011); arrest warrants (Al-Bashir 2009–10; the 2023 and 2024 warrants); complementarity. ⑦ PCA — 1899/1907 Hague framework; binding awards ( Island of Palmas 1928; Bay of Bengal 2014; Enrica Lexie 2020); consent-based, so the \"sanction\" is consent plus reputation. ⑧ Sanctions under human rights treaties — ICCPR OP1 individual communications; CAT Art. 20 inquiries; CEDAW OP; ECHR Art. 34/46; ACHR and ACHPR — the strongest regional remedy being the European Court's binding judgment supervised by the Committee of Ministers. 3. Horizontal sanctions. Retorsion (unfriendly but lawful: expulsion of diplomats, withdrawal of aid, tariff measures) and countermeasures (ARSIWA 2001 Arts. 22, 49–54): must respond to a prior wrongful act; must be aimed at inducing compliance, not punishment; must be proportionate (Art. 51; Air Services Agreement 1978; Naulilaa 1928); may not involve force or violate fundamental human rights/humanitarian obligations (Art. 50); must be suspended when the wrongful act ceases (Art. 52(3)). Examples: Magnitsky-style targeted measures; US measures after Tehran Hostages ; the 2022 sanctions on Russia. 4. Informal sanctions. Adverse public opinion (Rousseau's \"strongest fear\"); NGO naming and shaming (Amnesty, HRW, Greenpeace); reciprocity; the \"law habit\"; FATF grey-listing; suspension from sporting and cultural bodies (South Africa, Russia). 5. Evaluation — do sanctions \"determine effectiveness\"? Yes in part: without Chapter VII, Art. 2(4) would be exhortation; without the ICC, individual responsibility would be a slogan. But no: compliance in most cases precedes and exceeds enforcement; sanctions are politically selective and often counter-productive; and international law functions by consent, reciprocity and internalisation. Conclusion: sanctions are the visible edge of a larger structure of compliance — necessary to the credibility of the law, but neither necessary nor sufficient for its effectiveness. B.7 Model answer — \"Examine the role, functions and powers of the UN Human Rights Council.\" 2015 1. Origins. ECOSOC established the Commission on Human Rights (CHR) in February 1946 under Art. 68 of the Charter, assisted by the Office of the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights. The CHR was criticised for \"allowing countries with poor human rights records to be members.\" The GA replaced it by Res. 60/251 of 15 March 2006 , creating the Human Rights Council as a subsidiary organ of the GA, sitting in Geneva. 2. Composition. 47 members elected by the GA by secret ballot, distributed: Africa 13, Asia 13, Eastern Europe 6, Latin America and the Caribbean 8, Western Europe and Other States 7; three-year terms, no more than two consecutive terms; membership suspended by a two-thirds GA vote for gross and systematic violations (Libya, 2011). A candidate's human-rights record is supposed to be considered (\"members shall uphold the highest standards\"). 3. Mandate and functions. (i) Promote universal respect for the protection of all human rights; (ii) address situations of violations, including gross and systematic; (iii) make recommendations to the GA; (iv) promote coordination of human-rights activities in the UN system; (v) work in cooperation and through dialogue; (vi) Universal Periodic Review (UPR) — every UN member State is reviewed every four-and-a-half years (India: 2008, 2012, 2017, 2022); (vii) Special Procedures — Special Rapporteurs, Independent Experts and Working Groups with country or thematic mandates (torture, arbitrary detention, freedom of expression, etc.); (viii) Complaint Procedure (Res. 5/1) for consistent patterns of gross violations; (ix) Advisory Committee — a think-tank; (x) special sessions — a third of members may requisition (used for Syria, DPRK, Myanmar, Ukraine). 4. Powers and limitations. No enforcement power: its outputs are recommendations, resolutions, reports and public findings. Its authority is judicial in method (fact-finding, legal analysis) but political in effect (reputational, agenda-setting, feeding into SC designations and ICC referrals, and into the universal human-rights treaty-body system). The political calibration of members and the practice of \"bloc voting\" are its standing criticisms; the UPR's value lies in universality — all States, including the powerful, are examined. 5. Conclusion. The Council is the successor to the CHR and the institutional centre of UN human-rights diplomacy. It has converted piecemeal treaty machinery into a universal, periodic accountability mechanism. But because it lacks coercive powers, its influence depends on the accuracy of its findings and the cost of reputational exposure — which is precisely why States contest its agenda. B.8 Model answer — \"Is the individual a subject of international law?\" / \"Discuss the status of individuals in international law.\" / Brownlie's view 2015 2013 2012 2002 2001 1999 1998 1995 1994 1993 1992 1. The question framed. The controversy \"whether the individual is a subject or an object of international law\" turns on the definition of a subject. The accepted test is the Reparation formula — an entity \"capable of possessing international rights and duties, and … the capacity to maintain its rights by bringing international claims.\" Legal capacity has four constituents: capacity to make claims; treaty-making capacity; capacity to enjoy privileges and immunities; and capacity to bear responsibility. 2. The classical position. Under classical law the individual was an object: the law of nations was \"primarily a law of international conduct of States and not of their citizens\" (Oppenheim); damage to a national was an injury to his State ( Mavrommatis , 1924 — \"by taking up the case of one of its subjects … a State is in reality asserting its own rights\"); and only States may be parties before the ICJ (Art. 34(1) Statute). 3. The transformation — the sequence to reproduce. 1919 Treaty of Versailles (Mixed Arbitral Tribunals, ILO, minorities treaties) → 1945–46 Nuremberg and Tokyo Charters (individual criminal responsibility; \"crimes are committed by men, not by abstract entities\") → 1948 UDHR and Genocide Convention (Art. IV punishes rulers, public officials and private individuals) → 1950 ECHR Art. 34 (individual applications to the European Court) → 1963 VCCR Art. 36 (consular notification as an individual right) → 1966 ICCPR and the First Optional Protocol (individual petitions) → 1965 ICSID Convention (individuals and corporations may sue States directly) → 1979 CEDAW; 1984 CAT → 1993 ICTY; 1994 ICTR → 1998 Rome Statute. 4. Rights. The consular trilogy demonstrates the modern approach: LaGrand (ICJ Rep. 2001, p. 466) — Art. 36(1) VCCR \"creates individual rights,\" enforceable by the national State before the ICJ; Avena (2004) — the obligation of review and reconsideration; Jadhav (2019) — Pakistan's breach and the requirement of effective review and reconsideration for an Indian national. Add the individual's ability to petition the Human Rights Committee, the Committee against Torture, the CEDAW Committee and the Inter-American and African Commissions, and the individual's standing before the European Court of Human Rights. 5. Duties. Piracy (long a crime under customary law — UNCLOS Arts. 100–107); slavery; war crimes; the Nuremberg Principles (ILC, 1950, Principle I: \"Any person who commits an act which constitutes a crime under international law is responsible therefor and liable to punishment\"); Genocide Convention Art. IV; the Rome Statute Arts. 25 (individual criminal responsibility), 27 (irrelevance of official capacity), 28 (superior responsibility). The limit: Arrest Warrant (DRC v. Belgium), ICJ Rep. 2002 — an incumbent Head of State/Government/Foreign Minister enjoys immunity before foreign municipal courts, but not before international criminal tribunals. 6. The balance — Brownlie's formulation. \"There is no general rule that the individual cannot be a 'subject of international law' and in particular contexts he appears as a legal person on the international plane.\" So too the DU material: \"Ordinarily international law deals with the rights and duties of the States … This does not however mean that other entities or individuals are outside the scope of international law.\" But note the limits: no general treaty-making power; no standing before the ICJ; rights ordinarily available only where a treaty confers them; nationality still the principal connecting factor; and enforcement still requires the State. 7. Conclusion. The individual is a subject of international law in specific contexts — a partial, derivative, functional subject. The classical dichotomy (States = subjects; individuals = objects) has been replaced by a graduated scale. As the final point of the answer: \"the future of international law is one with the future of international organisation\" and \"the individual is the ultimate unit of all law\" are not competing slogans but the two ends of the same spectrum. B.9 Model answer — \"Does the UN possess a legal personality in international law? Discuss with special reference to the Reparation for Injuries Case (1949).\" 2015 / \"Reparations Case has confirmed the opinion that international organizations are subjects of international law. Critically analyse the judgment and the grounds upon which international organizations have been held so.\" 2000 / \"International Organisations as a subject of international law\" (short note) 2001 1998 1. The problem. The UN Charter never uses the words \"international legal personality.\" Yet no one doubts the UN may conclude treaties, sue and be sued, and claim reparation. The basis of that capacity had to be judicially constructed — and the construction is the Reparation opinion. 2. The facts and questions (GA Res. 258(III); Bernadotte's assassination, 17 September 1948; Questions I(a), I(b) and II — full text in §Case 1 above). 3. The reasoning. (i) subjects of law are not identical in nature or extent of rights — \"their nature depends upon the needs of the community\"; (ii) the Charter's silence is not decisive — the Court looked at the Organisation's characteristics: a centre with organs and tasks; Art. 2(5) obligations of Members; the GA's power of recommendation; Arts. 104–105 capacity, privileges and immunities; the power to conclude agreements; practice under the 1946 Convention on Privileges and Immunities (s. 35); (iii) hence \"the Organization is an international person\" — but \"that is not the same thing as saying that it is a State … Still less … that it is a super-State\"; (iv) Question I(a): affirmative, unanimously; (v) Question I(b): affirmative, 11 to 4 — the traditional rule of nationality of claims \"applies to claims brought by a State,\" and the Organisation asserts its own right, not the agent's; (vi) non-Member States: the 50 founder States had the power to create an entity with objective international personality ; (vii) Question II: no rule of priority between diplomatic and functional protection; the nationality of the agent, even that of the defendant State, is irrelevant. 4. The principles. Implied powers; functional necessity; relative personality; objective personality; functional protection; reparation as a general principle ( Factory at Chorzów ). 5. Critical analysis (this is where marks are won). (i) The opinion is a piece of creative judicial statecraft: functional necessity can be a slippery test — which necessities count? (ii) The Court's caution (not a State, not a super-State) was politically necessary to reassure States; Judge Winiarski's declaration recorded the anxiety. (iii) The limits were later made explicit in the WHO Nuclear Weapons opinion (1996): an implied power must serve the organisation's own constitutional functions. (iv) The ILC's 2011 Draft Articles on the Responsibility of International Organizations now mirror the State-responsibility Articles, but no convention has been adopted for organisations. (v) The practical result: organisations can act, claim, and be claimed against — but their personality remains derivative and functional. 6. Conclusion. The UN possesses international legal personality: not because the Charter says so, but because its functions make it indispensable. The opinion is the charter of the modern law of international organisations. B.10 Model answer — \"A subject of law possesses a distinct personality … What are the different constituents of legal capacity under international law? Whom out of the various entities possessing this capacity do you prefer most?\" 1999 / \"Describe the tests by which an entity can claim personality under international law.\" 2002 / \"As a pre-requisite to be a subject of international law, an entity should be endowed with the procedural capacity to sue and be sued … can the following be considered subjects: (a) inter-governmental organisation; (b) individual?\" 1998 (ICJ Reports / Landmark International Precedent)",
            "APP Appendices Appendix I — Treaty and Instrument Date-Chart (Topic 1) Table A.1 — Instruments every Topic-1 answer may cite Year Instrument Key provisions to remember 1648 Peace of Westphalia Sovereign equality; territorial State as the basic unit. 1815 Congress of Vienna; Final Act International rivers; diplomatic ranks; Swiss neutrality. 1856 Declaration of Paris Abolition of privateering; maritime warfare codified. 1864 Geneva Convention (ICRC) Care of the wounded — birth of humanitarian law. 1899 / 1907 Hague Conventions; PCA Pacific settlement (Arts. 37–90, 1907); laws of war. 1919 Treaty of Versailles; ILO; Covenant of the League Art. 297, 304 (minorities; mixed arbitral tribunals); ILO Part XIII. 1920 / 1921 League of Nations; PCIJ Membership open to \"any\" State (Art. 1). 1928 Kellogg–Briand Pact; General Act for Pacific Settlement; Havana Convention on Asylum Renunciation of war as an instrument of national policy. 1930 Hague Codification Conference Failure on nationality, territorial waters and State responsibility. 1933 Montevideo Convention on Rights and Duties of States Art. 1 — four criteria of Statehood. 1945 UN Charter (San Francisco, 26 June; in force 24 October) Arts. 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 13(1)(a), 24, 25, 27(3), 33, 39–42, 43–47, 48–50, 51, 55, 68, 92, 94, 103, 104–105. 1945 ICJ Statute Arts. 34, 36, 38, 41, 59, 60, 61, 65. 1945–46 Nuremberg & Tokyo Charters and Judgments Individual criminal responsibility (Art. 6); official position (Art. 7); superior orders (Art. 8). 1946 Convention on the Privileges and Immunities of the UN s. 35 — relied on in Reparation . 1948 UDHR (GA Res. 217A(III), 10 Dec.); Genocide Convention 48–0–8 vote; Genocide Art. IV (individual liability) and Art. IX (ICJ jurisdiction). 1949 Geneva Conventions I–IV Common Article 3 — non-international conflicts; ICRC mandate. 1950 European Convention on Human Rights; ILC Nuremberg Principles Art. 34 (individual applications); Art. 46 (supervision); Principle I (individual responsibility). 1951 Refugee Convention; Reservations to the Genocide Convention AO Non-refoulement; the ICJ's test for permissible reservations. 1960 / 1962 GA Res. 1514(XV) Declaration on Decolonisation; GA Res. 1803(XVII) Permanent Sovereignty over Natural Resources Self-determination as a legal right. 1961 / 1963 VCDR; VCCR Diplomatic and consular immunities; VCCR Art. 36 — the individual-right provision. 1965 CERD; ICSID Convention CERD Art. 22 compromissory clause (alternative preconditions — Ukraine v. Russia ); ICSID — investor–State arbitration. 1966 ICCPR + OP1; ICESCR (in force 23 March 1976 / 3 January 1976); GA Res. 2200A(XXI) International Bill of Rights; HRC; individual petitions. 1967 Outer Space Treaty Res communis; no national appropriation. 1969 VCLT Arts. 31–33 interpretation; Arts. 53, 64 jus cogens ; Art. 62 rebus sic stantibus . 1970 GA Res. 2625(XXV) — Friendly Relations Declaration Sovereign equality; non-intervention; self-determination; cited in Nicaragua . 1974 GA Res. 3201(S-VI) and 3281(XXIX) — NIEO and the Charter of Economic Rights and Duties of States; GA Res. 3314(XXIX) Definition of Aggression The Third World's economic charter (adopted 120–6–10). 1977 Protocols I & II to the Geneva Conventions Art. 96(3) — national liberation movements. 1982 UNCLOS (Montego Bay, 10 Dec.; in force 16 Nov. 1994) Arts. 100–107 (piracy); 279–296 (dispute settlement); Part XI (the Area); Annex VI (ITLOS). 1984 Convention against Torture (CAT) Arts. 20, 21, 22 — inquiry and communications. 1993 / 1994 ICTY (SC Res. 827) / ICTR (SC Res. 955) Ad hoc criminal tribunals established by the SC. 1994 / 1998 / 2000 Part XI Implementation Agreement; Rome Statute (17 July 1998, in force 1 July 2002); UN Millennium Declaration ICC jurisdiction; complementarity; Art. 27. 1999 ICSFT (9 Dec.) Arts. 2(1), 9(1), 24(1) — the Ukraine v. Russia treaty. 2001 ILC ARSIWA Arts. 1–3, 4–11 (attribution), 12, 22, 30, 31, 34–37 (reparation), 44, 49–54 (countermeasures). 2005 / 2006 World Summit Outcome (R2P, paras. 138–139); HRC established (GA Res. 60/251, 15 March 2006); UPR (Res. 5/1, 2007) Human-rights machinery and the responsibility to protect. 2007 UNDRIP (GA Res. 61/295) Collective rights of indigenous peoples. 2015 / 2024 Paris Agreement; ITLOS climate advisory opinion; ILC Draft Conclusions on jus cogens (2022) New frontiers of the law. Appendix II — Glossary (say these accurately) Table A.2 — Working vocabulary Term Meaning International personality Capacity to bear rights and duties under international law and to maintain them by bringing international claims. Functionalism / implied powers The doctrine that an organisation possesses the powers necessarily implied by its functions, even if not expressly conferred. Objective personality Personality that exists erga omnes and not merely as against the organisation's own members. Diplomatic protection A State's right to protect its national against another State's wrongful act; prerequisites: continuous nationality and exhaustion of local remedies. Functional protection An organisation's parallel right to protect its agent — the innovation of Reparation (1949). Erga omnes Obligations owed to the international community as a whole ( Barcelona Traction , 1970, para. 33). Jus cogens A peremptory norm from which no derogation is permitted (VCLT Arts. 53, 64; ILC 2022 Conclusions). Opinio juris The belief that a practice is followed as a matter of legal obligation — the subjective element of custom. Countermeasure An otherwise unlawful act rendered lawful by a prior breach of the target State (ARSIWA Arts. 22, 49–54). Retorsion An unfriendly but lawful act that engages no responsibility. Compromissory clause A treaty clause conferring jurisdiction on a court (e.g., ICSFT Art. 24(1); CERD Art. 22; VCCR Optional Protocol). Provisional measures Interim orders of the ICJ under Art. 41, binding since LaGrand (2001). Complementarity The rule that the ICC acts only where national jurisdictions are unwilling or unable (Rome Statute Art. 17). Codification / progressive development Art. 15 ILC Statute — declaratory systematisation vs. legislative drafting. Common heritage of mankind Shared management of the deep seabed, outer space and the moon — a Third-World contribution now in UNCLOS Part XI. TWAIL Third World Approaches to International Law — the critical school examining the colonial foundations of the discipline. Appendix III — The Documents in Your Project Folder and How Each Is Used Here Table A.3 — Source-map of the uploaded materials File What it is Where it is used IInd Term_Public InternationalLaw_LB205_2022.pdf DU Faculty of Law LB-205 Case Material — the syllabus (pp. iii–v) and the full text of the Reparation opinion (pp. 7–12). Everywhere — the primary source; Case 1 in full; the syllabus map. 1751431693.pdf DU LL.B. May/June 2025 LB-205 question paper. PYQ §A (the 2025 model answers). Exam Capsule LED PIL Complete.pdf DU-LLB exam capsule (LED) covering Topics 1–4: definitions, criticisms, true-law debate, theories, sanctions, subjects, state responsibility, human rights machinery. §1.1 (definitions & criticisms & theories), §1.4 (Starke's sanctions), §1.5 (the three theories; case summaries). PIL NOTES Stanzin Sir.pdf DU classroom notes (Dr. Stanzin Chostak, Faculty of Law, DU), citing Kapoor and Gurdip Singh. §1.1 (true-law debate, Austin's six reasons, Starke's twelve counter-arguments), §1.2 (development), §1.4, §1.5 (personality definition). An_Introduction_to_Public_International_Law_by_S_K_Verma_2nd_Edition.pdf Prescribed textbook (DU reading list). §1.1 (naturalist/positivist schools; fundamental rights; enforcement and the \"law habit\"), §1.2 (the full 19th-century history), §1.3 (codification & ILC), §1.6 (New States and International Law — the core of the Third World section). United Nations Charter (full text).pdf The Charter, full text. §1.2, §1.4 — verbatim Arts. 39–42, 51, 55, 68, 94; the peace ladder diagram. 9_6_2001.pdf ILC Draft Articles on Responsibility of States for Internationally Wrongful Acts, 2001, with commentaries. §1.4 (countermeasures; attribution), §1.5 (State responsibility and reparation). VCDR.pdf / VCCR.pdf Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations 1961; Vienna Convention on Consular Relations 1963. §1.4/§1.5 — diplomatic immunities and VCCR Art. 36 (the individual-right provision behind LaGrand , Avena , Jadhav ). Oppenheim-ScienceInternationalLaw-1908.pdf L. Oppenheim, \"The Science of International Law: Its Task and Method,\" AJIL 1908 — the seven tasks of the discipline; ends = peace among nations, peaceful settlement, laws of war. §1.1 (the juristic foundations of Oppenheim's definition), §1.3 (codification as a \"task\" of the science). Scott-LegalNatureInternational-1905.pdf / -1907.pdf James Brown Scott on the legal nature of international law — the sanction argument (\"the nature of a thing cannot depend upon a mere form of punishment\"). §1.1 (the \"true law\" debate — quoted). Sherman-NatureSourcesInternational-1921.pdf Gordon E. Sherman, AJIL 1921 — post-First-World-War re-examination: \"Does a system of justice in international action really exist? And has it enforceable sanctions?\" §1.1 (framing the sanction question), §1.2 (the inter-war crisis). Goodhart-NatureInternationalLaw-1936.pdf A.L. Goodhart, Grotius Society 1936 — answers Zimmern's \"imposture\" charge by dissecting the command theory (Bodin → Hobbes → Bentham → Austin → Holland). §1.1 (critique of Austin; the \"positive morality\" answer) — quoted. MacKenzie-NaturePlaceFunction-1939.pdf MacKenzie, AJIL 1939 — \"The Nature, Place and Function of International Law\" — answers the sceptics and the \"man on the street\". §1.1 (the function of international law as conflict-resolution). Hurst-NatureInternationalLaw-1944.pdf Sir Cecil Hurst, Grotius Society 1944 — \"The Nature of International Law and the Reason Why It Is Binding on States\". §1.1 (the question \"why is there any international law at all?\"). Kuwait Airways Corp v. Iraqi Airways Co.pdf House of Lords, [1995] 3 All ER 694 — act of State, State immunity, service on an embassy. §1.5 (§C, boxed illustration of the State as a subject). 39383202213150360392judgement24-mar-2025-592692.pdf · DOC-20250327-WA0000..pdf · image-1.png Two Indian Supreme Court judgments on gift deeds/succession (property law), and a photograph of the Topic-1 syllabus page. Not PIL material — noted for completeness; the syllabus image is the source of the topic wording quoted at the head of this document. Appendix IV — Bibliography (as prescribed by the DU syllabus, plus the primary instruments) (ICJ Reports / Landmark International Precedent)"
