          "arguments": "",
          "ratio": "<p><strong>Plan:</strong> ① the textual argument — “<em>begar</em> and other similar forms of forced labour”; the Court refused a narrow reading that would reduce the second limb to a “rope of sand” ② the economic-compulsion argument — force includes “compulsion arising from hunger and poverty, want and destitution”; the “Hobson’s choice” passage ③ the constitutional-design argument — Articles 17, 23 and 24 were placed in Part III precisely because they were to bind private persons ④ the consequences — liberalised <em>locus standi</em>, private-employer liability, principal-employer liability under the CLRA and the ISMW Act, and the State’s positive duty of enforcement ⑤ <em>criticism</em>: the reasoning blurs the line between statutory breach and constitutional wrong; the “economic compulsion” test is potentially unlimited (why stop at the minimum wage?); and enforcement depends on an inspectorate the Court itself distrusted — which is why it appointed Ombudsmen ⑥ <em>answer to the criticism</em>: the holding is carefully confined to payment below the <em>statutory</em> minimum, a figure the State itself has fixed as necessary; the criticism therefore reduces to a plea for a better minimum and better enforcement ⑦ conclusion: the case is best read as the constitutionalisation of the wage floor — and the reason a notified floor wage under §9 of the Code on Wages, 2019 has a constitutional as well as a statutory character.</p>",
