          "anchorId": "c71",
          "facts": "Facts (DU). The Bihar Assembly (243 members; majority = 122) completed its term on 6-3-2005 . February 2005 elections produced a hung House: NDA (BJP+JD(U)) 92 · RJD 75 · LJP 29 · Congress 10 · CPI(ML) 7 · SP 4 · NCP 3 · BSP 2 · Independents 17 · Others 9 . Smt. Rabri Devi resigned (28-2-2005); letters followed: LJP (Paswan) — supporting neither RJD nor BJP; BJP claimed 92 + 10–12 independents \"not to allow the RJD to form a Government\"; SP, CPI(ML) neutral; RJD staked a claim on 5-3-2005 — but even RJD + all 17 independents = 108 report of 6-3-2005 ) recorded that he had \"explored all possibilities\" and no party/group could claim majority → Notification GSR 162(E) dated 7-3-2005 : Art 356 Proclamation, Assembly kept in suspended animation (approved by the Lok Sabha on 19-3-2005 and the Rajya Sabha on 21-3-2005 — DU). Post-November-2005 elections again returned no combination at 122. On 21-5-2005 the Governor reported that 17–18 LJP MLAs were \"moving towards\" the JD(U) , that allurements were being offered and that a claim would \"distort the verdict of the people\"; a joint letter of the RJD–LJP leadership (22-5-2005, late evening) followed. The Union forwarded the material to the President — who was in Moscow : papers despatched at 11 p.m. on 22-5-2005, presidential action in the early IST hours of 23-5-2005 (01:52 / 03:50), and the Proclamation/notification dissolving the Assembly at 14:30 IST on 23-5-2005 — before the newly elected House had met even once . Parliament approved it (LS/RS, March 2005 sittings had approved the first Proclamation; the second approval followed under Art 356(3)). Writ petitions before the Patna H.C. failed; the Supreme Court passed an interim order on 7-10-2005 declaring the 23-5-2005 Proclamation unconstitutional while declining status quo ante , with reasons and final relief in the judgment.",
