          "id": "pyq-consti-u1-3",
          "year": "December 2024",
          "marks": 20,
          "unitNumber": 1,
          "unit": "Unit 1: General Principles, Constitutionalism & Basic Structure",
          "question": "The Republic of Dakshin is a federal polity whose Constitution imbues the principle of asymmetric federalism ; the State of Kailash holds special privileges. After a change of government at the Centre, the ruling party revoked Kailash’s special privilege by constitutional amendment; protests followed; the President then issued an Article 356 Proclamation citing failure of constitutional machinery. Kailash challenges it. “In light of the aforementioned facts, argue on behalf of the State of Kailash. Your arguments must discuss the jurisprudence of federalism, Article 356, as well as the extent and limits of judicial review.” (Law in pari materia with India.)",
          "answer": "How to structure (20-mark problem — issue-wise arguments) Frame three issues: (I) Validity of revocation of asymmetric privilege (federalism jurisprudence); (II) Validity of the Art. 356 Proclamation; (III) Extent & limits of judicial review. Issue I — Federalism & asymmetric privileges (for Kailash) Federalism is a basic feature — S.R. Bommai (Sawant J.: “States… neither satellites nor agents”; Ramaswami J.: “Federalism envisaged… is a basic feature”); Kuldip Nayar : “federal principle is dominant… one of its basic features”; Asymmetric federalism is part of the basic structure — Art. 370 judgment (2023): “Asymmetrical federalism, that is differential rights to certain federal sub-units is a part of the Indian federal scheme. It is a part of the basic structure, as is federalism”; Arts. 371A–371J show differential treatment is a constitutional norm, “difference… one of degree and not of kind”; So the special privilege, once constitutionally granted, cannot be destroyed by an amendment that damages the federal balance / the basic identity of the State — argue Kesavananda limit (amend ≠ destroy), Minerva Mills (balance between Arts. 14/19/31? — use “harmony and balance between fundamental rights and DPSPs and between Centre and States is integral to basic structure”); Caveat (be balanced): the Union’s textual powers (Arts. 2–4 analogue; Art. 368) are wide — counter-argument is Kuldip Nayar : “federalism leans in favour of a strong centre”. Argue that the revocation itself may be within Art. 368 but the resulting action (Proclamation) is ultra vires if motivated by the protest & political rivalry rather than genuine breakdown. Issue II — The Article 356 Proclamation (for Kailash) Grounds are insufficient: non-violent protests ≠ failure of constitutional machinery; the State government continues to function (no refusal to carry on governance in accordance with the Constitution); Bommai test: (a) material must exist; (b) material relevant & real; (c) no mala fide/improper purpose — here the purpose appears to be political retaliation against an opposition-ruled State for Centre’s own amendment → classic misuse; Floor test: if majority of the House is in question, the Assembly must be allowed to test confidence — Governor cannot report based on protests/letters (Bommai, Karnataka); Art. 74(2) is no bar to examining existence of material; Art. 356(3) gives Parliament only 2 months; dissolution before approval invalid (Bommai); Secularism/federalism/quasi-federal synthesis: Beg C.J.’s “amphibian” observation cuts both ways — even a “quasi-federal” constitution demands that the unitary slide be justified by genuine crisis (Ahmadi J.: label aside, “the thrust and implications… bearing on… Article 356” matter); Rameshwar Prasad : no proclamation where the challenge is political disaffection of the ruling party; Navalshankar Dave : prior consultation with CM is mandatory; State of Karnataka v. UOI (1978)? — cite Art. 355 duty framing; Relief: quash Proclamation, restore House if dissolved, direct floor test if majority genuinely questioned (Karnataka 2018 model). Issue III — Extent and limits of judicial review Extent: reviewable on (i) existence of material, (ii) relevance, (iii) mala fides/purpose, (iv) constitutional process (consultation, post-decisional approval), (v) reasonableness — plus FRs of citizens/MLAs (Arts. 14, 19) if the Assembly is dissolved; Limits: courts will not sit in appeal over policy; they will not inquire into the content of aid & advice (Art. 74(2)); if Parliament approves under Art. 356(3) and the object is achieved, relief is prospective/calibrated; “political question” doctrine rejected in India (Bommai) but self-restraint remains; Basic structure as the master-limit: any amendment or executive action destroying federalism/judicial review/democracy is void (Kesavananda; L. Chandra Kumar — Arts. 32/226 are basic features, courts can strike down the Proclamation’s consequences). Conclusion (counsel’s prayer) The Proclamation be quashed as issued without relevant material and for an improper purpose, offending the federal basic structure; the House be restored or a floor test directed within 48 hours; and Parliament be advised that Art. 356 remains “the last resort” (Sarkaria), judicial review being “a stringent duty” (Sawant J., Bommai para 106).",
