          "unit": "Unit 1: General Principles, Constitutionalism & Basic Structure",
          "question": "Write a short note on: Theory of Separation of Powers .",
          "answer": "Introduction Theory that governmental functions — legislative, executive, judicial — must be exercised by three distinct organs , each checking the others, to prevent tyranny. Origin: Aristotle (mixed constitution) → John Locke (executive/federative vs legislative) → Montesquieu , L’Esprit des Lois (1748): “when the legislative and executive powers are united in the same person… there can be no liberty.” Class-note anchor: Montesquieu = “legislate, execute, judicial”. Models Absolute/strict separation — USA (federal separation with checks & balances; impeachment; judicial review; fixed tenures); Fusion of powers — UK: “Parliamentary sovereignty… legislative, executive and judicial power all reside in the Queen in Parliament” ( Regina’s case , cited in Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy reading); cabinet is “a hyphen… a buckle” ( Ram Jawaya Kapur ); Indian model — functional, not rigid: Ram Jawaya Kapur para 12: “The Indian Constitution has not… recognised the doctrine of separation of powers in its absolute rigidity but the functions of the different parts… have been sufficiently differentiated… our Constitution does not contemplate assumption, by one organ… of functions that essentially belong to another.” Constitutional expression in India Arts. 53, 73, 154, 162 (executive), Arts. 107–222 (legislature), Arts. 124–231 (judiciary); Art. 50 (DPSP — separation of judiciary from executive); independence of judges (Arts. 124(4), 217 — removal by address); legislative privilege (Arts. 105, 194); quasi-judicial functions delegated to executive (upheld in Ram Jawaya Kapur & later cases); Art. 142’s unique blending power (SC can do “complete justice”); ordinances (Arts. 123, 213) = executive law-making with legislative check. Checks & balances (class-note map): Arts. 124, 217, 222 vs Arts. 61, 157; Art. 74(2)/163; immunity Arts. 361, 105, 194; writs Arts. 32, 226, 227. Judicial pronouncements Ram Jawaya Kapur (1955): executive need not await legislation; residue theory; but no organ may go “against the provisions of the Constitution or of any law” (Art. 154); Kesavananda (Sikri C.J.): “separation of powers between the legislature, the executive and the judiciary” is part of the basic structure ; a “genuinely independent” judiciary is integral; Indra Sawhney , L. Chandra Kumar : judicial review (the judiciary’s check) is basic structure — Arts. 32/226 cannot be ousted; M.P. Spl. Police Estab. v. M.P. (2004): legitimacy of delegated legislation; S.P. Gupta : “consultation” vs “concurrence” for judges — separation in appointments. Conclusion India follows “soft separation with checks and balances” — overlapping but not interchangeable functions, fused executive-legislature (parliamentary system), independent judiciary — the differentiation being fine enough to be a basic structural feature while flexible enough to permit delegated/subordinate legislation and limited judicial functions in the executive (Ram Jawaya Kapur). Also seen on those papers (outside Topic 1 — cross-reference only): Centenary paper Q3(a) pith & substance & Q3(b) repugnancy (Topic 7), Q6 Art. 301 (Topic 8), Q8(a) creation of new State (Topic 2); Dec-2024 paper Q1 cession of territory (Topic 2), Q3 Art. 254/colourable legislation (Topic 7), Q5(a) Krishna Kumar ordinances (Topic 5). Their syllabus homes are marked in the TOC of the full syllabus (pages 3–10 of the DU material).",
