          "unit": "Unit 2: The Union and Its Territory",
          "question": "“Procedure for creation of new State in India”",
          "answer": "Model Answer (10-mark version) Opening line: Creation of new States is governed by Part I — Arts. 2, 3 and 4 — a self-contained code (Berubari para 33); Parliament is the sole organ; states are creatures of the Constitution. Route A — Article 2 (external/new units): “Parliament may by law admit into the Union, or establish, new States on such terms and conditions as it thinks fit.” Used for units outside the existing State structure — e.g., admission of Sikkim by the Constitution (Thirty-Sixth Amendment) Act, 1975; Parliament’s terms must be consistent with the basic structure (R.C. Poudyal, AIR 1993 SC 1804 — conditions justiciable, admission upheld). Art. 4: such a law is not an Art. 368 amendment. Route B — Article 3(a) (internal carving): Parliament may by law “form a new State by separation of territory from any State or by uniting two or more States or parts of States or by uniting any territory to a part of any State” — Explanation II extends the power to uniting parts of UTs. Examples: Nagaland (1962), Haryana (1966), Meghalaya (1971), Chhattisgarh–Uttarakhand–Jharkhand (2000), Telangana (Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act, 2014). Step-by-step procedure (Art. 3 proviso + Art. 4): The Bill is introduced in either House only on the President’s recommendation . If it affects the area, boundaries or name of an existing State, the President must refer it to that State’s Legislature to express its views within the specified/further period (Explanations I: no such reference for UTs; UTs count as “States” in clauses (a)–(e)). Views are consultative only — not binding; no fresh reference for later amendments ( Babulal Parate , AIR 1960 SC 51). Passage by simple majority in both Houses; Presidential assent; Art. 4 permits amendment of the First/Fourth Schedules and consequential changes without Art. 368 procedure and without ratification by States (Berubari para 48). Special cases: territory acquired abroad enters automatically under Art. 1(3)(c) and is then constituted/absorbed by law under Art. 2 or Art. 3 (Berubari paras 34–35, Chandernagore); creating a Union territory from a State is covered by Art. 3(a) + Explanation I (Art. 370 Judgment para 503, Ladakh) — though whether statehood itself can be extinguished is left open (para 504). Illustrations to name-drop: Sikkim (1975, Art. 2 + 36th Amendment), Telangana (2014), the 2000 creations, Goa (1987), Bombay’s division (Bombay Reorganisation Act, 1960 — as validated through Parate’s framework), and the negative: no State may secede (Union of States; Art. 1). Closing sentence: Thus, creation of a new State = a political decision executed as ordinary legislation by Parliament with presidential recommendation, consultative reference, simple majority, and Schedule consequentialism — wide discretion, narrow procedure, judicially policed at the edges (Poudyal). Other probable PYQ angles for this topic (prepare, not printed in uploaded papers) “Cession of Indian territory requires a constitutional amendment — discuss with reference to Berubari and subsequent developments.” (classic repeat) “Article 3 is the root of all reorganisation but not of cession — comment.” “Can Parliament convert a State into a Union territory under Article 3?” (Art. 370 J. para 504 left-open point — a sure differentiator) “Explain Article 143 with reference to the Berubari Reference.” Compare: Tin Bigha (servitude) vs Berubari (cession) vs LBA (100th Amendment).",
