          "unit": "Unit 3: The Union & State Executives",
          "question": "\"Is it essential to have a Council of Ministers under Art. 74(1) even at a time when the House of the People has been dissolved or when its term has expired?\"",
          "answer": "Model answer Yes — absolutely essential. This is the precise question decided in U.N.R. Rao v. Indira Gandhi , AIR 1971 SC 1002 (Sikri, C.J. + 4 JJ.), where quo warranto was sought against Indira Gandhi's continuation as PM after the LS was dissolved on 27-12-1970. Reasoning (use the DU excerpts' paragraphs). (1) Art 74(1) is mandatory — \"shall\" must be read as \"shall\", not \"may\": \"The Constituent Assembly did not choose the Presidential system of Government.\" Without a CoM nobody would be responsible to the House, and the President \"with the aid of advisers … would be able to rule the country at least till he is impeached under Article 61\" — a result the Constitution does not contemplate (paras 7–8). (2) Art 53(1) is controlled by \"in accordance with this Constitution\" — the President cannot act directly through officers bypassing the CoM (para 6), and the Art 60 oath binds him to the Constitution. (3) Art 75(2) continues : Ministers hold office during the President's pleasure — \"The President has not said that it is his pleasure that the respondent shall not hold office\" (para 8). (4) Art 75(3) is harmonised : collective responsibility to the LS operates when the House is not dissolved or prorogued; on dissolution the CoM continues in office to carry on administration (para 9); the expiry-of-term case is dealt with by s. 14, RPA 1951 . (5) Arts 77(3) and 78 have full play — business allocation and the PM's information-duties continue during the interregnum (para 10). The Court quoted Ram Jawaya Kapur (paras 13–14) and A. Sanjeevi Naidu (\"the essence of joint responsibility\"). Conclusion. A Council of Ministers is a permanent constitutional requirement of the parliamentary system; dissolution suspends the responsibility mechanism (75(3)), not the existence of the Council — appeal dismissed, government legally continues until a new House is constituted.",
