          "unit": "Unit 8: Distribution of Legislative Powers",
          "question": "📜 Dec-2024 · Q3(a) · 10 marks “ ‘It does not appear to be a sound proposition to confine Article 254(1) only to the situation where the Central-State laws fall in the Concurrent List rather than when they fall in different Lists and yet are inconsistent to some extent.’ Do you agree with this statement? Explain your answer with help of relevant legal provisions and judgments. ”",
          "answer": "Model Answer (≈ 800–900 words · balanced “partially agree”) 1. Decode the statement (2 lines). The proposition attacks the Hoechst confinement of Art 254(1) (paras 69–70): repugnancy under 254(1) arises only where both laws occupy the same field of a Concurrent-List subject (A.S. Krishna’s two conditions); List-II-vs-List-I/III overlaps are ultra vires under Art 246(1) (“subject to” in 246(3)) — failure of competence, not repugnancy. The statement says this confinement is unsound ; so should you — partially agree . 2. Why confinement is attacked — the case FOR the statement (4 points, cite): Text: “a law made by Parliament which Parliament is competent to enact ” naturally includes Parliament’s List I and Arts 249–253 laws — competence runs wider than List III. M.P. Jain (Indian Constitutional Law — the DU reference) contends the phrase was chosen precisely to cover Union-list and special-power laws; and the words “with respect to … the Concurrent List” may grammatically attach only to the second limb (“existing law”). Exception vs main provision (P.K. Tripathi, “The Text is Explicit”, AIR 1986): cl (2) is an exception to cl (1); an exception is always narrower — there is nothing objectionable in cl (1) having larger content than cl (2). Using cl (2) as the “guide” (Hoechst’s method) therefore proves too much. Lacuna — the treaty-law problem: a law made under Art 253 (implementing conventions) on a State-subject matter conflicts with a State law: Art 246(1)’s non obstante cannot be invoked (253 is its own override), and on Hoechst’s reading 254(1) also fails → the State law survives though the Union law must prevail. Only the broad reading closes this gap (M.P. Jain). Same-entry rigour: requiring both laws to be on the same Concurrent entry was criticised in Vijay Kr Sharma — Sawant J. applied pith & substance/dominant purpose across different Concurrent entries; K. Ramaswamy J. (dissent) went further: entries “merely demarcate” fields and “the concerned entry or entries are not the source of power”. 3. Why confinement nevertheless has force — the case AGAINST (3 points): Systemic design: if 254(1) swallowed all “inconsistencies”, Art 246(1) (List I supremacy), Art 246(3) (“subject to”) and Art 251 (Arts 249/250 laws) would be rendered largely redundant — each overlap has its own allotted resolver; repugnancy is the intra-concurrent-field rule. Grammar settled: “with respect to … the Concurrent List” qualifies both limbs of 254(1) — Hoechst para 69 (“it is now settled”); A.S. Krishna two conditions; Mar Appraem Kuri (65) headnote H codifies the two routes: allotted-sphere overlap → Art 246(1) ; concurrent-field conflict → Art 254(1) . Competence, not repugnancy, explains the different-List case: a State law clashing irreconcilably with a Union-list law has no legislative field left (“subject to”) — declaring it void “to the extent of repugnancy” would mis-describe a total competence failure (Hoechst para 69). 4. Your verdict (the mark-fetcher). “ I partially agree. ” (i) It is not sound to confine 254(1) absolutely — the clause must cover laws Parliament makes under Arts 252/253 and other special heads (lacuna otherwise), and must not be read to require identity of entries (Vijay Kr Sharma: pith and substance governs even repugnancy). (ii) But for genuine List-II-vs-List-I inconsistencies the confinement is right — Art 246(1) is the true resolver, and Hoechst’s competence-route, Mar Appraem’s two-route scheme and Zaverbhai’s same-matter test together form the coherent architecture. (iii) Best synthesis: 254(1) = same-field conflicts (any Concurrent entries, incl. existing laws) + Parliament’s special-power laws; 246(1)/251 = allotted-sphere overlaps. Authorities kit: Arts 246(1),(3), 249–253, 254(1),(2), 251 · Hoechst paras 66–70 · A.S. Krishna · Mar Appraem Kuri (H) · Vijay Kr Sharma (Sawant J + Ramaswamy J dissent) · Zaverbhai · M.P. Jain; P.K. Tripathi (AIR 1986) · Bait phrases: “clause (2) as guide cuts down clause (1)”, “treaty-law lacuna”, “two routes of predomination”.",
          "statuteOrCase": "Unit 8 Constitutional Provisions"
