          "question": "📜 Dec-2024 · Q3(b) · 10 marks — “ Write a short note on the doctrine of colourable legislation. ”",
          "answer": "Model Answer (≈ 600–700 words) 1. Definition + maxim (open with these): A legislature commits a colourable exercise when, having apparently acted within its powers, it in substance and reality transgresses them — the transgression being “veiled by what appears … to be a mere pretence or disguise” ( Duff J. in AG for Ontario v. Reciprocal Insurers , [1924 AC 328, 337], quoted in K.C. Gajapati (60) ). The doctrine rests on the maxim “you cannot do indirectly what you cannot do directly.” 2. Essentials (the four exculpatory/incipient rules — from Gajapati paras 9–18): (i) it is a question of competence, not bona fides — no motive inquiry (“Whether a statute is constitutional or not is … always a question of power”, Cooley); (ii) it applies only to limited/qualified jurisdictions, and only where the real object lies outside them — patent, manifest, direct or “disguised, covert and indirect”; (iii) form is no defence — “the substance of the Act that is material, not merely the form”; a legislature cannot violate prohibitions “by employing an indirect method”; (iv) conversely , motive cannot invalidate: if the object is within power, however “unjust or inequitable” the choice, the law stands — and even an avowed trespass saves nothing if the enacting clauses are truly intra vires (Lefroy). 3. Illustrations (at least three): Gajapati itself — the Orissa Agricultural Income Tax (Amendment) Act, 1950 was challenged as a device to deflate zamindari compensation (Entry 42); held not colourable : Entry 46 taxing power genuinely exercised; rates may be unjust, but “that does not affect the competency”; pursuing the ministers’ motives was “a wrong line of enquiry” — appeal dismissed. Contrast Maharaja Kameshwar Singh v. State of Bihar [1952 SCR 889] — Bihar Land Reforms Act ss. 4(b), 23(f) rested on fictitious, fact-unrelated deductions dressed as a “principle of compensation” → colourable use of Entry 42. Comparative: Union Colliery v. Bryden (mining regulation used to exclude Chinese aliens); AG Alberta v. AG Canada (Lord Maugham: no acting “under the guise, or the pretence, or in the form” of own powers to trespass). 4. Colourable vs pith and substance (the discriminator examiners love): P&S asks “ which list does the law really belong to? ” (attribution; incidental trespass forgiven — Prafulla). Colourable asks “ is the real object beyond this legislature’s power altogether? ” (nothing is forgiven — pretence voids). A law with a bad-faith motive but a competent object fails neither (Gajapati). 5. Close: The doctrine polices the limits of legislative jurisdictions and fundamental-rights prohibitions while leaving legislative policy alone — “it would be colourable only if the real object is not attainable … by reason of any constitutional limitation or … lies within the exclusive field of another legislature” (Gajapati para 9). Kit: Gajapati paras 9–18 (Cooley p. 379; Reciprocal Insurers; Lefroy p. 75; Maxim; Kameshwar Singh; Lord Maugham) · contrast Prafulla/Drive-in (pith & substance) · bait phrases: “guise or pretence”, “competence, not mala fides”, “the substance, not the form”.",
          "statuteOrCase": "Unit 8 Constitutional Provisions"
