          "content": "id=\"s-review\"> §8 The Judicial-Review Matrix for Art 356 · Commissions · Timeline Syllabus item (c) asks for Grounds · Limitations · Parliamentary Control · Judicial Review . §5 covered grounds/limitations/parliamentary control as text; this section compresses the case-law overlay into the matrices an examiner can tick off — plus the institutional (extra-judicial) safeguards. Fig. 7 — From minimal review (1977) to floor-test orthodoxy (1994→2016), with the amendment and commission overlays. 🧪 What courts examine — the review stack Fig. 8 — Rajasthan gave courts Level 1; Bommai added Levels 2–5; Rameshwar walked the whole stack. ⚖️ Grounds on which a proclamation falls (settled list) Mala fide — improper purpose or personal bias (Rajasthan; Rameshwar ¶118: \"canons of propriety … undue haste … smacked of mala fides\"). Wholly extraneous / irrelevant grounds — e.g., punishing realignment, preventing a claim, mal-administration, Tenth-Schedule speculation (Rameshwar ¶¶140–159). Absence of material / ipse dixit — \"mere ipse dixit, suspicion, whims and fancies\" (¶136). Unverified facts / unascertained sources — no interviews, conflicting letters, unknown inputs (¶¶123–124). Refusal of a feasible floor test without written reasons (¶119; Bommai). Failure to explore alternatives — minority government available (¶120; Sarkaria/1970 Governors' Committee). Breach of Art 356(1) proviso or procedure — touching High Courts; non-approval within clocks; dissolving before Art 356(3) approval ",
          "unitTitle": "Unit 10: Emergency Provisions",
          "file": "sem 3/Constitution/Topic9-EmergencyProvisions-LB301-DU-Notes.html",
