        {
          "id": "crim-pyq-u7-1",
          "number": "Q5",
          "year": "2022",
          "marks": "15 Marks",
          "type": "Problem & Analytical",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: The Indian Prison System: Prisoner Rights, Open Prisons & Prison Reforms",
          "file": "SEM 5/Criminology/prison-system-notes.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq",
          "question": "According to NCRB 2019 data, prison occupancy stood at 118.4% with over 64% undertrials. To what extent have the recommendations in Rama Murthy v. State of Karnataka been applied? Critically evaluate.",
          "modelAnswer": "<p><b>Introduction:</b> The question highlights the persistent implementation gap in Indian prison reform. NCRB 2019 figures reveal 4,78,600 inmates packed against a sanctioned capacity of 4,03,739 (118.4% occupancy), with undertrials forming ~64%, more than two decades after <i>Rama Murthy v. State of Karnataka</i>, AIR 1997 SC 1739 delivered its comprehensive reform charter under Art. 21 and UN Standard Minimum Rules.</p>\n<p><b>The Rama Murthy Charter:</b> Arising from a prisoner's letter, the Court diagnosed 9 systemic pathologies (overcrowding, trial delay, torture, hygiene, poor diet, vice, communication barriers, unsystematic visits, open prison neglect) and formulated 10 actionable directions.</p>\n<p><b>Extent of Application — Tangible Gains:</b><br>\n1. <b>Legislative Relief:</b> Enactment of S. 436A CrPC (2005) now reinforced as S. 479 BNSS (release at 1/2 maximum term, 1/3 for first-time offenders).<br>\n2. <b>Institutional Machinery:</b> <i>Re: Inhuman Conditions in 1382 Prisons</i> (2016) institutionalized quarterly Undertrial Review Committees (UTRCs) in every district.<br>\n3. <b>Administrative Modernization:</b> Promulgation of the Model Prison Manual 2016 and Model Prisons Act 2023, shifting philosophy from deterrence to reformation.<br>\n4. <b>Constitutional Purging:</b> <i>Sukanya Shantha v. UOI</i> (2024) struck down caste-based labor and barrack segregation.<br>\n5. <b>Specialized Care:</b> MHA 2022 Transgender Advisory and women inmate safeguards post-<i>R.D. Upadhyaya</i>.</p>\n<p><b>Critical Evaluation — Systemic Shortfalls:</b> Despite judicial directives, overcrowding remains acute (over 130% in several States). Open prisons remain drastically under-utilized (~3,786 inmates vs ~25,776 capacity — Vibhute Review). Fundamental impediments include: (a) \"Prisons\" being a State subject (Entry 4 List II); (b) chronic fiscal under-funding; and (c) political apathy as prisoners lack voting franchise (S. 62(5) RP Act 1951).</p>\n<p><b>Conclusion:</b> <i>Rama Murthy</i> supplied the diagnosis and prescription; <i>1382 Prisons</i> built the machinery; but without executive political will and budgetary allocation, reform remains, in the Court's words, \"promises to keep and miles to go.\"</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "crim-pyq-u7-2",
          "number": "Q8(b)",
          "year": "2017",
          "marks": "15 Marks",
          "type": "Essay",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: The Indian Prison System: Prisoner Rights, Open Prisons & Prison Reforms",
          "file": "SEM 5/Criminology/prison-system-notes.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq",
          "question": "“Prisons serve reformation but suffer problems needing immediate attention, else breeding grounds of delinquency” — Discuss the reforms on foot.",
          "modelAnswer": "<p><b>Theoretical Premise:</b> Incarceration in modern penology aims at rehabilitation (<i>Mohd. Giasuddin</i>; Dr. Sethna: \"prisons as moral hospitals\"). However, systemic neglect transforms prisons into schools of crime where first-time offenders are contaminated by hardened recidivists.</p>\n<p><b>The 9 Core Problems & Ongoing Reforms:</b><br>\n1. <b>Overcrowding:</b> Addressed via S. 479 BNSS, UTRC reviews, plea bargaining, and Lok Adalat compounding.<br>\n2. <b>Speedy Trial:</b> Monitored through e-prisons infrastructure and video-conferencing production (S. 187 BNSS).<br>\n3. <b>Torture & Ill-treatment:</b> Controlled through CCTV mandates (<i>Paramvir Singh Saini</i>) and strict compliance with <i>Sunil Batra</i> directives.<br>\n4. <b>Health & Diet:</b> Implementation of Mulla Chapter 29 and mandatory regular health check-ups.<br>\n5. <b>Open Prisons:</b> Expansion of open-air camps (Sampurnanand, Sanganer) offering trust-based community living to foster reintegration.<br>\n6. <b>Parole & Furlough:</b> Liberalized quasi-judicial parole jurisprudence (<i>Poonam Lata</i>, <i>Dharamvir</i>) preventing snapped family ties.</p>\n<p><b>Conclusion:</b> Institutionalization of the Model Prisons Act 2023 provides a statutory framework for true reformation, provided ground-level funding and administrative training keep pace.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "crim-pyq-u7-3",
          "number": "P1",
          "year": "DU Expected / Practice",
          "marks": "10–15 Marks",
          "type": "Problem & Analytical",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: The Indian Prison System: Prisoner Rights, Open Prisons & Prison Reforms",
          "file": "SEM 5/Criminology/prison-system-notes.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq",
          "question": "Discuss the fundamental rights of prisoners under Article 21 with landmark Indian decisions.",
          "modelAnswer": "<p><b>Constitutional Matrix:</b> Incarceration does not extinguish fundamental rights. A convict retains all rights except those necessarily circumscribed by imprisonment.<br>\n1. <i>D.B.M. Patnaik</i> (1974): Inmates are not denuded of fundamental rights.<br>\n2. <i>Sunil Batra I & II</i> (1978, 1980): Ban on solitary confinement without judicial sanction; bar on bar-fetters; letter to judge treated as habeas corpus.<br>\n3. <i>Prem Shankar Shukla</i> (1980): Routine handcuffing violates human dignity.<br>\n4. <i>Francis Coralie Mullin</i> (1981): Right to interviews with family and legal counsel is part of Art. 21.<br>\n5. <i>R.D. Upadhyaya</i> (2007): Rights of female prisoners and children living in prisons.<br>\n6. <i>Sukanya Shantha</i> (2024): Ban on caste-based division of prison work.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": "crim-pyq-u7-4",
          "number": "P2",
          "year": "DU Expected / Practice",
          "marks": "10–15 Marks",
          "type": "Problem & Analytical",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: The Indian Prison System: Prisoner Rights, Open Prisons & Prison Reforms",
          "file": "SEM 5/Criminology/prison-system-notes.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq",
          "question": "Critically examine the concept of Open Prisons in India in the light of K.I. Vibhute's fifty-five years review.",
          "modelAnswer": "<p><b>Definition & Philosophy:</b> Open prisons (open-air institutions) function on minimal security, self-discipline, and inmate trust (UN 1955 SMR).<br>\n<b>Advantages:</b> Removes physical and psychological degradation of closed cells, prevents moral contamination, promotes economic self-sufficiency through agriculture/trades, and eases transition to free society.<br>\n<b>Vibhute's Findings & Critique:</b> Despite proven success (Sanganer camp, Sampurnanand Shivir), open prisons remain severely under-utilized (~15% of capacity) due to bureaucratic conservatism, ambiguous selection criteria, and exclusion of lifers under S. 433A CrPC.<br>\n<b>Recommendation:</b> Mandatory establishment of open institutions in every district as directed in <i>Rama Murthy</i>.</p>"
        }
