          "anchorId": "spl-pyq-u1-6",
          "question": "X, Y and Z have started a partnership firm. They have taken a loan from a nationalised bank and as security for the\n    loan they have given **20 per cent share in profit to the bank** as well as\n    **some control in management of the business of the firm**.\n    **Whether the bank would be liable to the third party as a partner?**",
          "modelAnswer": "**Answer: No — the bank is not a partner and is not liable to third parties as a partner.**\n\n    **Reasoning:**\n\n    \n\n      **s. 6, Explanation 2(a), PA 1932** applies directly: the bank is a\n          **lender of money** to persons engaged in business, and the receipt of a share of profits \"does not\n          of itself make him a partner\".\n      **The 20% share of profits is security/interest for the loan**, not participation in the\n          adventure. In *Mollwo* the commission was 20%; the Privy Council still held the lender to be a creditor.\n      **\"Some control in management\" is protective control.** A bank's standard covenants (inspection of\n          books, approval of large capital expenditure, veto on additional borrowing) are **negative**\n          covenants to protect the security. They confer no **initiative power** — the bank cannot direct what\n          goods are to be manufactured or sold.\n      **No agency:** X, Y and Z do not carry on the business acting for the bank; the bank is not their\n          principal. The implied agency of s. 18 arises *only* from partnership\n          (*Mollwo*: \"if there was no partnership, the implied agency which flows from that relation cannot\n          arise\").\n      **No holding out:** the bank has not represented itself as a partner and has not knowingly\n          permitted itself to be so represented — **s. 28** is therefore not attracted.\n    \n\n    **Conclusion:** the bank remains a secured creditor. Its remedy is against the firm's assets and the\n    partners; it incurs no liability to the firm's other creditors.\n\n    **Contrast:** if the bank were to **direct the day-to-day trading** — e.g., dictate what\n    to produce, to whom to sell and at what price — it would be acting as principal through the firm as its agent, and\n    the \"body and substance\" doctrine in *Mollwo* would expose it to liability. Note also that under\n    **RBI/FEMA norms and banking law**, banks are in any case prohibited from carrying on trading business,\n    which reinforces the inference that no partnership was intended.\n\n    **Also compare:** the DU 1998 Q.1(b) variant — three graduates take an SBI loan at 14% interest\n    plus 10% of profits and mortgage their buses; the business fails. Answer: **the bank is not liable**\n    (same reasoning).",
          "answer": "**Answer: No — the bank is not a partner and is not liable to third parties as a partner.**\n\n    **Reasoning:**\n\n    \n\n      **s. 6, Explanation 2(a), PA 1932** applies directly: the bank is a\n          **lender of money** to persons engaged in business, and the receipt of a share of profits \"does not\n          of itself make him a partner\".\n      **The 20% share of profits is security/interest for the loan**, not participation in the\n          adventure. In *Mollwo* the commission was 20%; the Privy Council still held the lender to be a creditor.\n      **\"Some control in management\" is protective control.** A bank's standard covenants (inspection of\n          books, approval of large capital expenditure, veto on additional borrowing) are **negative**\n          covenants to protect the security. They confer no **initiative power** — the bank cannot direct what\n          goods are to be manufactured or sold.\n      **No agency:** X, Y and Z do not carry on the business acting for the bank; the bank is not their\n          principal. The implied agency of s. 18 arises *only* from partnership\n          (*Mollwo*: \"if there was no partnership, the implied agency which flows from that relation cannot\n          arise\").\n      **No holding out:** the bank has not represented itself as a partner and has not knowingly\n          permitted itself to be so represented — **s. 28** is therefore not attracted.\n    \n\n    **Conclusion:** the bank remains a secured creditor. Its remedy is against the firm's assets and the\n    partners; it incurs no liability to the firm's other creditors.\n\n    **Contrast:** if the bank were to **direct the day-to-day trading** — e.g., dictate what\n    to produce, to whom to sell and at what price — it would be acting as principal through the firm as its agent, and\n    the \"body and substance\" doctrine in *Mollwo* would expose it to liability. Note also that under\n    **RBI/FEMA norms and banking law**, banks are in any case prohibited from carrying on trading business,\n    which reinforces the inference that no partnership was intended.\n\n    **Also compare:** the DU 1998 Q.1(b) variant — three graduates take an SBI loan at 14% interest\n    plus 10% of profits and mortgage their buses; the business fails. Answer: **the bank is not liable**\n    (same reasoning)."
