          "unit": "Unit 1: Unit 1: Concept of Agency & The Nature of Partnership",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic1_agency_partnership.html",
          "anchorId": "spl-pyq-u1-11",
          "question": "*P* authorises *A* to buy certain goods at the market rate. *A* buys at a higher rate but\n    *P* accepts the purchase. Afterwards *P* comes to know that the goods purchased belonged to A\n    himself. **Is ratification binding on P?** Explain.",
          "modelAnswer": "**Answer: No — the ratification is not binding on P. P may avoid the transaction.**\n\n    **Two independent grounds:**\n\n    **(1) s. 198 — knowledge materially defective.** \"No valid ratification can be made by a person whose\n    knowledge of the facts of the case is **materially defective**.\" When P \"accepted the purchase\" he knew\n    only that A had bought above the authorised rate. He did *not* know — and this is plainly material —\n    **that A had sold his own goods to him**. A principal's consent to be bound is vitiated where a\n    material fact is concealed. On discovering the truth, P is entitled to treat the ratification as invalid.\n\n    **(2) s. 215 — agent dealing on his own account.** An agent \"deals on his own account in the business\n    of the agency\" when he sells his own goods to his principal. Such dealing requires the principal's\n    prior consent after the agent has acquainted him with all material circumstances within his own\n    knowledge. If the agent deals without that consent and disclosure, the principal may\n    **repudiate the transaction** where either (a) a material fact has been\n    **dishonestly concealed**, or (b) the dealing has been **disadvantageous to him**. Here A\n    dishonestly concealed his ownership — the classic case under clause (a). Additionally, under\n    **s. 216**, \"if an agent, without the knowledge of his principal, deals in the business of the agency\n    on his own account instead of on account of his principal, the principal is entitled to\n    **claim from the agent any benefit which may have resulted to him from the transaction**\" — so P may\n    also claim A's profit on the sale.\n\n    **Note also s. 199:** had P's ratification been valid, he would have ratified the\n    *whole* transaction (including the higher price). But since the ratification itself fails under s. 198,\n    s. 199 does not arise.\n\n    **Conclusion:** The ratification is voidable at P's option and P may repudiate the purchase, recover\n    the price paid, and claim from A any profit A made. A also forfeits remuneration for the business he has misconducted\n    (**s. 220**).\n\n    **Alternative (where P had full knowledge):** if P had known that the goods were A's own and still\n    accepted, there would be a valid ratification but the transaction would stand as a sale by A to P with informed\n    consent; ss. 215–216 would not assist P. The answer turns entirely on *knowledge*.",
          "answer": "**Answer: No — the ratification is not binding on P. P may avoid the transaction.**\n\n    **Two independent grounds:**\n\n    **(1) s. 198 — knowledge materially defective.** \"No valid ratification can be made by a person whose\n    knowledge of the facts of the case is **materially defective**.\" When P \"accepted the purchase\" he knew\n    only that A had bought above the authorised rate. He did *not* know — and this is plainly material —\n    **that A had sold his own goods to him**. A principal's consent to be bound is vitiated where a\n    material fact is concealed. On discovering the truth, P is entitled to treat the ratification as invalid.\n\n    **(2) s. 215 — agent dealing on his own account.** An agent \"deals on his own account in the business\n    of the agency\" when he sells his own goods to his principal. Such dealing requires the principal's\n    prior consent after the agent has acquainted him with all material circumstances within his own\n    knowledge. If the agent deals without that consent and disclosure, the principal may\n    **repudiate the transaction** where either (a) a material fact has been\n    **dishonestly concealed**, or (b) the dealing has been **disadvantageous to him**. Here A\n    dishonestly concealed his ownership — the classic case under clause (a). Additionally, under\n    **s. 216**, \"if an agent, without the knowledge of his principal, deals in the business of the agency\n    on his own account instead of on account of his principal, the principal is entitled to\n    **claim from the agent any benefit which may have resulted to him from the transaction**\" — so P may\n    also claim A's profit on the sale.\n\n    **Note also s. 199:** had P's ratification been valid, he would have ratified the\n    *whole* transaction (including the higher price). But since the ratification itself fails under s. 198,\n    s. 199 does not arise.\n\n    **Conclusion:** The ratification is voidable at P's option and P may repudiate the purchase, recover\n    the price paid, and claim from A any profit A made. A also forfeits remuneration for the business he has misconducted\n    (**s. 220**).\n\n    **Alternative (where P had full knowledge):** if P had known that the goods were A's own and still\n    accepted, there would be a valid ratification but the transaction would stand as a sale by A to P with informed\n    consent; ss. 215–216 would not assist P. The answer turns entirely on *knowledge*."
