          "id": "spl-pyq-u2-3",
          "number": "Q3",
          "year": "2011",
          "marks": "5 marks",
          "paper": "Business Association–I (OC) · Q.1(b)",
          "type": "Essay",
          "unitNumber": 2,
          "unit": "Unit 2: Unit 2: Relations of Partners to One Another and to Third Parties",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic2_relations_of_partners.html",
          "anchorId": "spl-pyq-u2-3",
          "question": "**Majority rights.**",
          "modelAnswer": "**Source: s. 12(c), PA 1932** — \"Subject to contract between the partners … any difference arising\n    as to **ordinary matters** connected with the business may be decided by a\n    **majority of the partners**, and every partner shall have the right to express his opinion before the\n    matter is decided, but no change may be made in the nature of the business without the consent of all the\n    partners.\"\n\n    \n\n      **Scope.** Only differences as to **ordinary matters** connected with the business\n      — day-to-day trading decisions: how much stock to buy, whom to employ, what price to quote.\n      **What \"majority\" means.** A majority of the **partners**, not a majority of the\n      profit-sharing ratio, unless the deed provides otherwise. In a two-partner firm there is no majority and any\n      difference is a deadlock.\n      **The mandatory prior hearing.** \"Every partner shall have the right to express his opinion\n      before the matter is decided.\" A majority resolution passed without hearing a partner is bad.\n      **The two exceptions where a majority is not enough.**\n        \n\n          - **Change in the nature of the business → unanimity** (s. 12(c), second limb).\n\n          **Introduction of a new partner → consent of all** (s. 31(1)); likewise\n          **variation of the partnership contract** (s. 11(1)); and\n          **admission of a minor to the benefits** (s. 30(1)).\n        \n\n      \n      **Effect against outsiders.** A decision properly taken by majority is an act of the firm and, if\n      in the usual way of the business, binds the firm under **s. 19(1)**. But a private restriction agreed\n      among the partners does **not** bind a third party who does not know of it\n      (**s. 20**).\n      **Remedy where the majority acts oppressively.** Dissolution by the court under\n      **s. 44(d)** (persistent breach / conduct making it not reasonably practicable to carry on) or\n      **s. 44(g)** (just and equitable).\n    \n\n    **Example.** Three partners run a printing press. Two resolve, after hearing the third, to buy a\n    new press. That binds the firm. If the two then resolve to start a catering business in the same premises, the\n    third can restrain them — s. 12(c) requires his consent.\n\n    **Other matters requiring unanimity:** s. 12(c) (nature of business), s. 11(1) (variation),\n    s. 31(1) (introduction), s. 30(1) (minor), s. 40 (dissolution by agreement), s. 32(1)(a) (retirement by consent).",
          "answer": "**Source: s. 12(c), PA 1932** — \"Subject to contract between the partners … any difference arising\n    as to **ordinary matters** connected with the business may be decided by a\n    **majority of the partners**, and every partner shall have the right to express his opinion before the\n    matter is decided, but no change may be made in the nature of the business without the consent of all the\n    partners.\"\n\n    \n\n      **Scope.** Only differences as to **ordinary matters** connected with the business\n      — day-to-day trading decisions: how much stock to buy, whom to employ, what price to quote.\n      **What \"majority\" means.** A majority of the **partners**, not a majority of the\n      profit-sharing ratio, unless the deed provides otherwise. In a two-partner firm there is no majority and any\n      difference is a deadlock.\n      **The mandatory prior hearing.** \"Every partner shall have the right to express his opinion\n      before the matter is decided.\" A majority resolution passed without hearing a partner is bad.\n      **The two exceptions where a majority is not enough.**\n        \n\n          - **Change in the nature of the business → unanimity** (s. 12(c), second limb).\n\n          **Introduction of a new partner → consent of all** (s. 31(1)); likewise\n          **variation of the partnership contract** (s. 11(1)); and\n          **admission of a minor to the benefits** (s. 30(1)).\n        \n\n      \n      **Effect against outsiders.** A decision properly taken by majority is an act of the firm and, if\n      in the usual way of the business, binds the firm under **s. 19(1)**. But a private restriction agreed\n      among the partners does **not** bind a third party who does not know of it\n      (**s. 20**).\n      **Remedy where the majority acts oppressively.** Dissolution by the court under\n      **s. 44(d)** (persistent breach / conduct making it not reasonably practicable to carry on) or\n      **s. 44(g)** (just and equitable).\n    \n\n    **Example.** Three partners run a printing press. Two resolve, after hearing the third, to buy a\n    new press. That binds the firm. If the two then resolve to start a catering business in the same premises, the\n    third can restrain them — s. 12(c) requires his consent.\n\n    **Other matters requiring unanimity:** s. 12(c) (nature of business), s. 11(1) (variation),\n    s. 31(1) (introduction), s. 30(1) (minor), s. 40 (dissolution by agreement), s. 32(1)(a) (retirement by consent)."
