          "id": "spl-pyq-u2-21",
          "number": "Q21",
          "year": "2017",
          "marks": "10 marks",
          "paper": "LB-304 Special Contracts · Q.9",
          "type": "Essay",
          "unitNumber": 2,
          "unit": "Unit 2: Unit 2: Relations of Partners to One Another and to Third Parties",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic2_relations_of_partners.html",
          "anchorId": "spl-pyq-u2-21",
          "question": "**Distinguish between a partnership firm and a Limited Liability Partnership.**\n    (Asked in: 2017, 2015, 2014, 2013, 2012, 2011, 2010)",
          "modelAnswer": "| Basis | Partnership (PA 1932) | LLP (LLP Act 2008) |\n| Governing law | Indian Partnership Act, 1932 (and the ICA,1872 in default: s. 3 ) | Limited Liability Partnership Act, 2008; the PA 1932 does not apply ( s. 4, LLP Act ) |\n| Legal personality | No separate legal personality — a compendious name for the partners | Body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal ( s. 3(1) ); may sue and be sued, and hold property, in its own name |\n| Liability | Unlimited ; every partner jointly and severally liable ( s. 25 ) | Limited to the extent of his agreed contribution ( s. 26 ); an unlimited LLP is permitted under s. 26(2) but requires registration with that designation |\n| Mutual agency | Essential: each is principal and agent for the others ( s. 4 , s. 18 ) | Not essential; liability for another partner's wrongful act is limited to the LLP's loss of property ( s. 29 ) |\n| Formation | By agreement — oral or written ( s. 5 ); registration optional ( s. 56 ) | Only by incorporation with the Registrar ( s. 11–12 ); registration is mandatory |\n| Number | Minimum 2; maximum 10 for a banking business and 20 otherwise (s. 11, Companies Act 2013, r. 10 of the Companies (Miscellaneous) Rules 2014) | Minimum 2 designated partners ( s. 7(1) , at least one resident in India — s. 7(3)); no maximum |\n| Minors | May be admitted to the benefits ( s. 30 ) | Not permitted — a minor cannot be a partner or designated partner |\n| Transfer of interest | Possible with consent of all ( s. 31 ); s. 29 gives the transferee only economic rights | Possible through assignment of rights as per the LLP agreement ( s. 42 ) |\n| Winding up | By agreement, contingencies, court, or notice ( ss. 40–44 ) | Only under the LLP Act, 2008 — voluntary or by the Tribunal ( ss. 63–65 ) |\n| Compliance | Light — registration optional, no mandatory filings | Heavy — Annual Return (Form 11) , Statement of Account & Solvency (Form 8) , income-tax return, audit where turnover > ₹40 lakh or contribution > ₹25 lakh ( s. 34(1) , r. 24) |\n| Foreign nationals | Permitted | Permitted, subject to FDI policy; one designated partner must be resident in India |\n\n    \n    **Also state for marks.** (i) **Conversion** — a firm may be converted into an LLP\n    under **s. 55** and **Sch. II** of the LLP Act, and where the\n    **secured creditors' consent** (not less than three-fourths in value) is obtained, the assets vest in\n    the LLP under **s. 55(8)** — a \"no additional liability\" conversion.\n    (ii) The **limitation under s. 29 LLP Act**: \"An obligation of the limited liability partnership\n    … shall arise from an act or omission of its partners… but the liability of a partner… shall be limited to the\n    liability of the LLP to the extent of its property…\" — a far narrower liability than\n    **s. 25 of the PA 1932**.\n    (iii) The **residual application of Indian law**: under **s. 62** read with\n    **s. 67**, Indian law applies where more than 50% of the contribution is owned by resident Indians\n    or where the LLP is controlled in India.\n\n    **Conclusion.** The decisive differences are\n    **separate legal personality**, **limited liability**,\n    **the absence of mutual agency**, and **mandatory incorporation and filing** —\n    all of which the PA 1932 lacks.\n\n    **Marks map:** any 8 of the 11 distinctions (8) + s. 4 and s. 26/s. 29 LLP Act citations (1) +\n    conversion under s. 55 (1).",
          "answer": "| Basis | Partnership (PA 1932) | LLP (LLP Act 2008) |\n| Governing law | Indian Partnership Act, 1932 (and the ICA,1872 in default: s. 3 ) | Limited Liability Partnership Act, 2008; the PA 1932 does not apply ( s. 4, LLP Act ) |\n| Legal personality | No separate legal personality — a compendious name for the partners | Body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal ( s. 3(1) ); may sue and be sued, and hold property, in its own name |\n| Liability | Unlimited ; every partner jointly and severally liable ( s. 25 ) | Limited to the extent of his agreed contribution ( s. 26 ); an unlimited LLP is permitted under s. 26(2) but requires registration with that designation |\n| Mutual agency | Essential: each is principal and agent for the others ( s. 4 , s. 18 ) | Not essential; liability for another partner's wrongful act is limited to the LLP's loss of property ( s. 29 ) |\n| Formation | By agreement — oral or written ( s. 5 ); registration optional ( s. 56 ) | Only by incorporation with the Registrar ( s. 11–12 ); registration is mandatory |\n| Number | Minimum 2; maximum 10 for a banking business and 20 otherwise (s. 11, Companies Act 2013, r. 10 of the Companies (Miscellaneous) Rules 2014) | Minimum 2 designated partners ( s. 7(1) , at least one resident in India — s. 7(3)); no maximum |\n| Minors | May be admitted to the benefits ( s. 30 ) | Not permitted — a minor cannot be a partner or designated partner |\n| Transfer of interest | Possible with consent of all ( s. 31 ); s. 29 gives the transferee only economic rights | Possible through assignment of rights as per the LLP agreement ( s. 42 ) |\n| Winding up | By agreement, contingencies, court, or notice ( ss. 40–44 ) | Only under the LLP Act, 2008 — voluntary or by the Tribunal ( ss. 63–65 ) |\n| Compliance | Light — registration optional, no mandatory filings | Heavy — Annual Return (Form 11) , Statement of Account & Solvency (Form 8) , income-tax return, audit where turnover > ₹40 lakh or contribution > ₹25 lakh ( s. 34(1) , r. 24) |\n| Foreign nationals | Permitted | Permitted, subject to FDI policy; one designated partner must be resident in India |\n\n    \n    **Also state for marks.** (i) **Conversion** — a firm may be converted into an LLP\n    under **s. 55** and **Sch. II** of the LLP Act, and where the\n    **secured creditors' consent** (not less than three-fourths in value) is obtained, the assets vest in\n    the LLP under **s. 55(8)** — a \"no additional liability\" conversion.\n    (ii) The **limitation under s. 29 LLP Act**: \"An obligation of the limited liability partnership\n    … shall arise from an act or omission of its partners… but the liability of a partner… shall be limited to the\n    liability of the LLP to the extent of its property…\" — a far narrower liability than\n    **s. 25 of the PA 1932**.\n    (iii) The **residual application of Indian law**: under **s. 62** read with\n    **s. 67**, Indian law applies where more than 50% of the contribution is owned by resident Indians\n    or where the LLP is controlled in India.\n\n    **Conclusion.** The decisive differences are\n    **separate legal personality**, **limited liability**,\n    **the absence of mutual agency**, and **mandatory incorporation and filing** —\n    all of which the PA 1932 lacks.\n\n    **Marks map:** any 8 of the 11 distinctions (8) + s. 4 and s. 26/s. 29 LLP Act citations (1) +\n    conversion under s. 55 (1)."
