          "id": "spl-pyq-u5-7",
          "number": "Q7",
          "year": "2017 · 2012",
          "marks": "2017 Q2(b) · 2012 CT OC Q2",
          "paper": "Special Contracts · CT OC",
          "type": "Problem & Analytical",
          "unitNumber": 5,
          "unit": "Unit 5: Unit 5: Formation of Contracts of Sale",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic5_formation_contracts_sale.html",
          "anchorId": "setC2",
          "question": "2017 Q2(b)\n    Can the following be the subject matter of sale under the Sale of Goods Act, 1930?\n    (i) Electricity  (ii) Actionable claim  (iii) Standing timber.\n    \n\n    2012 CT OC Q2\n    State whether the following are subject matter of sale: (i) Electricity (ii) Currency in circulation\n    (iii) Actionable Claims (iv) Human organs.",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answer — the four-item version (2012), with 2017 items marked\n    \n    \n    \n| Item | Goods? | Reason and authority |\n| (i) Electricity 2017 + 2015 + 2012 | YES | s. 2(7) — all kinds of moveable property; not excluded. It can be transmitted, transferred, delivered, stored, possessed and stolen ( s. 39, Electricity Act 1910 ). M.P. Electricity Board ((1969) 1 SCC 200). |\n| (ii) Currency in circulation 2012 | NO | It is &lsquo;money&rsquo; , expressly excluded by s. 2(7). Contrast an old or rare coin sold as a curio, which is goods ( 2015 ). |\n| (ii)/(iii) Actionable claim 2017 + 2012 | NO | Expressly excluded by s. 2(7). &lsquo;Actionable claim&rsquo; is defined in s. 3 of the ICA 1872 as a claim to any debt, or to any beneficial interest in moveable property not in the possession … of the claimant, which the civil courts recognise as affording grounds for relief. Examples: a book debt, a claim for damages, a share in a partnership (though note that s. 3 ICA itself excludes a partner's share from the definition). |\n| (iii) Standing timber 2017 | YES | Third limb of s. 2(7) — things attached to or forming part of the land agreed to be severed before sale or under the contract of sale . Compare s. 3, Transfer of Property Act, 1882 , which uses the same words. A shade/fruit tree sold in situ is not goods. |\n| (iv) Human organs 2012 | NO | Two independent reasons. (a) No marketability — the Delhi Cloth Mills test is not satisfied. (b) Commercial dealing in human organs is a criminal offence under the Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act, 1994 (ss. 9, 18, 19), so any such contract would also be void under s. 23 of the ICA 1872 as involving unlawful consideration or object. |\n\n    \n    **Exam technique.** Two things lift this answer. First, quote the\n    **s. 3 ICA definition of &lsquo;actionable claim&rsquo;** — most candidates assert the\n    exclusion without defining the term. Secondly, on human organs, give\n    *both* grounds (no market under Delhi Cloth Mills *and* illegality under the 1994 Act\n    read with s. 23 ICA); a single ground scores half.",
          "answer": "Model answer — the four-item version (2012), with 2017 items marked\n    \n    \n    \n| Item | Goods? | Reason and authority |\n| (i) Electricity 2017 + 2015 + 2012 | YES | s. 2(7) — all kinds of moveable property; not excluded. It can be transmitted, transferred, delivered, stored, possessed and stolen ( s. 39, Electricity Act 1910 ). M.P. Electricity Board ((1969) 1 SCC 200). |\n| (ii) Currency in circulation 2012 | NO | It is &lsquo;money&rsquo; , expressly excluded by s. 2(7). Contrast an old or rare coin sold as a curio, which is goods ( 2015 ). |\n| (ii)/(iii) Actionable claim 2017 + 2012 | NO | Expressly excluded by s. 2(7). &lsquo;Actionable claim&rsquo; is defined in s. 3 of the ICA 1872 as a claim to any debt, or to any beneficial interest in moveable property not in the possession … of the claimant, which the civil courts recognise as affording grounds for relief. Examples: a book debt, a claim for damages, a share in a partnership (though note that s. 3 ICA itself excludes a partner's share from the definition). |\n| (iii) Standing timber 2017 | YES | Third limb of s. 2(7) — things attached to or forming part of the land agreed to be severed before sale or under the contract of sale . Compare s. 3, Transfer of Property Act, 1882 , which uses the same words. A shade/fruit tree sold in situ is not goods. |\n| (iv) Human organs 2012 | NO | Two independent reasons. (a) No marketability — the Delhi Cloth Mills test is not satisfied. (b) Commercial dealing in human organs is a criminal offence under the Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act, 1994 (ss. 9, 18, 19), so any such contract would also be void under s. 23 of the ICA 1872 as involving unlawful consideration or object. |\n\n    \n    **Exam technique.** Two things lift this answer. First, quote the\n    **s. 3 ICA definition of &lsquo;actionable claim&rsquo;** — most candidates assert the\n    exclusion without defining the term. Secondly, on human organs, give\n    *both* grounds (no market under Delhi Cloth Mills *and* illegality under the 1994 Act\n    read with s. 23 ICA); a single ground scores half."
