          "unit": "Unit 5: Unit 5: Formation of Contracts of Sale",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic5_formation_contracts_sale.html",
          "anchorId": "setE3",
          "question": "Distinguish between the following with relevant cases: (a) Sale and contract for work and labour.",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answer — distinction table\n    \n    \n    \n| Point | Contract of sale | Contract for work and labour |\n| Subject of the contract | Goods as goods, identified or to be identified | Work, skill and labour; materials are accessory |\n| Property | General property in the goods passes under the contract (s. 4(1), s. 2(11)) | No property in the thing produced as a whole ever vests in the worker ( Purshottam Premji ) |\n| When the chattel exists | It exists, or is to exist, as the seller's property before delivery | It comes into existence only on the customer's premises and becomes his at once ( Sentinel ) |\n| Whose materials | The seller's | Often the customer's ( Hindustan Aeronautics — the President owned the material) |\n| Mode of passing | By transfer under the contract | By accretion / accession ( Gannon Dunkerley ) |\n| Remedy for non-payment | Suit for price (s. 55(1)) | Quantum meruit (s. 65 ICA; Appleby v. Myres ) |\n| Severability | May be severable where two distinct agreements exist | One and indivisible ( Gannon Dunkerley ) |\n| Taxability pre-1983 | Taxable | Not taxable — beyond Entry 48 / Entry 54 |\n| Taxability post-1983 | Taxable | Taxable on the deemed sale of the goods element, at value at incorporation (Art. 366(29-A)(b)) |\n\n    \n    **Cases to cite:**\n    *Gannon Dunkerley* (1959 SCR 379) · *Purshottam Premji* ((1970) 2 SCC 287) ·\n    *Man Industrial Corpn.* ((1969) 1 SCC 567) · *Hindustan Aeronautics*\n    (AIR 1972 SC 744) · *Sentinel Rolling Shutters* (AIR 1978 SC 545) ·\n    *Vanguard Rolling Shutters* ((1977) 2 SCC 250) · *Northern India Caterers*\n    ((1978) 4 SCC 36; review (1980) 2 SCR 650) · *Udipikrishna Bhawan* ((1981) 3 SCC 76) ·\n    *Larsen & Toubro* ((2014) 1 SCC 708) · *Kone Elevators* ((2014) 7 SCC 1) ·\n    *Pro Lab* (AIR 2015 SC 1098).\n\n    **Exam technique.** Nine rows and eleven cases is the\n    target. The last row (pre- and post-1983 taxability) is the one most candidates omit, and it is the\n    one that carries the constitutional marks.",
          "answer": "Model answer — distinction table\n    \n    \n    \n| Point | Contract of sale | Contract for work and labour |\n| Subject of the contract | Goods as goods, identified or to be identified | Work, skill and labour; materials are accessory |\n| Property | General property in the goods passes under the contract (s. 4(1), s. 2(11)) | No property in the thing produced as a whole ever vests in the worker ( Purshottam Premji ) |\n| When the chattel exists | It exists, or is to exist, as the seller's property before delivery | It comes into existence only on the customer's premises and becomes his at once ( Sentinel ) |\n| Whose materials | The seller's | Often the customer's ( Hindustan Aeronautics — the President owned the material) |\n| Mode of passing | By transfer under the contract | By accretion / accession ( Gannon Dunkerley ) |\n| Remedy for non-payment | Suit for price (s. 55(1)) | Quantum meruit (s. 65 ICA; Appleby v. Myres ) |\n| Severability | May be severable where two distinct agreements exist | One and indivisible ( Gannon Dunkerley ) |\n| Taxability pre-1983 | Taxable | Not taxable — beyond Entry 48 / Entry 54 |\n| Taxability post-1983 | Taxable | Taxable on the deemed sale of the goods element, at value at incorporation (Art. 366(29-A)(b)) |\n\n    \n    **Cases to cite:**\n    *Gannon Dunkerley* (1959 SCR 379) · *Purshottam Premji* ((1970) 2 SCC 287) ·\n    *Man Industrial Corpn.* ((1969) 1 SCC 567) · *Hindustan Aeronautics*\n    (AIR 1972 SC 744) · *Sentinel Rolling Shutters* (AIR 1978 SC 545) ·\n    *Vanguard Rolling Shutters* ((1977) 2 SCC 250) · *Northern India Caterers*\n    ((1978) 4 SCC 36; review (1980) 2 SCR 650) · *Udipikrishna Bhawan* ((1981) 3 SCC 76) ·\n    *Larsen & Toubro* ((2014) 1 SCC 708) · *Kone Elevators* ((2014) 7 SCC 1) ·\n    *Pro Lab* (AIR 2015 SC 1098).\n\n    **Exam technique.** Nine rows and eleven cases is the\n    target. The last row (pre- and post-1983 taxability) is the one most candidates omit, and it is the\n    one that carries the constitutional marks."
