          "question": "Discuss the rule of &lsquo;*Caveat Emptor*&rsquo; and the exceptions to the rule, if any,\n    **with the help of decided cases**.",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answer\n    Identical to Set D2, but the words &ldquo;with the help of decided\n    cases&rdquo; require you to **attach one authority to every proposition**. Do it like\n    this: rule → s. 16 opening words + *Parkinson v. Lee* (2 East 314, cited in\n    *Jones v. Just*: goods *in esse*, inspectable, no fraud → caveat emptor applies even for\n    a latent defect). Exception 1 → *Priest v. Last*. Exception 2 → *Jones v. Just* and\n    *Bristol Tramways*. Exception 3 → *Wallis v. Pratt*. Exception 4 →\n    *British Paints*. Exception 5 → *Niblett*. Exception 7 → ICA ss. 17–19. Limit →\n    *Jones v. Just*. Shift → *Grant*. Nine authorities, one each, and the answer is\n    complete at about 450 words.\n\n    **Exam technique.** Make a rule for yourself:\n    every time a question says &ldquo;with the help of decided cases&rdquo;, your answer should contain a\n    **case against every numbered point**. It is the easiest way to convert a pass answer\n    into a first, because it is mechanical.",
          "answer": "Model answer\n    Identical to Set D2, but the words &ldquo;with the help of decided\n    cases&rdquo; require you to **attach one authority to every proposition**. Do it like\n    this: rule → s. 16 opening words + *Parkinson v. Lee* (2 East 314, cited in\n    *Jones v. Just*: goods *in esse*, inspectable, no fraud → caveat emptor applies even for\n    a latent defect). Exception 1 → *Priest v. Last*. Exception 2 → *Jones v. Just* and\n    *Bristol Tramways*. Exception 3 → *Wallis v. Pratt*. Exception 4 →\n    *British Paints*. Exception 5 → *Niblett*. Exception 7 → ICA ss. 17–19. Limit →\n    *Jones v. Just*. Shift → *Grant*. Nine authorities, one each, and the answer is\n    complete at about 450 words.\n\n    **Exam technique.** Make a rule for yourself:\n    every time a question says &ldquo;with the help of decided cases&rdquo;, your answer should contain a\n    **case against every numbered point**. It is the easiest way to convert a pass answer\n    into a first, because it is mechanical."
