          "anchorId": "setE3",
          "question": "**Q4.** Attempt briefly any two of: (a) **Condition as to title**\n     (b) Hire-Purchase Agreements  (c) Rules relating to delivery.\n\n    **Q8.** Write brief notes on any two of: (a) Nature of contract for works/labour\n     (b) Rules relating to auction sale  (c) Sale in market overt\n     (d) **Condition and Warranty**.",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answers\n    **Q4(a) Condition as to title** — see the note at Set E2(a) above; about\n    170 words, ending with *Rowland v. Divall* and *Niblett*.\n\n    **Q8(d) Condition and Warranty** — a &lsquo;note&rsquo; version of Set A. Four\n    sentences: **(i)** s. 12(1) sets up the binary; **(ii)** s. 12(2) condition\n    = essential to the main purpose, breach gives the right to treat the contract as repudiated;\n    **(iii)** s. 12(3) warranty = collateral to the main purpose, breach gives damages but no\n    right to reject; **(iv)** s. 12(4) — classification depends on construction and\n    &ldquo;a stipulation may be a condition, though called a warranty in the contract&rdquo;\n    (*Wallis v. Pratt*). Add the table of statutory examples: conditions in ss. 14(a), 15, 16(1),\n    16(2) and 17(2); warranties in ss. 14(b), 14(c) and 16(3). Finish with s. 13: waiver, election,\n    acceptance and impossibility.\n\n    **Exam technique.** A note on\n    &lsquo;Condition and Warranty&rsquo; asked in a *two-of-four* list carries about 7–8 marks,\n    not 15. Resist the temptation to write your full Set A answer. Quote the three sub-sections, give the\n    examples, cite one case.",
          "answer": "Model answers\n    **Q4(a) Condition as to title** — see the note at Set E2(a) above; about\n    170 words, ending with *Rowland v. Divall* and *Niblett*.\n\n    **Q8(d) Condition and Warranty** — a &lsquo;note&rsquo; version of Set A. Four\n    sentences: **(i)** s. 12(1) sets up the binary; **(ii)** s. 12(2) condition\n    = essential to the main purpose, breach gives the right to treat the contract as repudiated;\n    **(iii)** s. 12(3) warranty = collateral to the main purpose, breach gives damages but no\n    right to reject; **(iv)** s. 12(4) — classification depends on construction and\n    &ldquo;a stipulation may be a condition, though called a warranty in the contract&rdquo;\n    (*Wallis v. Pratt*). Add the table of statutory examples: conditions in ss. 14(a), 15, 16(1),\n    16(2) and 17(2); warranties in ss. 14(b), 14(c) and 16(3). Finish with s. 13: waiver, election,\n    acceptance and impossibility.\n\n    **Exam technique.** A note on\n    &lsquo;Condition and Warranty&rsquo; asked in a *two-of-four* list carries about 7–8 marks,\n    not 15. Resist the temptation to write your full Set A answer. Quote the three sub-sections, give the\n    examples, cite one case."
