          "id": "spl-pyq-u7-2",
          "number": "Q2",
          "year": "2012",
          "marks": "Q5(a) · the fullest version",
          "paper": "CT · OC",
          "type": "Essay",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: Unit 7: Effects of Contract of Sale: Transfer of Property & Nemo Dat",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic7_effects_of_contract_nemo_dat.html",
          "anchorId": "setA2",
          "question": "What do you understand by the maxim, &lsquo;Nemo dat quod non habet&rsquo;?\n    **Discuss the exceptions to this rule with decided cases.**",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answer\n    **Introduction.** [Paras 1–4 of Set A1 above, compressed into six\n    lines.] The maxim is enacted in **s. 27** of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930; it is\n    subject, by the section's own opening words, to the five statutory exceptions in ss. 28–30 and\n    to the mercantile-agent proviso.\n\n    **The exceptions.** Deal with each in the same four-part shape:\n    *text — conditions — authority — illustration*.\n\n    \n    \n    \n| Exception | Conditions | Decided case |\n| 1. Estoppel (inside s. 27) | Representation by the owner; reliance by the buyer; detriment (payment); no notice | Central Newbury Car Auctions v. Unity Finance [1957] 1 QB 371 · contrast Moorgate Mercantile v. Twitchings [1977] AC 890 |\n| 2. Mercantile agent (proviso to s. 27) | Mercantile agent (s. 2(9)); possession as such ; with the owner's consent; acting in the ordinary course of business; buyer in good faith without notice | Case 36, Pearson v. Rose & Young · Folkes v. King [1923] 1 KB 282 · Lowther v. Harris [1927] 1 KB 393 |\n| 3. Joint owner (s. 28) | Several joint owners; sole possession; by permission of the co-owners; buyer in good faith without notice | Mulla's illustration (A, B, C own cattle; A has the cow and sells to D); applied to a joint Hindu family |\n| 4. Voidable title (s. 29) | Possession under a contract voidable under s. 19 or s. 19A, Contract Act; not yet rescinded at the time of the sale; buyer in good faith without notice | Phillips v. Brooks [1919] 2 KB 243 · Lewis v. Averay [1972] 1 QB 198 · Butterworth v. Kingsway Motors [1954] 1 WLR 1286 |\n| 5. Seller in possession (s. 30(1)) | Having sold, he continues or is in possession of goods or a document of title ; sale, pledge or other disposition; transferee in good faith without notice of the previous sale | Pacific Motor Auctions v. Motor Credits [1965] AC 867 (PC) |\n| 6. Buyer in possession (s. 30(2)) | Having bought or agreed to buy , he obtained possession with the seller's consent ; transferee in good faith without notice of any lien or other right of the original seller | Worcester Works Finance v. Cooden Engineering [1972] 1 QB 210, approved in National Employers' Mutual v. Jones [1990] 1 AC 24 |\n\n    \n    **Then say what is *not* an exception.**\n    **Sale in market overt is not part of Indian law.** The Sale of Goods Act, 1930\n    contains no such provision; the Select Committee rejected it because it\n    &ldquo;would make British India an asylum for cattle stealers from the native States&rdquo;\n    (Mulla); and England itself abolished the rule by the\n    **Sale of Goods (Amendment) Act 1994**, in force **3 January 1995**.\n\n    **Conclusion.** The unifying principle: in each of the six exceptions the true owner\n    has, by his own voluntary act, **armed the seller with the appearance of ownership**.\n    Where he has done nothing — the goods were stolen — s. 27 bites and the buyer loses.\n\n    **Exam technique.** This is a 12–15-mark question and\n    the table does half the work. Quote **s. 27 verbatim** — the examiner is marking\n    statutory accuracy. Then the table. Then *Pearson*. Then the market-overt point, which is the\n    mark almost everyone misses.",
          "answer": "Model answer\n    **Introduction.** [Paras 1–4 of Set A1 above, compressed into six\n    lines.] The maxim is enacted in **s. 27** of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930; it is\n    subject, by the section's own opening words, to the five statutory exceptions in ss. 28–30 and\n    to the mercantile-agent proviso.\n\n    **The exceptions.** Deal with each in the same four-part shape:\n    *text — conditions — authority — illustration*.\n\n    \n    \n    \n| Exception | Conditions | Decided case |\n| 1. Estoppel (inside s. 27) | Representation by the owner; reliance by the buyer; detriment (payment); no notice | Central Newbury Car Auctions v. Unity Finance [1957] 1 QB 371 · contrast Moorgate Mercantile v. Twitchings [1977] AC 890 |\n| 2. Mercantile agent (proviso to s. 27) | Mercantile agent (s. 2(9)); possession as such ; with the owner's consent; acting in the ordinary course of business; buyer in good faith without notice | Case 36, Pearson v. Rose & Young · Folkes v. King [1923] 1 KB 282 · Lowther v. Harris [1927] 1 KB 393 |\n| 3. Joint owner (s. 28) | Several joint owners; sole possession; by permission of the co-owners; buyer in good faith without notice | Mulla's illustration (A, B, C own cattle; A has the cow and sells to D); applied to a joint Hindu family |\n| 4. Voidable title (s. 29) | Possession under a contract voidable under s. 19 or s. 19A, Contract Act; not yet rescinded at the time of the sale; buyer in good faith without notice | Phillips v. Brooks [1919] 2 KB 243 · Lewis v. Averay [1972] 1 QB 198 · Butterworth v. Kingsway Motors [1954] 1 WLR 1286 |\n| 5. Seller in possession (s. 30(1)) | Having sold, he continues or is in possession of goods or a document of title ; sale, pledge or other disposition; transferee in good faith without notice of the previous sale | Pacific Motor Auctions v. Motor Credits [1965] AC 867 (PC) |\n| 6. Buyer in possession (s. 30(2)) | Having bought or agreed to buy , he obtained possession with the seller's consent ; transferee in good faith without notice of any lien or other right of the original seller | Worcester Works Finance v. Cooden Engineering [1972] 1 QB 210, approved in National Employers' Mutual v. Jones [1990] 1 AC 24 |\n\n    \n    **Then say what is *not* an exception.**\n    **Sale in market overt is not part of Indian law.** The Sale of Goods Act, 1930\n    contains no such provision; the Select Committee rejected it because it\n    &ldquo;would make British India an asylum for cattle stealers from the native States&rdquo;\n    (Mulla); and England itself abolished the rule by the\n    **Sale of Goods (Amendment) Act 1994**, in force **3 January 1995**.\n\n    **Conclusion.** The unifying principle: in each of the six exceptions the true owner\n    has, by his own voluntary act, **armed the seller with the appearance of ownership**.\n    Where he has done nothing — the goods were stolen — s. 27 bites and the buyer loses.\n\n    **Exam technique.** This is a 12–15-mark question and\n    the table does half the work. Quote **s. 27 verbatim** — the examiner is marking\n    statutory accuracy. Then the table. Then *Pearson*. Then the market-overt point, which is the\n    mark almost everyone misses."
