          "id": "spl-pyq-u7-13",
          "number": "Q13",
          "year": "2013",
          "marks": "Q6(b) · &ldquo;Attempt briefly&rdquo; — a five-mark note",
          "paper": "CT · OC",
          "type": "Essay",
          "unitNumber": 7,
          "unit": "Unit 7: Unit 7: Effects of Contract of Sale: Transfer of Property & Nemo Dat",
          "file": "sem 3/spl contract/topic7_effects_of_contract_nemo_dat.html",
          "anchorId": "setD1",
          "question": "**Sale of unascertained goods.** [Attempt briefly any two of: (a) Risk follows property\n    · (b) Sale of unascertained goods · (c) Distinction between lien and stoppage in transit.]",
          "modelAnswer": "Model answer\n    **1. The rule — s. 18, verbatim.** &ldquo;Where there is a contract for the sale of\n    unascertained goods, no property in the goods is transferred to the buyer unless and until\n    the goods are ascertained.&rdquo; This is a\n    **condition precedent**, not merely a rule about timing.\n\n    **2. What &lsquo;unascertained&rsquo; means.** Goods not\n    &ldquo;identified and agreed upon at the time a contract of sale is made&rdquo;\n    (**s. 2(14)**, defining *specific* goods). Two kinds:\n    **generic** goods not yet picked out (10 tonnes of wheat), and\n    **future** goods — **s. 2(6)**, &ldquo;goods to be manufactured or\n    produced or acquired by the seller after the making of the contract of sale&rdquo;. By\n    **s. 6(3)** a purported present sale of future goods\n    &ldquo;operates as an **agreement to sell**&rdquo;.\n\n    **3. How they become ascertained — s. 23(1).** Goods of the contract description, in\n    a deliverable state, **unconditionally appropriated** to the contract by one party\n    **with the assent** of the other (express or implied, before or after the act).\n    **s. 23(2)**: delivery to the buyer, or to a carrier or other bailee for transmission,\n    *without reserving the right of disposal*, is\n    **deemed** unconditional appropriation. The second route is\n    **ascertainment by exhaustion** — where the whole of an identified bulk is sold, so no\n    selection is needed (*Wait & James v. Midland Bank* (1926) 31 Com Cas 172).\n\n    **4. The consequences of remaining unascertained.** The buyer has\n    **no proprietary interest at all** — not legal, not equitable — even if he has paid in\n    full. On the seller's insolvency he is an **ordinary unsecured creditor**\n    (*Re Wait* [1927] 1 Ch 606). The seller commits no conversion by refusing to deliver. And,\n    critically, **the seller has no right of resale under s. 54(2)** —\n    **Case 34**.\n\n    **5. The authority.** Case 34, P.S.N.S. Ambalavana Chettiar v. Express\n    Newspapers Ltd., AIR 1968 SC 741: a contract to buy\n    **300 tons out of a larger stock of 415 tons** is a contract for unascertained goods.\n    &ldquo;The condition is not fulfilled where there is a contract for sale of a portion of a\n    specified larger stock. Till the portion is identified and appropriated to the contract, no property\n    passes to the buyer.&rdquo; Quoting Bayley B. in *Gillett v. Hill* (1814) 149 ER 871:\n    &ldquo;There is **no individuality until it has been divided**.&rdquo;\n    *Note:* India has **no** equivalent of ss. 20A–20B of the English Sale of\n    Goods Act 1979, which give a prepaying buyer of an undivided share in an identified bulk a\n    statutory co-ownership.\n\n    **Exam technique.** &lsquo;Attempt\n    briefly&rsquo; means **ten to twelve lines, not three pages**. Five numbered points —\n    text, meaning, ascertainment, consequences, authority — is exactly the right length. Do not\n    write the s. 20–22 rules; they are about *specific* goods.",
          "answer": "Model answer\n    **1. The rule — s. 18, verbatim.** &ldquo;Where there is a contract for the sale of\n    unascertained goods, no property in the goods is transferred to the buyer unless and until\n    the goods are ascertained.&rdquo; This is a\n    **condition precedent**, not merely a rule about timing.\n\n    **2. What &lsquo;unascertained&rsquo; means.** Goods not\n    &ldquo;identified and agreed upon at the time a contract of sale is made&rdquo;\n    (**s. 2(14)**, defining *specific* goods). Two kinds:\n    **generic** goods not yet picked out (10 tonnes of wheat), and\n    **future** goods — **s. 2(6)**, &ldquo;goods to be manufactured or\n    produced or acquired by the seller after the making of the contract of sale&rdquo;. By\n    **s. 6(3)** a purported present sale of future goods\n    &ldquo;operates as an **agreement to sell**&rdquo;.\n\n    **3. How they become ascertained — s. 23(1).** Goods of the contract description, in\n    a deliverable state, **unconditionally appropriated** to the contract by one party\n    **with the assent** of the other (express or implied, before or after the act).\n    **s. 23(2)**: delivery to the buyer, or to a carrier or other bailee for transmission,\n    *without reserving the right of disposal*, is\n    **deemed** unconditional appropriation. The second route is\n    **ascertainment by exhaustion** — where the whole of an identified bulk is sold, so no\n    selection is needed (*Wait & James v. Midland Bank* (1926) 31 Com Cas 172).\n\n    **4. The consequences of remaining unascertained.** The buyer has\n    **no proprietary interest at all** — not legal, not equitable — even if he has paid in\n    full. On the seller's insolvency he is an **ordinary unsecured creditor**\n    (*Re Wait* [1927] 1 Ch 606). The seller commits no conversion by refusing to deliver. And,\n    critically, **the seller has no right of resale under s. 54(2)** —\n    **Case 34**.\n\n    **5. The authority.** Case 34, P.S.N.S. Ambalavana Chettiar v. Express\n    Newspapers Ltd., AIR 1968 SC 741: a contract to buy\n    **300 tons out of a larger stock of 415 tons** is a contract for unascertained goods.\n    &ldquo;The condition is not fulfilled where there is a contract for sale of a portion of a\n    specified larger stock. Till the portion is identified and appropriated to the contract, no property\n    passes to the buyer.&rdquo; Quoting Bayley B. in *Gillett v. Hill* (1814) 149 ER 871:\n    &ldquo;There is **no individuality until it has been divided**.&rdquo;\n    *Note:* India has **no** equivalent of ss. 20A–20B of the English Sale of\n    Goods Act 1979, which give a prepaying buyer of an undivided share in an identified bulk a\n    statutory co-ownership.\n\n    **Exam technique.** &lsquo;Attempt\n    briefly&rsquo; means **ten to twelve lines, not three pages**. Five numbered points —\n    text, meaning, ascertainment, consequences, authority — is exactly the right length. Do not\n    write the s. 20–22 rules; they are about *specific* goods."
