          "anchorId": "setD1",
          "question": "“Write notes on any two of the following, referring to case law to justify your answer: … (c) Unpaid seller & his rights.”",
          "modelAnswer": "Model short note (about 350–450 words)\n      **Who.** Section 45(1) deems a seller to be an unpaid seller when the whole of the price has not been paid or\n      tendered, or when a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument has been received as conditional payment and the condition has\n      failed by dishonour “or otherwise”. Section 45(2) extends the expression to any person in the position of a seller — an agent to\n      whom the bill of lading has been endorsed, a consignor, or an agent who has himself paid or is directly responsible for the price.\n      Part payment does not remove the seller from the section (*Longbottom & Co. Ltd. v. Bass, Walker & Co.* (1922) WN 245),\n      and a seller who has a decree for the price remains unpaid until it is satisfied (s. 49(2)).\n\n      **Rights against the goods — s. 46(1).** Even though the property has passed to the buyer, the unpaid seller has, by\n      implication of law, three rights: (a) a **lien** for the price while in possession (ss. 47–49); (b) a right of\n      **stoppage in transit** where the buyer has become insolvent and the seller has parted with possession (ss. 50–52);\n      and (c) a right of **re-sale** as limited by s. 54. If the property has not passed, s. 46(2) gives him a right of\n      withholding delivery, co-extensive with those rights. These rights are possessory and self-help in character and are therefore\n      valuable precisely when the buyer is insolvent, because the goods never enter the insolvent estate.\n\n      **Rights against the buyer personally.** The seller may sue for the **price** where the property has passed\n      or the price is payable on a day certain (s. 55), sue for **damages for non-acceptance** (s. 56), treat the contract as\n      repudiated before the due date (s. 60), and claim **interest and special damages** (s. 61) — all subject to s. 73 of the\n      Indian Contract Act, 1872, which requires him to mitigate.\n\n      **Limits.** The rights are subject to s. 53: a sub-sale of the goods does not affect them, but a transfer of a\n      document of title to a good-faith transferee for value defeats them if it is by way of sale and subordinates them if by way of\n      pledge (*Morvi Mercantile Bank Ltd. v. Union of India*, AIR 1965 SC 1954). Exercise of the lien or of stoppage does not\n      rescind the contract (s. 54(1)); the buyer may still pay and claim the goods. And the re-sale power in s. 54(2) is conditional on\n      perishability or notice, a reasonable time for payment and a re-sale within a reasonable time — as Mysore Sugar Co. Ltd. v.\n      Manohar Metal Industries, AIR 1982 Kant. 283 (DU Case 37) demonstrates, where a three-month delay in a falling market cost the\n      seller his damages.\n\n      **Examiner’s expectation:** a short note should have a definition, a three-part structure (rights against goods /\n      against buyer / limits), four or five sections quoted precisely, and at least three cases — *Longbottom*,\n      *Morvi Mercantile Bank* and *Mysore Sugar* would be ideal.",
          "answer": "Model short note (about 350–450 words)\n      **Who.** Section 45(1) deems a seller to be an unpaid seller when the whole of the price has not been paid or\n      tendered, or when a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument has been received as conditional payment and the condition has\n      failed by dishonour “or otherwise”. Section 45(2) extends the expression to any person in the position of a seller — an agent to\n      whom the bill of lading has been endorsed, a consignor, or an agent who has himself paid or is directly responsible for the price.\n      Part payment does not remove the seller from the section (*Longbottom & Co. Ltd. v. Bass, Walker & Co.* (1922) WN 245),\n      and a seller who has a decree for the price remains unpaid until it is satisfied (s. 49(2)).\n\n      **Rights against the goods — s. 46(1).** Even though the property has passed to the buyer, the unpaid seller has, by\n      implication of law, three rights: (a) a **lien** for the price while in possession (ss. 47–49); (b) a right of\n      **stoppage in transit** where the buyer has become insolvent and the seller has parted with possession (ss. 50–52);\n      and (c) a right of **re-sale** as limited by s. 54. If the property has not passed, s. 46(2) gives him a right of\n      withholding delivery, co-extensive with those rights. These rights are possessory and self-help in character and are therefore\n      valuable precisely when the buyer is insolvent, because the goods never enter the insolvent estate.\n\n      **Rights against the buyer personally.** The seller may sue for the **price** where the property has passed\n      or the price is payable on a day certain (s. 55), sue for **damages for non-acceptance** (s. 56), treat the contract as\n      repudiated before the due date (s. 60), and claim **interest and special damages** (s. 61) — all subject to s. 73 of the\n      Indian Contract Act, 1872, which requires him to mitigate.\n\n      **Limits.** The rights are subject to s. 53: a sub-sale of the goods does not affect them, but a transfer of a\n      document of title to a good-faith transferee for value defeats them if it is by way of sale and subordinates them if by way of\n      pledge (*Morvi Mercantile Bank Ltd. v. Union of India*, AIR 1965 SC 1954). Exercise of the lien or of stoppage does not\n      rescind the contract (s. 54(1)); the buyer may still pay and claim the goods. And the re-sale power in s. 54(2) is conditional on\n      perishability or notice, a reasonable time for payment and a re-sale within a reasonable time — as Mysore Sugar Co. Ltd. v.\n      Manohar Metal Industries, AIR 1982 Kant. 283 (DU Case 37) demonstrates, where a three-month delay in a falling market cost the\n      seller his damages.\n\n      **Examiner’s expectation:** a short note should have a definition, a three-part structure (rights against goods /\n      against buyer / limits), four or five sections quoted precisely, and at least three cases — *Longbottom*,\n      *Morvi Mercantile Bank* and *Mysore Sugar* would be ideal."
