          "htmlContent": "id=\"s13\"><span class=\"snum\">13</span><span class=\"stxt\"><small>Section 13</small>Revision sheet, tables and index</span></h2>\n\n<p class=\"lead\">Everything in the topic, compressed for the last week before the examination: the scheme in ten lines, the\nnumbers to remember, the mistakes to avoid, the comparison tables worth reproducing, and an index of every authority\nmentioned.</p>\n\n<h3 id=\"s13-1\"><span class=\"tick\">◆</span>13.1 The whole topic in ten propositions</h3>\n\n<div class=\"card\">\n<ol style=\"margin-bottom:0\">\n<li><strong>Chapter V (ss. 60–64) attributes income to a person who did not earn it</strong>, in four defined\nsituations, and s. 65 makes the earner liable for the tax attributable to it. It is a rule of computation, not of\nownership.</li>\n<li><strong>Three mechanisms:</strong> income transferred without the assets (s. 60); transfer of assets with a power\nreserved (ss. 61–63); relationship-based attribution (s. 64(1), 64(1A), 64(2)).</li>\n<li><strong>s. 60</strong> applies \"whether revocable or not and whether effected before or after the commencement of\nthis Act\", where there is <strong>no transfer of the assets</strong>. <strong>s. 61</strong> applies to a transfer of\nassets which keeps a string attached; the <strong>proviso</strong> excludes transfers irrevocable during the life of the\nbeneficiary or transferee (or pre-1961 transfers irrevocable for more than six years), provided the transferor takes no\ndirect or indirect benefit. <strong>s. 62</strong> fixes the date; <strong>s. 63</strong> defines \"transfer\" and\n\"revocable\" very widely.</li>\n<li><strong>s. 64(1)(ii)</strong> clubbing requires: an individual; a concern; substantial interest (≥20%, a\n<strong>floor</strong>); income to the <strong>spouse</strong> by way of <strong>remuneration</strong>; and failure of the\nproviso, whose two limbs are <strong>cumulative</strong>. Where both spouses qualify, both salaries are clubbed in the\nhands of the spouse with the <strong>greater</strong> total income.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Concern\" includes a profession and a proprietary concern; 100% is a substantial interest</strong> —\n<em>J.M. Mokashi</em>, reader pp. 159–160.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Technical or professional qualification\"</strong> need not be a degree from a recognised body\n(<em>Batta Kalyani</em>), but on the stricter and fuller view it must be the qualification <strong>necessary</strong> for\nthe particular profession or job, and knowledge and experience alone are not enough (<em>Mokashi</em>). On either view,\nthe income must be <strong>solely attributable</strong> to it — a question of fact on which the assessee bears the\nburden.</li>\n<li><strong>s. 64(1)(iv)</strong> clubs the income from assets transferred to the spouse otherwise than for adequate\nconsideration or in connection with an agreement to live apart; the relationship must subsist at <strong>both</strong>\nmaterial times (<em>Thomas</em>); \"indirectly\" reaches <strong>substituted</strong> assets provided the nexus is\n<strong>proximate</strong> (<em>Thapar</em>); and the clause is <strong>subject to s. 27(i)</strong> for house\nproperty.</li>\n<li><strong>s. 64(1)(vi)–(viii)</strong> extend the same idea to the son's wife (transfers on or after 1-6-1973) and to\ntransfers to any person for the benefit of the spouse or the son's wife, proportionately. <strong>s. 64(1A)</strong>\nclubs a minor child's income with the higher-income parent, subject to the s. 80U, manual-work and talent exceptions, and\n₹1,500 per child under s. 10(32).</li>\n<li><strong>s. 64(2)</strong> deems the conversion of separate property into HUF property to be a transfer <em>through\nthe family</em>; the income is the <strong>individual's \"and not the family's\"</strong>; and after a partition, partial\nor total, the income from the <strong>spouse's share</strong> is taxed on the <strong>spouse</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The judicial method is two-stage:</strong> a strict (<em>legal</em>) nexus question — is the case within the\nwords, the inc"
